/* Pearson Edexcel GCSE English Language (International GCSE, Specification A) (4EA1) — room kits built by hand in a Claude Code session: written by
   Opus 5 subagents to the contract in src/kit-validator.js, every question re-solved and the lesson read against
   the specification map by a fresh Opus 5 subagent before shipping. See .claude/skills/course-builder/references/depth.md. */
module.exports = { ID: 'EDX-4EA1', KITS: {"anthology-part1":{"id":"EDX-4EA1","topic":"anthology-part1","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section A of Component 1 is 45 of the paper’s 90 raw marks, and you are advised to give it 1 hour 30 minutes including reading time. The specification is explicit that “Candidates will be provided with the anthology text in the examination”, so nothing here is a memory test: what you carry into the hall is a ready-made reading of each Part 1 text — its writer, form, purpose, audience, methods and shape — so that you can spend your time selecting rather than deciding what the text is doing. Three questions do three different jobs: Question 1 is AO1 only and worth 11 marks, so it rewards selection and interpretation and gives nothing for naming devices; Question 2 is AO2 only and worth 12 marks, so it gives nothing for retelling; Question 3 is AO3 only and worth 22 marks, the single largest reading answer in the qualification and the only place AO3 is examined anywhere. Question 3 sets your anthology text against a previously unseen extract, which means half of that answer can be prepared in advance and half cannot — which is exactly why knowing these ten texts cold is worth so much.","idea":[{"code":"From The Danger of a Single Story","h":"Adichie: the single story, and who holds the power to tell it","t":"A talk turned into an essay, delivered by a Nigerian novelist to a largely Western audience, so its purpose is to persuade by disarming. Adichie argues through anecdote and then converts each anecdote into a proposition: the child who wrote stories full of white, blue-eyed characters playing in snow and eating apples, because those were the only books she had; the family of the houseboy Fide, whose poverty was the only thing she knew about them until she saw the beautiful basket his brother had made; the American room-mate astonished that she spoke English and disappointed by her pop music. She then implicates herself by admitting she once held a single story of Mexico. Her key move is the word nkali, which she glosses as being greater than another: stories, she says, are told by whoever has the power, and the trouble with a stereotype is not that it is false but that it is incomplete. A top-level answer treats the anecdotes as her evidence rather than her subject, and shows the structure moving outwards from a child’s bookshelf to a theory of power."},{"code":"From A Passage to Africa","h":"Alagiah: the smile that turns the reporter into the reported","t":"A BBC correspondent’s memoir of covering the Somali famine, written to show a reader at home what reporting atrocity does to the person doing it. The extract stacks up images of suffering in the village of Gufgaduud — among them a woman whose broken leg has been left to rot — and Alagiah is honest that he has learned to process such sights professionally, grading them by how much of a reaction they will get, because ordinary hunger no longer makes the news. All of that is set-up. The turn comes with a man who smiles at him, an embarrassed, apologetic smile, as though the shame of being found in that state belonged to the starving and not to the world watching. The reversal is the point: a reporter normally takes something away from the people he films, and here he is given something he cannot repay. A top-level answer does not treat the catalogue of horror as the content; it shows that the horror is the structure that makes one smile land."},{"code":"From The Explorer’s Daughter","h":"Herbert: beauty, need and a loyalty she refuses to resolve","t":"Kari Herbert grew up partly in Thule, in north-west Greenland, and returned as an adult; this extract watches a narwhal hunt in the fjord from the shore. Two registers run side by side. One is lyrical: the light on the water, the animals’ mystery and grace, the hunters in kayaks holding absolutely still. The other is informative and unsentimental: mattak, the narwhal skin, is one of the very few sources of vitamin C in a place where nothing grows, the hunt feeds families through the winter, and a wounded narwhal can kill the man who harpooned it. Herbert states her own divided feelings openly — she wills the animals to escape and knows what their escape would cost the community she is standing among. A top-level answer reads that division as a deliberate perspective rather than as indecision, and shows how the ordering of the extract, from beauty to necessity to danger, makes the reader hold both sides at once."},{"code":"Explorers, or boys messing about? Either way, taxpayer gets rescue bill","h":"Morris: a news report that keeps two verdicts alive at once","t":"A newspaper report from 2003 on Steve Brooks and Quentin Smith, whose helicopter came down in the sea off Antarctica during an attempt to fly from pole to pole; they got into a life raft and were picked up by a Chilean naval vessel. The headline does the work of an argument before the article begins: two labels, explorers and boys messing about, are offered as alternatives, and the second half of the headline settles the reader’s attention on the bill. The report then behaves like news — facts and location first, then quotation from those close to the men, then the wider question of who pays — so the scepticism is never asserted in the reporter’s own voice; it is built out of what is placed next to what, and out of the clash between the vocabulary of adventure and the vocabulary of cost. A top-level answer shows the viewpoint being engineered by selection, ordering and attribution rather than stated."},{"code":"From 127 Hours: Between a Rock and a Hard Place","h":"Ralston: panic and engineering in the same paragraph","t":"A first-person account of the accident in Blue John Canyon in Utah, in which a falling boulder pinned Aron Ralston’s hand against the canyon wall. The writing runs two voices at once. One is the body’s: he heaves uselessly at the rock, swears, screams, and the sentences shorten to the length of a breath. The other is the trained mechanical engineer’s: he names the chockstone, weighs it, works out what leverage he has and what is on his harness, and the sentences lengthen and take on technical vocabulary. The extract keeps cutting between them, so the reader feels the panic and is also made to think the problem through. A retrospective narrator sits above both, letting us know that the situation is worse than the man in it yet understands. A top-level answer describes that alternation as the pattern of the whole extract, not as two separate features."},{"code":"Young and Dyslexic? You've got it going on","h":"Zephaniah: speaking straight to the reader the system failed","t":"An article addressed to young dyslexic readers, and the address is the argument. Zephaniah sets the boy he was — written off at school, leaving at thirteen unable to read and write, treated as stupid or as trouble — against the adult he became, a poet, novelist and professor. From that gap he turns the charge round: the fault is not in the dyslexic child but in a system that can measure children only one way. The register is spoken and colloquial, right down to the youth slang of the title, because he wants to sound like an ally rather than an authority; the second person keeps a reader in the room; the anecdotes are short and each one is cashed in for a claim; and he relabels the condition, so that a supposed disability becomes a different, creative way of thinking. A top-level answer reads the voice as the method, and shows the piece ending in advice because it was always meant to be useful rather than only true."},{"code":"From A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat","h":"Levine: an event you are not allowed to watch calmly","t":"Travel writing about the unofficial sports of the region, this extract follows a donkey race on a road outside Karachi, watched from a car among the crowd. The comedy comes from the mismatch between what the writer expects and what happens: a long, hot, shapeless wait in which nobody will say when the race will start, then the sudden burst when it does, and then the discovery that the spectators’ cars and motorbikes chase the donkeys down the road so that the audience becomes part of the event. Levine writes the chaos with short clauses, verbs of movement, dust, horns and engine noise, and she keeps herself in the picture as a comic figure thrown about in the vehicle. The ending withholds the result — nobody finds out who won — and that anticlimax is the perspective: the point of the day was never the winner. A top-level answer shows that the shapelessness is controlled by a writer who has arranged it."},{"code":"From Beyond the Sky and Earth: A Journey into Bhutan","h":"Zeppa: fear first, then the place comes into focus","t":"A Canadian teacher’s memoir of arriving in Bhutan, and the extract is an arrival in the fullest sense: the flight in between mountains, the road that climbs through cloud and forest, prayer flags, unfamiliar houses, food, language and faces. What makes it more than a list is the movement of feeling underneath it. Zeppa is honest about being frightened and homesick, about wanting to be somewhere she understands, and the extract tracks the slow turn from alarm to attention and then to pleasure as she begins to see rather than merely notice. Her methods are the accumulation of precise detail, the naming of things she has no word for, comparison with the Canada she has left, and a sentence rhythm that slows as she settles. A top-level answer charts that movement and locates its turning point, instead of listing the sights in the order they appear."},{"code":"From H is for Hawk","h":"Macdonald: the wrong hawk out of the box","t":"A memoir of grief in which the writer buys and trains a goshawk; the extract is the collection of the bird from a breeder on a Scottish quayside. Macdonald delays the real hawk deliberately: the first bird lifted out of the box is the wrong one, and she describes it in a rush of imagery drawn from myth, from illuminated manuscripts and from reptiles, so that the creature is both holy and prehistoric and the two registers jar against each other. Close focus does the rest — the eye, the feathers, the feet — with short sentences breaking up long cumulative ones so that the prose keeps flinching. Only then is the smaller, right bird produced, and the reader, having waited, sees her through the writer’s already overloaded eyes. A top-level answer proves that the imagery is doing emotional work, carrying the writer’s fear and grief into a description that is nominally of a bird."},{"code":"From Chinese Cinderella","h":"Yen Mah: a child reading a father’s face","t":"An autobiography of a 1940s Chinese childhood, in a wealthy family that treats the writer as an inconvenience. In the extract she is summoned to her father’s study and assumes, as the household has taught her to assume, that she is in trouble; the reason for the summons is withheld from the reader as it is from her, so the suspense of the scene is structural. What has happened is that her school has written to say she has been elected class president, and her father is pleased. The adult writer keeps the child’s viewpoint and keeps the sentences plain, which is what makes the joy so exposed: the reward is tiny, and it is enough to make her dizzy. A top-level answer says why the smallness matters — it measures how little affection she has had — rather than simply reporting that she was happy."},{"code":"Component 1, Section A — the anthology text in the examination","h":"Section A: one text is given to you, the other you have never seen","t":"The specification says Section A sets “a mixture of short- and long-answer questions related to a non-fiction text from Part 1 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology and one previously unseen extract”, that all questions in the section are answered, that it carries 45 marks, and that “Candidates will be provided with the anthology text in the examination”. The tariffs tell you how to spend the time: Question 1 is 11 marks of AO1, Question 2 is 12 marks of AO2 and Question 3 is 22 marks of AO3, so roughly half of Section A is the comparison. The anthology is described as a resource providing examples of different types of non-fiction text, and students are encouraged to read beyond it. A top-level answer budgets by tariff, and arrives with the anthology half of Question 3 already thought through."}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning one moment into an AO1 point (Question 1, 11 marks)","setup":"Text One is the extract from A Passage to Africa. Question 1 is worth 11 marks and assesses AO1 only: “What do you understand about the writer’s feelings towards the people he is reporting on?” The moment you have chosen is the one near the end of the extract in which a starving man in Gufgaduud smiles at Alagiah — a faint, embarrassed, apologetic smile — and Alagiah cannot get it out of his mind afterwards. Build one paragraph on it. Remember that AO1 pays for selection, supporting reference and interpretation, and pays nothing for naming techniques.","steps":["Open with an interpretation of the feeling, not a description of the scene: his strongest feeling is not pity but discomfort at his own position.","Anchor it in the detail that the shame in the smile is the starving man’s and not the onlooker’s, which is precisely what Alagiah finds unbearable.","Interpret the reversal he draws from it: a reporter usually takes a story away from people, and here he is the one who has been given something.","Set that against the earlier feeling in the extract, where he admits to grading suffering by how much reaction it will produce back home.","Close the paragraph with what this tells us about him: the extract is an account of a professional being returned to himself by someone with nothing."],"cues":["Is your first sentence about the man or about Alagiah’s feelings?","Whose shame does the smile seem to carry, and why does that disturb him?","Which way does the usual traffic between reporter and subject run, and what happens to it here?","What feeling did he admit to earlier in the extract, and how does it contrast?","What is your one-sentence understanding of his feelings overall?"]},{"title":"Naming the device and proving the effect (Question 2, 12 marks)","setup":"Text One is the extract from H is for Hawk. Question 2 is worth 12 marks and assesses AO2 only: “How does the writer use language and structure to convey the effect the hawk has on her?” Look again at the section on the quayside where the breeder opens the first box and the wrong, larger hawk comes out, is described, and is then put away before the smaller bird Macdonald has come for is produced. Write one paragraph. AO2 pays for the selection of devices, the example and the explanation of the effect; it pays nothing for retelling the trip.","steps":["Take the structural choice first: the wrong hawk is lifted out before the right one, so the reader is made to wait and the arrival is charged before the real bird appears.","Show the effect of that delay: by the time the correct hawk is produced we are already looking through eyes that have been overwhelmed.","Move to the imagery, which pulls in two directions at once — the language of myth and illuminated manuscripts against the language of reptiles and prehistory.","Explain what the clash achieves: the bird is made sacred and inhuman in the same breath, so awe and fear arrive together and neither cancels the other.","Add the sentence work: short sentences interrupt long accumulating ones, so the prose keeps catching its breath, and finish by saying what all three devices are for."],"cues":["Which bird comes out first, and what does that ordering do to a reader?","What state is the reader in by the time the right hawk appears?","Which two very different fields is the imagery drawn from?","Why put the holy and the reptilian together rather than choosing one?","What do the sentence lengths add that the images cannot?"]},{"title":"One comparative paragraph for Question 3 (22 marks)","setup":"Question 3 is worth 22 marks and assesses AO3 only: “Refer to Text One and Text Two. Compare how the writers present their own reaction to something they are watching. Support your answer with detailed examples from both texts.” For practice, take Text One as the extract from The Explorer’s Daughter and Text Two as the extract from A Passage to Africa; in the examination Text Two will be an extract you have never seen. Two things the texts give you that you will need: Herbert explains that mattak, the narwhal skin, is one of the few sources of vitamin C available to the people of Thule, so the hunt she is willing to fail is also the hunt the community needs; and Alagiah admits that he had been grading the suffering he filmed by how much reaction it would get back home, ordinary hunger no longer counting as news, until the man’s apologetic smile. Write the first comparative paragraph, remembering that AO3 pays for links and connections between ideas and perspectives and for how those perspectives are conveyed.","steps":["Set the shared idea as the paragraph’s topic sentence: both writers are watching something they cannot intervene in, and both make their own discomfort part of the subject.","Give the Herbert side: she states her divided loyalty openly, willing the narwhal away while explaining that mattak is one of the few sources of vitamin C in Thule.","Give the Alagiah side on the same idea, not on a new one: his discomfort surfaces only when the man smiles, and he admits to having graded suffering professionally until then.","Weigh how each perspective is conveyed: Herbert braids lyrical description with factual explanation so the two loyalties sit in the same sentence, while Alagiah withholds his feeling and lets a single gesture detonate it.","End the paragraph with a comparative judgement: Herbert asks us to hold a contradiction, Alagiah asks us to watch one being forced on him."],"cues":["What single idea can carry both texts in one paragraph?","Which two things is Herbert holding at once, and what fact makes the hunt necessary?","At what moment does Alagiah’s discomfort finally surface?","Do the two writers deliver their discomfort in the same way, or on different schedules?","What is your comparative verdict in one sentence?"]},{"title":"Finding the viewpoint in a news report","setup":"Text One is the newspaper report headlined “Explorers, or boys messing about? Either way, taxpayer gets rescue bill”, about two British men whose helicopter came down in the sea off Antarctica and who were rescued by a Chilean naval vessel after getting into a life raft. Question 2 asks: “How does the writer use language and structure to present the two men?” Nobody in the article says outright that the men were foolish. Work out how the report gets its viewpoint across anyway.","steps":["Start with the headline, which offers two labels for the same men and makes the reader choose before a single fact has been reported.","Notice what the second half of the headline does: whichever label wins, the cost lands on someone who did not go, so the argument is framed as a question of money.","Track the ordering of the report: the events and the rescue come first, then the voices of those close to the men, then the question of who pays — the last position being the one a reader remembers.","Show the vocabulary working against itself, with the language of expedition and adventure sitting next to the language of bills, rescue services and taxpayers.","Finish by naming the technique: the scepticism is never in the reporter’s own voice, it is produced by what is placed beside what, which is what makes it hard to argue with."],"cues":["What has the headline already asked the reader to decide?","Who is the third party the headline brings into a story about two men?","Which idea does the report leave the reader with last?","Which two vocabularies are rubbing against each other?","If no sentence states the opinion, where does the opinion live?"]},{"title":"Structure as the answer: where the writing turns","setup":"Text One is the extract from 127 Hours: Between a Rock and a Hard Place, in which a boulder shifts in Blue John Canyon and traps Aron Ralston’s hand against the wall. Question 2 asks: “How does the writer use language and structure to convey his response to the accident?” A weak answer says that he was scared and that short sentences show panic. Build a better paragraph by working out the pattern of the whole extract rather than one feature of it.","steps":["Describe the two voices the extract keeps swapping between: the body that hauls at the rock and shouts, and the mechanical engineer who starts measuring the problem.","Match the writing to each voice — clipped clauses and physical verbs for the first, longer sentences and technical vocabulary such as the naming of the chockstone for the second.","State the pattern rather than the instance: every collapse into panic is followed by a return to method, and the extract cycles between them instead of moving once from fear to calm.","Explain the effect on a reader: we are made to panic and to problem-solve at the same time, which is why the passage is exhausting to read.","Add the retrospective narrator, who knows how bad this is going to get, and close by saying what the alternation is for."],"cues":["Which two versions of the same man are speaking here?","What does the sentence length do differently for each voice?","Does the extract move once from panic to calm, or keep moving?","What is the reader made to do while reading it?","Who is telling this, and how much does he already know?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which assessment objective does Question 1 of Section A test, and what does it pay nothing for?","a":"AO1 only. It pays nothing for naming or analysing techniques — that is Question 2’s job."},{"q":"Why is it worth knowing the Part 1 texts thoroughly when the anthology text is printed in the exam paper?","a":"Because the time saved works out as selection time: you arrive already knowing the writer’s purpose, methods and shape, and half of the 22-mark comparison is the anthology half."},{"q":"In A Passage to Africa, what is the catalogue of suffering there for?","a":"It is structural set-up: it establishes the professional detachment that the man’s apologetic smile then breaks."},{"q":"What does the specification define a “textual reference” as?","a":"Any reference to supporting evidence from the text — direct quotations or references to specific moments or events within it."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 1 Section A carries 45 marks and is a mixture of short- and long-answer questions on one Part 1 anthology text and one previously unseen extract; candidates are advised to allow 1 hour 30 minutes including reading time.","The three Section A questions and their assessment objectives: Question 1 — 11 marks, AO1 only; Question 2 — 12 marks, AO2 only; Question 3 — 22 marks, AO3 only.","Question 3 is the only place AO3 is assessed anywhere in the qualification, and it is the paper’s longest reading answer.","The specification states that candidates will be provided with the anthology text in the examination; Text Two in Question 3 is previously unseen.","A “textual reference” is any reference to supporting evidence from the text — direct quotations or references to specific moments or events; an “example” is a specific feature of language and/or structure cited from the text.","The ten Part 1 texts: The Danger of a Single Story (Adichie); A Passage to Africa (Alagiah); The Explorer’s Daughter (Herbert); Explorers, or boys messing about? (Morris); 127 Hours (Ralston); Young and Dyslexic? You’ve got it going on (Zephaniah); A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat (Levine); Beyond the Sky and Earth (Zeppa); H is for Hawk (Macdonald); Chinese Cinderella (Yen Mah).","Forms represented in Part 1: a talk turned essay, autobiography and memoir, travel writing, newspaper journalism and a first-person survival account — the anthology exists to give examples of different types of non-fiction text.","AO1 is marked in four levels, from limited and basic selection to detailed and persuasive selection with discriminating use of supporting quotations and a convincing understanding of information, ideas and perspectives; the grid itself (AO1 out of 12) is the one the specification prints under Component 3, since it prints none for Component 1, and Section A’s Question 1 is marked on the same descriptors at its own 11-mark tariff.","AO2 is marked in five levels, climbing from limited comment, through basic and simple comment on the effect and then clear explanations of the effects of language and structure, to a detailed exploration and finally a perceptive analysis with subtle and discriminating selection; the grid (AO2 out of 18) is again the Component 3 one, borrowed because Section A’s Question 2 is marked on the same descriptors at its own 12-mark tariff."],"questions":[{"q":"State the number of marks and the assessment objective of each of the three questions in Component 1, Section A. (3 marks)","a":"Question 1 — 11 marks, AO1; Question 2 — 12 marks, AO2; Question 3 — 22 marks, AO3.","sol":"Question 1 carries 11 marks and assesses AO1 only, so it rewards the selection and interpretation of information, ideas and perspectives with supporting reference. Question 2 carries 12 marks and assesses AO2 only, so it rewards the selection of linguistic and structural devices and the explanation of their effects. Question 3 carries 22 marks and assesses AO3 only, so it rewards links and connections between the writers’ ideas and perspectives and how these are conveyed. Together that is 45 marks, and the section is advised 1 hour 30 minutes including reading time.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The three questions do three different jobs; no objective is tested twice.","One of the three is worth roughly as much as the other two put together.","Set them out as a list, giving the tariff and the objective for each in turn."],"codes":["Component 1, Section A — the anthology text in the examination"]},{"q":"Explain, in one sentence, what Adichie means by a single story, and state what she says the real problem with a stereotype is. (2 marks)","a":"A single story is what forms when one narrative about a person or place is repeated until it becomes the only one available; the problem with a stereotype is not that it is untrue but that it is incomplete.","sol":"A single story is the result of showing a people or a place as one thing, over and over, until that becomes the only story a listener has, and so the only identity available to them. Adichie’s sharpest formulation is that the trouble with a stereotype is not falsehood but incompleteness: her room-mate’s picture of Africa was not invented, it was simply the only picture she had been given, and a story that is true but alone does more damage than one that is plainly wrong.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["She is talking about quantity of stories, not about accuracy.","Think about what she says is missing rather than what she says is false.","Define the term first, then give her judgement on stereotypes in a second clause."],"codes":["From The Danger of a Single Story"]},{"q":"In The Explorer’s Daughter, what is mattak, and why does Herbert say the hunt matters to the community in Thule? (2 marks)","a":"Mattak is narwhal skin; it is one of the very few sources of vitamin C available in Thule, where nothing grows, so the hunt feeds and sustains the community.","sol":"Mattak is the skin of the narwhal, and Herbert gives it as one of the few available sources of vitamin C in a region where vegetables cannot be grown. That single factual sentence does a great deal of work in the extract: it converts what the reader has been watching as a beautiful and cruel spectacle into a matter of survival, and it is the reason her sympathy is divided. She wills the narwhal to escape and simultaneously understands that the hunters, who are risking their own lives in small kayaks, need the catch.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["It is a part of the animal, not a tool or a technique.","Think about what a diet in the high Arctic cannot easily include.","Name the substance first, then give the nutritional reason she supplies."],"codes":["From The Explorer’s Daughter"]},{"q":"For the Steven Morris article, identify the form of the text, the audience it was written for and the two competing views of the two men that the headline sets up. (3 marks)","a":"A newspaper news report for a national British readership; the headline offers “explorers” against “boys messing about”, and adds that the taxpayer pays either way.","sol":"The form is newspaper journalism — a news report, with the facts of the incident placed first and quotation and background following. The audience is the general British newspaper reader, which matters because the third party in the headline, the taxpayer, is that reader. The headline sets two labels against each other: the men as explorers, which is the heroic reading their supporters take, and the men as boys messing about, which is the dismissive one; and its second half removes the comfort of either verdict by pointing out that the rescue bill arrives regardless.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The form is given away by where the facts sit in the article.","The headline names a third group who are neither explorer nor rescuer.","Answer in three parts: form, audience, then the two labels."],"codes":["Explorers, or boys messing about? Either way, taxpayer gets rescue bill"]},{"q":"State where the accident in the 127 Hours extract happens and what has trapped the writer, and name the two contrasting voices the account keeps moving between. (3 marks)","a":"Blue John Canyon in Utah; a boulder, which he names as a chockstone, has pinned his hand against the canyon wall. The two voices are the panicking body and the calm mechanical engineer.","sol":"The accident happens in Blue John Canyon in Utah, where a boulder shifts as Ralston moves past it and crushes his hand against the wall of the canyon; he uses the climber’s term chockstone for it. The account then alternates between two versions of the same man: the body, which hauls at the rock, swears and shouts in clipped, breathless clauses, and the trained mechanical engineer, who names the object, estimates what he is dealing with, and takes stock of what he is carrying, in longer and more technical sentences.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The place is a slot canyon in the American south-west.","Ask what his training was before you describe the calmer voice.","Give the location and the object first, then label the two voices."],"codes":["From 127 Hours: Between a Rock and a Hard Place"]},{"q":"Read Text One, the extract from Chinese Cinderella. What do you understand about the writer’s relationship with her family from this extract? You may support your points with brief quotations. (11 marks)","a":"That she expects punishment rather than affection, that approval is so rare it overwhelms her, and that the school values her in a way the household does not.","sol":"What the extract shows is a child who has been trained to expect the worst from her own family. Summoned to her father’s study, she assumes she is in trouble, and the fear is automatic rather than reasoned — she does not need a reason, because in this house being noticed has always meant being blamed. Yen Mah withholds the reason for the summons, so the reader shares the child’s dread and is ambushed by the truth alongside her: the school has written to say she has been elected class president, and her father is pleased. The size of her reaction is the real evidence. A brief moment of paternal approval is enough to leave her dizzy with happiness, and a reader measures from that exactly how little affection she has been given: joy on this scale is only possible where there has been a famine of it. I also understand that her worth is recognised outside the home before it is recognised inside it, since it takes a letter from strangers to make her father look at her at all. The relationship, then, is not straightforwardly cruel in this scene; it is far colder than that, because the smallest ordinary kindness registers as an event.","m":11,"d":2,"hints":["AO1 pays for interpretation, so do not retell the walk to the study.","The strength of her reaction is your best evidence about the family — ask what it implies.","Make your first sentence a claim about the relationship, then support it from two separate moments."],"codes":["From Chinese Cinderella"]},{"q":"Read Text One, the extract from H is for Hawk. Look again at the section on the quayside where the boxes are opened. How does the writer use language and structure to convey the effect the hawk has on her? Support your views with detailed reference to the text. (12 marks)","a":"Structurally she delays the right hawk by producing the wrong one first; the imagery clashes the mythic and the reptilian; close focus and broken sentence rhythm carry her shock.","sol":"Macdonald’s first device is structural: the wrong hawk is lifted out of the box before the one she has come for. The reader is made to wait, and the waiting is not neutral — by the time the correct bird appears we are already looking through eyes that have been overwhelmed once, so the second bird inherits the charge of the first. Her imagery then pulls hard in two directions at once. The hawk is rendered through the language of myth and of illuminated manuscripts, which makes it sacred and ancient, and simultaneously through the language of reptiles and of things older than us, which makes it inhuman and faintly horrifying. Because these registers are not allowed to settle, awe and fear arrive in the same breath, and that is precisely the effect the bird has on her: she cannot decide what she is looking at. The focus is extremely close — eye, feather, foot — so the creature is never described whole, which keeps the reader inside her stunned, fragmentary attention rather than at a comfortable distance. Finally the sentences themselves keep flinching: short, stopped sentences interrupt long accumulating ones, so the prose reproduces the rhythm of somebody taking in something faster than they can process it. Taken together, the delay, the clashing imagery, the close focus and the broken rhythm do not tell us that she is overwhelmed; they make the reader undergo it.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["AO2 gives nothing for retelling the journey — every sentence must be about a method.","Deal with one structural choice and at least two language choices, and explain the effect of each.","Begin with the ordering of the two birds and what that ordering does to the reader."],"codes":["From H is for Hawk"]},{"q":"Read Text One, the extract from 127 Hours: Between a Rock and a Hard Place. How does the writer use language and structure to convey his response to being trapped? Support your views with detailed reference to the text. (12 marks)","a":"The alternation between a panicking physical voice and an engineer’s analytical voice, marked by sentence length and vocabulary, held throughout in a present tense that keeps the accident happening.","sol":"The governing structural feature is alternation. Ralston does not move once from panic to composure; the extract keeps cutting between two versions of him, and the pattern, not any single instance of it, is the answer. In the physical passages the clauses shorten to the length of a breath, the verbs are all body and effort, and the writing loses its punctuation-room, so the reader is hurried and cannot think. In the analytical passages the sentences lengthen and the vocabulary turns technical — he names the chockstone, weighs what he is up against, inventories what is on him — and the reader is invited to solve the problem with him. Placing these next to each other means we panic and calculate at the same time, which is exhausting in exactly the way the experience was. Ralston also uses the present tense to keep the accident happening rather than remembered, so the reader has no safe distance. Because the writing stays inside that tense, nothing is ever reported back to us from a point of safety: the reader is given only what he knows as he knows it, and the dread comes from the withheld future rather than from any announcement of it. The response the extract conveys, therefore, is not simply fear: it is the sight of a mind repeatedly losing and recovering control of itself, which is far more frightening than fear described.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["“Short sentences show panic” is Level 2 territory — look for the pattern that contains them.","Consider what his professional training adds to the vocabulary of the calm passages.","Open by naming the two voices, then show the writing changing as it moves between them."],"codes":["From 127 Hours: Between a Rock and a Hard Place"]},{"q":"Read Text One, the article Young and Dyslexic? You’ve got it going on. What do you understand about the writer’s attitude to the education system from this text? You may support your points with brief quotations. (11 marks)","a":"That he holds the system, not the dyslexic child, responsible: it measures children one way, mistakes difference for stupidity, and his own later success is his evidence against it.","sol":"Zephaniah’s attitude is not resentment at individual teachers but a settled judgement about a system. He was written off at school and left at thirteen unable to read and write, treated as stupid or as trouble, and his argument is that a system which can measure children in only one way will keep producing that verdict about children who think in another. The proof he offers is his own life: the boy the school failed became a poet, a novelist and a professor, and since he did not stop being dyslexic on the way, the change must be in the measuring rather than in him. From this he reverses the usual labelling, treating dyslexia not as a deficiency to be corrected but as a different, creative way of thinking that schooling is simply not built to notice. I also understand that his anger is controlled and aimed forwards rather than backwards: he addresses young dyslexic readers directly, and the text ends in encouragement and advice rather than in complaint, which tells us he regards the system as changeable and the children in it as the ones worth talking to.","m":11,"d":2,"hints":["Separate what he thinks of schools from what he thinks of himself.","His own career is not a boast in this article — ask what it is being used to prove.","Start with a claim about where he places the blame, then show two places the text supports it."],"codes":["Young and Dyslexic? You've got it going on"]},{"q":"Read Text One, the extract from A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat. How does the writer use language and structure to convey the atmosphere of the donkey race? Support your views with detailed reference to the text. (12 marks)","a":"A long shapeless build-up released into a sudden burst; verbs of movement and sensory overload; the writer as comic figure; and an anticlimactic ending in which the result is never known.","sol":"Levine builds the atmosphere by controlling the shape of the reader’s wait. The extract spends a long time in heat, delay and vagueness, with nobody able or willing to say when anything will begin, so the prose is deliberately shapeless and slightly tedious; when the race does start it starts without warning, and the sudden switch to short clauses, verbs of motion and stacked sensory detail — dust, engines, horns, bodies — makes the reader lurch in the way the passengers in the car do. The most telling structural choice is that the spectators join in: cars and motorbikes chase the donkeys down the road, so the event has no edge to watch it from, and Levine reproduces that by writing the chase rather than the race. Her own presence is handled comically; she is thrown about, out of her depth and honest about it, and that self-deprecation invites a reader in rather than positioning her above the crowd. The ending refuses the payoff we have been trained to expect, because nobody establishes who won, and that anticlimax is the writer’s perspective in structural form: the atmosphere — the noise, the risk, the collective abandon — was always the event, and a result would have been beside the point.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["The boredom at the start is not weak writing — ask what it is preparing.","Look at what the spectators do when the race begins, and what that leaves the reader with no position to watch from.","Begin with the shape of the extract as a whole, then take the language of the burst."],"codes":["From A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat"]},{"q":"Practice pairing for Question 3 (in the examination Text Two is previously unseen). Refer to Text One, the extract from The Explorer’s Daughter, and Text Two, the extract from A Passage to Africa. Compare how the writers present their own reaction to what they are watching. Support your answer with detailed examples from both texts. (22 marks)","a":"Both make their own discomfort part of the subject; Herbert states her divided loyalty and braids beauty with necessity, while Alagiah withholds his and lets one gesture break his professional detachment.","sol":"Both writers are watching something they cannot alter, and both decide that their own reaction is part of what has to be reported — but they release that reaction on completely different schedules. Herbert declares her division almost at once. She writes the narwhal as beautiful and mysterious and then, in the same breath, explains that mattak is one of very few sources of vitamin C in Thule and that the hunters in their kayaks are risking their lives for a winter’s food. The lyrical and the factual registers are braided, so the reader is never allowed to settle into admiration of the animals or into approval of the hunt, and her perspective is an ambivalence held deliberately open. Alagiah does the opposite: for most of his extract his reaction is suppressed, and he is honest that professionally he has learned to grade suffering by how much reaction it will get, which is why ordinary hunger no longer makes the news. His feeling is detonated by a single gesture — the starving man’s faint, apologetic smile, carrying a shame that should not be his — and the shock is created by the contrast with everything withheld before it. The conveying is therefore as different as the perspectives are alike. Herbert places her two loyalties side by side in the sentences themselves; Alagiah builds a wall of professional detachment and then knocks it down at the end, so that the reader experiences the collapse rather than being told about it. One further link: each writer ends up reversing the usual relationship with the people watched. Herbert stops being a tourist looking at a hunt and becomes a guest in a community whose survival she has to weigh; Alagiah stops being the reporter who takes a story and becomes a man who has been given something he cannot repay. My judgement is that Herbert asks the reader to hold a contradiction, while Alagiah forces one on the reader — and that the second is more powerful precisely because it is delayed. A full answer would sustain four or five such paragraphs, each on one shared idea and quoting from both texts.","m":22,"d":3,"hints":["Do not write half the answer about one text and half about the other — the unit of this answer is the shared idea.","Ask when in each extract the writer’s own feeling becomes visible, and what that timing achieves.","Make your first sentence a comparison, then take Text One and Text Two on that same idea before you weigh the methods."],"codes":["From The Explorer’s Daughter","From A Passage to Africa","Component 1, Section A — the anthology text in the examination"]},{"q":"Practice pairing for Question 3. Refer to Text One, Young and Dyslexic? You’ve got it going on, and Text Two, the extract from The Danger of a Single Story. Compare how the writers present the effect of other people’s assumptions. Support your answer with detailed examples from both texts. (22 marks)","a":"Both argue that a single imposed label limits a person, and both use personal anecdote converted into argument; Zephaniah speaks from inside the label in a colloquial second person, Adichie analyses it and implicates herself.","sol":"The writers share a thesis: a label supplied by other people can replace a person, and the damage is done less by malice than by having only one description available. Zephaniah presents that from inside. He was assumed to be stupid or troublesome because he could not read, left school at thirteen, and the assumption travelled with him; his answer is to set the boy the school dismissed against the poet and professor he became, which proves the measure was wrong rather than the child. Adichie presents it from both ends at once. Her American room-mate assumed she could not speak English and expected a single kind of music, having been given one story of Africa; but Adichie then turns the same charge on herself, admitting she once held a single story of Mexico, which converts the piece from accusation into argument. How the two conveyed their ideas differs sharply. Zephaniah works in a spoken, colloquial register and speaks in the second person straight to young dyslexic readers, so the piece functions as solidarity and ends in practical advice; the voice is the argument, because a man once judged incapable of language is demonstrating command of it. Adichie builds by accumulation of anecdote, each story cashed in for a proposition, moving from a child’s bookshelf out to the idea of nkali — power — and to her formulation that a stereotype is damaging because it is incomplete rather than because it is false. Her structure is the essay’s ascent from personal to political; his is the horizontal move from one person to another. My judgement is that Zephaniah wants a particular reader to be changed and Adichie wants every reader to be implicated, and that is why one of them ends in advice and the other in a redefinition. A full answer would sustain four or five such paragraphs with examples from both texts.","m":22,"d":3,"hints":["Both writers use their own lives, but not for the same job — ask what each life is being used to prove.","Compare who each writer is talking to, and what that does to register and to the ending.","Open on the shared idea of a single imposed description, and only then split the paragraph between the texts."],"codes":["Young and Dyslexic? You've got it going on","From The Danger of a Single Story"]},{"q":"Practice pairing for Question 3. Refer to Text One, the extract from Beyond the Sky and Earth: A Journey into Bhutan, and Text Two, the extract from A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat. Compare how the writers present being an outsider in an unfamiliar place. Support your answer with detailed examples from both texts. (22 marks)","a":"Both write themselves as out of place, but Zeppa’s outsiderness is anxious and resolves into attention and pleasure, while Levine’s is comic and never resolves — she is swept into an event she cannot follow.","sol":"Both writers make their own foreignness the subject rather than a background condition, and both use accumulation of unfamiliar detail as their basic method — but the arc each builds out of it is opposite. Zeppa’s outsiderness is anxious. Her arrival in Bhutan is written as a sequence of things she has no word for: mountains, cloud, prayer flags, houses, food, faces, and she is honest about fright and homesickness rather than performing intrepidness. Crucially the extract moves. As she stops comparing everything with what she has left and starts looking, the detail changes from evidence of strangeness into evidence of beauty, and the sentence rhythm slows with her; the perspective it conveys is that being an outsider is a temporary condition that attention can cure. Levine’s outsiderness is comic and stays that way. She cannot find out when the donkey race will start, nobody explains the rules, and when it does start she is flung about in a vehicle inside a chasing convoy of cars and motorbikes; she never learns who won. The structure is a long shapeless wait released into a burst and then dropped, and the anticlimax is the perspective: the event refused to become legible and she declines to pretend otherwise. The methods follow from this. Zeppa names and reflects, and her ordering traces an inner change; Levine accumulates noise, dust and motion in short clauses and keeps herself in the frame as the least competent person present, using self-deprecation to earn the reader’s trust. My judgement is that Zeppa presents being an outsider as something to be worked through, and Levine as something to be enjoyed — which is why one extract ends in understanding and the other ends in a question nobody answers. A full answer sustains four or five paragraphs of this kind, with examples from both texts.","m":22,"d":3,"hints":["Both feel out of place — the comparison lives in what each writer does with that feeling.","Ask whether each extract changes the writer by its ending, and what the ending is for.","Take the shared idea first, then show how one arc rises into understanding and the other deliberately refuses to."],"codes":["From Beyond the Sky and Earth: A Journey into Bhutan","From A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat"]},{"q":"In the examination, Question 3 sets your Part 1 anthology text against a previously unseen extract. Explain what you can prepare before the exam and what you can only decide in the hall, and how you would spend the first minutes of the question. (6 marks)","a":"Everything about the anthology text — purpose, perspective, methods, shape, key moments — can be prepared; the shared ideas, the paragraph topics and the order can only be chosen once Text Two is read.","sol":"Half of this answer is preparable and half is not, and knowing which half is which is what stops the question going wrong. Preparable: the anthology writer’s purpose and audience, the perspective the extract takes, the three or four methods that writer really depends on, the shape of the extract, and four or five moments precise enough to use as evidence under pressure. None of that depends on Text Two. Not preparable: the ideas the two texts actually share, and therefore the topic of every paragraph, because the comparison must be built on the unseen text’s terms as much as on the anthology’s — a comparison forced onto a prepared theme is the commonest way to lose AO3 marks. In the hall I would read Text Two once for its perspective, asking what the writer thinks about the subject and how the extract is shaped; then annotate it for two or three ideas that also exist in the anthology text; then write those ideas down as paragraph topics, checking that each one can be supported from both texts before I commit to it. Only then would I start writing, leading each paragraph with the shared idea, taking both texts inside it, and weighing how differently each writer conveys that perspective. AO3 pays for links and connections and for how these are conveyed, so the plan must be a list of shared ideas, never a list of texts.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Sort what you know into things that depend on Text Two and things that do not.","The commonest failure is a prepared theme forced onto an unseen text — build the fix into your method.","Answer in two halves, prepared and decided, and finish with the order of moves in the first minutes."],"codes":["Component 1, Section A — the anthology text in the examination","From A Passage to Africa"]},{"q":"Choose two Part 1 texts you would pair to practise AO3 on the idea of the observer’s guilt, and justify the pairing by reference to both writers’ perspectives and methods. (6 marks)","a":"Any defensible pair with reasons — for example A Passage to Africa with The Explorer’s Daughter, since both writers are watching suffering or killing they will not stop and both make their own position part of the subject.","sol":"I would pair A Passage to Africa with The Explorer’s Daughter. Both writers are watching something they will not and arguably cannot intervene in, and in both the writer’s own position becomes part of what is being described rather than a neutral vantage point. Alagiah admits to having learned to rank suffering by how much reaction it will produce, and is undone by an apologetic smile from a man who has nothing; Herbert wills the narwhal to escape while explaining that the hunt is the community’s food, and refuses to resolve the contradiction. The methods are different enough to make the comparison productive: Herbert braids lyrical and factual registers so that both loyalties sit in the same sentences, while Alagiah withholds feeling and then breaks it with a single gesture placed at the end. A second defensible pairing would be A Passage to Africa with Explorers, or boys messing about?, since both raise the question of what an onlooker owes the people they are watching — though there the methods are far apart, one a memoir working by confession and the other a news report working by juxtaposition and headline. The test for any pairing is not that the subjects match but that a single idea can carry paragraphs supported from both texts.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["A good pairing shares an idea, not a setting.","Check that your chosen idea can be supported from both texts before you commit to it.","Name the pair, give the shared perspective, then show the methods differing enough to be worth comparing."],"codes":["From A Passage to Africa","From The Explorer’s Daughter","Explorers, or boys messing about? Either way, taxpayer gets rescue bill"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does Component 1 Section A carry, and how long are candidates advised to spend on it?","a":"45 marks; 1 hour 30 minutes including reading time."},{"q":"Which Section A question assesses AO3, how many marks is it worth, and what two texts does it use?","a":"Question 3, 22 marks, using the Part 1 anthology text and one previously unseen extract."},{"q":"In A Passage to Africa, what single gesture changes the writer’s relationship with the man he is filming?","a":"The man’s faint, embarrassed, apologetic smile."},{"q":"What does Adichie say the real problem with a stereotype is?","a":"Not that it is untrue, but that it is incomplete — it is the only story available."}]},"cards":[{"front":"The single story","code":"From The Danger of a Single Story","open":false,"back":"Adichie’s term for what forms when one narrative about a people or place is repeated until it is the only one available. Her verdict: a stereotype does harm by being incomplete, not by being false."},{"front":"Nkali","code":"From The Danger of a Single Story","open":false,"back":"The word Adichie glosses as being greater than another. She uses it for power over stories: who tells them, how they are told, when, and how many are told."},{"front":"The apologetic smile in A Passage to Africa","code":"From A Passage to Africa","open":false,"back":"The starving man’s faint, embarrassed smile, carrying a shame that belongs to the watching world rather than to him. It breaks Alagiah’s professional detachment and reverses the usual debt between reporter and subject."},{"front":"Mattak","code":"From The Explorer’s Daughter","open":false,"back":"Narwhal skin, which Herbert gives as one of very few sources of vitamin C in Thule, where nothing grows. The fact converts the hunt from spectacle into survival and divides her sympathies."},{"front":"Name one method Herbert uses to hold both of her loyalties together","code":"From The Explorer’s Daughter","open":true,"back":"Braiding lyrical description of the narwhal with factual explanation of the hunt’s necessity; stating her divided feeling openly; ordering the extract from beauty to need to the danger the hunters face; keeping the reader on the shore with her, watching and unable to act."},{"front":"The headline of the Steven Morris article","code":"Explorers, or boys messing about? Either way, taxpayer gets rescue bill","open":false,"back":"It offers two labels for the same men, explorers or boys messing about, and then removes the comfort of either by putting the rescue bill on the taxpayer. The argument arrives before the facts do."},{"front":"Chockstone","code":"From 127 Hours: Between a Rock and a Hard Place","open":false,"back":"The climber’s term Ralston uses for the boulder that shifts in Blue John Canyon and pins his hand. Naming it belongs to his engineer’s voice, which alternates with the panicking physical one."},{"front":"Direct address in Young and Dyslexic?","code":"Young and Dyslexic? You've got it going on","open":false,"back":"Zephaniah writes in the second person to young dyslexic readers in a colloquial, spoken register. The voice is the argument: a man once judged incapable of language commands it, and the piece ends in advice."},{"front":"The anticlimax in A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat","code":"From A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat","open":false,"back":"The donkey race ends with nobody establishing who won. Levine withholds the result deliberately: the noise, the chasing convoy and the collective abandon were the event, so a winner would be beside the point."},{"front":"The arc of the Beyond the Sky and Earth extract","code":"From Beyond the Sky and Earth: A Journey into Bhutan","open":false,"back":"Arrival in Bhutan moves from fright and homesickness, through accumulating unfamiliar detail, to attention and pleasure. Zeppa’s perspective is that being an outsider is a condition attention can cure."},{"front":"The wrong hawk in H is for Hawk","code":"From H is for Hawk","open":false,"back":"Macdonald has the breeder produce the larger, wrong bird first, describing it through mythic and reptilian imagery at once. The delay charges the arrival of the hawk she has actually come for."},{"front":"The summons to the study in Chinese Cinderella","code":"From Chinese Cinderella","open":false,"back":"Yen Mah expects punishment; the reason is withheld from the reader too. Her school has written about her election as class president, and the size of her joy measures how little affection she is given."},{"front":"Textual reference (the specification’s definition)","code":"Component 1, Section A — the anthology text in the examination","open":false,"back":"Any reference to supporting evidence from the text — a direct quotation or a reference to a specific moment or event. An “example” is a specific feature of language or structure cited from the text."},{"front":"The three Section A questions","code":"Component 1, Section A — the anthology text in the examination","open":false,"back":"Question 1: 11 marks, AO1 only, selection and interpretation. Question 2: 12 marks, AO2 only, language and structure and their effects. Question 3: 22 marks, AO3 only, the anthology text against a previously unseen extract."}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 (Question 1, AO1, 11 marks)","items":["The question: Read Text One, the extract from A Passage to Africa. What do you understand about the writer’s feelings towards the people he is reporting on? You may support your points with brief quotations. (11 marks)","The paragraph: What I understand is that Alagiah’s dominant feeling is not pity but a disturbance about his own position, and the extract is arranged to bring him to it. For most of the piece the feelings he admits to are professional: he has looked at the dead and the dying of Gufgaduud often enough to read them as material, and he is unsparing about the fact that suffering has to be graded by how much reaction it will get, because ordinary hunger is no longer news. That is not callousness, it is self-knowledge, and it tells us he already suspects what the job has done to him. The feeling changes at the smile. When the man he is filming smiles at him — faint, embarrassed, apologetic — the shame in it belongs to the wrong person, and Alagiah cannot file that professionally. What he takes from it is a reversal he keeps returning to: a reporter ordinarily takes something away from the people he films, and here he has been given something he cannot repay. His feelings towards these people, then, are not the viewer’s feelings of horror; they are closer to obligation, and the discomfort of owing a debt to someone who has nothing.","About the label: the specification prints no assessment grid for Component 1, so the level named here is borrowed from the AO1 grid it prints under Component 3 (out of 12, in four levels) — Question 1 of Section A is marked on those same descriptors at its own 11-mark tariff, not on the Component 3 mark ranges.","Why this reaches Level 4: the selection is detailed and persuasive — two moments, one early and one late, chosen because they measure a change rather than because they are striking.","Why this reaches Level 4: the supporting reference is discriminating; the smile is used as evidence for an interpretation, not retold as an event.","Why this reaches Level 4: the understanding is convincing, because it names a feeling the text never names and shows where the text earns it.","Why this reaches Level 4: it stays inside AO1 — no device is named, no effect on the reader analysed, because Question 1 pays for neither."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (Question 1, AO1, 11 marks)","items":["The same question: Read Text One, the extract from A Passage to Africa. What do you understand about the writer’s feelings towards the people he is reporting on? (11 marks)","The paragraph: Alagiah feels very sorry for the people in the village. He goes to Gufgaduud with his camera crew and he sees lots of people who are starving and dying, and there is a woman whose leg is broken and has been left, which is really horrible and makes the reader feel sad too. He uses lots of description to show how bad the famine was in Somalia. Then at the end a man smiles at him and this is the bit he remembers most, and he says he will never forget it. This shows that he was very affected by what he saw and that he felt sympathy for the people there, which is understandable because the situation was so bad.","About the label: the specification prints no assessment grid for Component 1, so the level named here is borrowed from the AO1 grid it prints under Component 3 (out of 12, in four levels) — Question 1 of Section A is marked on those same descriptors at its own 11-mark tariff, not on the Component 3 mark ranges.","Why this sits at Level 2: the selection is basic — the material chosen is simply the most upsetting, not the material that shows a change of feeling.","Why this sits at Level 2: the smile is reported rather than interpreted; the answer says it mattered to him without ever saying what he concluded from it.","Why this sits at Level 2: the understanding stops at sympathy, the feeling any reader would have, so nothing is added to the text.","Why this sits at Level 2: it drifts out of AO1 (“his description makes the reader feel sad”) and into weak AO2, which earns nothing on this question.","The single change that lifts it: cut the retelling, keep the earlier admission about grading suffering, and use the smile to argue that his feeling is obligation rather than pity."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5 (Question 2, AO2, 12 marks)","items":["The question: Read Text One, the extract from H is for Hawk. Look again at the section on the quayside where the boxes are opened. How does the writer use language and structure to convey the effect the hawk has on her? (12 marks)","The paragraph: Macdonald’s most consequential choice is structural and comes before any image: the wrong hawk is lifted from the box first. Because the reader is made to wait, the bird she has actually come for arrives into an attention that has already been overloaded, so the delay does the work that a description of shock would otherwise have to do. Into that delay she puts imagery pulling in two incompatible directions — the hawk belongs to myth and to the illuminated bestiary, ancient and half-sacred, and simultaneously to the reptile world, something older and colder than the person holding the box. The registers are never reconciled, and that is exactly the effect being conveyed: she cannot decide whether what she is seeing is holy or horrifying, and the prose declines to decide for her. The focus tightens to eye, feather and foot, so the creature is never given to us whole; we get the fragments a stunned person actually registers, which keeps the reader inside her perception instead of beside it. Even the sentence lengths enact it, short stopped sentences cutting across long accumulating ones so the writing keeps flinching. The methods are therefore not separate features but one system: delay, clashing registers, fragmentary focus and broken rhythm all conspire to make a reader undergo the encounter rather than hear it reported.","About the label: the specification prints no assessment grid for Component 1, so the level named here is borrowed from the AO2 grid it prints under Component 3 (out of 18, in five levels) — Question 2 of Section A is marked on those same descriptors at its own 12-mark tariff, not on the Component 3 mark ranges.","Why this reaches Level 5: the selection is subtle and discriminating — it opens on a structural decision most answers miss and treats the images as a system rather than a list.","Why this reaches Level 5: the analysis is perceptive; the unresolved clash of registers is explained as the meaning, not just identified as a contrast.","Why this reaches Level 5: every device is tied back to the question’s terms — the effect the hawk has on her — rather than to a general claim about vivid writing.","Why this reaches Level 5: it never retells the journey or the transaction, because AO2 pays nothing for narrative."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (Question 2, AO2, 12 marks)","items":["The same question: Read Text One, the extract from H is for Hawk. How does the writer use language and structure to convey the effect the hawk has on her? (12 marks)","The paragraph: Macdonald uses a lot of imagery to describe the hawk. She compares it to creatures from myth and to something reptilian, which makes it sound strange and frightening, and this shows the reader that she is amazed by the bird. She also uses short sentences, which creates tension and makes the reader want to read on. There is a structural device too, because the wrong hawk comes out of the box first and the right one comes out afterwards, which builds suspense. All of these techniques help the writer to show that the hawk has a powerful effect on her.","About the label: the specification prints no assessment grid for Component 1, so the level named here is borrowed from the AO2 grid it prints under Component 3 (out of 18, in five levels) — Question 2 of Section A is marked on those same descriptors at its own 12-mark tariff, not on the Component 3 mark ranges.","Why this sits at Level 3: the devices are correctly identified and the comments on effect are clear, which is what Level 3 asks for — but every explanation stops at one step.","Why this sits at Level 3: “strange and frightening” and “builds suspense” are generic effects that would fit almost any description of an animal; nothing is specific to this writer’s encounter.","Why this sits at Level 3: the two imagery fields are named side by side but the clash between them is never explained, so the most important effect goes unanalysed.","Why this sits at Level 3: “makes the reader want to read on” is a claim about reading in general rather than about the hawk’s effect on Macdonald.","The single change that lifts it: take the myth and reptile imagery together and argue that the refusal to settle between holy and horrifying is the effect — then the same evidence is doing Level 5 work."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the ten Part 1 non-fiction texts","items":["The Danger of a Single Story — writer and form: Chimamanda Ngozi Adichie, a Nigerian novelist; the text is an extract from a talk delivered to a largely Western audience and widely published in transcript, so it is speech written for the ear.","The Danger of a Single Story — purpose and audience: to persuade an educated, largely Western listener that a single repeated narrative about a people is a form of power, by disarming them with anecdote before making the argument.","The Danger of a Single Story — structure: a sequence of personal anecdotes, each cashed in for a proposition, moving outwards from a child’s reading and writing, through the houseboy Fide’s family and the American room-mate, to Adichie’s own single story of Mexico and then to the idea of power.","The Danger of a Single Story — key methods: anecdote used as evidence rather than illustration; self-implication, so the argument cannot be dismissed as accusation; repetition of the governing phrase; the glossed word nkali, being greater than another; the reframing of a stereotype as incomplete rather than false.","The Danger of a Single Story — moments an examiner rewards: the stories she wrote as a child, peopled with white, blue-eyed characters in snow, because she had only British and American books; Fide’s brother’s beautiful basket; the room-mate surprised she spoke English and disappointed by her music; her admission about Mexico.","A Passage to Africa — writer and form: George Alagiah, a BBC foreign correspondent; the text is an extract from his memoir, so a professional’s retrospective account of his own reporting.","A Passage to Africa — purpose and audience: to show a general reader at home what covering famine does to the person covering it, and to question the economics of pity that decides which suffering becomes news.","A Passage to Africa — structure: a catalogue of sights in the Somali village of Gufgaduud that establishes professional detachment, then a single late gesture that breaks it, then reflection on what he owes the man who made it.","A Passage to Africa — key methods: accumulation and escalation of images of suffering; frank admission of the grading of horror by its news value; understatement around the smile so that one small gesture outweighs the catalogue; the closing reversal of the usual traffic between reporter and subject.","A Passage to Africa — moments an examiner rewards: the woman whose broken leg has been left to rot; his admission that ordinary hunger no longer makes news; the man’s faint, embarrassed, apologetic smile; his conclusion that he was given something rather than taking something away.","The Explorer’s Daughter — writer and form: Kari Herbert, daughter of a polar explorer, who lived as a child and returned as an adult to Thule in north-west Greenland; the text is an extract from a memoir with strong travel-writing features.","The Explorer’s Daughter — purpose and audience: to bring a distant reader inside a hunt they would probably condemn from home, by giving both the beauty of the animals and the necessity of killing them.","The Explorer’s Daughter — structure: watching from the shore as the hunters wait in kayaks, moving from the narwhal’s beauty, to the community’s need, to the danger the hunters run, and ending in an ambivalence she refuses to resolve.","The Explorer’s Daughter — key methods: two braided registers, lyrical description and plain factual explanation; precise sensory detail of light and water; the explicit statement of her own divided loyalty; the switch of viewpoint from the watched animals to the watching families.","The Explorer’s Daughter — moments an examiner rewards: the hunters holding still in their kayaks; the explanation that mattak, narwhal skin, is one of very few sources of vitamin C where nothing grows; the risk that a wounded narwhal poses to the hunter; her willing the animals to escape while knowing what escape costs.","Explorers, or boys messing about? — writer and form: Steven Morris, writing a news report for a British national newspaper in 2003; the form is journalism, with the facts first and comment implied rather than stated.","Explorers, or boys messing about? — purpose and audience: to report the rescue to a general British readership and, at the same time, to raise the question of who should pay for adventures undertaken by choice.","Explorers, or boys messing about? — structure: a headline that offers two verdicts and a cost; then the incident and the rescue; then the voices of those close to the men; then the wider question of expense, left in the position a reader remembers.","Explorers, or boys messing about? — key methods: the two competing labels of the headline; juxtaposition of the vocabulary of expedition and adventure with the vocabulary of bills and rescue services; attribution, so scepticism is voiced by others rather than by the reporter; the taxpayer positioned as the reader.","Explorers, or boys messing about? — moments an examiner rewards: the helicopter coming down in the sea off Antarctica; the two men getting into a life raft; the rescue by a Chilean naval vessel; the pole-to-pole ambition set beside the cost of retrieving them.","127 Hours: Between a Rock and a Hard Place — writer and form: Aron Ralston, an American climber and trained mechanical engineer; the text is an extract from his first-person survival account.","127 Hours: Between a Rock and a Hard Place — purpose and audience: to put a general reader inside the minutes of an accident, so that the reader panics and calculates at the same time as the writer.","127 Hours: Between a Rock and a Hard Place — structure: the fall of the boulder, then repeated cycles between panic and method, held in the present tense so the reader is kept inside the minutes as they happen rather than told about them afterwards.","127 Hours: Between a Rock and a Hard Place — key methods: present tense for immediacy; sentence length contrast, clipped physical clauses against longer technical sentences; the engineer’s precise vocabulary, including the naming of the chockstone; alternation rather than a single move from fear to calm.","127 Hours: Between a Rock and a Hard Place — moments an examiner rewards: the boulder shifting in Blue John Canyon, Utah, and pinning his hand against the wall; the useless heaving and shouting; the switch into measuring the problem and taking stock of his kit; the moment he understands the rock will not move.","Young and Dyslexic? You’ve got it going on — writer and form: Benjamin Zephaniah, poet, novelist and professor, who grew up in Birmingham; the text is a newspaper article addressed to young dyslexic readers.","Young and Dyslexic? You’ve got it going on — purpose and audience: to argue that the education system, not the dyslexic child, is at fault, and to encourage young dyslexic readers directly — so the piece both argues and advises.","Young and Dyslexic? You’ve got it going on — structure: the boy written off at school and leaving at thirteen unable to read and write, set against the writer and professor he became, from which the argument is drawn and turned outwards into advice.","Young and Dyslexic? You’ve got it going on — key methods: second-person direct address; colloquial spoken register, beginning with the youth slang of the title; short anecdotes each converted into a claim; relabelling, so a supposed disability becomes a different, creative way of thinking; imperatives at the close.","Young and Dyslexic? You’ve got it going on — moments an examiner rewards: being treated as stupid or as trouble at school; leaving school at thirteen without being able to read and write; the adult career as poet, novelist and professor used as proof that the measure was wrong; the closing advice to young dyslexic readers.","A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat — writer and form: Emma Levine, a travel writer whose book pursues unofficial and traditional sports across Asia; the extract is travel writing in the first person.","A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat — purpose and audience: to entertain a reader at home with an event that has no rules they can follow, and to convey the atmosphere of the crowd rather than the outcome of the race.","A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat — structure: a long, hot, shapeless wait outside Karachi in which nobody will say when the donkey race begins; a sudden burst when it does; the spectators’ vehicles joining the chase; and an anticlimax in which the winner is never established.","A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat — key methods: contrast of pace between the wait and the burst; short clauses, verbs of motion and stacked sensory detail of dust, horns and engines; self-deprecating humour that keeps the writer in the frame as the least competent person present; the withheld result.","A Game of Polo with a Headless Goat — moments an examiner rewards: the uncertainty about when anything will start; the race beginning without warning; the convoy of cars and motorbikes chasing the donkeys so that the audience becomes part of the event; being thrown about inside the vehicle; nobody finding out who won.","Beyond the Sky and Earth: A Journey into Bhutan — writer and form: Jamie Zeppa, a Canadian who travelled to Bhutan to teach; the text is an extract from a travel memoir written in the first person.","Beyond the Sky and Earth: A Journey into Bhutan — purpose and audience: to convey to a reader who has never left home what arrival in an entirely unfamiliar place feels like from the inside, fear included.","Beyond the Sky and Earth: A Journey into Bhutan — structure: the arrival and the journey through mountains and cloud, an accumulation of unfamiliar sights, and a movement of feeling from alarm and homesickness towards attention and pleasure.","Beyond the Sky and Earth: A Journey into Bhutan — key methods: accumulation of precise unfamiliar detail and the naming of things; comparison with the Canada she has left; honest admission of fright rather than performed intrepidness; sentence rhythm that slows as she settles.","Beyond the Sky and Earth: A Journey into Bhutan — moments an examiner rewards: the flight in among the mountains; the road climbing through cloud and forest; prayer flags and unfamiliar houses; the homesickness and the wish to be somewhere she understands; the point at which strangeness starts to register as beauty.","H is for Hawk — writer and form: Helen Macdonald, writing a memoir of grief and falconry; the extract is first-person narrative prose with the density of poetry.","H is for Hawk — purpose and audience: to make a general reader feel an encounter with something wholly other, and to carry the writer’s grief into a description that is nominally of a bird.","H is for Hawk — structure: the meeting with the breeder on a Scottish quayside; the wrong, larger hawk lifted out of the box first and described at length; that bird put away; and only then the smaller hawk she has come for.","H is for Hawk — key methods: delay as a structural device; imagery drawn at once from myth and illuminated manuscripts and from reptiles and prehistory, never reconciled; extreme close focus on eye, feather and foot; short sentences cutting across long accumulating ones.","H is for Hawk — moments an examiner rewards: the boxes on the quayside; the wrong hawk emerging and overwhelming her; the clash of holy and reptilian in the same description; the exchange itself, and her leaving with a creature she cannot fully look at.","Chinese Cinderella — writer and form: Adeline Yen Mah, writing autobiography about her 1940s Chinese childhood in a wealthy family that treated her as an inconvenience.","Chinese Cinderella — purpose and audience: to let a general reader inside a child’s experience of being unwanted, by keeping the child’s viewpoint rather than explaining it from adulthood.","Chinese Cinderella — structure: the summons to her father’s study, the withheld reason, the dread built on the way, the reversal when he turns out to be pleased, and the joy that follows.","Chinese Cinderella — key methods: structural suspense through withholding; the adult writer holding the child’s limited viewpoint; plain, unadorned sentences that leave the emotion exposed; the contrast between the school’s regard for her and the household’s indifference.","Chinese Cinderella — moments an examiner rewards: her automatic assumption that being summoned means punishment; her father reading the school’s letter about her election as class president; his rare approval; the disproportion of her happiness, which measures how little affection she receives."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: Component 1, Section A — how the anthology is examined","items":["Section A sets a mixture of short- and long-answer questions on one non-fiction text from Part 1 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology and one previously unseen extract; all questions in the section are answered.","Section A carries 45 marks and candidates are advised to allocate 1 hour 30 minutes to it, including reading time; Component 1 carries 90 raw marks in total.","The specification states that candidates will be provided with the anthology text in the examination, so no anthology quotation has to be memorised.","Question 1 is 11 marks and assesses AO1 only: reading and understanding, selecting and interpreting information, ideas and perspectives.","Question 2 is 12 marks and assesses AO2 only: understanding and analysing how writers use linguistic and structural devices to achieve their effects.","Question 3 is 22 marks and assesses AO3 only: exploring links and connections between writers’ ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed, across the anthology text and the unseen extract.","AO3 is assessed nowhere else in the qualification, which is why Question 3 on its own is worth roughly as much as Questions 1 and 2 together — 22 marks against their 23 (11 + 12).","Section B of the same paper is Transactional Writing: one task from a choice of two, 45 marks, AO4 27 and AO5 18, with 45 minutes advised.","AO1 is marked in four levels: limited and basic; some and developing; clear and relevant interpretation showing clear understanding; detailed and persuasive selection with discriminating use of quotations and convincing understanding.","AO2 is marked in five levels, in this order: limited comment on effect (Level 1); some and developing, which is where basic and simple comment on the effect sits (Level 2); clear explanations of the effects of language and structure (Level 3); a detailed exploration (Level 4); and a perceptive analysis with subtle and discriminating selection of devices (Level 5).","Where these level descriptors come from: the specification prints no assessment grid for Component 1 — the AO1 grid (out of 12, four levels) and the AO2 grid (out of 18, five levels) are the ones printed under Component 3, and this kit borrows them because Section A’s questions are marked on the same descriptors at their own tariffs of 11 and 12 marks.","Marking is on a best-fit basis within a level: an answer may show characteristics of more than one level, and markers use professional judgement to decide which level is most appropriate.","The anthology is described as a resource providing examples of different types of non-fiction text, and students are encouraged to read beyond it to support their learning."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Question 3, Section A (22 marks, AO3): comparing the anthology text with the unseen extract","items":["The question, in the board’s wording: Refer to Text One and Text Two. Compare how the writers present their own reaction to what they are watching. Support your answer with detailed examples from both texts. (Total for Question 3 = 22 marks)","Decoding the question — “Compare” means two-sided and about the writers’ ideas, perspectives and methods, never two separate summaries stacked one after the other; the unit of the answer is the shared idea, not the text.","Decoding the question — AO3 pays for two things in every paragraph: the link or connection between the writers’ ideas and perspectives, and how each of those perspectives is conveyed. A paragraph with only the first is half-marked.","Decoding the question — “detailed examples from both texts”: each paragraph needs support from Text One and from Text Two, and a textual reference may be a short direct quotation or a reference to a specific moment.","Before the exam, prepare only the half that does not depend on Text Two: for the anthology text, its writer, form, purpose, audience, perspective, three or four methods it really depends on, its shape, and four or five moments precise enough to use as evidence under pressure. Worked here with A Passage to Africa as Text One.","In the hall, first move: read Text Two once for perspective — what does this writer think about the subject, and how is the extract shaped? Do not hunt for devices yet.","In the hall, second move: write down two or three ideas that genuinely exist in both texts, and reject any prepared theme the unseen will not support. Forcing a prepared theme onto Text Two is the commonest way to lose AO3 marks.","Paragraph 1 — the shared idea: both writers make their own discomfort part of what is being reported rather than standing outside it. Text One: Alagiah admits to grading suffering by how much reaction it will produce. Text Two: find the equivalent moment where the writer’s own position enters the account, and say whether it is admitted early or late.","Paragraph 2 — how each perspective is conveyed: Text One withholds feeling behind professional language and then detonates it with one small gesture, the man’s apologetic smile. Ask whether Text Two releases feeling gradually or in a single moment, and compare the timing directly, in the same sentences.","Paragraph 3 — the relationship with the people being watched: Alagiah’s closing reversal, that a reporter usually takes a story and here was given one. Test Text Two for the equivalent: what does its writer take from, or owe to, the people in it?","Paragraph 4 — the effect on the reader: Text One recruits the reader into the professional detachment and then strands them in it. Compare what Text Two asks its reader to do, and weigh which method is the more demanding.","The comparison thread running through all four paragraphs: not what each writer feels, but when and how each lets the reader see it — that single thread keeps every paragraph comparative rather than parallel.","Connectives that keep the answer two-sided: whereas, by contrast, similarly, in the same way, where Text One … Text Two … . If a paragraph can be read without them, it is probably two summaries.","What the conclusion must say: a judgement on the comparison itself, not a summary of both texts — which writer’s method conveys the shared perspective more powerfully, and why the difference in method follows from a difference in purpose, form or audience.","Timing: on the tariff, Question 3 is worth roughly as much as Questions 1 and 2 together, so it deserves close to half of the section’s time; spend a few minutes of that on reading Text Two and listing shared ideas before writing."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"How to use evidence in this room","items":["The anthology text is printed in your examination paper, so the exam expects you to lift exact words from it. This room deliberately names moments instead of reproducing the writers’ sentences — these texts are in copyright, and a study kit may not reprint them.","That is not a workaround: the specification itself defines a textual reference as “any reference to supporting evidence from the text - direct quotations or references to specific moments/events within the text”, so a precisely named moment is legitimate evidence.","Use the kit this way: revise from the named moments here, then open your anthology and mark the exact words for each one, so that in the exam you can quote briefly and accurately.","Keep exam quotations short. A quotation long enough to be retelling earns nothing extra; what earns marks is the comment, explanation, exploration or analysis attached to it.","For Question 2 and Question 3 the safest habit is short quotation plus named method plus explained effect, in that order, so that no sentence is left doing description only."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:05:03.430941+00:00","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Truthful, well-shaped and unusually disciplined about copyright — it names moments in the ten texts instead of quoting them, and every biographical and textual claim I checked (Gufgaduud and the apologetic smile, mattak and vitamin C in Thule, Brooks and Smith and the Chilean naval vessel, Blue John Canyon and the chockstone, the wrong hawk out of the box first, the withheld donkey-race result) holds up; the faults worth fixing before publication are all in the two factfile bullets on tariffs and AO2 levels."}}},"unseen-nonfiction":{"id":"EDX-4EA1","topic":"unseen-nonfiction","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section A of Component 1 is forty-five raw marks, and one of the two texts in front of you is a text you have never seen. You cannot revise it, so what you revise instead is a way of reading: the conventions of the non-fiction forms the specification names, the methods writers of those forms use, and the three questions that are always set on them - eleven marks for selecting and interpreting, twelve for analysing how the writer achieves effects, twenty-two for exploring links and connections with the anthology text. This room builds that toolkit on passages written specially for it, so you practise the reading rather than memorising a text.","idea":[{"h":"Wide reading is the revision for a text you cannot revise","t":"The specification says students 'should read a variety of high-quality, challenging non-fiction texts, in preparation to respond to one unseen non-fiction text in the examination', and that they 'should be able to read substantial pieces of writing, including whole and extended texts that make significant demands in terms of content, structure and the quality of the language', developing 'the skills of interpretation and analysis' throughout the qualification. Read that as an instruction about speed as well as breadth: the marks are not for having met the text but for interpreting it under time pressure. A top-level answer shows reading habits rather than luck - it moves past the surface subject to the writer's perspective on it within the first paragraph, because that student has done this to a hundred articles before.","code":"Content — reading in preparation for the unseen text"},{"h":"Forms you can predict: journalism, speeches, journals, reference extracts","t":"The specification says 'text types studied should include a range of non-fiction forms, such as journalism (for example articles and reviews), speeches, journals and reference book extracts.' Each form carries conventions you can name before you have read a word: a review reaches a verdict and supports it with specifics; a speech is written to be heard once, so it repeats, addresses its audience directly and builds to a call; a journal is private, provisional and unexplained; a reference extract is impersonal and organised by category rather than by feeling. A top-level answer uses the form as the first analytical move, saying what the writer does with the convention - keeping it, stretching it, or breaking it - rather than listing devices in the order they appear.","code":"Content — range of non-fiction forms"},{"h":"Literary non-fiction: where the AO2 marks live","t":"The specification says 'text types should also include literary non-fiction texts, such as selections from autobiography, letters, obituaries and travel writing. These lists are not exhaustive.' These are the forms that use the methods of literature on true material: imagery, patterned syntax, a shaped narrative, a voice. They are also where the Part 1 anthology texts sit, so the unseen extract is very often one of them. A top-level answer treats a memoir or a travel piece exactly as it would treat a poem - asking what the writer has chosen to withhold, where the sentence lengths change, what the last line does to the first - because Question 2 rewards 'a perceptive analysis of the effects of language and structure' and nothing less.","code":"Content — literary non-fiction texts"},{"h":"What will never be set, and what a digital location changes","t":"The specification says 'texts that are essentially transient, such as instant news feeds and advertisements, will not form part of the assessment.' That rules out the very short, the purely promotional and the throwaway, so you should not walk into the examination expecting to analyse a strapline or a tweet. It also says 'the questions set may refer to a digital location for the texts, such as a website', so the extract may be presented as an online article, complete with a headline, a standfirst and a byline. A top-level answer treats those digital signals as structure worth analysing - a headline that withholds, a standfirst that frames the argument - rather than as decoration to be skipped over on the way to the first paragraph.","code":"Content — texts excluded from the assessment"},{"h":"The shape of Section A: three questions, forty-five marks, ninety minutes","t":"The specification says 'students are advised to allocate 1 hour and 30 minutes including reading time to Section A. There will be a mixture of short- and long-answer questions related to a non-fiction text from Part 1 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology and one previously unseen extract. Students will answer all questions in this section. Total of 45 marks for this section.' The raw marks divide as eleven for Question 1 (AO1), twelve for Question 2 (AO2) and twenty-two for Question 3 (AO3). Pro rata that is roughly twenty-two minutes, twenty-four minutes and forty-four minutes, and the specification times the section as a whole rather than the individual questions. A top-level answer is written by a student who protects the long answer: the twenty-two-mark comparison is worth almost half the section, so a beautifully overwritten Question 1 that eats Question 3 is a losing trade.","code":"Assessment overview — Section A: Non-fiction"},{"h":"AO3: links and connections, and how they are conveyed","t":"AO3 asks students to 'explore links and connections between writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed'. It is fifteen per cent of the International GCSE, all twenty-two of its raw marks sit in Component 1 Question 3, and it is the only objective not assessed in Component 2 or Component 3 - so it is examined once, in one answer, and there is no second chance at it. The specification's Key qualification features require students to 'make comparisons between texts and analyse the ways in which writers achieve their effects'. Note the two halves: the idea or perspective, and the method that conveys it. A top-level answer never writes two summaries joined by 'whereas'; it builds each paragraph on a single shared idea, puts both texts inside that paragraph, and ends by weighing which writer's method presses the perspective harder, and why.","code":"AO3 (Component 1 only)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Question 1: selecting and interpreting a travel writer's attitude","setup":"Passage A, 'Night Boat to Alesund', a travel piece written for this room. 'The ferry pulled out of Bergen at nine and for an hour the fjord did nothing worth writing down. Grey water. Grey rock. A gull that followed us for a while and then thought better of it. I had paid for a cabin and a view, and so far I had a cabin. Then the light changed. It did not fade; it drained, the way colour drains out of a face. The cliffs on the port side turned from green to iron, and the water underneath them stopped being water and became a long black corridor with a ceiling of cloud. A man beside me at the rail said something in Norwegian, laughed, and gave up on me. I stayed out until my hands stopped working. There was nothing to photograph, which was the point. In eleven years of writing about places I have learned that the ones worth returning to are the ones that refuse to pose. Alesund, when it finally arrived at four in the morning, was a smear of orange lamps and a smell of diesel and fish, and I was sorry to have reached it.' The task, in the style of Section A Question 1 (AO1 only, eleven marks): from this extract, select and interpret what you understand about the writer's attitude to the journey and to arriving. Use your own words and support each point with a quotation or a reference to a specific moment.","steps":["Decide what is actually being asked: not whether the crossing was enjoyable, but what the writer values, which the piece keeps circling - the wish that the journey would stop performing.","Take the opening complaint as evidence of expectation, not of boredom: 'I had paid for a cabin and a view, and so far I had a cabin' shows a traveller who came with a tourist's bargain in mind and is amused to have been short-changed.","Interpret the turn: once the light 'drained' and the fjord became 'a long black corridor', the writer stays out 'until my hands stopped working', so discomfort is the sign that the place has become real.","Read the line 'there was nothing to photograph, which was the point' as the writer's own statement of value, and link it to the places 'that refuse to pose'.","Close by interpreting the arrival: the town is reduced to lamps, diesel and fish, and the writer is 'sorry to have reached it', so for this writer a journey is something that ends too early rather than somewhere that begins."],"cues":["What is the difference between describing the trip and interpreting the writer's attitude to it?","What does the joke about the cabin tell you the writer expected before boarding?","Why does the writer stay outside once the view has gone?","Which single sentence states the writer's standard for a place worth writing about?","How does the arrival change what the whole journey has been about?"]},{"title":"Question 2: analysing method in an opinion column","setup":"Passage B, 'Somebody Please Ban the Leaf Blower', an opinion column written for this room. 'There is a machine in my street that costs about ninety pounds, weighs less than a cat, and can ruin a Saturday for four hundred people at once. It is the petrol leaf blower, and it is time we admitted what it is: a tool for moving a problem three metres to the left. Consider what it actually achieves. Leaves are lifted from a path. Leaves land on another path. A man in ear defenders - and note that he wears ear defenders, while the rest of us are issued with nothing - returns on Tuesday to lift the same leaves again. We used to have a solution to this. It was called a rake. It cost eight pounds, it lasted thirty years, it made no sound louder than a sigh, and it had the additional benefit of tiring out the person holding it. I am not asking for much. I am not proposing fines, registers, inspectors or any of the other machinery that makes people hate a good idea. I am asking for one quiet street, one morning a week. Surely that is not too much to sweep up.' The task, in the style of Section A Question 2 (AO2 only, twelve marks): analyse how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to persuade the reader that the leaf blower is absurd.","steps":["Start with the structural withholding: the opening sentence describes the object by price, weight and nuisance before naming it, so the reader is made to guess and is already judging the thing by the time the label 'petrol leaf blower' arrives.","Analyse the deflating definition 'a tool for moving a problem three metres to the left', where the mock-technical phrasing of a product description is turned against the product.","Analyse the paired declaratives 'Leaves are lifted from a path. Leaves land on another path.', each opening on 'Leaves' and closing on 'path', so the identical syntax enacts the pointless cycle the sentences describe, then note the paragraph's return on 'Tuesday' to close the loop.","Analyse the dash-bracketed aside about ear defenders, which drops the comic register for a grievance about who is protected and who is not, so the joke acquires a real complaint underneath it.","Analyse the closing pattern, where the anaphora of 'I am not asking', 'I am not proposing' and 'I am asking' shrinks the demand until refusing it would look unreasonable, and the pun in 'too much to sweep up' ties the argument back to the rake."],"cues":["Why is the object not named in the first sentence?","Whose language is being imitated in the definition of what the machine does?","What does the pair of sentences about the leaves do that a longer explanation could not?","What changes in tone inside the dashes, and why is it placed there?","How does the last paragraph make disagreeing with the writer feel unreasonable?"]},{"title":"Question 3: building one comparison paragraph across two texts","setup":"Two passages written for this room. Passage C, 'The Shop Bell', a memoir: 'My father's shop had a bell on a curved strip of brass, and I can still hear exactly how it sounded when a customer came in and how it sounded when I came in. The difference was in me, not the bell, but I was nine and I did not know that yet. I was not supposed to be behind the counter. I was supposed to be upstairs doing my homework at the table under the window, where my mother could see the top of my head from the yard. I spent four years learning how to get down the stairs in the gaps between the bell. What I wanted was not the sweets, which I could have asked for. It was the talk. Mrs Doran's hip. The bus timetable, discussed as though it were policy. A man whose name I never learned, who came in every Friday for a newspaper he did not read and stayed for forty minutes. My father died before I could tell him that the shop taught me my trade. He thought I had wasted those years on the stairs. I was listening. That was the whole of it.' Passage D, a speech to volunteers: 'Friends - and after eleven Saturdays in a river, I think I am allowed to call you that - thank you. Let me tell you what you have done. When we started, this stretch of the Brayford held nine supermarket trolleys, two motorbikes and a quantity of plastic I am not going to describe while you are eating. Today a child stood on that bank and pointed at a fish. A fish! In water that, eleven weeks ago, you could have stood on. Now, I have heard what is said about us. That we are a handful of people with nets. That the problem is too big, that it is upstream, that it belongs to somebody with a budget and a title. And I say: yes. It is too big. It is upstream. And we pulled out nine trolleys anyway. So I am asking you for one more season. Not for the river - the river does not know we exist. For the child on the bank. For the fish, who has made, I would say, a brave decision. Bring gloves. Bring someone who says it is pointless. I will handle them.' The task, in the style of Section A Question 3 (AO3 only, twenty-two marks): explore the links and connections between the writers' ideas and perspectives on effort that other people dismiss, and how these are conveyed. Write the first comparison paragraph only.","steps":["Fix a shared idea that both texts can be tested against, rather than a shared topic: each writer answers somebody who has already judged the effort worthless.","Put the two pieces of evidence for that idea side by side - the father who 'thought I had wasted those years on the stairs', and the dismissal that the volunteers are 'a handful of people with nets'.","Compare how each perspective is conveyed: the memoir answers the charge in retrospect and alone, in three plain statements at the very end, the last two of them only three words and six, while the speaker answers it live and out loud, conceding 'yes' to every objection before turning it with 'anyway'.","Find the difference inside the similarity: the memoir's defence can never be delivered to the person who made the charge, so its quietness is a loss, whereas the speech's concession is a tactic performed for an audience who can act on it.","End the paragraph with a weighed judgement of which method presses the perspective harder on its own reader, and say why the form makes that so."],"cues":["What idea, not what topic, can both texts be tested against?","Which moment in each text carries the dismissal?","How does each writer answer the charge, and in what voice?","Where does the similarity between the two answers break down?","What must a comparison paragraph do before it ends?"]},{"title":"Reading the form in the first four minutes","setup":"Passage E, a restaurant review written for this room, with the headline 'Review: The Lighthouse Cafe, Whitby'. 'The Lighthouse Cafe has nine tables, six of which face a wall painted the colour of tinned peas, and it does not care what you think of them. I arrived at half past eleven on a wet Tuesday and was handed a laminated menu with four items on it and the words that is it by way of explanation. The fish was the best I have eaten in three years. It arrived in a batter that shattered rather than bent, on a plate that had plainly been warmed, with chips that had been fried twice by somebody who counts. The coffee is terrible. I want to be clear about this, because everything else here is so good that you will be tempted to forgive the coffee, and you must not. Nine pounds forty. No music, no candles, no theatre. If you want a view, the sea is outside and it is free.' The task: you have four minutes with this extract before the questions. Name the form, state the conventions you can now expect, and decide which two methods you will analyse if Question 2 asks how the writer conveys a judgement.","steps":["Name the form from its signals: a headline that announces a review, a named place, a visit on a specified occasion, a price and a recommendation - this is journalism of the reviewing kind, one of the forms the specification names.","Set out what the convention lets you predict: a verdict, specific sensory evidence for it, a concession that proves the reviewer is not simply flattering, and a closing line that tells the reader what to do.","Check the text against the prediction and find where it stretches the convention: the concession is not a small politeness but is pressed into a second-person prohibition, 'you must not', so the reader is forbidden to forgive the coffee.","Choose the first method to analyse: the tactile precision of the praise, where the batter 'shattered rather than bent' and the chips were fried by 'somebody who counts', so the verdict rests on evidence rather than adjectives.","Choose the second: the two verbless sentences at the close, 'Nine pounds forty. No music, no candles, no theatre.', and the refusal of atmosphere, which makes the review's standard of value - food, not theatre - the last thing the reader is left holding."],"cues":["Which signals in the first lines fix the form?","If this is that form, what must appear somewhere in it?","Where does the text do more with a convention than you predicted?","Which words make the praise checkable rather than vague?","What is the last thing the reader is left holding, and in what kind of sentences is it delivered?"]},{"title":"Turning feature-spotting into analysis","setup":"A student has written this sentence about Passage B, the opinion column on leaf blowers: 'The writer uses a rhetorical question at the end to make the reader think.' The relevant moment is the column's final paragraph: 'I am not asking for much. I am not proposing fines, registers, inspectors or any of the other machinery that makes people hate a good idea. I am asking for one quiet street, one morning a week. Surely that is not too much to sweep up.' The question the student is answering asks how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to persuade the reader that the leaf blower is absurd. The task: rewrite the sentence so that it would sit at the top of the AO2 grid, which rewards a perceptive analysis of effects and subtle and discriminating selection rather than basic and simple comment.","steps":["Diagnose two faults at once: the device is misnamed, since the closing sentence 'Surely that is not too much to sweep up.' is a declarative assertion rather than a question, and the effect named, 'make the reader think', would fit any text ever written.","Replace the vague effect with what this sentence actually does to the reader: 'Surely' assumes agreement already exists, so a reader who disagrees is placed outside a consensus the writer has simply asserted.","Bring in the structure that sets it up, since the three preceding sentences shrink the demand from a ban, through the refusal of 'fines, registers, inspectors', down to 'one quiet street, one morning a week'.","Add the language detail the first version missed: 'sweep up' is a pun that returns the argument to the rake, so the alternative to the machine is still in the reader's hand as the piece closes.","Assemble the rewritten sentence so that effect leads and the terms follow, and end on a judgement of how far this closing move carries the absurdity argument."],"cues":["What is wrong with the device named and with the effect claimed?","What does the opening word of that final sentence do to a reader who disagrees?","What has the writer done to the size of the demand just before it?","Which two words carry more than one meaning here?","In what order should effect, evidence and term appear in the finished sentence?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which question in Section A is worth the most, and which assessment objective does it test on its own?","a":"Question 3, worth twenty-two of the forty-five marks, testing AO3 only - links and connections between the writers' ideas and perspectives, and how these are conveyed."},{"q":"Why is it a mistake to analyse the writer's language in an answer to Question 1?","a":"Question 1 is AO1 only. It rewards selection and interpretation of information, ideas and perspectives with supporting quotations or references; analysis of method earns its marks in Question 2 instead, and time spent on it in Question 1 is time taken from Question 3."},{"q":"A student writes a paragraph on the anthology text, then a paragraph on the unseen extract, then a sentence saying both writers care about their subject. What has that answer failed to do?","a":"It has failed to explore links and connections. AO3 marks come from paragraphs built on one shared idea with both texts inside them, comparing how each writer's method conveys the perspective, not from two summaries joined at the end."},{"q":"The extract is presented as an article on a website, with a headline and a standfirst. What does the specification say about that, and what should you do with them?","a":"The specification says 'the questions set may refer to a digital location for the texts, such as a website'. Treat the headline and standfirst as structure: what they withhold, what they promise and how the opening paragraph answers them are analysable choices."}]},"room":{"facts":["Section A of Component 1 is worth forty-five raw marks; students are advised to allocate one hour and thirty minutes including reading time, and all questions in the section must be answered.","The three questions: Question 1 is eleven marks and AO1 only; Question 2 is twelve marks and AO2 only; Question 3 is twenty-two marks and AO3 only.","The section always sets two texts: a non-fiction text from Part 1 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology and one previously unseen extract.","AO3 is 'explore links and connections between writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed'; it carries fifteen per cent of the qualification and every one of its raw marks sits in Component 1 Question 3.","A 'textual reference' is 'any reference to supporting evidence from the text - direct quotations or references to specific moments/events within the text'; an 'example' is 'citing specific examples of features of language and/or structure from the text'.","Forms to expect: journalism such as articles and reviews, speeches, journals, reference book extracts, and literary non-fiction such as autobiography, letters, obituaries and travel writing. Texts that are essentially transient, such as instant news feeds and advertisements, will not be set, but a text may be sited at a digital location such as a website."],"questions":[{"q":"State the mark tariff and the assessment objective of each of the three questions in Section A of Component 1, and the total for the section.","a":"Question 1: eleven marks, AO1. Question 2: twelve marks, AO2. Question 3: twenty-two marks, AO3. Forty-five marks in total.","sol":"Question 1 is worth eleven marks and tests AO1 only: reading and understanding, selecting and interpreting information, ideas and perspectives. Question 2 is worth twelve marks and tests AO2 only: understanding and analysing how writers use linguistic and structural devices to achieve their effects. Question 3 is worth twenty-two marks and tests AO3 only: exploring links and connections between the writers' ideas and perspectives as well as how these are conveyed, across the anthology text and the previously unseen extract. Eleven plus twelve plus twenty-two is forty-five, the total for the section, for which one hour and thirty minutes including reading time is advised.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The three questions are not equal in weight, and one of them is worth nearly half the section.","Each question tests a single assessment objective: one for selection and interpretation, one for how effects are achieved, one for links across two texts.","Write the three questions in a column, put one objective beside each, then check that your three tariffs add up to the section total."],"codes":["Assessment overview — Section A: Non-fiction","AO3 (Component 1 only)"]},{"q":"The specification rules certain texts out of the assessment. State which kinds it excludes, and state what it says about where a set text may be located.","a":"Texts that are essentially transient, such as instant news feeds and advertisements, will not form part of the assessment; but the questions set may refer to a digital location for the texts, such as a website.","sol":"The specification says that 'texts that are essentially transient, such as instant news feeds and advertisements, will not form part of the assessment', so the extract will not be a live news feed or a piece of advertising copy. It also says that 'the questions set may refer to a digital location for the texts, such as a website', so the extract may well be an online article and may arrive with the furniture of one - a headline, a standfirst, a byline. The distinction matters in the examination: digital does not mean transient, and the online presentation is part of the text's structure and can be analysed.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["One category is ruled out because of how short-lived it is, not because of where it is published.","The specification separates the nature of a text from the place it is sited.","Give the excluded category with the two examples the specification names, then add its sentence about location."],"codes":["Content — texts excluded from the assessment"]},{"q":"Identify three non-fiction forms and two literary non-fiction forms that the specification says students should have read, and state one convention you could predict for each of the five before reading a word of an unseen example.","a":"Any three of journalism (articles, reviews), speeches, journals, reference book extracts, plus any two of autobiography, letters, obituaries, travel writing - each with one predictable convention.","sol":"Non-fiction forms: journalism, for example articles and reviews - a review reaches a verdict and supports it with specific, checkable evidence; speeches - written to be heard once, so they repeat, address the audience directly and build towards a call to act; journals - private and provisional, so they leave people and events unexplained because the writer already knows them; reference book extracts - impersonal and organised by category rather than by feeling. Literary non-fiction: autobiography - a double voice, the self who lived the moment and the older self who now judges it; letters - written to one named reader, so tone is shaped by the relationship; obituaries - a life told for the sake of its meaning, usually shaped around achievement and character; travel writing - a first-person traveller who is also a character, and a place judged by how it resists or meets expectation. Naming the convention first gives you the analytical move that opens a Question 2 answer.","m":5,"d":1,"hints":["The specification gives two lists: one of non-fiction forms, one of literary non-fiction.","A convention is what the form must do to count as that form, not a device it happens to use.","Set the five forms out as a list and write one sentence beside each beginning with the words this form must."],"codes":["Content — range of non-fiction forms","Content — literary non-fiction texts"]},{"q":"How long are students advised to allocate to Section A, what does that time include, how many questions must be answered, and what two texts does the section use?","a":"One hour and thirty minutes including reading time; all questions in the section are answered; the texts are a non-fiction text from Part 1 of the anthology and one previously unseen extract.","sol":"The specification advises one hour and thirty minutes for Section A, and states that this includes reading time, so the time taken to read both texts comes out of the ninety minutes rather than being given on top of it. Students answer all the questions in the section - there is no choice. The section sets a mixture of short- and long-answer questions on a non-fiction text from Part 1 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology and one previously unseen extract, for a total of forty-five marks. Dividing the time in proportion to the marks gives roughly twenty-two minutes for Question 1, twenty-four for Question 2 and forty-four for Question 3, although the specification times the section as a whole and never times individual questions.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The advised time is for the whole section, and the specification is explicit about one thing it contains.","Ask yourself whether there is any choice of question in this section.","Answer in four parts: the time, what it includes, how many questions, and the two texts."],"codes":["Assessment overview — Section A: Non-fiction"]},{"q":"State what AO3 requires, the percentage of the qualification it carries, and where all of its marks sit. Then state the one thing a student must stop doing in order to earn them.","a":"AO3 is to explore links and connections between writers' ideas and perspectives as well as how these are conveyed; fifteen per cent; all twenty-two raw marks in Component 1 Question 3. Stop writing two separate summaries.","sol":"AO3 requires students to 'explore links and connections between writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed'. It carries fifteen per cent of the International GCSE, and all of its raw marks - twenty-two of them - sit in Component 1, Section A, Question 3; it is the only assessment objective not assessed in Component 2 or Component 3, so it is examined once and only once. The habit to break is writing one block on the first text, one block on the second, and a closing sentence that says both writers feel strongly. That structure cannot show links and connections because the two texts are never in the same paragraph. Instead each paragraph takes one shared idea, holds both texts inside it, and compares the methods by which each writer conveys that perspective.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["The wording of the objective has two halves joined by the phrase as well as.","Count where in the qualification AO3 can possibly be tested, given the components a student sits.","Give the wording, then the percentage, then the single question it lives in, then the structural habit that makes it unreachable."],"codes":["AO3 (Component 1 only)"]},{"q":"Read Passage A, 'Night Boat to Alesund', printed in full in the passages section of this room. From this extract, select and interpret what you understand about the writer's attitude to the journey and to arriving. Use your own words and support each point with a quotation or a reference to a specific moment. (11 marks, AO1)","a":"The writer expects and enjoys being short-changed; values the fjord only once it stops being scenic; treats discomfort as proof the place has become real; judges places by their refusal to pose; finds arrival a disappointment because the journey ends.","sol":"The writer arrives with a tourist's bargain in mind and is amused to find it unhonoured: 'I had paid for a cabin and a view, and so far I had a cabin', and the first hour of the fjord 'did nothing worth writing down'. That complaint is not boredom but a set-up, because what changes the writer's mind is not a better view but the loss of the view altogether. Once the light 'drained' and the cliffs turned 'from green to iron', the water becomes 'a long black corridor', and the writer stays at the rail 'until my hands stopped working' - physical discomfort is the evidence that the place has become real rather than picturesque. The standard is then stated openly: 'there was nothing to photograph, which was the point', and the places worth returning to are the ones 'that refuse to pose'. The attitude to arriving follows from this. Alesund is given only as 'a smear of orange lamps and a smell of diesel and fish', and the reaction to it is to be 'sorry to have reached it'. For this writer the journey is the experience and the destination is its end, so travel is valuable precisely while it is unfinished; notice too that the final clause states only the writer's regret at arriving and passes no verdict on the town, so the disappointment is presented as a fact about the traveller rather than about Alesund.","m":11,"d":2,"hints":["The question asks what you understand about the writer's attitude, not what happened on the crossing.","Look for the moments where the writer says what is valued rather than what is seen.","Group your points as expectation, the turn, the stated standard, and arrival, and attach a short quotation to each."],"codes":["Content — literary non-fiction texts","Assessment overview — Section A: Non-fiction"]},{"q":"Read Passage B, the opinion column 'Somebody Please Ban the Leaf Blower', printed in full in the passages section of this room. Analyse how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to persuade the reader that the leaf blower is absurd. (12 marks, AO2)","a":"Withheld naming in the opening; mock-technical deflation; parallel short declaratives enacting the cycle; the dash-bracketed grievance about ear defenders; the nostalgic rake paragraph; anaphora shrinking the demand; the closing pun.","sol":"The column withholds its subject: the opening sentence itemises a machine by price, weight and nuisance before naming it, so the reader has already convicted the object by the time 'petrol leaf blower' arrives. The writer then deflates it with a mock-technical definition - 'a tool for moving a problem three metres to the left' - which borrows the flat register of a product description and turns it against the product. Structure carries the absurdity next: the paired declaratives 'Leaves are lifted from a path. Leaves land on another path.' are built on identical syntax, each opening on 'Leaves' and closing on 'path', so the sentence pattern enacts the pointless cycle it describes, and the return 'on Tuesday' closes the loop. The dash-bracketed aside - 'and note that he wears ear defenders, while the rest of us are issued with nothing' - breaks the comic register for a moment of real grievance about who is protected, which stops the humour becoming weightless. The paragraph on the rake works by contrast: the five-word sentence 'It was called a rake.' delivers the solution as a deliberate anticlimax, and the accumulating clauses about cost, lifetime and quiet end on the joke that the rake tires out the person holding it. Finally the anaphora of 'I am not asking', 'I am not proposing' and 'I am asking' shrinks the demand step by step until refusal looks unreasonable, and the pun in 'too much to sweep up' returns the argument to the rake, leaving the alternative in the reader's hand.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Not every persuasive device here is a word choice; ask what the arrangement of the column - the order in which things are revealed and withheld - is doing to you as you read.","For each device, write the effect on the reader first and let the term follow it.","Work through the column in order, mark the four places where the register changes, and analyse one method in each."],"codes":["Content — range of non-fiction forms","Assessment overview — Section A: Non-fiction"]},{"q":"Read Passage C, the memoir 'The Shop Bell', printed in full in the passages section of this room. Analyse how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to convey what the shop meant to the writer. (12 marks, AO2)","a":"The bell as a structural motif and the double voice of memoir; the geography of the stairs; the list of overheard talk; the withheld payoff; the three plain statements at the close.","sol":"The memoir opens on a sound and makes it a measuring device: the bell rings differently for a customer and for the child, and the adult voice immediately corrects the child's understanding - 'the difference was in me, not the bell, but I was nine and I did not know that yet'. That is the double voice of autobiography, and it sets up the whole piece, because everything the child valued will be re-valued at the end. Structure is then organised by prohibition: the writer 'was not supposed to be behind the counter' and 'was supposed to be upstairs', and the repeated construction turns the shop into forbidden territory, while 'the gaps between the bell' makes the rhythm of the shop into the map of a childhood. The payoff is withheld and a list of talk is given instead - 'Mrs Doran's hip. The bus timetable, discussed as though it were policy.' - where the elevated word 'policy' applied to a bus timetable conveys affection for the seriousness of ordinary life, and the unnamed man who stayed forty minutes over an unread newspaper shows that the shop's business was company, not trade. The ending turns on sentence length: after the sentence recording the father's death the piece closes on three plain statements - 'He thought I had wasted those years on the stairs. I was listening. That was the whole of it.' - the last two only three words and six, and of those nine words all but 'listening' are of one syllable. The meaning is delivered flatly, and the flatness is the grief, since the correction can never be made to the person it is addressed to.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["A memoir has two speakers in it, and one of them knows more than the other.","Track where the sentences get shorter, and ask what the writer gains by refusing to explain at that point.","Take the piece in four movements - the bell, the prohibition, the overheard talk, the ending - and analyse one method in each."],"codes":["Content — literary non-fiction texts","Content — reading in preparation for the unseen text"]},{"q":"Read Passage D, the speech to volunteers, printed in full in the passages section of this room. From this speech, select and interpret what you understand about the speaker's view of the volunteers and of the task in front of them. Use your own words and support each point with a quotation or a reference to a specific moment. (11 marks, AO1)","a":"The speaker treats the volunteers as friends who have earned the word; measures success by one small living proof; concedes the task is too big; still asks for another season; welcomes the sceptics.","sol":"The speaker sees the volunteers as people whose work has earned them a relationship: they are called 'Friends', but the word is licensed by the labour behind it - 'after eleven Saturdays in a river, I think I am allowed to call you that'. What they have achieved is measured not by scale but by evidence, setting the inventory of trolleys, motorbikes and undescribed plastic against a single living sign, the child who 'pointed at a fish'; the exclamation 'A fish!' shows that one small recovery counts as proof, which tells us the speaker expects progress to be modest and visible rather than total. On the task itself the speech is unusually honest. The objections are repeated rather than hidden - that the problem 'is too big', that 'it is upstream', that it belongs to somebody with 'a budget and a title' - and each is conceded before being answered with the fact of the work done 'anyway'. The view, then, is that hopelessness is accurate and irrelevant. The ask is for 'one more season', and the reason is defined carefully: not the river, which 'does not know we exist', but the child and the fish, so the point of the work is what it means to the people doing and seeing it. The closing instruction to bring 'someone who says it is pointless' shows that scepticism is regarded as recruitable rather than threatening.","m":11,"d":2,"hints":["The speech repeats what has been said about the volunteers; that is a view of the task, not an interruption of it.","Separate what the speaker thinks of the volunteers from what the speaker thinks of the job, then find evidence for each.","Build four or five short points, each opening with what the speaker understands and closing with a quotation."],"codes":["Content — range of non-fiction forms","Assessment overview — Section A: Non-fiction"]},{"q":"Read Passage E, the review of The Lighthouse Cafe, printed in full in the passages section of this room. Analyse how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to convey a judgement of the cafe. (12 marks, AO2)","a":"Unflattering opening detail that establishes honesty; tactile, checkable praise; the concession paragraph that refuses forgiveness; verbless fragments and the free sea at the close.","sol":"The review earns the right to praise by opening with what is wrong: the wall 'painted the colour of tinned peas' and the laminated menu of four items establish a reviewer who will not flatter, and the personified indifference of a cafe that 'does not care what you think' turns the ugliness into character rather than failure. The praise, when it comes, is made checkable by tactile precision: the batter 'shattered rather than bent', the plate 'had plainly been warmed', the chips were fried twice 'by somebody who counts'. The verb 'shattered' does the analytical work of a whole adjective list, and 'somebody who counts' gives the judgement a moral edge, since the standard being applied is care. The review's structural centre is its concession, and it is stretched far beyond convention: the four-word declarative 'The coffee is terrible.' stands alone, and forgiveness is then ruled out in the second person - 'you will be tempted to forgive the coffee, and you must not' - so the concession becomes a prohibition and the reviewer's authority is reinforced rather than weakened. The close is two verbless sentences - 'Nine pounds forty. No music, no candles, no theatre.' - whose bareness matches the place being described, and the final conditional, that if a view is wanted the sea 'is outside and it is free', delivers the verdict as a value judgement about what a reader should be paying for.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["Ask why the least attractive detail in the cafe is placed in the first sentence.","The words chosen for the food are physical rather than evaluative; work out what that buys the writer.","Divide the review into opening, praise, concession and close, and analyse one method in each."],"codes":["Content — range of non-fiction forms","Content — reading in preparation for the unseen text"]},{"q":"Passage C, the memoir 'The Shop Bell', and Passage D, the speech to volunteers, are printed in full in the passages section of this room. Explore the links and connections between the writers' ideas and perspectives on effort that other people dismiss, and how these are conveyed. In the examination one of your two texts is the anthology text you have studied and the other is previously unseen; here both stand in for that pair. (22 marks, AO3)","a":"Both answer a dismissal of their effort; both offer one small living proof rather than scale; the memoirist answers alone and too late in three plain statements, the speaker answers a live audience by conceding and turning; the difference is between private vindication and public recruitment.","sol":"Both writers begin from the same position: somebody else has already judged the effort worthless, and the text is the answer. In the memoir the judgement is the father's, that the writer 'had wasted those years on the stairs'; in the speech it is a public one, that the volunteers are 'a handful of people with nets'. The link is the answer each gives - that the work was not what it looked like - and the difference lies in who can hear it. The memoir's reply, 'I was listening. That was the whole of it.', runs to nine words, all of one syllable but 'listening', and it is placed after the sentence recording the father's death, so the vindication arrives too late by design and the plainness carries the loss. The speech's reply is performed for people who can still act: the objections are repeated aloud, conceded with a single 'yes', and turned with one word, 'anyway'. Both writers also refuse scale as evidence and choose one small living sign instead. The shop's value is proved by overheard trivia - Mrs Doran's hip, a bus timetable 'discussed as though it were policy' - and the river's by a child pointing at a fish, greeted with the exclamation 'A fish!'. Yet the perspectives behind these choices differ. The memoirist is interpreting a completed past, so the method is retrospective structure: the bell that rang differently for the child is set up in the first sentence so that the ending can reinterpret it. The speaker is shaping an unfinished future, so the method is live rhetoric: direct address, the repeated 'That' clauses that list the objections, concession, and the imperatives that end the speech, 'Bring gloves. Bring someone who says it is pointless.' Finally, the two texts differ in what they want from the dismissal. The memoir absorbs it; the speech uses it, even inviting the sceptic into the room. Weighed together, the speech presses its perspective harder on its own reader because it demands a decision, but the memoir's is the more unsettling, since it shows what happens to a defence when there is no longer anyone to make it to.","m":22,"d":3,"hints":["Find an idea both texts can be tested against, not a topic they share.","Each paragraph must hold both texts and must compare the method as well as the idea, since AO3 asks how the perspectives are conveyed.","Draft three links first - the dismissal, the proof each writer offers, and what each wants the reader to do - and order them so the last is the most interesting."],"codes":["AO3 (Component 1 only)","Content — literary non-fiction texts"]},{"q":"Passage A, the travel piece 'Night Boat to Alesund', and Passage E, the review of The Lighthouse Cafe, are printed in full in the passages section of this room. Explore the links and connections between the writers' ideas and perspectives on what makes a place worth recommending, and how these are conveyed. (22 marks, AO3)","a":"Both value places that refuse to perform and both withhold praise first; the traveller judges by discomfort and loss, the reviewer by physical evidence and price; one is reflective and retrospective, the other addresses the reader directly in the present.","sol":"The two writers share a standard: a place is worth recommending when it is not trying to please you. The travel writer states it outright - the places worth returning to 'refuse to pose' - and the reviewer enacts it, opening on a wall 'painted the colour of tinned peas' in a cafe that 'does not care what you think'. Both therefore build credibility by beginning with what is unattractive, and both keep their praise physical rather than decorative: the fjord becomes 'a long black corridor', the batter 'shattered rather than bent'. The connection runs deeper than tone, because in each text the enemy is the same thing - display. For the traveller it is the photograph, and 'there was nothing to photograph, which was the point'; for the reviewer it is atmosphere, dismissed in one verbless sentence of three refusals, 'No music, no candles, no theatre.' How the perspective is conveyed is where they separate. The travel writer measures value by what it has cost the writer: the rail is kept 'until my hands stopped working', and the verdict is a feeling, that of being 'sorry to have reached it'. The structure runs from expectation through a turn to a muted arrival, and the past tense keeps everything inside one remembered crossing. The reviewer measures value by what it will cost the reader, and speaks to that reader directly: a price is given, 'Nine pounds forty'; forgiveness is ruled out in a second-person modal, 'you will be tempted to forgive the coffee, and you must not'; and the last sentence is a conditional offer, 'If you want a view, the sea is outside and it is free.' Those second-person constructions, together with the present tense used for what the cafe permanently is - 'The coffee is terrible.' - make the judgement transferable to anyone who goes, which is what the form requires. So the link is a shared suspicion of places that perform, and the difference is the direction of the writing: the travel piece turns the standard inward into a way of travelling, while the review turns it outward into advice. Of the two, the review is the more useful and the travel piece the more persuasive, because a standard that has cost the writer something is harder to argue with.","m":22,"d":3,"hints":["Both texts pay a compliment only after saying something unflattering; ask what that structure buys each writer.","The forms differ in whom the judgement is for, and in whether the reader is addressed at all; let that shape your comparison of method.","Plan three shared ideas and, for each, name the method in both texts before you write a sentence of prose."],"codes":["AO3 (Component 1 only)","Content — range of non-fiction forms"]},{"q":"Passage B, the opinion column on leaf blowers, and Passage D, the speech to volunteers, are printed in full in the passages section of this room. Explore the links and connections between the writers' ideas and perspectives on how ordinary people should be persuaded to act, and how these are conveyed. (22 marks, AO3)","a":"Both shrink the ask and both refuse the language of bureaucracy; the columnist persuades a solitary reader by humour and a shrinking demand, the speaker persuades a present audience by concession, direct address and imperatives; print allows irony, live speech needs repetition.","sol":"Both writers believe that people are moved by small, concrete requests rather than by the scale of a problem, and both say so by refusing official language. The columnist rules out 'fines, registers, inspectors or any of the other machinery that makes people hate a good idea'; the speaker rejects the idea that the river belongs to 'somebody with a budget and a title'. In each case the enemy is the same - the apparatus that turns a shared annoyance into someone else's paperwork - and in each case the writer's alternative is deliberately tiny: 'one quiet street, one morning a week' and 'one more season'. How that shared perspective is conveyed is governed by form. The column is read alone and can therefore rely on irony and delay: the machine is described before it is named, its purpose is deflated as 'moving a problem three metres to the left', and the argument is closed with a pun, 'too much to sweep up', which a reader has time to notice. The speech is heard once and can rely on none of that, so it repeats instead: the objections are stated aloud and conceded - 'It is too big. It is upstream.' - before being turned with 'anyway', and the ending is a pair of imperatives, 'Bring gloves. Bring someone who says it is pointless.' There is a second real difference. The columnist has no audience present and so must manufacture consensus, which is what 'Surely' does in the column's last sentence: it assumes agreement rather than earning it. The speaker already has an audience in the room, and the rhetoric is built on what they have done together, the eleven Saturdays and the child on the bank, so the proof offered is shared memory rather than assertion. Weighing them, the speech is the more generous, since it invites the sceptic in, while the column is the more efficient, because a joke that lands does in one sentence what a speech needs a paragraph of concession to achieve.","m":22,"d":3,"hints":["Look at the size of what each writer actually asks for, and at what each refuses to ask for.","One text is read in private and one is heard once; let that difference drive the comparison of method.","Choose three links, and make sure one of them is a place where the two writers' methods genuinely disagree."],"codes":["AO3 (Component 1 only)","Content — range of non-fiction forms"]},{"q":"The unseen extract in your paper is presented as an article on a website, with a headline, a standfirst and a byline, and the question refers you to that digital location. Using the specification's statements about the texts that may be set, explain what this does and does not change about how you read the extract in the first minutes of Section A, and what it offers you in Question 2.","a":"It changes nothing about the status of the text - it is not transient, since transient texts are excluded - but the headline, standfirst and byline are structural features that can be analysed for what they withhold, promise and establish.","sol":"The specification states that 'texts that are essentially transient, such as instant news feeds and advertisements, will not form part of the assessment', and separately that 'the questions set may refer to a digital location for the texts, such as a website'. Those two statements together settle what a website article is: a piece of journalism that happens to be sited online, not a transient text, so it will be substantial and it will repay the same reading as a printed article. What does not change is the process: identify the form, which is journalism, predict its conventions, and read for the writer's perspective rather than the subject. What does change is that you are given extra structural material to analyse. A headline can withhold its subject to create a question the first paragraph answers; a standfirst frames the argument before it is made, so the body text can be written as if the reader already agrees; a byline establishes authority, and a writer who is named as an expert or as a resident can then make claims the reader will accept. In Question 2, which asks how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices, these are gifts: the relationship between headline, standfirst and opening paragraph is a structural choice, and most candidates skip it entirely to reach the first simile.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Two separate statements in the specification are relevant, and they pull in different directions.","Ask what a headline and a standfirst are for, and whether that counts as structure.","Answer in three parts: what the digital siting does not change, what it adds, and what you would do with it in the twelve-mark question."],"codes":["Content — texts excluded from the assessment","Content — range of non-fiction forms","Assessment overview — Section A: Non-fiction"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks is Question 3 of Section A worth, and which assessment objective does it test?","a":"Twenty-two marks, AO3 only."},{"q":"Which two kinds of text does the specification name as transient and therefore excluded?","a":"Instant news feeds and advertisements."},{"q":"How does the specification define a textual reference?","a":"Any reference to supporting evidence from the text - direct quotations or references to specific moments or events within the text."},{"q":"How long are students advised to allocate to Section A, and does that include reading time?","a":"One hour and thirty minutes, and yes, it includes reading time."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Section A of Component 1","back":"The non-fiction section: a text from Part 1 of the anthology and one previously unseen extract, three questions, all of them answered, forty-five marks, one hour and thirty minutes advised including reading time.","code":"Assessment overview — Section A: Non-fiction","open":false},{"front":"Question 1 of Section A","back":"Eleven marks, AO1 only. Select and interpret information, ideas and perspectives, supporting every point with a quotation or a reference to a specific moment. Analysis of the writer's methods earns nothing here.","code":"Assessment overview — Section A: Non-fiction","open":false},{"front":"Question 2 of Section A","back":"Twelve marks, AO2 only. Analyse how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to achieve their effects: select the device, cite the exact words or the pattern, explain the effect on the reader.","code":"Assessment overview — Section A: Non-fiction","open":false},{"front":"Question 3 of Section A","back":"Twenty-two marks, AO3 only, worked across the anthology text and the previously unseen extract. Explore links and connections between the writers' ideas and perspectives, and how these are conveyed.","code":"AO3 (Component 1 only)","open":false},{"front":"AO3","back":"Explore links and connections between writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed. Fifteen per cent of the qualification, and every one of its raw marks sits in Component 1 Question 3.","code":"AO3 (Component 1 only)","open":false},{"front":"Textual reference","back":"Any reference to supporting evidence from the text - direct quotations or references to specific moments or events within the text.","code":"Assessment overview — Section A: Non-fiction","open":false},{"front":"Example, as the specification defines it","back":"Citing specific examples of features of language and/or structure from the text. It is evidence of how the text is written, where a textual reference can simply be evidence of what it says.","code":"Assessment overview — Section A: Non-fiction","open":false},{"front":"Literary non-fiction","back":"Non-fiction that uses the methods of literature on true material. The specification names selections from autobiography, letters, obituaries and travel writing, and adds that these lists are not exhaustive.","code":"Content — literary non-fiction texts","open":false},{"front":"Transient texts","back":"Texts that are essentially transient, such as instant news feeds and advertisements, will not form part of the assessment. A website article is not transient: the questions may refer to a digital location.","code":"Content — texts excluded from the assessment","open":false},{"front":"One convention you can predict of a speech before you read it","back":"It is written to be heard once, so it repeats and patterns its phrases; it addresses the audience directly; it builds towards a call to act, often through imperatives or a concession that is then turned.","code":"Content — range of non-fiction forms","open":true},{"front":"One convention you can predict of travel writing","back":"The traveller is a character as well as a narrator; the place is judged by how far it meets or refuses the writer's expectation; sensory detail carries the judgement instead of stating it.","code":"Content — literary non-fiction texts","open":true},{"front":"Standfirst","back":"The short block of text beneath a headline in an article, framing the argument before it is made. In an extract sited on a website it is structure you can analyse, not decoration to skip.","code":"Content — texts excluded from the assessment","open":false},{"front":"Concession","back":"Stating the opposing case yourself and then turning it. In a speech it disarms an objection before the audience can raise it, and makes the answer that follows sound honest rather than defensive.","code":"Content — range of non-fiction forms","open":false},{"front":"The double voice of autobiography","back":"The self who lived the moment and the older self who now judges it. The gap between the two is where a memoir's meaning sits, and it is always worth analysing.","code":"Content — literary non-fiction texts","open":false},{"front":"Wide reading, as the specification requires it","back":"A variety of high-quality, challenging non-fiction, including whole and extended texts that make significant demands in content, structure and quality of language, read in preparation for one unseen text.","code":"Content — reading in preparation for the unseen text","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"notes","title":"The five passages written for this room - read these before the questions","items":["Passage A, travel writing: 'Night Boat to Alesund'. The ferry pulled out of Bergen at nine and for an hour the fjord did nothing worth writing down. Grey water. Grey rock. A gull that followed us for a while and then thought better of it. I had paid for a cabin and a view, and so far I had a cabin. Then the light changed. It did not fade; it drained, the way colour drains out of a face. The cliffs on the port side turned from green to iron, and the water underneath them stopped being water and became a long black corridor with a ceiling of cloud. A man beside me at the rail said something in Norwegian, laughed, and gave up on me. I stayed out until my hands stopped working. There was nothing to photograph, which was the point. In eleven years of writing about places I have learned that the ones worth returning to are the ones that refuse to pose. Alesund, when it finally arrived at four in the morning, was a smear of orange lamps and a smell of diesel and fish, and I was sorry to have reached it.","Passage B, an opinion column: 'Somebody Please Ban the Leaf Blower'. There is a machine in my street that costs about ninety pounds, weighs less than a cat, and can ruin a Saturday for four hundred people at once. It is the petrol leaf blower, and it is time we admitted what it is: a tool for moving a problem three metres to the left. Consider what it actually achieves. Leaves are lifted from a path. Leaves land on another path. A man in ear defenders - and note that he wears ear defenders, while the rest of us are issued with nothing - returns on Tuesday to lift the same leaves again. We used to have a solution to this. It was called a rake. It cost eight pounds, it lasted thirty years, it made no sound louder than a sigh, and it had the additional benefit of tiring out the person holding it. I am not asking for much. I am not proposing fines, registers, inspectors or any of the other machinery that makes people hate a good idea. I am asking for one quiet street, one morning a week. Surely that is not too much to sweep up.","Passage C, memoir: 'The Shop Bell'. My father's shop had a bell on a curved strip of brass, and I can still hear exactly how it sounded when a customer came in and how it sounded when I came in. The difference was in me, not the bell, but I was nine and I did not know that yet. I was not supposed to be behind the counter. I was supposed to be upstairs doing my homework at the table under the window, where my mother could see the top of my head from the yard. I spent four years learning how to get down the stairs in the gaps between the bell. What I wanted was not the sweets, which I could have asked for. It was the talk. Mrs Doran's hip. The bus timetable, discussed as though it were policy. A man whose name I never learned, who came in every Friday for a newspaper he did not read and stayed for forty minutes. My father died before I could tell him that the shop taught me my trade. He thought I had wasted those years on the stairs. I was listening. That was the whole of it.","Passage D, a speech to the volunteers of a river clean-up. Friends - and after eleven Saturdays in a river, I think I am allowed to call you that - thank you. Let me tell you what you have done. When we started, this stretch of the Brayford held nine supermarket trolleys, two motorbikes and a quantity of plastic I am not going to describe while you are eating. Today a child stood on that bank and pointed at a fish. A fish! In water that, eleven weeks ago, you could have stood on. Now, I have heard what is said about us. That we are a handful of people with nets. That the problem is too big, that it is upstream, that it belongs to somebody with a budget and a title. And I say: yes. It is too big. It is upstream. And we pulled out nine trolleys anyway. So I am asking you for one more season. Not for the river - the river does not know we exist. For the child on the bank. For the fish, who has made, I would say, a brave decision. Bring gloves. Bring someone who says it is pointless. I will handle them.","Passage E, a review: 'Review: The Lighthouse Cafe, Whitby'. The Lighthouse Cafe has nine tables, six of which face a wall painted the colour of tinned peas, and it does not care what you think of them. I arrived at half past eleven on a wet Tuesday and was handed a laminated menu with four items on it and the words that is it by way of explanation. The fish was the best I have eaten in three years. It arrived in a batter that shattered rather than bent, on a plate that had plainly been warmed, with chips that had been fried twice by somebody who counts. The coffee is terrible. I want to be clear about this, because everything else here is so good that you will be tempted to forgive the coffee, and you must not. Nine pounds forty. No music, no candles, no theatre. If you want a view, the sea is outside and it is free.","These five passages were written for this room so that you can practise on non-fiction nobody has taught you. In the examination your two texts are the Part 1 anthology text you have studied and an extract you have never seen; here, any pairing of these five stands in for that pair."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile - the non-fiction forms the specification names, and what to look for in each","items":["Journalism: articles. The specification names journalism first. An article has a headline and often a standfirst, an opening that makes the case interesting before it makes it, a perspective held throughout, and evidence chosen to support it. Look for where the writer moves from anecdote to argument, and for the register shifts that carry the humour or the anger.","Journalism: reviews. A review must reach a verdict and prove it with specific, checkable evidence, usually sensory. Conventions include a concession (something the reviewer disliked) that proves the praise is honest, a note of price or value, and a closing recommendation. Look for the moment the reviewer stops describing and starts instructing the reader.","Speeches. Written to be heard once, so structure is audible: repetition, tricolons, direct address in the second person, rhetorical questions, a concession that is turned, and imperatives at the close. Look for what the speaker concedes, because the concession is usually where the real argument is made.","Journals. Private, dated, provisional writing, unrevised for a reader. People and places go unexplained because the writer already knows them, thoughts are abandoned mid-way, and judgements change between entries. Look for what the writer assumes, and for the gap between what is recorded and what is felt.","Reference book extracts. Impersonal, organised by category rather than by feeling, present tense, definitions before examples, technical vocabulary explained in passing. Look for where an apparently neutral text still selects and orders, since the choice of what to include is itself a perspective.","Autobiography. Literary non-fiction built on a double voice: the self who lived the moment and the older self who judges it. Conventions include a shaped rather than a complete life, significant small objects, and a retrospective turn near the end. Look for the sentence where the older voice takes over.","Letters. Written to one named reader, so tone and register are shaped by the relationship, and the reader's expected reply is often anticipated inside the letter. Look for how the writer positions themselves - apologising, persuading, confiding - and for what is left unsaid because the recipient already knows it.","Obituaries. A life told for the sake of its meaning: a summarising opening that fixes why the person mattered, a chronological middle, characteristic anecdotes, and a close on legacy. Look for the qualities the writer chooses to make representative, since that choice is the argument.","Travel writing. The traveller is a character as well as a narrator; place is measured against expectation; sensory detail carries the judgement rather than stating it, and the journey usually changes the traveller more than the destination. Look for the standard of value the writer states or implies.","The specification's own caution: after listing autobiography, letters, obituaries and travel writing it says 'these lists are not exhaustive'. Expect a form you have not practised, and fall back on the two questions that work on any of them - who is this written for, and what does the writer want them to think?"]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Factfile - how Section A is set, timed and marked","items":["The section: a mixture of short- and long-answer questions on a non-fiction text from Part 1 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology and one previously unseen extract. Students answer all questions. Total of forty-five marks.","Timing: one hour and thirty minutes is advised for Section A, and the specification states that this includes reading time. It times the section as a whole and never times individual questions; pro rata, the marks suggest about twenty-two, twenty-four and forty-four minutes.","Tariffs: Question 1 eleven marks, AO1 only; Question 2 twelve marks, AO2 only; Question 3 twenty-two marks, AO3 only. Component 1 as a whole carries ninety raw marks, and the qualification totals one hundred and fifty.","AO1 is 'read and understand a variety of texts, selecting and interpreting information, ideas and perspectives'. The reading levels reward selection of information and ideas, use of quotations and textual references, and understanding; at the top the selection is 'detailed and persuasive', the use of quotations 'discriminating' and the understanding 'convincing'.","AO2 is 'understand and analyse how writers use linguistic and structural devices to achieve their effects'. Its levels climb from 'basic and simple comment on the effect', through 'clear explanations of the effects', to 'a detailed exploration' and finally 'a perceptive analysis of the effects of language and structure' with 'subtle and discriminating selection'.","AO3 is 'explore links and connections between writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed'. It carries fifteen per cent of the qualification and is not assessed in Component 2 or Component 3, so Question 3 is the only place it can be earned.","Two kinds of support are defined. A 'textual reference' is 'any reference to supporting evidence from the text - direct quotations or references to specific moments/events within the text'. An 'example' is 'citing specific examples of features of language and/or structure from the text'.","Marking is by levels of response and on a best-fit basis: 'students should be awarded the appropriate mark within any range on a best fit basis, balancing strengths and weaknesses in each essay' (the specification puts the words best fit in quotation marks of its own). Answers 'may display characteristics of more than one level', and markers 'must use professional judgement to decide which level is most appropriate', so a script that is mostly one level with flashes of the next sits high in the lower band.","The printed assessment grids appear in the specification under Component 3, where AO1 is marked out of twelve in four levels and AO2 out of eighteen in five levels. There is no separate printed AO3 grid, so for Question 3 work from the wording of the objective itself: links and connections, ideas and perspectives, and how these are conveyed."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - Explore the links and connections between the writers' ideas and perspectives on ordinary life, and how these are conveyed, in Passage B and Passage C (22 marks, AO3)","items":["Decoded question: 'explore links and connections' is AO3, so nothing is credited unless both texts are inside the same paragraph; 'ideas and perspectives' means what each writer thinks about ordinary life, not what each text is about; 'how these are conveyed' means half the answer must be method; 'ordinary life' is the thread, and every paragraph must be tested against it rather than decorated with it.","Thread sentence for the introduction: both writers argue that ordinary life deserves closer attention than it gets, but the columnist defends it by attacking what interrupts it, while the memoirist defends it by recording what it sounded like, so one perspective is adversarial and public and the other is retrospective and private.","Paragraph 1 - the shared idea that small things carry the value. The column's demand is 'one quiet street, one morning a week'; the memoir's treasure is 'Mrs Doran's hip' and a bus timetable 'discussed as though it were policy'. Method: the column deliberately shrinks its request so that refusing it looks unreasonable; the memoir elevates trivia by the word 'policy', so the humour in each does opposite work - the column's makes a demand look modest, the memoir's makes a small thing look large.","Paragraph 2 - the enemy in each text. For the column it is noise and the 'machinery' of fines, registers and inspectors; for the memoir it is the adult judgement that the writer had 'wasted those years on the stairs'. Method: the column uses a list of bureaucratic nouns and an ironic definition, 'a tool for moving a problem three metres to the left'; the memoir uses a delayed reveal, holding the father's verdict back until the final lines so that the whole piece becomes an answer to it.","Paragraph 3 - the reader each writer is speaking to, which is where the perspectives really separate. The column addresses a fellow-sufferer and manufactures agreement with 'Surely'; the memoir addresses nobody who can act, since the person who should hear it has died. Method: anaphora and a closing pun in one text; in the other, three plain statements at the very end, the last two of them only three words and six - 'I was listening. That was the whole of it.'","Evaluation thread to carry through all three paragraphs: which writer's method makes ordinary life feel genuinely valuable rather than merely defended? Keep returning to it so the judgement is earned by the analysis rather than announced at the end.","Conclusion: state which text conveys the shared perspective more powerfully and why the form makes it so - that the column wins the argument because it is designed to be won, while the memoir is more convincing precisely because it has no argumentative shape and nothing to gain. Do not summarise; answer."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5 (the AO2 grid, on a Question 2 asking how the writer persuades the reader that the leaf blower is absurd)","items":["Question: analyse how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to persuade the reader that the leaf blower is absurd. (12 marks, AO2)","Point: the column's persuasion is structural before it is verbal, because the writer arranges the reader's judgement to arrive before the object is named.","Evidence: the opening sentence gives only price, weight and nuisance - a machine that 'costs about ninety pounds, weighs less than a cat, and can ruin a Saturday for four hundred people at once' - and the noun 'petrol leaf blower' is withheld until the sentence after.","Analysis: the tricolon grades its three facts from the trivial to the absurd, so the reader is invited to weigh cost against harm and finds the ratio ridiculous while the object is still unidentified; the comparison to a cat makes the machine domestic and slight, which is exactly the disproportion the argument depends on. When the name finally arrives it lands as confirmation rather than information, and the mock-technical definition that follows - 'a tool for moving a problem three metres to the left' - completes the move by adopting the flat register of a product specification and turning it against the product. The reader has agreed before being asked to.","Mini-judgement: this is the most efficient device in the piece, because it converts the reader from juror to witness in two sentences, and every later joke is spent on a case that has already been conceded.","Why this is Level 5: the selection is subtle and discriminating - it takes a structural choice most answers miss rather than the obvious rhetorical question - and the comment is a perceptive analysis of effects, tracking what the reader does at each stage rather than labelling devices."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 (the same question, visibly weaker)","items":["Question: analyse how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to persuade the reader that the leaf blower is absurd. (12 marks, AO2)","Point: the writer uses short sentences and a comparison to show that the leaf blower does not really do anything useful.","Evidence: 'Leaves are lifted from a path. Leaves land on another path.', and earlier the machine is said to weigh 'less than a cat'.","Analysis: the two short sentences show that the leaves only end up somewhere else, so the job has not actually been done, and putting one straight after the other makes the reader see the leaves being moved back and forth. Saying the machine weighs 'less than a cat' makes it sound small, which helps the writer's point that it is not worth all the noise it makes.","Mini-judgement: this shows that the writer thinks the leaf blower is a waste of time.","Why this is Level 2 and not higher: the examples are relevant and correctly identified, and there is some comment on the effect - the repeated sentence shape is tied to the pointlessness of the job, which is a reading of this text rather than a label that would fit any text, and that is what lifts it clear of the bottom band. But it stays at some understanding and some comment: each effect is asserted in one clause and never traced through what the reader does next, no term is used for the parallel syntax or for the withholding of the machine's name, nothing structural is chosen beyond the single sentence pair, and the closing line restates the writer's opinion instead of judging the method. Level 3 asks for clear explanations of the effects, so every example here would need developing rather than noting."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — top-band Question 3 comparison (the specification prints no separate AO3 grid, so this is written to the wording of the objective)","items":["Question: explore the links and connections between the writers' ideas and perspectives on effort that other people dismiss, and how these are conveyed, in Passage C and Passage D. (22 marks, AO3)","Point: both texts are written against a verdict that has already been passed on the writer's work, and the difference between them is whether that verdict can still be answered.","Evidence: in the memoir the charge is the father's, that the writer 'had wasted those years on the stairs'; in the speech it is the public one that the volunteers are 'a handful of people with nets', and that the problem 'is too big' and 'is upstream'.","Analysis: the memoir answers in nine plain words placed after the sentence recording the father's death - 'I was listening. That was the whole of it.' - so the reply is structurally too late by design, and the bareness of the statements is the grief rather than a lack of feeling. The speech answers live, and its method is concession: the objections are repeated aloud, granted with a single 'yes', and reversed with one word, 'anyway'. Both writers therefore refuse to argue with the dismissal on its own terms, but where the memoir absorbs it into a private reckoning, the speech recruits it, to the point of inviting the sceptic along - 'Bring someone who says it is pointless.'","Mini-judgement: the speech presses its perspective harder because it demands a decision from people who can still make one, but the memoir's is the more disturbing account of dismissal, since it shows a defence that has outlived the person it was for.","Why this is top-band work: one shared idea holds both texts in the same paragraph; the comparison is of method as well as of perspective, which is what 'as well as how these are conveyed' requires; and the difference is found inside the similarity rather than announced as a contrast."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"What earns nothing in Section A, and what to write instead","items":["Retelling the extract. Question 1 rewards selection and interpretation, and paraphrase sits at the bottom of the reading levels. Instead, make every point a statement about what the writer thinks or values, and attach the quotation to it afterwards.","Analysing method in Question 1. The eleven marks are AO1 only, so a beautiful paragraph on sibilance earns nothing there and costs you minutes you need for the twenty-two-mark answer. Instead, save method for Question 2 and for the comparison.","Feature-spotting in Question 2. Naming a metaphor, a triplet or an emotive adjective without saying what it does to this reader in this text is 'basic and simple comment'. Instead, write the effect first, then the evidence, then the term - and choose one structural feature, because most candidates never do.","Two summaries joined at the end in Question 3. AO3 marks are for links and connections, and a marker cannot award them to a comparison that appears once, in the last sentence. Instead, build each paragraph on one shared idea and put both texts inside it.","Similarity-only comparison. 'Both writers feel strongly and both use imagery' is true and worthless. Instead, find the difference inside the similarity - the two writers agree about something but do it for different readers, in different forms, with different costs.","Running out of time. Question 3 is worth almost half of Section A, and the ninety minutes include reading. Instead, read both texts once with the questions already in mind, spend no more than the marks justify on the first two answers, and plan three links before writing the long answer."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:22:00Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Every fault in the first verdict is genuinely fixed, not papered over: the false 'two shortest sentences' superlative is gone from question 6 and from the worked example's cue; 'monosyllabic' has been replaced by the countable and correct claim that the memoir's last nine words are all of one syllable but 'listening' (and the last two sentences are three words and six, which they are); the memoirist is no longer gendered anywhere in the kit; the imperatives are now attributed to Passage D, where they exist, instead of to the review; question 5's unsupported inference about the town has been replaced by an accurate observation that the final clause passes no verdict on Alesund; the worked example and question 7 now agree that there are three closing statements; and the weaker model paragraph's rationale has been rewritten so that 'some understanding and some comment' honestly places it at Level 2 rather than convicting itself of Level 1 wording. I re-counted every numerical claim against the printed passages - the five-word 'It was called a rake.', the four-word 'The coffee is terrible.', the three-word and six-word closing sentences of the memoir, the nine words of the memoir's reply, the tricolon in the leaf-blower opening, the two verbless sentences at the close of the review - and all are right; all 212 quoted spans in the kit trace to the printed passages or to the specification and mark-conventions text, with no misquotation; and the 11/12/22 tariffs, the AO1/AO2/AO3 split, the forty-five marks, the ninety minutes including reading time, the fifteen per cent for AO3 sitting only in Component 1 Question 3, the ninety and one hundred and fifty raw-mark totals, the definitions of 'textual reference' and 'example', and the Component 3 grid shapes all match the specification exactly. What is left is two device labels that overstate what the text does ('identical syntax', 'the imperatives that end the speech') and one over-helpful hint - fix those sentences and this is publishable as it stands."}}},"transactional-writing":{"id":"EDX-4EA1","topic":"transactional-writing","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section B of Component 1 is the single biggest block of marks you will ever be asked to write for: 45 marks, of which AO4 carries 27 and AO5 carries 18, inside a paper worth 90. Across the whole qualification AO4 is worth 30% and AO5 a further 20%, so half of your grade is decided by how well you write to order. You are advised 45 minutes, you choose one of two prompts, and the prompt hands you an audience, a form or a purpose in one of six named text types. Nothing is left to inspiration: the examiner is checking whether you can hold a form, a tone and a register for a named reader, and then whether your vocabulary, paragraphing, punctuation and sentence structures are accurate and deliberate. This room teaches the six forms, the grid that marks them, and the planning and proofreading habits that move an answer up a level.","idea":[{"h":"What Section B is for","t":"The specification sets Section B to “explore and develop transactional writing skills”. Transactional writing is non-fiction that does a job for a real reader: it informs, advises, argues, persuades, judges. You are told to use what you have learned about different text types, to practise a range of non-fiction writing techniques, and to practise planning and proofreading. Those last two words are not padding — they are named in the specification because they are what separates a piece that reads as designed from one that reads as drifting. A top-level answer spends the first five minutes deciding audience, form, purpose, the single line of argument and the paragraph order, and the last five minutes reading back for spelling, punctuation and paragraph breaks, because AO4 and AO5 both reward control rather than speed.","code":"Section B: Transactional Writing"},{"h":"The six text types — a closed list","t":"Issue 7 of the specification names exactly what you can be asked to write: article for a magazine or newspaper; speech; letter; guide; review; text of a leaflet. The list is closed, so there are no surprises on the day — a diary entry, a story or a script will not be set, and if you write one you have not done the task. Because the task is set “based on a choice of two prompts involving a given audience, form or purpose”, each prompt will hand you at least one of those three and imply the other two. A top-level answer names the form in its own head before writing a word, and then lets the form show from the first line: a salutation for a letter, a greeting for a speech, a headline for an article, a section heading for a leaflet or a guide.","code":"Content — the transactional text types"},{"h":"Article for a magazine or newspaper","t":"An article is written for the readership of a named publication, so the prompt’s publication is your register instruction: a national newspaper, a local paper, a school magazine and a magazine read by parents are four different rooms. The form wants a headline that carries the angle, an opening that hooks and states that angle, developed paragraphs that each advance one part of the case, a counter-argument met rather than ignored, and a final line that lands the angle again. The commonest failure is having a topic but no angle — “school uniform” is a topic, “our uniform rules cost parents more than they save” is an angle. A top-level answer chooses one arguable claim, keeps every paragraph in service of it, and adapts its vocabulary to the people who actually buy that publication.","code":"article for a magazine or newspaper"},{"h":"Speech","t":"A speech is written to be heard by a room you can name, so it opens by greeting that room and saying why these listeners in particular matter to the argument. The form rewards direct address, an opening hook, rhetorical patterning — a triple, a repeated sentence opening, a rhetorical question, an inclusive “we” — and a structural return at the end to the image or person you opened with, followed by a call to action. Patterning used as a checklist is obvious and thin; used once, in the right place, it carries the listener. Remember that a listener cannot re-read, so signposting and short sentences do work here that they would not do on a page. A top-level answer orders its arguments for the ear, putting last the thing it wants the room to walk out holding.","code":"speech"},{"h":"Letter","t":"A letter has a single named recipient, and the first decision is the relationship: a manager, a councillor, an editor or a head teacher is formal; a friend or a relative is personal. Formal letters take a salutation and the matching sign-off — “Dear Mr Shah” with “Yours sincerely”, “Dear Sir or Madam” with “Yours faithfully” — an opening line saying who is writing and why, paragraphs that build the case in order, a clear statement of what you want done, and a courteous close. Anger is allowed; insult is not, because a reader who feels attacked will not act, and AO4 marks whether you have grasped “the expectations/requirements of the intended reader”. A top-level answer makes it easy for the recipient to say yes, and shows feeling through precise detail rather than through volume.","code":"letter"},{"h":"Guide","t":"A guide informs and advises somebody who does not yet know: new students, visitors to a town, first-time cyclists, people joining a club. That means you assume no prior knowledge, you order the information the way the reader will meet it rather than the way you happen to know it, and you use sections or headings so the meaning is clear at a glance. Second person and imperatives carry advice naturally — “bring a lock”, “you will need about twenty minutes” — and the tone is helpful, direct and confident, never sarcastic and never full of undefined jargon. The prompt may place the guide on a website, which changes nothing about the marking but does license short sections and clear headings. A top-level answer sounds like somebody who has been there, giving the reader the one detail nobody thinks to mention.","code":"guide"},{"h":"Review","t":"A review describes and judges. The judgement is the point, so the verdict is stated early, carried through every paragraph and confirmed at the end; a review that summarises the plot of a film or lists the dishes on a menu has not done the task. Evidence is what makes the verdict credible: precise description of one moment, one dish, one song, rather than adjectives piled up. One honest reservation inside a positive review buys the reader’s trust in everything else you have said. The register is personal but controlled, pitched at the readership the prompt names — a website read by people your own age is not the arts page of a national paper. A top-level answer lets the reader make a decision, because that is what a review is for.","code":"review"},{"h":"Text of a leaflet","t":"Read the instruction exactly: the paper asks for the text of a leaflet, which is the wording and not a designed artefact. You are not marked for drawing boxes, and you gain nothing for describing the photographs you imagine on it — those minutes are simply lost. What you write is a title, an opening section that answers “what is this?”, then sections in the order the reader’s questions arrive, then a closing section carrying the practical detail and the call to action. Headings and discourse markers do the cohesive work here, and the register is short, direct and purposeful, informing and persuading at once. A top-level answer uses its section headings as part of the argument, so that a reader who reads only the headings still receives the message.","code":"text of a leaflet"},{"h":"AO4 — communication, form, tone and register","t":"AO4 asks you to “communicate effectively and imaginatively, adapting form, tone and register of writing for specific purposes and audiences”, and it carries 27 of Section B’s 45 marks — 30% of the whole qualification, the largest single objective. The levels climb from communication that is “at a basic level, and limited in clarity” with “little awareness of form, tone and register”, through writing that communicates clearly with a clear sense of purpose and a secure grasp of the reader, to communication that is “perceptive and subtle” with “sophisticated use of form, tone and register”. Subtlety is the word that matters at the top: the reader is handled rather than shouted at. A top-level answer realises the task for the writer’s purpose and for the expectations of the intended reader, and never drops the form it started in.","code":"AO4 (Section B)"},{"h":"AO5 — accuracy and control of written language","t":"AO5 asks you to “write clearly, using a range of vocabulary and sentence structures, with appropriate paragraphing and accurate spelling, grammar and punctuation”, and it carries 18 of Section B’s 45 marks and 20% of the qualification. The levels reward developing and connecting information and ideas, varied vocabulary with correct spelling of irregular patterns, paragraphing that makes meaning clear, and accurate, varied punctuation; at the top they reward “a range of sentence structures accurately and selectively to achieve particular effects”. Selectively is the key word — a short sentence lands a point only if the sentences around it are longer. A top-level answer varies sentence length and shape on purpose, punctuates ambitiously but accurately, and leaves five minutes to proofread, because errors cost marks that content cannot buy back.","code":"AO5 (Section B)"},{"h":"The shape of the task on the day","t":"The specification is explicit: “Students are advised to allocate 45 minutes to Section B. There will be one writing task, based on a choice of two prompts involving a given audience, form or purpose. Students will answer one question in this section. Total of 45 marks for this section.” In the breakdown of raw marks the pair appears as Questions 4 and 5, carrying AO4 27 and AO5 18, and the specification notes that the questions set may refer to a digital location for the texts, such as a website. So: read both prompts, choose in under a minute, plan for about five, write for about thirty-five and proofread for the last five. A top-level answer chooses the prompt it has most concrete material for, not the one that sounds easiest, and never begins writing before the audience, form and purpose are settled.","code":"Assessment overview — Section B"}],"examples":[{"title":"From prompt to plan: a speech","setup":"Section B offers a choice of two prompts. Prompt 4: “Your local council is deciding whether to close the town’s only youth centre. Write a speech to be delivered to the council in which you persuade them to keep it open.” Prompt 5 is an article about school uniform. You have chosen Prompt 4 and you have five minutes before you start writing. Produce the plan: the audience, form and purpose, the arguments, their order, and the opening and closing moves.","steps":["Underline the givens and label them in the margin: form is a spoken address, audience is a room of decision-makers, purpose is to move them towards one outcome.","Fix the register from that room: councillors expect respect and evidence, so the tone is formal and warm rather than angry, and they are addressed as the people whose vote decides it.","Choose three arguments these listeners can act on: what the centre does for young people now, what closing it would cost the town later, and a cheaper alternative to closure.","Order them for the ear: the human picture first, the cost second, the alternative last, because the last thing a listener hears is the thing carried into the vote.","Set the opening and closing moves: open on one named young person’s evening at the centre, close by returning to that same person and asking directly for the vote."],"cues":["Which of audience, form and purpose does this prompt state outright, and which must you infer?","Who is in the room, and what tone will they actually listen to?","Which arguments can these particular listeners act on?","Which of your three arguments should the room hear last, and why?","What should your first and last sentences do for a listener?"]},{"title":"Holding register in a letter","setup":"Task: “Write a letter to the manager of a leisure centre you use, asking for the swimming timetable to be changed so that more pool time is open to the public.” Your first attempt at an opening reads: “Oi, your pool timetable is a joke and nobody can ever get in.” Rewrite the opening so that the form, tone and register fit the reader, and set out the paragraph sequence that follows it.","steps":["Name what the recipient can do: this is a person with the power to change a timetable, so the job of the letter is to make saying yes easy.","Diagnose the opening: insult with no information. It gives the reader nothing to act on and invites a defensive reply.","Redraft it as identity, evidence and request: “I have swum at Fairfield Leisure Centre three mornings a week for the past two years, and I am writing to ask you to look again at how the pool timetable divides club time and public time.”","Sequence the paragraphs: what the timetable does now, who is affected, the change requested, what it would cost or save the centre, then the close.","Choose the close and the sign-off: thank the reader, offer the times you have collected, and end with the sign-off that the salutation you chose demands."],"cues":["What is this reader able to do, and what would stop them doing it?","What exactly is wrong with the opening you have been given?","What three things should a formal opening line contain?","In what order does this reader need the information?","What must the last paragraph do, and what must match the salutation?"]},{"title":"Finding the angle for an article","setup":"Task: “Write an article for a national newspaper in which you argue that every school should teach cooking.” You have the topic and nothing else: no headline, no angle, no order. Produce the angle, a headline, the opening move and the paragraph order, including where the opposing view goes.","steps":["Narrow the topic to one arguable claim: not that cooking is nice, but that a school leaver who cannot feed themselves has been failed by their timetable.","Turn the claim into a headline that carries it and makes a reader stop: “We taught them Pythagoras. We never taught them to make soup.”","Choose the opening move: a single concrete scene — a first-year university student in front of an empty fridge at midnight — then the claim stated flat in one short sentence.","Order the case: cost of living, health, independence, and the objection that there is no room in the timetable, answered by naming what you would drop.","Draft the first two sentences so the angle is unmistakable, and note that the last line will return to the student at the fridge."],"cues":["What is the difference between a topic and an angle here?","What does a headline have to do besides name the subject?","How will you hook a reader who does not yet care?","Where should the opposing view sit, and what must you do with it?","What should the final line echo?"]},{"title":"Sections and signposts: the text of a leaflet","setup":"Task: “Write the text of a leaflet for families visiting a city farm, telling them what there is to see and how to make the most of a visit.” Note the wording: the paper asks for the text, not a design. Decide the sections and their order, fix the register, and write the opening section.","steps":["Rule out the design trap: no drawn boxes, no descriptions of photographs — every mark here is for wording.","List the reader’s questions in the order a family actually asks them: what is this place, what will the children see, how long does it take, what does it cost, how do we get there.","Turn each question into a heading that does work by itself: “Meet the animals”, “Plan your day”, “Getting here and getting in”.","Fix the register: second person, warm, short sentences, imperatives for advice — written for a parent reading while holding a toddler’s hand.","Write the opening section so it answers the first question and sells the visit at once: “Ten minutes from the ring road, forty animals, and not a screen in sight.”"],"cues":["What does the phrase “text of a leaflet” forbid?","Whose questions decide the order of your sections?","What makes a heading useful rather than decorative?","Who is holding this leaflet, and how long will they read for?","What two jobs must the opening section do at once?"]},{"title":"Judging with evidence: a review","setup":"Task: “Write a review of a place you have visited for a website read by people your own age.” You have chosen a café near your school. You know you liked it. Produce the verdict, the evidence, the reservation and the opening lines, pitched at that readership.","steps":["Settle the verdict before writing: good value and genuinely welcoming, but slow at lunchtime — one sentence you could say out loud.","Choose evidence that is seen rather than asserted: the price of a hot chocolate against the chain across the road, the owner who knows the regulars by name, the tables big enough to spread homework across.","Add the reservation honestly, because unbroken praise reads as advertising: the queue between twelve and one swallows half a free period.","Pitch the register for the site’s readers: personal, quick, funny if it is funny, but in Standard English and never sneering.","Write the opening so it carries the verdict from the first line, and note that the close will turn it into a recommendation with a condition attached."],"cues":["What must you decide before you write a single sentence of a review?","What is the difference between describing and merely asserting?","Why does an honest reservation make the praise stronger?","Who reads this site, and what register will they trust?","What must the final paragraph give the reader?"]},{"title":"Upgrading AO5: sentence structures used selectively","setup":"This paragraph from a speech is accurate but flat: “Our library is very important to the students. It is open every lunchtime. Many students use it for homework. The council wants to reduce its hours. This would be bad for us.” Every sentence is short, simple and begins with its subject. Rewrite the paragraph for the top AO5 band without adding any new content.","steps":["Name the fault precisely: five sentences of the same length and the same subject-verb opening, so nothing is emphasised because everything is.","Pick the one idea that deserves isolation and keep it short, so that its shortness does the work: the harm to students.","Combine the middle sentences with a subordinate clause so the detail is carried rather than listed: open the sentence with “Because”, not with the subject.","Use one piece of ambitious punctuation for a reason: a colon before the council’s proposal, so the proposal arrives as the consequence of everything before it.","Write the rewrite and read it aloud: “Because our library opens every lunchtime, it has become the place where homework actually gets done. Now the council proposes one thing: fewer hours. We would pay for that.”"],"cues":["What is wrong with the paragraph, in technical terms?","Which single idea would gain most from a short sentence?","How can two of these sentences be joined without making a list?","Which punctuation mark would make the proposal land as a consequence?","Does the rewrite still contain exactly the same information?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which of the six transactional text types is written to be heard rather than read?","a":"The speech."},{"q":"A prompt gives you an audience, a form and a purpose. Which of the three decides your register?","a":"The audience decides it first, with the purpose shaping the tone: register follows the reader and what you want that reader to do."},{"q":"Why does the instruction “text of a leaflet” matter?","a":"You are marked on the wording, not a design: write the words in clearly signalled sections rather than drawing boxes or describing pictures."},{"q":"How are Section B’s 45 marks divided?","a":"AO4 27 marks and AO5 18 marks."}]},"room":{"facts":["Section B of Component 1 carries 45 marks: AO4 27 and AO5 18. Students are advised to allocate 45 minutes to it.","There is one writing task based on a choice of two prompts involving a given audience, form or purpose; you answer one question in this section.","The six transactional text types you can be asked to write are: article for a magazine or newspaper; speech; letter; guide; review; text of a leaflet.","AO4: communicate effectively and imaginatively, adapting form, tone and register of writing for specific purposes and audiences. 30% of the qualification.","AO5: write clearly, using a range of vocabulary and sentence structures, with appropriate paragraphing and accurate spelling, grammar and punctuation. 20% of the qualification.","The writing levels are five bands; at the top AO4 rewards communication that is perceptive and subtle with sophisticated use of form, tone and register, and AO5 rewards a range of sentence structures used accurately and selectively to achieve particular effects.","The questions set may refer to a digital location for the texts, such as a website."],"questions":[{"q":"State the six transactional text types the specification says students will be asked to write.","a":"Article for a magazine or newspaper; speech; letter; guide; review; text of a leaflet.","sol":"The six are: article for a magazine or newspaper; speech; letter; guide; review; text of a leaflet. The list was set out in Issue 7 of the specification and it is closed, so a Section B prompt will always name one of these six, giving you an audience, a form or a purpose to write to.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The specification gives a closed list, so nothing outside it can be set.","Two of the six belong to journalism, two are addressed to a named reader or room, and two exist to inform and advise.","Group them as you recall them: print journalism, direct address, information forms."],"codes":["Content — the transactional text types"]},{"q":"State how many marks Section B carries and how those marks are divided between the assessment objectives.","a":"45 marks in total: AO4 27 marks and AO5 18 marks.","sol":"Section B carries 45 marks. AO4, which rewards communicating effectively and imaginatively and adapting form, tone and register for the purpose and audience, carries 27 of them. AO5, which rewards clarity, vocabulary, sentence structures, paragraphing, spelling, grammar and punctuation, carries the other 18. Students are advised to allocate 45 minutes to the section.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The two writing objectives are the only ones assessed in this section.","Communication and adaptation to the reader carries more than accuracy does.","Set out the total first, then split it between the two objectives."],"codes":["AO4 (Section B)","AO5 (Section B)","Assessment overview — Section B"]},{"q":"Read this prompt: “Write a letter to the head teacher of your old primary school, offering advice to pupils who are about to move up to secondary school.” Identify the form, the audience and the purpose, and say what each one tells you about your register.","a":"Form: letter. Audience: the head teacher, and through them the pupils about to move up. Purpose: to advise. Register: formal and courteous to the head teacher, warm and reassuring about the pupils.","sol":"The form is a letter, so it needs a salutation, a first line saying who is writing and why, ordered paragraphs and a matching sign-off. The audience is the head teacher directly, with the pupils as the people the advice is for, so the register is formal and courteous towards the adult reader while the advice itself is warm and concrete. The purpose is to advise, so the content is practical, second person where the advice is quoted, and free of scare stories. A strong answer would state that the double audience is the difficulty and would solve it by addressing the head teacher throughout while quoting the advice they could pass on.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Every Section B prompt hands you at least one of audience, form and purpose, and implies the rest.","Ask who physically opens this envelope, and then who the words are ultimately for.","Name the three givens in a line each before saying anything about tone."],"codes":["letter","AO4 (Section B)"]},{"q":"State four things AO5 rewards in a Section B answer.","a":"Developing and connecting information and ideas; varied vocabulary and accurate spelling; paragraphing that makes meaning clear; accurate and varied punctuation; sentence structures used selectively for effect.","sol":"AO5 rewards: information and ideas developed and connected so the piece moves forward; a range of vocabulary, including irregular spellings used correctly; paragraphing that makes the meaning clear to the reader; accurate and varied punctuation; and, at the top of the grid, a range of sentence structures used accurately and selectively to achieve particular effects. Any four of these, expressed in the specification’s own terms, answers the question.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["This objective is about how the writing is built, not what it argues.","Work from the largest unit downwards: whole piece, paragraph, sentence, word, mark.","Give each of your four as a short labelled point rather than a paragraph."],"codes":["AO5 (Section B)"]},{"q":"How long are you advised to spend on Section B, how many prompts are offered, and how many do you answer? Say how you would use the time.","a":"45 minutes; a choice of two prompts; answer one. Roughly a minute to choose, five to plan, thirty-five to write, five to proofread.","sol":"Students are advised to allocate 45 minutes to Section B, where there is one writing task based on a choice of two prompts involving a given audience, form or purpose, and you answer one question. A sensible division is about a minute to read both prompts and choose the one you have the most concrete material for, about five minutes to plan audience, form, purpose, the line of argument and the paragraph order, about thirty-five minutes to write, and the last five to proofread for spelling, punctuation, paragraph breaks and the sign-off. Planning and proofreading are named in the specification as skills to be practised, and both are paid for in the AO4 and AO5 grids.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The advised time for the section is printed in the assessment overview.","Two skills named in the specification need time carved out at each end of the writing.","Give the total first, then the four blocks of time in order."],"codes":["Assessment overview — Section B","Section B: Transactional Writing"]},{"q":"Write an article for a magazine read by parents in which you argue that children now spend too much of their free time online. (45 marks: AO4 27, AO5 18)","a":"An article with a headline and a single angle held throughout, pitched at parents, arguing the case with concrete detail, meeting the obvious objection, and closing on the angle.","sol":"A top-band opening: “Screen Time Is Not the Enemy. Empty Evenings Are.” — My son can build a city from nothing in a game I do not understand, and he can do it for four hours without once looking up. I used to think the problem was the screen. I have come to think the problem is what we quietly stopped offering him: a park that felt safe after six, a bus that came, a club that did not cost forty pounds a term. We handed our children a device and then took away everywhere else, and now we are surprised that they live in it. / The shape that follows: paragraph two establishes the scale with specific, ordinary detail rather than statistics the reader cannot check; paragraph three argues the real cost — sleep, conversation, the slow boredom in which children invent things; paragraph four meets the objection head on, that this generation is simply socialising differently, and concedes what is true in it before answering; paragraph five gives parents something they can actually do this week; the last line returns to the son and the four hours. Register: first person, wry, never preaching at a reader who is already tired. AO5: paragraph lengths varied, one deliberate short sentence per section, a dash and a colon used once each and correctly.","m":45,"d":2,"hints":["The publication in the prompt is your register instruction: who buys this magazine and what do they already feel about this subject?","An argued article needs one angle, not a list of everything true about the subject; decide the angle before the headline.","Plan five paragraphs with the objection placed fourth, then write an opening that puts one concrete picture before the claim."],"codes":["article for a magazine or newspaper","AO4 (Section B)","AO5 (Section B)"]},{"q":"Your school is considering making all after-school clubs compulsory. Write a speech to be given at a meeting of students and staff in which you argue for or against the proposal. (45 marks: AO4 27, AO5 18)","a":"A speech that greets both groups, takes one side, orders its arguments for a listener, meets the other side, and closes with a call to action.","sol":"A top-band opening: “Good afternoon. There are two kinds of people in this hall this afternoon: those of you who already stay behind on a Tuesday, and those of you who are counting the minutes until you can leave. I am going to spend the next four minutes arguing that the second group matters more than the first — and that making clubs compulsory is the fastest way to lose them both.” / The shape that follows: argument one, what compulsion does to something voluntary, built on one named club that works precisely because nobody is made to attend; argument two, the students the school says it is trying to help are the ones with jobs, buses and younger siblings, and compulsion punishes them for their circumstances; a turn to the opposing case, generously put — clubs do widen horizons — conceded and then answered with a workable alternative, a term of taster sessions; a close that returns to the two kinds of people in the hall and asks the staff present for one specific thing. Register: formal but spoken, direct address throughout, one triple and one rhetorical question, both placed where the argument turns. AO5: sentences varied deliberately, the shortest reserved for the call to action.","m":45,"d":2,"hints":["Name the room before you name your position: who is listening, and what does each group in it fear?","A listener cannot re-read, so decide which argument they should be holding when they leave and put it last.","Draft a greeting and one opening image, then plan three sections and the concession that precedes your close."],"codes":["speech","AO4 (Section B)","AO5 (Section B)"]},{"q":"The park nearest your home has been closed for repairs for over a year. Write a letter to the leader of your local council in which you argue that the work should be finished. (45 marks: AO4 27, AO5 18)","a":"A formal letter with salutation and matching sign-off, an opening stating who is writing and why, evidence of the effect of the closure, a clear request, and a courteous close.","sol":"A complete top-band answer, written out in full (about 470 words), so that you see a whole Section B piece and not only an opening: / Dear Councillor Whitfield, / I am a Year 11 student at Brackenhill School and I have lived on Ellis Road, fifty metres from the Marsh Road park gates, for eleven years. Those gates have now been chained for fourteen months. I am writing to ask you to give residents a completion date for the repairs, and to press for that date to be brought forward. / When the hoardings went up, the notice fixed to them promised a resurfaced path, a rebuilt play area and a reopening the following spring. I pass that notice every morning on my way to the bus. Two springs have come and gone, and the only thing that has changed behind the hoardings is the height of the grass. / I want to be fair to the council. A park is not a hospital or a school, and I understand that when budgets are cut the things that can wait are made to wait. But I would ask you to look at what the waiting has cost this part of town, because it is neither nothing nor invisible. / The small children who used to use the play area now play on the verge beside the bus stop, a metre from moving traffic. The Sunday football that filled the top field has moved to the supermarket car park, where the manager moves it on. The older residents who walked the loop path for exercise have simply stopped walking; my neighbour, who is seventy-nine, told me in March that she had not been further than the corner shop since the gates closed. None of these people have written to you. That is why I am writing. / I am not asking for money the council does not have. I am asking for three things, in order of how easily they can be done. First, a date, published on the council website and on the hoardings themselves, so that residents know whether they are waiting months or years. Second, an explanation of the delay: if a contractor has failed, say so, because people forgive a problem they understand far more readily than a silence. Third, if the funding for the play area is genuinely not yet there, reopen the half of the site that is finished. A locked gate around completed work is the hardest thing of all to defend. / I would be glad to bring a petition from Ellis Road and Marsh Road to one of your surgeries, and I can be contacted through my school. I am sixteen, so I cannot yet vote for you. The children on the verge cannot either. I hope that will not turn out to be the reason this park stays shut. / Yours sincerely, / Amira Sadiq, Ellis Road / Why it is top band: AO4 — the salutation is named and the sign-off matches it, the identity and the request arrive in the first paragraph, the concession to the council is genuine, and the register stays firm and courteous towards the one reader who can act. AO5 — the evidence is carried in long sentences, the request is broken into an ordered list, the shortest sentences are saved for the things that must land, and dates, ages and figures are punctuated accurately.","m":45,"d":2,"hints":["Decide first whether this recipient is formal or personal, because it fixes both the salutation and the sign-off.","A complaint letter that only says what is wrong gives the reader nothing to do; work out what you are asking for before you plan the paragraphs.","Open with identity, then evidence, then the request, and keep the sign-off matched to the greeting you chose."],"codes":["letter","AO4 (Section B)","AO5 (Section B)"]},{"q":"Write the text of a leaflet for families visiting a nature reserve for the first time, telling them what to expect and how to enjoy their visit. (45 marks: AO4 27, AO5 18)","a":"The wording only, organised into clearly signalled sections in the order a family needs them, second person, informative and persuasive, ending with practical detail and a call to action.","sol":"A top-band opening: “HOLLOWBROOK NATURE RESERVE — Three hundred acres. No ticket. Bring boots. / What is this place? Twenty minutes from the town centre, Hollowbrook is a stretch of wet meadow, oak woodland and one very patient heron. It is not a theme park and it does not pretend to be: there is nothing here that beeps. That is the point.” / The shape that follows: section two, “What you will see”, month by month in a sentence each, so the leaflet is useful in February as well as June; section three, “Making a day of it”, with imperatives — start at the boardwalk, keep the youngest ones ahead of you, save the hide until they are tired; section four, “Before you come”, covering boots, pushchairs, dogs and the café’s opening days; section five, “Getting here”, with the practical detail and a closing call to action inviting families to the weekend wardens’ walk. Register: warm, direct, second person, short sentences, headings that carry meaning on their own. Note that no marks are lost for the absence of a design, and none would be gained by describing photographs.","m":45,"d":2,"hints":["Read the instruction exactly: what is being asked for is the wording, and time spent on anything else is time lost.","Let the reader’s own questions, in the order they ask them, decide your sections.","Draft your headings first, then check that someone reading only the headings would still get the message."],"codes":["text of a leaflet","AO4 (Section B)","AO5 (Section B)"]},{"q":"Write a review of a place you have visited for a travel website read by people of your own age. (45 marks: AO4 27, AO5 18)","a":"A review with the verdict stated early, precise evidence for it, one honest reservation, a register pitched at young readers, and a clear recommendation.","sol":"A top-band opening: “Go to Whitby in November. I know. Everyone goes in August, eats chips on the harbour wall, queues for an hour at the abbey and comes home sunburnt and slightly disappointed. Go in November instead, when the town belongs to the people who live there and the sea is doing something interesting.” / The shape that follows: the verdict is already stated, so paragraph two supplies the evidence — the specific café where the windows fog, the price of a bed in a hostel out of season, the abbey with eleven people in it instead of eleven hundred; paragraph three continues the evidence with one thing that genuinely surprised you; paragraph four gives the honest reservation, that half the seafront closes and the last train back is inconveniently early, because unbroken praise reads as an advertisement; paragraph five turns the verdict into a recommendation with a condition attached — go, but go with a coat and a timetable. Register: personal, quick, humorous where the material is funny, Standard English throughout, never sneering at the people who go in August.","m":45,"d":2,"hints":["Decide the verdict in one sentence before writing anything, because everything else exists to support it.","Evidence in a review means precise description of particular moments, not adjectives stacked up.","Plan where the honest reservation goes, and make sure the last paragraph lets the reader decide something."],"codes":["review","AO4 (Section B)","AO5 (Section B)"]},{"q":"Write a guide, to appear on your school’s website, for students who are joining the school from another country and will arrive in the middle of the school year. (45 marks: AO4 27, AO5 18)","a":"A guide assuming no prior knowledge, ordered as the newcomer meets it, with sections or headings, second person and imperatives, and a helpful, direct register that avoids unexplained jargon.","sol":"A top-band opening: “Arriving in January / Starting a new school in the middle of the year sounds worse than it is. Everybody else has already made their friends, yes — but they have also already made their mistakes, and you get to watch instead of making them. Here is what nobody will think to tell you. / Your first morning. Come to reception, not to the main gate: the gate is locked after half past eight and reception is the low brick building with the blue door.” / The shape that follows: sections in the order the reader meets them — the first morning, the school day and what the bells actually mean, food and how paying works, the words this school uses that you will not find in a dictionary (form, tutor, set, mufti), where to go when you are lost, and how to join something in week one. Every section gives one concrete detail a real newcomer needs. Register: second person, imperatives, friendly and confident, every school-specific term defined the first time it appears; sentences kept short for a reader working in a second language, with variation for emphasis rather than for ornament. The website setting licenses clear headings and short sections.","m":45,"d":2,"hints":["This reader knows nothing about this school and may be reading in a second language; that changes both content and sentence length.","Order the information the way the newcomer will meet it, not the way you happen to know it.","Draft the headings as a sequence of moments, then write the section that covers the first morning."],"codes":["guide","AO4 (Section B)","AO5 (Section B)"]},{"q":"You have already planned a speech to a public meeting arguing that your town needs a late-night bus service. Using the same evidence, write the text of a leaflet to be handed to shoppers in the town centre, persuading them to support the service. (45 marks: AO4 27, AO5 18)","a":"The wording only, in a title and named sections a walking reader can scan: what is proposed, why it matters, the answer to the obvious objection, and a closing section of practical detail with a call to action. The speech's evidence carries over; its greeting, spoken rhythm and build towards a final climax do not.","sol":"A top-band opening: “THE LAST BUS LEAVES AT 6.40 p.m. / That is the whole problem, on one line. If you work an evening shift, study late, or simply want to eat in your own town centre after dark, this town stops serving you before most of the country has finished work.” / The shape that follows: section two, “What we are asking for”, states the proposal in two sentences — an hourly service to midnight, Thursday to Sunday, on the existing route; section three, “Who it is for”, uses the same evidence the speech used, but one case per short paragraph: the hospital cleaner, the sixth-former on a Saturday job, the restaurant that closes its kitchen early because its staff cannot get home; section four, “But can the town afford it?”, meets the objection in the reader's own words and answers it with the cost per resident rather than the total; section five, “What you can do today”, carries the consultation date, the address to write to and the petition link. / What changes from the speech and what does not: the greeting, the sustained rhetorical patterning and the climax-last order all go, because a leaflet is scanned by someone still walking and may be read in any order, so the message goes in the title and the first section and the rest is broken into headed chunks a reader can dip into. The register shortens: second person, imperatives, sentences a shopper can take in during fifteen seconds. The close changes purpose — a speech asks a room to support a proposal, a leaflet must give a stranger the practical detail that lets them act. The evidence itself does not change; it serves both forms, which is why planning evidence well pays twice. / AO5: headings and discourse markers carry the cohesion, paragraphs are short and single-minded, and the one long sentence in the leaflet is saved for the case that deserves it.","m":45,"d":3,"hints":["Start from the difference between a listener who has sat down to hear you and a shopper who is still walking.","The evidence carries over; the greeting, the spoken rhythm and the build to a final climax do not — decide what takes their place before you write.","Draft the title and the section headings first, then write the opening section, remembering that a leaflet must deliver its message before the reader decides whether to keep it."],"codes":["speech","text of a leaflet","AO4 (Section B)","AO5 (Section B)"]},{"q":"Write a review for your local newspaper of a community event you have attended, judging it honestly and, at the same time, persuading readers that the council should fund it again next year. (45 marks: AO4 27, AO5 18)","a":"A review with the verdict early and supported by precise description, including one honest reservation, that carries a second purpose alongside the first: every piece of evidence is chosen so that it also answers whether the event is worth funding, and the close recommends to both the ordinary reader and the decision-maker reading over their shoulder.","sol":"A top-band opening: “Nothing about the Riverside Lantern Parade should work. It happens in November, it is run by eleven volunteers and a man with a van, and it costs the town about the price of resurfacing one car park. I went expecting forty damp people and a raffle. I came home three hours later having walked a mile in the dark behind a paper heron the size of a car, in a crowd of two thousand, and I have not stopped talking about it since.” / The shape that follows: the verdict is already given, so paragraph two supplies the evidence for it — the particular moment the lanterns turned on to the bridge, the queue at the one food stall, the primary school choir singing to people who had come out of their houses to listen; paragraph three gives the honest reservation, because unbroken praise reads as an advertisement and persuades nobody: the parking was chaos and the route was not accessible to wheelchairs for its last two hundred metres; paragraph four is where the second purpose does its work, turning the same evidence towards the funding question — two thousand people, eleven volunteers, the cost of a car park — and answering the councillor's real objection, that a one-night event leaves nothing behind, with what the school choir's parents said afterwards; the close recommends the parade to the reader and, in the same sentence, tells the council what it would be cancelling. / The difficulty this task adds: two purposes in one piece. Neither may be announced. The persuasion is carried entirely by which evidence the review chooses and where it is placed — and the reservation is part of the persuasion, because a reader who sees you concede the parking believes you about the crowd. / Register: personal, quick, humorous where the material is funny, Standard English throughout, never sneering at volunteers. AO5: sentence lengths varied, the shortest kept for the verdict and for the final claim, numbers and names punctuated accurately.","m":45,"d":3,"hints":["Two things are being asked of one piece of writing; work out which one the reader must never see you doing.","Choose evidence that answers both questions at once — whether the event was any good, and whether it is worth paying for.","Decide the verdict and the one honest reservation before you plan, then place the reservation where it makes the funding argument more believable rather than less."],"codes":["review","AO4 (Section B)","AO5 (Section B)"]},{"q":"Write an article for a national newspaper arguing that mobile phones should be allowed in schools. Your readers are mainly adults who already believe the opposite. (45 marks: AO4 27, AO5 18)","a":"An article that concedes the reader’s position honestly before answering it, holds one angle, uses concrete evidence rather than assertion, and keeps a register that respects a hostile readership.","sol":"A top-band opening: “I Agree With You About Phones. That Is Why I Would Let Them In. / You have read the stories, and so have I: the ruined lessons, the filmed fights, the eleven-year-old awake at two in the morning. I am not going to pretend those things are invented. I am going to argue something narrower and, I think, harder to dismiss — that a blanket ban teaches a child nothing about the device they will carry for the rest of their life, and that a school which confiscates has simply moved the problem to the school gate.” / The shape that follows: paragraph two concedes fully and specifically, because a hostile reader stops reading the moment they feel misrepresented; paragraph three narrows the claim to what is actually defensible, supervised use in named situations; paragraph four supplies evidence a sceptical adult can weigh, drawn from what happens when the bell goes rather than from statistics they cannot check; paragraph five answers the strongest objection, not the weakest; the close returns to the eleven-year-old awake at two in the morning and asks who is going to teach her, if not the school. Register: calm, unsneering, adult to adult — with a hostile readership, courtesy is a technique. AO5: long sentences carrying concession, short ones carrying the claim.","m":45,"d":3,"hints":["A reader who disagrees will stop at the first sign that you have not understood their position.","Concession is not weakness: work out what you can honestly grant before you work out what you will argue.","Plan the concession as your second paragraph, then narrow your claim to the part of it a hostile reader cannot easily refuse."],"codes":["article for a magazine or newspaper","AO4 (Section B)","AO5 (Section B)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does Section B carry, and how are they divided between AO4 and AO5?","a":"45 marks: AO4 27 and AO5 18."},{"q":"Which two of the six transactional text types are forms of journalism?","a":"The article for a magazine or newspaper, and the review."},{"q":"The prompt says “Write the text of a leaflet”. What must you not spend time on?","a":"A design: no drawn boxes or described pictures — write the wording in clearly signalled sections."},{"q":"A formal letter opens “Dear Sir or Madam”. How must it sign off?","a":"Yours faithfully."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Transactional writing","back":"Non-fiction writing that does a job for a real reader: informing, advising, arguing, persuading or judging. Section B sets one such task and marks it on AO4 and AO5.","code":"Section B: Transactional Writing","open":false},{"front":"The six transactional text types","back":"Article for a magazine or newspaper; speech; letter; guide; review; text of a leaflet. The Issue 7 list is closed, so every Section B prompt names one of these.","code":"Content — the transactional text types","open":false},{"front":"Article: what the form requires","back":"A headline and an opening that fix one angle, developed paragraphs, the opposing view met, and a last line that lands the angle. Register set by the publication named in the prompt.","code":"article for a magazine or newspaper","open":false},{"front":"Speech: what the form requires","back":"Direct address to the named room: a greeting, an opening hook, patterning used sparingly and for a reason, a turn to the opposing view, and a close with a call to action.","code":"speech","open":false},{"front":"Letter: what the form requires","back":"Salutation and matching sign-off, an opening line saying who is writing and why, paragraphs that build the case, a clear statement of what you want done, and a courteous close.","code":"letter","open":false},{"front":"Guide: what the form requires","back":"Written for someone who knows nothing yet: information ordered as the reader will meet it, sections or headings, second person and imperatives, a helpful and direct tone, jargon defined.","code":"guide","open":false},{"front":"Review: what the form requires","back":"A verdict stated early and carried through, precise evidence rather than piled adjectives, one honest reservation, and a recommendation for the readers the prompt names. Never a plot summary.","code":"review","open":false},{"front":"Text of a leaflet: what the form requires","back":"The wording, not a design: a title, a section answering what this is, sections answering the reader's next questions in order, and a closing section with practical detail and a call to action.","code":"text of a leaflet","open":false},{"front":"AO4 in Section B","back":"Communicate effectively and imaginatively, adapting form, tone and register for specific purposes and audiences. Worth 27 of Section B's 45 marks and 30% of the qualification.","code":"AO4 (Section B)","open":false},{"front":"AO5 in Section B","back":"Write clearly, using a range of vocabulary and sentence structures, with appropriate paragraphing and accurate spelling, grammar and punctuation. Worth 18 of Section B's 45 marks and 20% of the qualification.","code":"AO5 (Section B)","open":false},{"front":"Section B assessment overview","back":"One writing task from a choice of two prompts involving a given audience, form or purpose; answer one. 45 marks, 45 minutes advised. A prompt may refer to a digital location such as a website.","code":"Assessment overview — Section B","open":false},{"front":"Register","back":"The level of formality and the kind of vocabulary a particular reader expects. Set it from the audience and purpose given in the prompt, then hold it to the final line.","code":"AO4 (Section B)","open":false},{"front":"Rhetorical techniques that suit a speech to a decision-making audience","back":"A triple building to the decision itself; a rhetorical question aimed at the listeners' own experience; direct address that names the vote they are about to take; an inclusive “we” that places speaker and listeners on the same side.","code":"speech","open":true},{"front":"Sentence structures used selectively (top AO5 band)","back":"Length and shape varied on purpose: a short sentence to land a point, a subordinate clause to carry detail without listing, a colon or dash used once and correctly to open out an idea.","code":"AO5 (Section B)","open":false},{"front":"Planning and proofreading in Section B","back":"Both are named in the specification. Five minutes at the start for audience, form, purpose, the thread and paragraph order; five at the end for spelling, punctuation, paragraph breaks and the sign-off.","code":"Section B: Transactional Writing","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5 (top band, AO4 and AO5)","items":["Grid used — and a warning about mark ranges: the specification prints its writing assessment grids only under Component 3 Assignment B, where AO4 is marked out of 18 in five levels and AO5 out of 12 in five levels. Those printed ranges belong to a 30-mark coursework task (and match Component 2 Question 2); they are not the ranges for this 45-mark task. Component 1 Section B is judged on the same five level descriptors, but on its own grid, which scales AO4 to 27 marks and AO5 to 18. So “Level 5” below means the top of the five levels for AO4 and for AO5 on the Section B grid — not a mark of 16–18.","Task this opening answers: “Write an article for your local newspaper in which you argue that young people should be given more of a say in how your town is run.”","The opening, as written: “The bus shelter on Marsh Road has been broken since last November. I know, because I stand in it in the rain at ten past eight every weekday morning, along with about thirty other people under the age of eighteen. Not one of us was asked about it. Not one of us was asked about the new one-way system either, or about the youth centre, or about moving the library's opening hours to the exact three hours a week when every teenager in this town is sitting in a classroom. We are not this town's future problem. We are a present, soaking, patient part of it — and it is time somebody handed us a pen.”","The point it makes: young people are already affected by local decisions, so excluding them from those decisions is not caution but carelessness. The angle is stated as a claim a reader could dispute, not as a topic.","The evidence it uses: three named, ordinary, checkable local details — the broken shelter, the one-way system, the library hours — all chosen because a local newspaper reader recognises them, which is how the article adapts to the publication named in the prompt.","The chain of reasoning: the concrete image comes first so the reader is standing in the rain before the argument arrives; the anaphora of “Not one of us was asked” turns three separate grievances into one pattern; the final short clause converts the grievance into a request, which is what an argued article must eventually do.","Why it sits in the top AO4 band: communication is perceptive and subtle — the reader is persuaded by being shown rather than told; form is sophisticated, with an article's angle established in the first paragraph and a headline implied by it; tone is wry rather than aggrieved, which is the tone a local readership will actually finish; register is adult and Standard English throughout while remaining recognisably the voice of a sixteen-year-old.","Why it sits in the top AO5 band: sentence structures are varied selectively — a long opening sentence, two short parallel sentences, an extended list, then a closing sentence broken by a dash for emphasis; punctuation is accurate and ambitious; the triple “present, soaking, patient” is punctuated correctly; vocabulary is precise rather than showy, and the paragraph is a single developed unit rather than a series of unconnected statements.","Mini-judgement: this opening earns the top band because every device is doing a job the prompt set — the detail is local because the paper is local, the tone is wry because the reader is tired of being lectured, and the last clause is short because a request must be unmistakable."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (the middle of the grid, and why it stops there)","items":["Grid used: the same five writing levels as the Level 5 model, applied to the same task — “Write an article for your local newspaper in which you argue that young people should be given more of a say in how your town is run.” As above, the specification prints mark ranges only for Component 3 Assignment B (AO4 out of 18, AO5 out of 12); Component 1 Section B uses the same descriptors on its own grid of AO4 27 and AO5 18. “Level 3” here therefore means the middle of the five levels, not a particular mark.","The opening, as written: “Lots of young people feel that they are ignored by the people who run our town. In this article I am going to argue that young people should be given more of a say in local decisions. Firstly, young people use many of the facilities in the town, such as the bus stops, the library and the youth centre. Secondly, we will be living here for much longer than the adults who make the decisions. I think the council should listen to us more because it would make the town a better place for everyone.”","The point it makes: the same point as the Level 5 version — young people are affected and should be consulted. The content is not the problem.","The evidence it uses: the same three facilities, but named as a list rather than shown. Nothing here could only have been written by someone who lives in this town, so the article has not adapted to the publication.","Why it is clearly written but only mid-band for AO4: it communicates clearly and there is a clear sense of purpose, which is exactly what the middle of the grid describes; but the form barely shows — announcing “In this article I am going to argue” is a school essay habit, not a newspaper opening — and tone and register are generic, addressed to no particular readership. The reader is told the writer's opinion rather than moved towards it.","Why it is only mid-band for AO5: the writing is accurate, which earns its place in the middle, but every sentence is roughly the same length and opens with its subject; the connectives are mechanical (“Firstly”, “Secondly”); vocabulary repeats (“young people”, “town”, “decisions”) where a synonym or a pronoun would connect the ideas; and no punctuation beyond the comma and full stop is used for effect.","The three moves that would raise it: replace the announcement with one concrete local image; replace “Firstly/Secondly” with detail specific enough that only a resident could have written it; vary the sentence lengths so that the final claim is the shortest sentence in the paragraph.","Mini-judgement: a competent, accurate opening that a marker would place in the middle of both grids — it does the task but does not do it to anybody in particular, and that missing reader is the whole distance between this and the top band."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the six transactional text types (the Issue 7 list)","items":["The closed list: article for a magazine or newspaper; speech; letter; guide; review; text of a leaflet. Section B sets the task in one of these six and in nothing else.","Every prompt gives at least one of audience, form and purpose and implies the other two; name all three in your plan before writing a word.","Article — form: a headline, an opening that fixes the angle, developed paragraphs, and a final line that returns to the angle. No address block, no salutation, no bullet points.","Article — audience cue: the prompt names the publication (a local newspaper, a national newspaper, a school magazine, a magazine read by parents); that readership sets the register.","Article — purpose cue: verbs such as argue, persuade, explain, inform. An argued article needs one arguable claim, not a survey of the subject.","Article — structure: angle in paragraph one, three or four developed sections, the opposing view met head on, and a close that lands the angle again.","Article — register: first person is allowed and usually helps; Standard English throughout; contractions only where the publication is informal.","Speech — form: direct address to a live audience you can name. No address block and no headings; every word is meant to be heard.","Speech — audience cue: the prompt names the room (governors, the school council, a year group, a public meeting). Greet them and say why these listeners in particular matter.","Speech — purpose cue: persuade, argue, convince, welcome, thank. A persuasive speech ends with a call to action.","Speech — structure: greeting and hook, the case in ordered sections, a turn to the opposing view, and a close that returns to the opening image.","Speech — register: rhetorical patterning (a triple, a repeated sentence opening, a rhetorical question, an inclusive “we”) used sparingly and at the turns of the argument, never as a checklist.","Letter — form: a salutation with the matching sign-off (“Dear Mrs Okafor” with “Yours sincerely”; “Dear Sir or Madam” with “Yours faithfully”) and a first line saying who is writing and why.","Letter — audience cue: a single named recipient. Decide at once whether the relationship is formal (a manager, a councillor, an editor, a head teacher) or personal (a friend, a relative).","Letter — purpose cue: complain, request, apply, advise, persuade, thank. A complaint letter must say what it wants done, not only what went wrong.","Letter — structure: why I am writing; what the situation is; what I want; what happens next.","Letter — register: courteous even when angry. Feeling is shown in precise detail, because a reader who feels attacked will not act — and AO4 marks your grasp of the reader's expectations.","Guide — form: informative and advisory prose for someone who does not know yet. Sections or headings are allowed and usually help the reader.","Guide — audience cue: the prompt names the newcomer (new students, visitors to a town, first-time cyclists). Assume no prior knowledge and define every specialist term.","Guide — purpose cue: inform and advise. The second person and the imperative carry advice naturally.","Guide — structure: order the information the way the reader will meet it, not the way you happen to know it — before, during, after.","Guide — register: helpful, direct and confident. No sarcasm, no showing off, and one concrete detail per section that only someone who has been there would know.","Review — form: a judgement supported by evidence, written for a named publication or website. A plot summary or a list of contents is not a review.","Review — audience cue: the prompt names the readers (a website read by people your age, a local paper, a school magazine). Give them what they need in order to decide.","Review — purpose cue: describe and judge. The verdict comes early, is carried through, and is confirmed at the end.","Review — structure: what it is; the verdict; the evidence; one honest reservation; the recommendation.","Review — register: personal but controlled. Opinion expressed through precise description rather than through piled-up adjectives.","Text of a leaflet — form: the wording only. Write the words that would appear, in named sections. Do not draw boxes and do not describe photographs instead of writing text.","Text of a leaflet — audience cue: the prompt names the people who will pick it up (families, visitors, parents, new members) and often where they will pick it up.","Text of a leaflet — purpose cue: usually inform and persuade at once — say what this is, then move the reader to do something.","Text of a leaflet — structure: a title; an opening section answering “what is this?”; sections answering the reader's next questions in order; a closing section with the practical detail and the call to action.","Text of a leaflet — register: short, direct and cohesive. Headings and discourse markers carry the cohesion the AO5 grid rewards."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — a Section B task in the board's wording (one task, a choice of two prompts)","items":["The paper: Component 1, Section B — Transactional Writing. Questions 4 and 5 are one writing task based on a choice of two prompts involving a given audience, form or purpose; you answer one. 45 marks (AO4 27, AO5 18), 45 minutes advised.","Question 4: “Your school is thinking of replacing the annual sports day with a whole-school charity walk. Write a speech to be given at a meeting of students and staff in which you argue for or against the change.” (45 marks)","Question 5: “Write a guide for students joining your school in September, advising them on how to settle in. Your guide will appear on the school website.” (45 marks)","Choice, decided in under a minute: Question 4. Reason: it gives an audience you can picture and a genuine two-sided argument, which is easier to sustain for 45 minutes than a guide that can drift into a list.","Decoded: form — a speech, so it must be heard, greeted and structured for the ear. Audience — students and staff in the same hall, two groups with different worries: students fear losing an event they enjoy, staff fear the organisation. Purpose — argue for or against one specific change, not sports day in general.","Position taken: for the change. Reason for taking it: the case against collapses into nostalgia, which gives you three paragraphs of feeling and no argument; the case for lets you address both halves of the room.","The thread to sustain (state it once in the plan and return to it in every section): sports day serves the few and asks everyone else to watch; a charity walk serves everyone and still serves the few.","Opening: greet both groups, then one sentence that separates them — the people who won something on sports day, and the people who spent it on the grass bank. Name both. Then the claim, in a short sentence.","Paragraph one: what sports day actually is for most students — the grass bank, the three-hour wait between two races — evidence by description, not by assertion. Return to the thread.","Paragraph two: what a walk does instead — everybody moves, nobody is eliminated, and the school raises money for something the students choose. Concrete: name the distance, the route, the charity vote.","Paragraph three: answer the staff's worry directly, because half the room is thinking it — supervision, cost, the risk of chaos — and answer it with a workable arrangement rather than a promise.","Paragraph four (the turn to the opposing view): grant honestly what is true in it — for the athletes among us, sports day is the one day of the year that belongs to them — then answer it by keeping the track events as a shortened morning before the walk. Conceding the strongest point, not the weakest, is what lifts AO4.","Close: no new argument. Return to the two kinds of people named in the opening, name the decision the room is about to make, and end on the shortest sentence in the speech, asking for one specific thing.","What the conclusion must say: that the change is not about abolishing sport but about who the day is for — and that the room can have both, if it votes for both.","AO5 plan: vary paragraph length deliberately; one short sentence per section, kept for the point that matters; one colon and one dash across the whole piece, used correctly; check the spelling of the words you will repeat (charity, achievement, definitely, separate) before you write them.","Last five minutes: read back for missing full stops, homophones (their/there, your/you're), paragraph breaks that have disappeared, and a close that has quietly introduced a new argument."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"The 45 minutes, minute by minute","items":["Minute 0–1: read both prompts. Choose the one you have concrete material for, not the one that sounds easier. Write the form, audience and purpose at the top of your plan.","Minutes 1–6: plan. The thread in one sentence; four or five paragraph headings in order; where the opposing view sits; the first image and the last line.","Minutes 6–40: write, keeping the form visible from the first line — salutation, greeting, headline or section heading, depending on the text type.","Minutes 40–45: proofread. Spelling first, then punctuation, then paragraph breaks, then the close and sign-off. Errors you find are marks you keep.","Length: there is no word count in Section B. A well-shaped piece of about five or six developed paragraphs, finished and proofread, beats an unfinished longer one every time.","The four commonest ways to lose marks: writing the wrong form, ignoring the named audience, announcing your intentions instead of carrying them out, and running out of time before the close."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:00:07Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Specification facts are accurate throughout — every quotation from the spec verifies verbatim, the 45-mark Section B tariff with AO4 27 / AO5 18, the 45 minutes, the choice of two prompts and the closed Issue 7 list of six text types are all correct — so the fixes needed before publishing are the borrowed Component 3 mark ranges in the two model paragraphs, the invented 6-mark tariff on the last two questions, and at least one complete top-band model answer for a 45-mark task."}}},"anthology-part2":{"id":"EDX-4EA1","topic":"anthology-part2","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This room is the whole of Component 2, Section A: one essay question, thirty marks, forty-five minutes advised, on one poetry or prose text from Part 2 of the Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology. The text is printed in the examination paper, so nothing here is a memory test — the marks are for what you do with the words in front of you. The split matters more than anything else on this paper: AO1 carries twelve marks and AO2 carries eighteen, so two thirds of the essay is paid for by analysis of how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to achieve their effects, and only one third by selecting and interpreting ideas and perspectives. An essay that retells the poem or the story in order, however fluently, is sitting in the bottom band of the larger objective. The same texts are also the material for Assignment A of Component 3, the non-examined alternative, where three texts (at least one poetry and one prose) are discussed in about twelve hundred words. This room teaches the method that serves both routes: name the method, quote or locate it precisely, explain the effect on the reader, and keep answering the question as you go.","idea":[{"code":"Disabled","h":"Owen, Disabled: the man in the chair, and the structure that puts his past beside him","t":"Owen writes in the third person about a young man maimed in the war — the second line has him legless, with the sleeves sewn short at the elbow, so the arms are taken as well — who now sits in a wheelchair in a park at dusk, waiting to be put to bed. The poem is built on alternation: the cold, grey, sexless present, and the warm, lit, admired past that keeps breaking into it — the evenings of dancing and glow-lamps, the girls' glances, the football match after which, half-drunk and wanting to please a girl, he enlisted. Owen makes the recruiting officers complicit in a single clause: they smiled as they wrote down the lie about his age. The colour imagery does the work of the argument — the colour that has drained from his face is set against the blood he poured away, so that the loss is figured as a body emptying. The close is a shrunken repetition, the man asking why nobody comes to take him in. A top-level answer does not narrate this sequence; it argues that the structure itself is the meaning, because every return to the past is a measurement of what the present lacks, and it reads the passive constructions as the poem's way of showing a man to whom things are now done."},{"code":"“Out, Out–“","h":"Frost, “Out, Out–“: a personified saw, a swerve into indifference","t":"Frost's title borrows Macbeth's cry over a snuffed candle, so the poem is framed as a statement about how quickly a life goes out before it has begun. The poem runs as one unbroken verse paragraph of blank verse — no stanza break to prepare you for the accident — set on a Vermont farmstead with the mountain ranges visible at sunset. The buzz-saw is personified from the first line, and the specification itself cites the violent verbs 'snarled' and 'rattled' as an example of citing a feature of language. Frost delays the catastrophe with a sweet-scented, almost pastoral landscape, then lets the saw seem to leap at the boy's hand when his sister calls supper. The boy is the one who understands first, and his appeal not to lose the hand is the only direct speech given weight. After the ether and the failing pulse, the watchers turn back to their work because they are not the ones who are dead. A top-level answer reads the ending as deliberately withheld judgement: Frost refuses to comment, and the flat last clause is itself the technique, forcing the reader to supply the outrage the poem will not."},{"code":"An Unknown Girl","h":"Alvi, An Unknown Girl: a hennaed hand and a divided sense of belonging","t":"The speaker sits in an evening bazaar in India while a girl she does not know applies henna to her hand. Alvi shapes the poem as one long, narrow column of short lines, which slows the reading to the speed of the design being drawn and mimics the thin line of henna itself. The refrain about the evening bazaar and the repeated phrase about the hennaing of the hand return like a chant, so the encounter is remembered as ritual rather than as transaction. Around it Alvi places the modern, commercial, half-Western surface of the bazaar — neon, bright signs, displayed goods — against the traditional patterning on the skin, and the henna's peacock design is the image the speaker clings to. The ending turns the hand into longing: the pattern will fade, but the speaker imagines reaching back across a whole country towards the girl and the place. A top-level answer argues that the form carries the theme of a divided identity — the poem cannot settle into stanzas any more than the speaker can settle into one culture — and that the fading of the henna is what makes the ending an image of loss rather than of arrival."},{"code":"The Bright Lights of Sarajevo","h":"Harrison, The Bright Lights of Sarajevo: couplets holding ordinary life against a siege","t":"Harrison, writing as a reporter-poet during the siege of Sarajevo, sets the daily labour of survival — the queues for bread rations and for refills of gas canisters, the water carried up flights of stairs, the shell craters in the pavement, the danger of moving about — against the ordinary spectacle of young people coming out at dusk to walk and meet each other. The poem's method is contrast sustained at every level: the rhyming couplets sound orderly and even jaunty while the content is lethal, and the rhyme keeps clicking shut over images of shrapnel and bereavement. Because there is no electric light, the poem is lit only by the moon and by small flames, and the closing image gives a young man and woman a moment of lit intimacy in the dark. Harrison's diction moves between the journalistic and the tender, and the enjambment runs sentences across the couplets so the verse never sounds complacent. A top-level answer does not simply say the poem is about hope in war; it argues that the form is the argument, because the neatness of the rhyme is what measures the disorder it describes, and it shows how the final source of light is human and momentary rather than civic and reliable."},{"code":"Still I Rise","h":"Angelou, Still I Rise: repetition as the engine of defiance","t":"The poem addresses an unnamed oppressor directly as 'you', which makes the reader overhear a confrontation rather than a meditation. Angelou builds most of it in quatrains with a regular rhyme on alternate lines, then breaks that pattern at the end into shorter lines in which the phrase about rising is repeated until it closes the poem. The specification's own example of citing a structural feature comes from here: the clear pattern of repetition in the three consecutive lines that each begin by conceding what the oppressor may do. Rhetorical questions taunt the addressee about the speaker's confidence and sexuality; similes and metaphors of wealth — oil, gold mines, diamonds — reclaim value for a body that has been treated as worthless; and the closing movement widens from one speaker to the inherited hope of enslaved ancestors. A top-level answer shows that the repetition is not decoration but argument: each concession granted to the oppressor is immediately overturned by the refrain, so the structure enacts the rising it describes, and the shift out of the quatrains marks the point at which the speaker stops answering and simply asserts."},{"code":"The Story of an Hour","h":"Chopin, The Story of an Hour: a reversal built on a withheld point of view","t":"Chopin gives the reader the heart trouble in the first sentence, so everything that follows is read under threat. Louise Mallard is told, with great care, that her husband Brently has been killed in a railroad disaster; she weeps at once, then goes alone to her room. There Chopin switches the narration inward, and the window becomes the structural device: the new spring life in the treetops, the patches of blue sky, the sounds from the street all arrive as sensation before the feeling is named. The famous whisper of freedom is withheld until the reader has almost guessed it, and Chopin is careful to record that Louise knows she will weep again at the funeral, which prevents the story from being a simple celebration. The ending reverses twice — Brently walks in alive, and the doctors misread her death as joy. A top-level answer argues that the irony is structural rather than verbal: the story is short and its point of view is tightly controlled so that the reader knows what the doctors cannot, and the last line convicts a whole society of misreading a woman's inner life."},{"code":"The Necklace","h":"Maupassant, The Necklace: an ironic plot that tests a character's values","t":"Maupassant opens with narratorial judgement rather than scene: Mathilde Loisel is a pretty woman born into a family of clerks who believes herself made for luxuries she does not have. Her husband, a minor clerk, obtains an invitation to a ministerial ball and gives up his own savings so that she can have a dress; she then borrows a diamond necklace from her wealthy friend Madame Forestier. The specification cites the moment she disregards the other jewels and selects the diamonds as an example of interpretation through a textual reference. She triumphs at the ball, loses the necklace, and the couple replace it with one costing thirty-six thousand francs, borrowed at ruinous terms, then spend ten years in poverty repaying it, which coarsens and ages her. The last line reveals the original was paste, worth at most five hundred francs. A top-level answer treats the ending not as a trick but as the completion of the story's argument about appearance and value, and shows how the calm, economical narration and the exact figures let the irony land without the narrator ever having to moralise."},{"code":"Significant Cigarettes (from The Road Home)","h":"Tremain, Significant Cigarettes: a migrant's journey told from inside his head","t":"The extract follows Lev, a forty-two-year-old man from an Eastern European village whose sawmill has closed, on a long coach journey west to England. Tremain narrates in the third person but stays tightly inside Lev's consciousness, so the coach, the strangers and the road are all filtered through his grief for his dead wife Marina and his anxiety about the daughter, Maya, he has left with his mother, Ina. The cigarettes of the title are how he measures the journey: he rations them, and each one marks a stage, a decision or a memory, which is Tremain's way of turning an ordinary habit into a structural device. Beside him sits Lydia, who is studying English phrases and who offers him food, and their halting exchanges supply the only dialogue and the first English words of the new life. A top-level answer shows how the shifting between present journey and remembered past controls the reader's sympathy, and how the small physical details — the smell of the coach, the shared food, the carefully counted cigarettes — carry the large theme of dislocation without Tremain ever stating it."},{"code":"Whistle and I’ll Come to You (from The Woman in Black)","h":"Hill, Whistle and I’ll Come to You: atmosphere built from sound and withholding","t":"Hill's title alludes to M. R. James's ghost story, which signals to the reader the kind of tale this is before anything happens. The extract is narrated in the first person by Arthur Kipps, looking back on the nights he spent alone at an isolated house reachable only across a tidal causeway, with marsh, estuary water and sea mist on every side. Hill's method is almost entirely aural and structural: the tension is made from the sounds the narrator hears in the dark and cannot see the cause of, from the dog's reaction arriving before the narrator's own, and from long, clause-heavy sentences that slow the pace before short sentences break it. The whistle out in the night and the dog's rush into the marsh, followed by the narrator's own terrified wading after her, put the reader in physical danger as well as supernatural. A top-level answer argues that the fear comes from what Hill withholds — the retrospective narrator keeps hinting at knowledge he has not yet shared — and analyses the pacing of sentence length as the instrument that produces suspense."},{"code":"Night","h":"Munro, Night: a retrospective narrator releasing information slowly","t":"Munro's narrator is an adult looking back on an episode when she was about fourteen, in rural Ontario. She is operated on for appendicitis, and something else — something the adults compare in size to a turkey's egg — is removed and only half explained to her, which plants the story's method of partial knowledge. The following winter she cannot sleep, and a thought arrives that terrifies her: that she might harm her sleeping younger sister, Catherine. The horror of the story is that nothing happens; the fear is entirely interior, and Munro's long retrospective sentences keep circling the thought without naming it for as long as possible. The release comes when the narrator goes outside in the night and finds her father awake, and he answers her confession calmly, treating such thoughts as ordinary, which dissolves the terror without dramatising it. A top-level answer analyses the double voice — the child living it and the adult telling it — and shows how the withholding and release of information, not any event, is what makes the story frightening and then moving."},{"code":"Assessment overview — Section A (Component 2)","h":"What Section A actually asks: one essay, thirty marks, two thirds of them AO2","t":"The specification sets one essay question on a poetry or prose text from Part 2 of the anthology; the text is made available in the examination and the section is worth thirty marks, with forty-five minutes advised. The marks divide as twelve for AO1 and eighteen for AO2, so an essay must be built on how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to achieve effects, with interpretation of ideas and perspectives running alongside rather than instead. The board defines the two kinds of support it pays for: a textual reference is any supporting evidence from the text, whether a direct quotation or a reference to a specific moment, and an example is a citation of a specific feature of language or structure. Marking is by levels on a best-fit basis. A top-level answer answers the question in its first sentence, takes a method per paragraph rather than a stanza per paragraph, quotes briefly and precisely, explains the effect on the reader, and returns to the question at the end of every paragraph and at the close."},{"code":"Assignment A: Poetry and Prose Texts (Component 3)","h":"The coursework route: three texts, one focus, about twelve hundred words","t":"Component 3 is the non-examined alternative to Component 2, internally set and assessed and externally moderated. Assignment A asks students to select three texts from Part 2 of the anthology, with at least one poetry text and one prose text among them, to choose a focus such as setting, character, event or theme, and to plan the response. The task is set in the board's own wording: discuss how the writers use language and structure to present the chosen focus in the three texts, discussing the writers' ideas and perspectives on that focus, discussing how they use language and structure to achieve their effects, and including textual references to illustrate the points made. It is thirty marks, twelve for AO1 and eighteen for AO2, in approximately twelve hundred words. A top-level answer picks a focus narrow enough to be argued, and organises by the focus rather than text by text, so that each section puts the three writers' methods side by side and judges which handling of the focus does what to the reader."}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning a structural observation into AO2 marks (Still I Rise)","setup":"The question is the Section A essay: explore how Angelou presents defiance in Still I Rise, supporting your answer with close reference to the text, including brief quotations (30 marks, AO1 12 and AO2 18). The feature you have chosen is the run of three consecutive lines in which the speaker concedes what the oppressor may do to her — shooting, cutting, killing — each opening with the same two words, followed by the refrain about rising. Write one paragraph on it. Remember AO2 pays for the selection of a device, an accurate example of it, and explanation of its effect, and pays nothing for naming the device alone.","steps":["Name the device precisely: this is anaphora, and the specification's own wording for it is a clear pattern of repetition across three consecutive lines, not just 'repetition'.","Locate the example without copying the lines out: say that each of the three lines begins by granting the oppressor a violent act, and that each act is more final than the last.","Explain the effect of the concession: by handing the oppressor the worst he can do, the speaker removes his power to threaten her with it.","Explain the effect of the refrain that follows: the same short assertion answers all three lines at once, so the structure performs the rising it describes.","Judge it in a clause that answers the question: the defiance is presented as inevitable rather than hopeful, because the pattern makes the reply automatic."],"cues":["Which named device is this, and how would the specification itself word it?","How do you cite three lines of a poem in copyright without copying them out?","What does the speaker gain by granting the threat rather than denying it?","What job does the repeated short line do after those three?","Which word in the question has your paragraph not yet used?"]},{"title":"Reading an ending you are told not to explain (Out, Out–)","setup":"The question is: explore how Frost presents the reactions of those around the boy in “Out, Out–“ (30 marks). The moment is the close of the poem: after the doctor's ether, the boy's pulse weakens and stops, and the watchers, because they are not the ones who are dead, turn back to their own work. You have already written a paragraph on the personified saw. Build the paragraph that uses this ending. The poem runs as a single unbroken verse paragraph of blank verse with no stanza break.","steps":["Start from the narration, not the event: Frost's speaker reports the death and the turning away in the same breath and offers no comment on either.","Cite the structural fact that supports it: there is no break, no pause and no closing reflection, so the poem ends almost mid-stride.","Analyse the effect of the withheld judgement: the reader, given nothing to agree with, supplies the outrage the poem refuses to state.","Set it against the earlier warmth of the sunset landscape and the sister's call to supper, so the coldness is felt as a change of temperature.","Judge: Frost presents the reaction not as cruelty but as the ordinary survival of people whose work cannot stop, which is the harsher reading."],"cues":["Whose behaviour is the question actually about?","What can you say about the poem's shape that supports it?","If the poet will not judge, who has to?","What earlier part of the poem makes this ending feel colder?","Is 'the neighbours are heartless' the strongest reading available?"]},{"title":"Making an AO1 point that interprets rather than retells (The Story of an Hour)","setup":"The question is: explore how Chopin presents Louise Mallard's feelings in The Story of an Hour (30 marks, AO1 12 and AO2 18). The moment is the time Louise spends alone in her room facing the open window, before she names what she is feeling. You must write the paragraph that earns AO1: selection, supporting textual reference, and interpretation of ideas and perspectives. Chopin has already told the reader, in the story's first sentence, that Louise has a heart condition.","steps":["Interpret first: what she feels in the chair is not grief ending but a possibility arriving, and she resists it before she accepts it.","Support it by reference to a specific moment rather than a long quotation: the spring life in the treetops and the patches of blue sky reach her as sensation before she has a word for them.","Deepen the interpretation: Chopin is careful to record that Louise expects to weep again at the funeral, so her feeling is not simple relief and not dislike of her husband.","Connect it to the perspective the story offers: what she is released from is the private will of another person over her own, however kindly meant.","Close with what this tells the reader about her, in the question's own terms: her feeling is presented as self-recognition, which is why the doctors at the end cannot name it."],"cues":["Does your first sentence say what she feels, or what happens?","Which short, exact moment carries that feeling?","What detail stops this being read as hatred of Brently?","What is the story saying she has been freed from?","How does this paragraph make the final line inevitable?"]},{"title":"Building an atmosphere paragraph from pacing (Whistle and I'll Come to You)","setup":"The question is: explore how Hill creates tension in Whistle and I'll Come to You (30 marks). You have chosen the night sequence in which the narrator, alone in the isolated house across the causeway, hears sounds in the dark, the dog reacts before he does, and he ends up out on the marsh in the mist trying to reach her. The narration is first person and retrospective: Kipps is telling this years later, and he keeps hinting at what he did not yet know. Write one paragraph on how the writing itself, rather than the events, produces the fear.","steps":["Name the controlling method: Hill builds the fear aurally, giving the narrator sounds whose source is never shown.","Analyse the sentence structure as the instrument: long, accumulating sentences stretch the wait, then a short sentence lands the shock, so the reader's pulse is set by the punctuation.","Add the animal as a device: the dog registers the disturbance first, which confirms that something is there and removes the comfort of imagination.","Use the retrospective voice: the older narrator's hints that worse is coming make every ordinary noise provisional.","Judge in the question's terms: the tension is created by withholding, so the most frightening thing in the passage is the thing Hill never lets the narrator see."],"cues":["Which sense is doing nearly all the work here?","What can you say about sentence length that a reader would feel?","Why does the dog's reaction matter more than the narrator's?","What does the older, telling voice add that the younger one cannot?","In one clause, what is the source of the fear?"]},{"title":"Choosing and organising a Component 3 focus across three texts","setup":"This is Assignment A of Component 3: discuss how the writers use language and structure to present a chosen focus in three texts from Part 2 of the anthology, at least one poetry and one prose, in about twelve hundred words, for thirty marks (AO1 12, AO2 18). You have been given a free choice of focus and are considering 'the effect of war on the individual', for which you would use Disabled, The Bright Lights of Sarajevo and Significant Cigarettes. Decide whether the focus works and set out how the response should be organised.","steps":["Test the focus against the third text first: Lev leaves an economically ruined village, not a war, so the focus does not fit and must be narrowed or the text changed.","Narrow the focus to something all three texts genuinely share: 'how writers present a life that has been interrupted and cannot be resumed'.","Check the specification's rule on the set: with two poems and one prose text, the requirement for at least one poetry and one prose text is met.","Organise by the focus, not by text: one section on how the interruption is first shown, one on how the past is made to intrude, one on how each writer ends.","Fix the balance before writing: since AO2 carries the larger tariff, each section must be led by a named method — Owen's alternation, Harrison's couplets, Tremain's rationed cigarettes — with the ideas argued through them."],"cues":["Does every chosen text actually meet the proposed focus?","What wording would cover all three without straining?","Does the chosen set satisfy the board's rule about text types?","What is wrong with three sections each headed by a title?","Which objective carries more marks, and what does that mean for each section's first sentence?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How do the thirty marks of Component 2, Section A divide between the assessment objectives?","a":"Twelve marks for AO1 and eighteen for AO2, so two thirds of the essay is analysis of language and structure."},{"q":"What does the specification mean by a 'textual reference' as opposed to an 'example'?","a":"A textual reference is any supporting evidence from the text — a direct quotation or a reference to a specific moment or event; an example is the citing of a specific feature of language or structure."},{"q":"Why is a paragraph organised as 'stanza one, then stanza two, then stanza three' unlikely to reach the top levels?","a":"It organises by the text rather than by method, so it tends to retell; the levels reward selection of devices and explanation of their effects, which needs a paragraph per method."},{"q":"What must an Assignment A response for Component 3 contain that a Component 2 answer need not?","a":"Three texts from Part 2, including at least one poetry text and one prose text, discussed under one chosen focus in about twelve hundred words."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 2, Section A is one essay question on a poetry or prose text from Part 2 of the anthology: 30 marks, 45 minutes advised, the text made available in the examination.","The 30 marks divide as AO1 12 and AO2 18 — two thirds of the essay is for analysis of how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to achieve effects.","AO1 is marked in four levels out of 12 (1–3, 4–6, 7–9, 10–12); AO2 is marked in five levels out of 18 (1–3, 4–6, 7–10, 11–14, 15–18), on a best-fit basis.","A 'textual reference' is any reference to supporting evidence from the text — direct quotations or references to specific moments or events; an 'example' is the citing of a specific feature of language and/or structure.","Part 2 contains five poems — Disabled (Owen), “Out, Out–“ (Frost), An Unknown Girl (Alvi), The Bright Lights of Sarajevo (Harrison), Still I Rise (Angelou).","Part 2 contains five prose texts — The Story of an Hour (Chopin), The Necklace (Maupassant), Significant Cigarettes from The Road Home (Tremain), Whistle and I'll Come to You from The Woman in Black (Hill), Night (Munro).","Component 3 is the non-examined alternative to Component 2; Assignment A is about 1200 words on three Part 2 texts, at least one poetry and one prose, on a chosen focus, for 30 marks (AO1 12, AO2 18).","Component 3 Assignment B is imaginative writing of about 700 words, 30 marks (AO4 18, AO5 12), whose purpose should be to narrate, describe or entertain; the word count is advisory with no penalty for exceeding it.","The AO2 command is 'understand and analyse how writers use linguistic and structural devices to achieve their effects' — the levels climb from basic comment on effect, to clear explanation, to detailed exploration, to perceptive analysis."],"questions":[{"q":"State the number of marks, the assessment objectives and their tariffs, and the advised time for Component 2, Section A. (4 marks)","a":"30 marks in total: AO1 12 and AO2 18; 45 minutes advised; one essay on one poetry or prose text from Part 2.","sol":"Section A carries 30 marks and sets one essay question on a poetry or prose text from Part 2 of the anthology, which is made available in the examination. Of the 30 marks, 12 are for AO1 — reading and understanding, selecting and interpreting information, ideas and perspectives — and 18 are for AO2 — understanding and analysing how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to achieve effects. The advised time is 45 minutes. Because AO2 carries the larger tariff, the essay must be organised around methods and their effects rather than around the events of the text.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Everything here is set by the assessment overview for the section, not by any one text.","Two objectives are assessed, and they are not equally weighted.","Answer as a short list: total, each objective with its tariff, the time, and what the question is set on."],"codes":["Assessment overview — Section A (Component 2)"]},{"q":"Distinguish between a 'textual reference' and an 'example' as the specification defines them, and give the consequence for how you write a paragraph. (3 marks)","a":"A textual reference is any supporting evidence — a quotation or a reference to a specific moment; an example cites a specific feature of language or structure. A paragraph needs both: the method named and evidenced, and the moment located.","sol":"The specification defines a textual reference as any reference to supporting evidence from the text, whether a direct quotation or a reference to a specific moment or event within it, and an example as the citing of specific examples of features of language and/or structure. The distinction matters because AO1 pays for the reference that supports an interpretation, while AO2 pays for the example of a named feature and the explanation of its effect. In practice a strong paragraph does both jobs: it locates the moment precisely, names the device operating there, and then explains what the device does to the reader, so that the same short piece of evidence earns under both objectives.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Both terms are defined word for word in the specification's marking guidance.","One term is about evidence in general, the other about features of language and structure in particular.","Define each in a sentence, then say which objective each mainly serves."],"codes":["Assessment overview — Section A (Component 2)"]},{"q":"State the requirements the specification places on the set of texts and the length of Component 3, Assignment A. (3 marks)","a":"Three texts from Part 2, including at least one poetry text and one prose text; a chosen focus; approximately 1200 words; 30 marks (AO1 12, AO2 18).","sol":"Assignment A requires the student to select three texts from Part 2 of the anthology, and there must be at least one poetry text and one prose text among them. The student chooses a focus for the assignment — for example setting, characters, events or themes — and makes notes and plans the response before writing. The task is set in the form 'Discuss how the writers use language and structure to present the chosen focus in the three texts', and requires discussion of the writers' ideas and perspectives on the focus, discussion of how they use language and structure to achieve their effects, and textual references to illustrate the points made. The response is approximately 1200 words and carries 30 marks, 12 for AO1 and 18 for AO2.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The rule is about the number of texts and about the kinds of text.","The board also fixes what the student chooses before writing, and roughly how long the piece is.","List the constraints in order: how many texts, which kinds, what is chosen, how long, how marked."],"codes":["Assignment A: Poetry and Prose Texts (Component 3)"]},{"q":"Identify the linguistic feature the specification itself cites from “Out, Out–“ and state what it is said to convey. (2 marks)","a":"The violent verbs 'snarled' and 'rattled', used of the buzz-saw, which the specification says convey shock.","sol":"The specification's own worked example of citing a feature of language is taken from Frost: the author uses violent verbs, 'snarled' and 'rattled', to convey shock. Both verbs belong to the personification of the buzz-saw, which is given an animal's voice in the poem's opening lines and is later made to seem to leap at the boy's hand. Citing them in an essay is only the first half of an AO2 point; the marks come from explaining that the animal verbs make the machine a predator with a will of its own, so that the accident, when it comes, reads as something done by the saw rather than something that merely happened.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["The specification prints this example inside its guidance on what an 'example' means.","It concerns the machine at the centre of the poem and the kind of word used for it.","Name the two verbs and the effect the specification attaches to them."],"codes":["“Out, Out–“"]},{"q":"Name the structural feature the specification cites from Still I Rise, and state where it occurs. (2 marks)","a":"A clear pattern of repetition — three consecutive lines beginning 'You may…' — at lines 21 to 23.","sol":"The specification's example of citing a structural feature is taken from Angelou: the author structures the poem using a clear pattern of repetition, three consecutive lines at lines 21 to 23 that each begin by conceding an act of violence the addressee may perform. The device is anaphora, and in the essay it must be explained rather than merely named: by granting the oppressor the worst he can do, the speaker empties the threat of its power, and the short refrain that answers the three lines makes the recovery seem automatic, so the structure performs the rising the poem asserts.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["It is a structural, not a lexical, observation, and it spans more than one line.","The specification quotes the opening words of three consecutive lines and gives their line numbers.","Name the device, say what the three lines have in common, and give the line reference."],"codes":["Still I Rise"]},{"q":"Explore how Owen uses structure to present the young man's loss in Disabled. Support your answer with close reference to the poem, including brief quotations. (Component 2, Section A: 30 marks — AO1 12, AO2 18)","a":"Alternation between the grey present and the lit, admired past; the passive role he now occupies; the diminished, repeated question at the close.","sol":"Owen structures the poem as a movement between two times, and the loss is measured in the gap between them. The present is fixed and cold: the man sits in a wheeled chair in a park at dusk in a suit of grey the poem calls 'ghastly' and describes as altered — he is legless, and the sleeves are sewn short at the elbow, so the war took his arms too — waiting for someone to put him to bed, and Owen keeps him in constructions where things are done to him. Into that present Owen lets the past break repeatedly — the lit evenings, the dancing, the glances of girls, the football match after which, half-drunk and hoping to please a girl, he enlisted and lied about his age while the recruiters smiled and wrote the lie down. Each return to the past is a measurement, because each remembered pleasure is one the present cannot supply, and the sequence is arranged so that the enlistment is revealed as trivial in motive, which makes the cost obscene. The colour imagery ties the two times together: the colour that has gone from his face is set against the blood poured away, so the emptying of the body is also the draining of the life. At the close the poem contracts to a small repeated question about why nobody comes for him, and the diminishment of the verse is itself the point: a man who once chose, however foolishly, now cannot even choose when to go indoors. The structure, then, does not describe the loss; it enacts it.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The question is about structure, so the answer is about how the poem is arranged in time.","Track what the poem does each time it leaves the present, and what the reader is invited to compare.","Open by stating the organising contrast, then take the transitions in order and finish on the close."],"codes":["Disabled"]},{"q":"Explore how Chopin uses structure to shape the reader's response in The Story of an Hour. Support your answer with close reference to the text, including brief quotations. (Component 2, Section A: 30 marks — AO1 12, AO2 18)","a":"The heart trouble planted in the first sentence; the withheld interior view released at the window; the double reversal and the doctors' misdiagnosis.","sol":"Chopin's control of sequence is the story's principal method. The first sentence gives the reader Louise's heart trouble, which makes every later scene provisional and plants the ending before the story has begun. The news of Brently's death is delivered with elaborate care by Josephine and Richards, and Louise weeps at once — Chopin refuses the reader the expectation that she is cold. Only when Louise is alone does the narration move inside her, and even then Chopin delays: the open window, the new spring life in the treetops, the patches of blue sky and the sounds of the street arrive as sensation before any feeling is named, so the reader senses what is coming a moment before Louise does, and is made complicit in it. The whisper of freedom is therefore a release for the reader as much as for her. Chopin then guards the story against sentimentality by recording that Louise knows she will weep at the funeral, which keeps the feeling about self-possession rather than dislike of a husband. The ending reverses twice: Brently walks in unharmed, and Louise dies. The last line, in which the doctors attribute the death to the joy that kills, is structurally devastating because the reader alone knows what she has lost, and the misdiagnosis convicts the world of the story of being unable to imagine a woman's inner life at all.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Ask what the reader is told first, and what difference that makes to everything after it.","Track where the narration moves inside Louise and what Chopin delays before naming her feeling.","Begin with the planted detail in the opening sentence and end with what the final line does to the reader."],"codes":["The Story of an Hour"]},{"q":"Explore how Maupassant presents Mathilde Loisel's values in The Necklace. Support your answer with close reference to the text, including brief quotations. (Component 2, Section A: 30 marks — AO1 12, AO2 18)","a":"The judging narrative voice at the opening; the choice of the diamonds over the other jewels; the exact financial figures; the final revelation.","sol":"Maupassant presents Mathilde's values through a narrator who judges before the plot begins: she is introduced as a pretty woman born into a family of clerks who suffers because she believes herself made for the delicacies and luxuries she does not possess, so her dissatisfaction is established as a habit of mind rather than a response to events. Her husband's kindness sharpens the point, since he surrenders his own savings for her dress and is met with complaint. The specification's own example of interpretation comes from the borrowing scene: offered her friend's jewels, she disregards the many elegant pieces and chooses the diamond necklace, which shows that she values not beauty but the appearance of expense. Maupassant's narration is economical and unemotional, and the exact figures do the moral work — the replacement bought for thirty-six thousand francs, the ruinous borrowing, the ten years of scrubbing and haggling that coarsen her hands, her voice and her looks. The irony is completed by the last line's revelation that the original was paste worth at most five hundred francs, which is not a trick but the argument's conclusion: Mathilde has spent a real decade of her life paying for an appearance, exactly as she had always wished to live. The judgement the story invites is therefore double-edged, since the world it describes really does reward the appearance she pursued.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Start with how the reader is positioned before Mathilde has done anything.","Three moments carry her values: the dress, the choice of jewel, and the decade of repayment.","Take the moments in order and make the last line the completion of the argument, not a surprise ending."],"codes":["The Necklace"]},{"q":"Explore how Hill creates tension in Whistle and I'll Come to You. Support your answer with close reference to the text, including brief quotations. (Component 2, Section A: 30 marks — AO1 12, AO2 18)","a":"Aural horror with unseen causes; sentence length controlling pace; the dog reacting first; the retrospective narrator withholding what he knows.","sol":"Hill's tension is manufactured almost entirely out of sound and delay. The narrator is alone in an isolated house that can only be reached across a tidal causeway, so the setting itself removes the possibility of help before anything happens; the marsh, the estuary water and the sea mist make sight unreliable and force the narrator, and the reader, to depend on hearing. What is heard in the dark is never shown, and that withholding is the method: an explained noise is finished with, an unexplained one keeps working. Hill controls the pace through syntax, accumulating long, clause-heavy sentences that stretch out the waiting and then dropping into short sentences at the moments of shock, so the reader's breathing is set by the punctuation rather than by the events. The dog is used as an instrument of proof: because she reacts before the narrator does, the reader cannot dismiss the disturbance as nerves, and when she is drawn out into the dark the danger becomes physical as well as supernatural, with the narrator wading into the marsh after her. Over all of this sits the retrospective first-person voice, an older Kipps who keeps hinting at knowledge he has not yet shared, so that every ordinary sound is provisional. The most frightening thing in the passage is therefore the thing Hill never allows to be seen.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Work out which sense the passage relies on, and why the setting makes the other senses useless.","Look at what the sentences are doing at the slow moments and at the sudden ones.","Lead with the controlling method, then take the instruments in turn, ending on the narrator's voice."],"codes":["Whistle and I’ll Come to You (from The Woman in Black)"]},{"q":"Explore how Alvi uses form and structure to present the speaker's sense of identity in An Unknown Girl. Support your answer with close reference to the poem, including brief quotations. (Component 2, Section A: 30 marks — AO1 12, AO2 18)","a":"The single long narrow column of short lines; the two refrains; the contrast of neon commerce with traditional patterning; the fading henna at the close.","sol":"Alvi's form is the argument. The poem runs as one long, narrow column of very short lines with no stanza divisions, which does two things at once: it slows the reading to the pace of a design being drawn, and it refuses the settled shape that stanzas would give, so the poem's body is as unresolved as the speaker's sense of where she belongs. Across that column Alvi lays two refrains — the return to the evening bazaar and the return to the hennaing of the hand — and repetition turns a brief commercial transaction into a ritual the speaker wants to belong to. The setting is deliberately doubled: the bazaar's neon, its bright commercial surface and its displayed Western goods are set against the traditional pattern being drawn on the skin, so the speaker is surrounded by a culture that is itself in two minds. The hennaed peacock design becomes the thing she clings to, an identity worn on the outside of the body. The ending is what makes the poem a poem of loss rather than of arrival: the pattern is already fading as skin renews itself, and the closing image has the speaker imagining herself reaching across a whole country towards the girl and the place. The form allows no closure, and that is the point.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Describe the shape of the poem on the page before you say anything about its meaning.","Two phrases keep coming back; ask what repeating them does to an ordinary transaction.","Set the shape and the refrains against what happens to the henna at the end."],"codes":["An Unknown Girl"]},{"q":"Explore how Harrison presents the contrast between ordinary life and the siege in The Bright Lights of Sarajevo. Support your answer with close reference to the poem, including brief quotations. (Component 2, Section A: 30 marks — AO1 12, AO2 18)","a":"Rhyming couplets whose neatness rubs against lethal content; queues and shell damage against evening promenading; light reduced to moon and small flames.","sol":"Harrison writes as a reporter, and the poem's contrast is organised at every level. Its content alternates between the labour of survival — the queues for bread rations and for refills of gas canisters, the water that has to be carried up flights of stairs, the shell-scarred pavements, the risk of being in the open — and the ordinary human business of young people coming out at dusk to walk and to meet each other, and the effect of placing the second inside the first is to make courtship look like defiance without Harrison ever saying so. The form carries the same contrast: the poem moves in rhyming couplets whose click of closure sounds orderly, even jaunty, while the material they enclose is lethal, and enjambment runs the sentences across the rhymes so that the verse never settles into comfort. The diction moves the same way, from the flat vocabulary of reportage to something much tenderer as the couple come together. Light is the poem's central symbol and its most economical stroke: with the power gone, the only brightness is the moon and the small flames people carry, so the title's promise of bright lights is fulfilled by a single lighter flaring long enough to show a young woman's face. The judgement the poem invites is measured rather than consoling: what survives the siege is not the city's brightness but a human, momentary one.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The contrast is not only in what is described; ask what the verse form is doing while it is described.","Follow the poem's treatment of light from the title to the final moment.","Open with the organising contrast, give it in content and in form, and judge what the ending claims."],"codes":["The Bright Lights of Sarajevo"]},{"q":"Component 3, Assignment A task: Discuss how the writers use language and structure to present a mind under strain in Significant Cigarettes, Night and Disabled. Include textual references to illustrate the points you make. (30 marks: AO1 12, AO2 18; approximately 1200 words on three Part 2 texts, at least one poetry and one prose)","a":"Tremain: third person held inside Lev, journey structure, rationed cigarettes marking stages, memory intruding. Munro: adult retrospective voice, circling syntax, withheld then released thought, resolution through the father. Owen: third-person portrait, present cut into by the lit past, passive constructions. Organised by aspects of the focus, not text by text.","sol":"All three writers keep the reader inside a mind rather than in front of an event, but they arrive there by different routes. Tremain narrates in the third person yet restricts everything to Lev's consciousness, so the coach, the road west and the strangers around him are given only as he registers them, and his grief for his dead wife Marina and his anxiety about the daughter he has left behind arrive as intrusions into an otherwise dull journey. The structure is the journey itself, and the cigarettes of the title are the device that segments it: rationed and counted, each one marks a stage, a decision or a memory, so a small habit is made to carry the shape of the narrative. The strain is shown, not stated, in the smallness of what Lev can control. Munro uses a first-person adult voice recalling herself at about fourteen, and the doubling of that voice is her method: the older narrator knows the outcome and withholds it, circling the unspeakable thought — that she might harm her sleeping sister Catherine — in long, qualifying sentences before she will name it. Where Tremain's strain is caused by loss that is external and continuing, Munro's is entirely interior and produces no event at all. Owen, the poetry text the set requires, works from the outside in: a third-person portrait fixes the young man in his wheeled chair at dusk and lets the lit past break into the grey present, while the passive constructions that leave things being done to him put the reader inside a mind that can no longer choose. The endings divide the three: Tremain leaves Lev still travelling, with the strain unresolved and only a stranger's kindness to set against it; Munro releases the tension through the father's calm, unhorrified answer in the night, which normalises the thought and dissolves it; Owen contracts to a small repeated question nobody answers. Because AO2 carries eighteen of the thirty marks, an Assignment A response must put the three texts together under aspects of the focus rather than one after another, lead each section with a named method, and keep its textual references brief and exact. The judgement available is that strain is presented as bearable only where somebody else is listening, which is why Munro's is the one ending that lifts.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Begin from how each text gets the reader inside a head, and notice that the three narrators are not the same kind of narrator.","Each writer has one controlling structural device; find it in each and put all three side by side in the same section.","Build sections on aspects of the focus rather than one text after another, and let the three endings carry the judgement."],"codes":["Significant Cigarettes (from The Road Home)","Night","Disabled","Assignment A: Poetry and Prose Texts (Component 3)"]},{"q":"Component 3, Assignment A task: Discuss how the writers use language and structure to present suffering that is watched by others in Disabled, “Out, Out–“ and The Story of an Hour. Include textual references to illustrate the points you make. (30 marks: AO1 12, AO2 18; approximately 1200 words)","a":"Owen: onlookers' eyes pass over him, passive constructions, past–present alternation. Frost: unjudging narration, no stanza break, the turning away. Chopin: watchers outside the door, controlled point of view, the doctors' misreading.","sol":"All three texts put a sufferer in front of spectators who fail them, and each writer uses a different instrument to make the failure felt. Owen's onlookers are present throughout Disabled: the boys' voices in the park, the women whose eyes now pass over him to the strong men, the crowd that cheered him home less than it had cheered a goal. He builds the man's isolation grammatically as much as visually, keeping him in constructions where he is acted upon and will be taken indoors when someone decides, so the structural alternation between the lit past and the grey present is also an alternation between being looked at with admiration and being looked past. Frost's watchers are given almost nothing: the poem runs unbroken, without a stanza break to mark the death, and the narration declines to judge the people who, because they are not the ones who are dead, return to their work. The withholding is the device, since the reader is left to supply the condemnation and must then notice that the alternative — stopping — is not available to people whose living depends on the day's labour. Chopin's watchers are the most solicitous and the most wrong: Josephine kneels outside the door begging to be let in, imagining an illness, while inside Louise is discovering herself, and the doctors close the story by attributing her death to the joy that kills. The methods differ — Owen's imagery and passivity, Frost's flat unjudging syntax, Chopin's controlled point of view and double reversal — but the argument each reaches is the same, and Chopin states it most sharply: the watchers' account of the suffering is not merely inadequate, it is the exact opposite of the truth, and the reader's privileged knowledge is what makes the ending land.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Find what is genuinely common: in each text somebody is being observed and the observers get it wrong.","Each writer fails the sufferer differently — by looking past, by turning away, by misnaming; pair each failure with a named method.","Organise by the shared idea rather than text by text, and finish by judging which text's observers are most damning and why."],"codes":["Disabled","“Out, Out–“","The Story of an Hour","Assignment A: Poetry and Prose Texts (Component 3)"]},{"q":"Practice planning task (not an exam question: Assignment A itself is marked as one response out of 30). A student plans an Assignment A response on 'the presentation of freedom' using Still I Rise, The Story of an Hour and An Unknown Girl. Evaluate whether this set and focus meet the requirements, and set out how the response should be organised. (8 marks)","a":"The set is valid (two poems and one prose text, so at least one of each) and the focus fits all three; organise by aspects of the focus, lead each section with a named method, keep to about 1200 words.","sol":"The set satisfies the specification, which requires three texts from Part 2 with at least one poetry text and one prose text: Still I Rise and An Unknown Girl are poetry and The Story of an Hour is prose. The focus also holds across all three, though it must be defined precisely, because freedom means something different in each: in Angelou it is a defiance asserted against an addressed oppressor, in Chopin it is a private self-possession glimpsed and then destroyed, and in Alvi it is a more ambiguous freedom to choose which culture one belongs to, which the poem declines to resolve. The response should therefore be organised by aspects of the focus rather than text by text: one section on how each writer first signals that freedom is at stake, one on the language through which freedom is imagined — Angelou's similes of wealth, Chopin's spring imagery at the open window, Alvi's patterned henna — and one on what each ending decides. Because AO2 carries eighteen of the thirty marks, every section must be led by a named method with an accurate example, and the writers' ideas and perspectives argued through the method rather than beside it; textual references should be brief and exact. About twelve hundred words means roughly three developed sections and a short conclusion, so breadth must be sacrificed to depth. The conclusion should judge rather than summarise: the strongest line available is that only Angelou presents freedom as achieved, while Chopin and Alvi present it as glimpsed and then withdrawn, and that this difference is produced by their forms — a refrain that can repeat indefinitely against a story and a poem that both end by taking something away.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Check the set against the board's rule about text types before you judge the focus.","The word 'freedom' is not the same idea in all three; decide what would have to be defined for the focus to work.","Plan sections that put all three writers together under one aspect of the focus, and remember which objective carries the larger tariff."],"codes":["Assignment A: Poetry and Prose Texts (Component 3)","Still I Rise","An Unknown Girl"]},{"q":"Explain why a paragraph that identifies three devices in a stanza but says nothing about their effects cannot reach the top AO2 levels, and rewrite the method it should follow. (6 marks)","a":"Feature-spotting sits in the lowest AO2 levels because those levels reward comment on, explanation of and finally analysis of effects; the fix is one device per paragraph, accurately exemplified, with the effect on the reader explained and linked to the question.","sol":"The AO2 levels are built on what the student does after identification. The lowest levels describe basic and simple comment on effect, the middle levels clear explanations of the effects of language and structure, and the top level a perceptive analysis of those effects with subtle and discriminating selection of the devices themselves. A paragraph listing three devices has therefore only completed the part of the task that earns nothing on its own, and worse, listing three shows undiscriminating selection, which is the opposite of what the top band describes. The method to follow is single-tracked: choose one device that genuinely does something to the reader; cite it exactly, either as a brief quotation or as a precise reference to the moment; explain what it does, moving from what the reader notices to why the writer would want that; develop the explanation by connecting it to something else in the text, such as an earlier contrast or the text's shape; and close the paragraph by returning to the question in a judgement. One device explored in five sentences will always outscore three devices named in one.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Read the AO2 level descriptors and ask what verb separates the bottom band from the top.","Consider what 'discriminating selection' implies about the number of devices a paragraph should carry.","Set out the fix as an ordered method, one move per sentence, ending with the question."],"codes":["Assessment overview — Section A (Component 2)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does Component 2, Section A carry, and how do they split between AO1 and AO2?","a":"30 marks: AO1 12 and AO2 18."},{"q":"Which two Part 2 text types must appear in a Component 3 Assignment A set of three texts?","a":"At least one poetry text and at least one prose text."},{"q":"Which two violent verbs does the specification cite from “Out, Out–“?","a":"'snarled' and 'rattled'."},{"q":"In Maupassant's The Necklace, what was the borrowed necklace actually worth?","a":"At most five hundred francs — it was paste, not diamond."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Component 2, Section A — the task","back":"One essay question on a poetry or prose text from Part 2 of the anthology, which is printed in the paper. 30 marks, 45 minutes advised.","code":"Assessment overview — Section A (Component 2)","open":false},{"front":"The AO split in the Section A essay","back":"AO1 12 marks, AO2 18 marks. Two thirds of the essay is analysis of how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to achieve effects.","code":"Assessment overview — Section A (Component 2)","open":false},{"front":"Textual reference (specification definition)","back":"Any reference to supporting evidence from the text — direct quotations, or references to specific moments or events within the text.","code":"Assessment overview — Section A (Component 2)","open":false},{"front":"Assignment A, Component 3 — the requirements","back":"Three Part 2 texts, at least one poetry and one prose; a chosen focus such as setting, character, event or theme; about 1200 words; 30 marks (AO1 12, AO2 18).","code":"Assignment A: Poetry and Prose Texts (Component 3)","open":false},{"front":"Disabled — the poem's organising structure","back":"Alternation between a cold, grey present in the wheeled chair at dusk and a lit, admired past of dancing, girls and the football match before he enlisted.","code":"Disabled","open":false},{"front":"Disabled — the recruiting officers","back":"They smiled as they wrote down his lie about being nineteen: the adults are made complicit in a boy's enlistment.","code":"Disabled","open":false},{"front":"“Out, Out–“ — the title's source and what it signals","back":"Macbeth's cry over a snuffed candle, so the poem frames a boy's death as a life put out before it has begun.","code":"“Out, Out–“","open":false},{"front":"An Unknown Girl — form on the page","back":"One long, narrow column of short lines with no stanzas, mirroring the thin henna line and refusing the settled shape the speaker's identity lacks.","code":"An Unknown Girl","open":false},{"front":"The Bright Lights of Sarajevo — the form that carries the contrast","back":"Rhyming couplets, run across by enjambment: the neat closure of the rhyme sounds orderly while the content is lethal.","code":"The Bright Lights of Sarajevo","open":false},{"front":"Still I Rise — the structural feature the specification cites","back":"A clear pattern of repetition at lines 21 to 23, in the specification's own wording: three consecutive lines each conceding what the oppressor may do, each answered by the refrain about rising. The device is anaphora.","code":"Still I Rise","open":false},{"front":"The Story of an Hour — the detail planted in the first sentence","back":"Louise Mallard's heart trouble, which makes every later scene provisional and prepares the doctors' misdiagnosis at the close.","code":"The Story of an Hour","open":false},{"front":"The Necklace — the figures that carry the irony","back":"The replacement necklace cost thirty-six thousand francs and took ten years of poverty to repay; the borrowed original was paste, worth at most five hundred.","code":"The Necklace","open":false},{"front":"Significant Cigarettes — the device in the title","back":"Lev rations and counts his cigarettes on the coach west, so each one marks a stage, a decision or a memory and segments the journey.","code":"Significant Cigarettes (from The Road Home)","open":false},{"front":"Whistle and I'll Come to You — how Hill builds the fear","back":"Aural horror: sounds whose cause is never shown; long clause-heavy sentences stretching the wait, broken by short ones at the shocks; the dog reacting before the narrator, which proves the disturbance is real.","code":"Whistle and I’ll Come to You (from The Woman in Black)","open":false},{"front":"Name one method Munro uses in Night to build unease","back":"A doubled voice, the adult narrator withholding what the girl feared; long circling sentences that delay naming the thought; the calm father who dissolves it.","code":"Night","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5 for AO2 (15–18), Component 2 Section A, 30 marks","items":["The question: Explore how Hill creates a sense of threat in Whistle and I'll Come to You. You should support your answer with close reference to the text, including brief quotations. (30 marks)","The paragraph: Hill's threat is manufactured out of sound, and the setting is arranged first so that hearing is the only sense left working. The house stands beyond a tidal causeway with marsh and estuary water on every side, and the sea mist takes sight away, so before anything supernatural occurs the narrator has already been reduced to listening. What he then hears is never shown to him, and the withholding is the technique rather than a delay before one: an explained noise is finished with, while an unexplained one keeps working on the reader for as long as the passage lasts. The pacing is managed through syntax. Hill accumulates long, clause-heavy sentences that stretch the waiting until the reader is reading faster than the events allow, and then drops into short sentences at the moment of shock, so the rhythm of the prose, not the incident, sets the reader's pulse. The dog is used as proof rather than company: because she stiffens and reacts before the narrator does, the reader cannot file the disturbance under nerves, and when she is drawn out into the dark and he must wade after her into the marsh, the danger becomes physical as well as spectral, which renews a fear that had been purely atmospheric. Over all of it sits the retrospective first-person voice, an older Kipps who keeps conceding that he did not yet understand what he was hearing, so every ordinary sound is provisional and the reader is never allowed to relax into an explanation. The threat, then, is presented as something the narrative refuses to let anyone see, and that refusal is exactly why it persists after the passage ends.","Why this is Level 5: the selection of devices is subtle and discriminating — syntax and narrative voice rather than obvious adjectives — and the analysis of effects is perceptive and sustained, with each device explained in terms of what it does to the reader.","It also earns AO1 alongside: the reading that the fear comes from withholding is an interpretation of the writer's method, not a retelling, and it is supported by precise reference to specific moments.","Textual support is handled by reference to exact moments rather than by long quotation, which the specification explicitly accepts as a textual reference.","Every sentence after the first is doing analytical work; there is no plot summary anywhere in the paragraph."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 for AO2 (7–10), the same question","items":["The question: Explore how Hill creates a sense of threat in Whistle and I'll Come to You. (30 marks)","The paragraph: Hill creates a sense of threat by using a scary setting. The house is out on its own across a causeway and there is marsh all around it, which makes the reader feel that the narrator is trapped and that nobody could help him if anything happened. She also uses sound, because the narrator hears noises in the dark and does not know what they are, and this makes the reader nervous too. Another technique is the dog, which reacts to something before the narrator does, so we know that there really is something there. Hill also uses short sentences to build tension at the scary moments. All of this makes the reader feel frightened and want to read on.","Why this is only Level 3: the devices are correctly identified and the explanations of effect are clear but general — 'makes the reader nervous', 'want to read on' — rather than explored, and there is no analysis of why an unexplained sound works differently from an explained one.","The selection is undiscriminating: four devices are named in seven sentences, so nothing is developed, and the top band's requirement for subtle and discriminating selection is not met.","There is no textual reference precise enough to count: 'noises in the dark' locates nothing in particular, where the Level 5 paragraph points to specific moments.","The sentence about short sentences asserts an effect without demonstrating it; the stronger version shows the contrast with the long sentences that precede them.","To raise it: delete two of the four devices, keep sound and syntax, and spend the freed space explaining what withholding the cause of a sound does to a reader."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 4 for AO1 (10–12), Component 2 Section A","items":["The question: Explore how Chopin presents Louise Mallard's feelings in The Story of an Hour. (30 marks; this paragraph is written for the AO1 marks)","The paragraph: What Louise experiences in the armchair is not the end of grief but the arrival of a possibility she has never been permitted to want, and Chopin is careful to show her resisting it before she accepts it. She has already wept once, immediately and in her sister's arms, which prevents the reader from concluding that she is cold or that the marriage was cruel; and the story goes out of its way to record that she knows she will weep again at the funeral, so the feeling is not relief at a death. What she is released from is subtler and more damning: the pressing of another will on her own, which Chopin presents as something that happens in kind marriages as well as unkind ones. This is why the feeling comes to her as sensation first — the new spring life in the treetops, the patches of blue sky, the sounds from the street below — and can only be named afterwards, in a whisper, because she has no vocabulary in which to have wanted it. Louise's feelings, then, are presented as self-recognition rather than as joy, and that is exactly the reading the doctors at the end are incapable of.","Why this is Level 4 (top band) for AO1: the interpretation is perceptive and the understanding of the ideas and perspectives is convincing; the selection is detailed and persuasive, and the supporting references are used discriminatingly.","It answers the question in its first clause and keeps answering it, rather than arriving at an interpretation only in the final sentence.","It uses references to specific moments rather than extended quotation, which the specification defines as legitimate textual reference.","It anticipates and closes off the weak reading (that she hated her husband) instead of leaving the examiner to wonder whether the student has noticed it."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 2 for AO1 (4–6), the same question","items":["The question: Explore how Chopin presents Louise Mallard's feelings in The Story of an Hour. (30 marks)","The paragraph: Louise Mallard feels sad at first because her husband has died in a train accident and her sister Josephine has to tell her gently because she has a heart problem. She cries and then goes upstairs to her room on her own. When she is sitting in the chair she looks out of the window and sees the trees and the sky and she starts to feel better. She says that she is free now. Then her husband comes in at the door because he was not in the accident after all, and she dies. The doctors say she died of joy.","Why this is only Level 2: the response shows some understanding but works by retelling the plot in order, so the selection is basic and the interpretation barely present.","'She starts to feel better' is the point at which the answer needed to interpret; the whole of the essay's argument lives in what she is feeling better about, and the paragraph does not ask.","The details chosen — the sister, the train, the door — are the events, not the evidence; nothing here supports an idea, because no idea has been proposed.","The last sentence reports the doctors' verdict without noticing that it is wrong, which is the single most rewarded interpretation in the whole story.","To raise it: begin with a claim about what she feels rather than what happens, then use the window and the delayed naming of the feeling as evidence for that claim.","Note also that the AO1 levels reward interpretation, not narration: a fluent retelling stays in the bottom half however well written it is."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the ten Part 2 anthology texts","items":["Disabled — Wilfred Owen. Poetry. Third-person portrait of a young man maimed in the war — legless, and with the sleeves sewn short at the elbow, so his arms are gone too — sitting in a wheeled chair in a park at dusk in a suit of grey the poem calls 'ghastly' and describes as altered, waiting to be taken indoors.","Disabled — structure: repeated movement between the grey present and the lit, admired past (evenings of dancing, girls' glances, the football match after which, half-drunk and hoping to please a girl, he enlisted).","Disabled — key moment: the recruiting officers smile as they write down his lie about his age, which makes the adults complicit; a further key contrast is the crowd that cheered him home less than it had cheered a goal.","Disabled — method to cite: colour imagery of drained face against poured-away blood; passive constructions that leave things being done to him; the shrunken repeated question at the close.","“Out, Out–“ — Robert Frost. Poetry. A boy sawing wood on a Vermont farmstead at sunset, with mountain ranges visible, cuts his hand when his sister calls supper; he dies under ether while the doctor works.","“Out, Out–“ — title from Macbeth's cry over a snuffed candle; the poem runs as a single unbroken verse paragraph of blank verse, with no stanza break to mark the accident or the death.","“Out, Out–“ — the specification's own example of citing a feature of language: the violent verbs 'snarled' and 'rattled', used of the personified buzz-saw, to convey shock.","“Out, Out–“ — key moments: the boy's own appeal not to lose the hand, which shows he understands before the adults do; the watchers turning back to their affairs because they are not the ones who are dead.","An Unknown Girl — Moniza Alvi. Poetry. The speaker sits in an evening bazaar in India having henna applied to her hand by a girl she does not know.","An Unknown Girl — form: one long, narrow column of short lines with no stanza divisions, slowing the reading to the pace of the design and refusing a settled shape.","An Unknown Girl — methods: the two refrains, on the evening bazaar and on the hennaing of the hand, which turn a transaction into a ritual; the neon and commercial Western surface of the bazaar set against traditional patterning.","An Unknown Girl — the close: the henna pattern will fade as the skin renews, and the speaker imagines reaching across a whole country towards the girl and the place, so the poem ends in longing rather than belonging.","The Bright Lights of Sarajevo — Tony Harrison. Poetry, written as reportage during the siege of Sarajevo: queues for bread rations and for refills of gas canisters, water carried up flights of stairs, shell-damaged pavements, the danger of being in the open.","The Bright Lights of Sarajevo — form: rhyming couplets run across by enjambment, so the orderly click of rhyme is set against lethal content.","The Bright Lights of Sarajevo — key contrast: young people come out at dusk to walk and to meet, so ordinary courtship is placed inside a siege without the poet having to call it courage.","The Bright Lights of Sarajevo — the close: with no electricity, the light is the moon and the small flames people carry, and a lighter's flare shows a young woman's face — the title's bright lights reduced to something human and momentary.","Still I Rise — Maya Angelou. Poetry. The speaker addresses an unnamed oppressor directly as 'you', so the reader overhears a confrontation.","Still I Rise — form: mostly quatrains rhyming on alternate lines, breaking at the end into shorter lines in which the assertion about rising is repeated to close the poem.","Still I Rise — the specification's own example of citing a structural feature: a clear pattern of repetition at lines 21 to 23, three consecutive lines each conceding an act of violence the addressee may perform.","Still I Rise — methods: rhetorical questions taunting the addressee; similes of wealth, each introduced by 'like' — oil wells, gold mines, diamonds — that reclaim value for a body treated as worthless; a closing widening to the inherited hope of enslaved ancestors.","The Story of an Hour — Kate Chopin. Prose. Louise Mallard, who has heart trouble, is told by her sister Josephine and her husband's friend Richards that Brently Mallard has been killed in a railroad disaster.","The Story of an Hour — structure: the heart trouble is planted in the first sentence; Louise weeps at once, then goes alone to her room, where the narration moves inside her at the open window.","The Story of an Hour — key moment: spring life in the treetops, patches of blue sky and street sounds arrive as sensation before the feeling is named in a whisper about being free; Chopin records that Louise knows she will weep again at the funeral.","The Story of an Hour — the close: Brently walks in unharmed, Louise dies, and the doctors attribute it to the joy that kills — a misdiagnosis only the reader can correct.","The Necklace — Guy de Maupassant. Prose. Mathilde Loisel, a pretty woman born into a family of clerks, is married to a minor clerk and believes herself made for luxuries she does not have.","The Necklace — plot in order: the invitation to the ministerial ball; the husband surrendering his savings for a dress; the necklace borrowed from Madame Forestier; triumph at the ball; the loss; the replacement; ten years of poverty; the revelation.","The Necklace — the specification's own example of interpretation through a textual reference: Madame Loisel's materialism is shown when she disregards her friend's many elegant jewels and selects the diamond necklace instead.","The Necklace — the figures: the replacement necklace cost thirty-six thousand francs, repaid over ten years of drudgery that coarsen her hands, voice and looks; the borrowed original was paste, worth at most five hundred francs.","Significant Cigarettes (from The Road Home) — Rose Tremain. Prose. Lev, aged forty-two, travels by coach from his Eastern European village, whose sawmill has closed, to England.","Significant Cigarettes — the people: his wife Marina has died; his daughter Maya has been left behind with his mother Ina; beside him on the coach sits Lydia, who is studying English phrases and who offers him food.","Significant Cigarettes — method: third-person narration held tightly inside Lev's consciousness, so the road and the strangers are given only as he registers them, with memory intruding into the dull present of the journey.","Significant Cigarettes — structural device: the rationed and counted cigarettes of the title, each marking a stage, a decision or a memory; the specification offers the extract as a starting point for imaginative writing about the next episode in Lev's story.","Whistle and I'll Come to You (from The Woman in Black) — Susan Hill. Prose. First-person retrospective narration by Arthur Kipps, alone at an isolated house reached across a tidal causeway, with marsh, estuary water and sea mist around it.","Whistle and I'll Come to You — the title alludes to M. R. James's ghost story, signalling the genre before anything happens.","Whistle and I'll Come to You — methods: aural horror in which sounds are heard but their cause never shown; long clause-heavy sentences stretching the wait, broken by short sentences at the shocks; the dog reacting before the narrator, which proves the disturbance is real.","Whistle and I'll Come to You — key moment: the dog is drawn out into the dark and the narrator must wade into the marsh after her, which turns atmospheric fear into physical danger.","Night — Alice Munro. Prose. An adult narrator recalls herself at about fourteen in rural Ontario, operated on for appendicitis, with something else removed that the adults compare in size to a turkey's egg and only half explain to her.","Night — the central fear: in the following winter she cannot sleep, and the thought arrives that she might harm her sleeping younger sister, Catherine; nothing happens, and the horror is entirely interior.","Night — method: the doubled voice of the child living it and the adult telling it, with long circling sentences that withhold the thought before naming it, so information is released rather than narrated.","Night — the resolution: she goes outside in the night and finds her father awake; he answers her confession calmly, treating such thoughts as ordinary, and the terror dissolves without any dramatic event."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: how Part 2 is assessed in Component 2 and Component 3","items":["Component 2, Section A: one essay question on a poetry or prose text from Part 2, which is made available in the examination. Total 30 marks for the section, 45 minutes advised.","The tariff: AO1 12 marks, AO2 18 marks — two thirds of the essay is analysis of linguistic and structural devices and their effects.","AO1 is 'read and understand a variety of texts, selecting and interpreting information, ideas and perspectives'; it is marked in four levels out of 12 (1–3, 4–6, 7–9, 10–12).","AO2 is 'understand and analyse how writers use linguistic and structural devices to achieve their effects'; it is marked in five levels out of 18 (1–3, 4–6, 7–10, 11–14, 15–18).","Marking is best fit: an answer may show characteristics of more than one level and markers use professional judgement, so a script with one strong section among weaker work can land at the top of the lower level.","A 'textual reference' is any reference to supporting evidence from the text — a direct quotation or a reference to a specific moment or event. Long quotation is not required and wins nothing on its own.","An 'example' is the citing of a specific feature of language and/or structure; naming the feature is only the start of an AO2 point, because the levels pay for the explanation of its effect.","Students take Component 1 plus either Component 2 (examination) or Component 3 (non-examined assessment); this course models the examined route.","Component 3 Assignment A: approximately 1200 words on three Part 2 texts, at least one poetry and one prose, on a chosen focus such as setting, characters, events or themes. 30 marks (AO1 12, AO2 18).","Component 3 Assignment A task wording: discuss how the writers use language and structure to present the chosen focus, discussing the writers' ideas and perspectives, how they achieve their effects, and including textual references.","Component 3 Assignment B: imaginative writing of approximately 700 words, 30 marks (AO4 18, AO5 12); the purpose should be to narrate, describe or entertain, and the word count is advisory with no penalty for exceeding it.","Component 3 is internally set and assessed and externally moderated by Pearson; the two marks out of 30 are combined into one mark out of 60, and centres with more than one teacher must standardise internally."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan — Component 2, Section A (30 marks): Explore how Owen presents the effects of war on the young man in Disabled","items":["The question decoded: 'explore how' is an AO2 instruction, so the essay is about method. 'Presents' means the poem's choices, not the man's biography. 'The effects of war' is plural and must be taken as physical, social and temporal, not just the lost legs.","Mark allocation to plan against: 18 marks AO2 and 12 marks AO1, so every paragraph is led by a named device and the interpretation of ideas and perspectives is argued through it.","Opening sentence: answer the question at once — Owen presents the effects of war as the emptying of a life that has been reduced from choosing to being chosen for, and the poem's alternating structure is what measures the loss.","Paragraph 1 — structure: the alternation between grey present and lit past. Show that each return to the past is a measurement of the present, so the reader experiences the loss comparatively rather than being told of it. Judgement: the poem is arranged as a subtraction.","Paragraph 2 — grammar and voice: the passive constructions, the waiting to be taken indoors and the diminished repeated question that closes the poem. Argue that agency is removed at the level of the sentence and that the verse contracts as his world has, so the form runs out at the same moment he does. Judgement: the worst effect is not the injury but the loss of choice.","Paragraph 3 — imagery: colour drained from the face set against the blood poured away, and the warm, lit imagery attached to the remembered evenings. Argue that the body and the past are lit and coloured while the present is grey, so the imagery keeps the two times separable at a glance.","Paragraph 4 — the enlistment and the onlookers: the football match, the drink, the wish to please a girl, the officers who smile as they write the lie about his age, and afterwards the women's eyes passing over him to the strong men and the cheering that was smaller than for a goal. Argue that Owen makes the motive trivial deliberately, so that the cost is grotesquely out of proportion and the adults are implicated, and that the effect he most wants felt is being looked past rather than looked at.","The evaluation thread running through: at every stage ask whether the effect is physical or social, and build towards the judgement that the social and temporal effects are presented as the graver ones.","Evidence policy: brief, exact quotation where you are certain of the words, otherwise precise reference to the moment — both count as textual reference under the specification.","What the conclusion must do: return to the question's own words and give the judgement, not a summary — that Owen presents war's effect as the removal of a future, which is why the poem can only end by repeating a question that will not be answered.","What the conclusion must avoid: new evidence, a list of devices used, and the biographical statement that Owen fought and died in the war, which earns nothing in a paper with no AO3 marks.","Timing on the day within the 45 advised minutes: 5 annotating the printed poem and planning, 3 on the opening sentence and its claim, 8 on each of the four body paragraphs (32 in all), and 5 on a conclusion that is written rather than dashed off — 5 + 3 + 32 + 5 = 45.","Adapting the plan for a prose text: the same shape works, but replace the stanza-to-stanza alternation with the ordering of events, the point of view and the pacing."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"How to use evidence in this room — and the copyright rule that shapes it","items":["Most Part 2 texts are still in copyright, so quote briefly: a phrase, never a whole line of a poem or consecutive sentences of prose. The specification accepts a reference to a specific moment as full textual reference, so nothing is lost.","A safe and high-scoring formula: name the device, locate the moment exactly ('at the point where the sister calls supper'), give at most a few words in quotation marks, then spend the rest of the sentence on the effect.","If you cannot remember the exact words, do not approximate them inside quotation marks. A misquotation is worse than a reference, because it suggests the text in front of you was not used.","In the examination the text is printed, so precision is expected; in Component 3 coursework you have the text open, so there is no excuse for a vague reference anywhere in the assignment.","Quantity of evidence is not rewarded: the top AO2 level asks for subtle and discriminating selection, which means fewer, better-chosen examples explored further.","Keep context out unless it explains a method. There are no AO3 marks in Component 2 Section A, so a paragraph on the poet's biography is a paragraph not spent on language or structure."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:16:51Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Truth to the ten texts is sound throughout - every checkable name, figure, event and structural claim holds, quotation is deliberately minimal and the three phrases actually quoted ('snarled', 'rattled', 'You may') are the specification's own, so there is no misquotation anywhere; fix the tariffs (the practice questions should be set at 30 marks like the model paragraphs), the simile/metaphor slip and the 'anaphora' attribution before publishing."}}},"imaginative-writing":{"id":"EDX-4EA1","topic":"imaginative-writing","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Section B of Component 2 is a single 30-mark imaginative writing task chosen from three prompts, and it is a fifth of the whole International GCSE. Nobody marks your idea: 18 marks go to AO4 for communicating effectively and imaginatively and for form, tone and register held to purpose and reader, and 12 go to AO5 for vocabulary, sentence structures, paragraphing, spelling, grammar and punctuation. That means a quiet piece about one afternoon, shaped and proofread, beats an ambitious plot told flatly. This room teaches you to choose the prompt you can control, plan a shape in five minutes, write for the reader rather than for the teacher, and spend the last three minutes proofreading, so that in 45 minutes you produce something that reads as if it were meant to be read.","idea":[{"h":"What Section B is actually for","t":"The specification sets Section B as 'Section B: Imaginative Writing - explore and develop imaginative writing skills'. It says students 'should use what they have learned about the writer's craft in their reading of fiction to inspire and influence their own imaginative writing' and 'should develop a range of creative writing techniques, planning and proofreading skills'. Read that as three demands, not one. Technique means the moves you have seen novelists make - a delayed reveal, a shift of time, an object that carries the feeling. Planning means the shape is decided before the first sentence. Proofreading means the last minutes are spent as a reader, not a writer. A top-level answer shows all three at once: it is recognisably shaped, its effects look chosen rather than lucky, and it is clean enough that nothing in the writing distracts the examiner from the writing.","code":"Section B: Imaginative Writing"},{"h":"The reading that feeds the writing","t":"The content for this section is reading. The Pearson Edexcel International GCSE English Anthology 'is a resource that supports teachers by providing examples of prose fiction', and students 'should read a variety of additional prose fiction from a range of genres and cultures'. They should be able to read 'extended texts' that make 'significant demands' in 'content, structure and the quality of language' and should 'develop the skills of inference and analysis'. The point is that analysis is a writer's tool here, not just a reader's: when you notice that a story withholds a name for two pages, you have found a device you can use. A top-level answer borrows structures and effects, never sentences. Bring three or four moves you have stolen from your reading - an opening in the middle of the action, a repeated image, a final line that reframes everything - and choose between them at the planning stage.","code":"Content — reading fiction to inform writing"},{"h":"The shape of the examined task","t":"The assessment overview is exact: 'Students are advised to allocate 45 minutes to Section B. There will be one 30-mark imaginative writing task, based on one of three given prompts. Students will answer one question in this section. Total of 30 marks for this section.' The raw marks split AO4 18 and AO5 12. Three prompts, one answer: the choice is part of the assessment, so spend one minute on it and choose the prompt you can furnish with real detail, not the one that sounds most dramatic. A workable division of the 45 minutes is five minutes planning, about 35 writing and the last three to five reading back. There is no set word count for the examined task, so length is whatever a controlled piece needs - roughly 500 to 700 words for most students. A top-level answer treats the prompt as a constraint to be honoured exactly: a prompt that must be the first line or the last line is worth no marks if it is paraphrased.","code":"Assessment overview — Section B (Component 2)"},{"h":"AO4: the 18 marks for imaginative communication","t":"AO4 asks you to 'communicate effectively and imaginatively, adapting form, tone and register of writing for specific purposes and audiences', out of 18 marks in five levels: 1–3, 4–7, 8–11, 12–15 and 16–18. Level 1 communicates 'at a basic level, and limited in clarity' with 'little awareness of form, tone and register'. Level 3 'communicates clearly' with 'appropriate use of form, tone and register'. Level 5 is communication that 'is perceptive and subtle', 'sharply focused on purpose and the expectations/requirements of the intended reader', with 'sophisticated use of form, tone and register'. The word to notice is reader. Clear writing that tells the reader everything is Level 3; writing that decides what to withhold, what to imply and what to let an object say is Level 5. A top-level answer keeps one voice from first line to last, and every detail in it is doing a job - setting a mood, revealing a character, or preparing the ending.","code":"AO4 (Imaginative Writing)"},{"h":"AO5: the 12 marks for the written language itself","t":"AO5 asks you to 'write clearly, using a range of vocabulary and sentence structures, with appropriate paragraphing and accurate spelling, grammar and punctuation', out of 12 marks in five levels: 1–2, 3–4, 5–7, 8–10 and 11–12. The bottom level has 'basic vocabulary, often misspelt' and punctuation used 'with basic control'. Level 5 'manipulates complex ideas, utilising a range of structural and grammatical features to support coherence and cohesion', 'uses extensive vocabulary strategically' and 'punctuates writing with accuracy to aid emphasis and precision'. Strategically and precision are the load-bearing words: a long word used wrongly costs more than a plain word used well, and a dash earns nothing unless it does something a comma could not. These 12 marks are the most reliably winnable in the paper, because paragraphing, spelling and punctuation are under your control even on a bad day. A top-level answer varies sentence length on purpose - a short one lands a moment - and has been read back for the errors you personally make.","code":"AO5 (Imaginative Writing)"},{"h":"The coursework route: Assignment B","t":"If a centre takes Component 3 instead of Component 2, the imaginative writing is Assignment B: 'A piece of imaginative writing based on a selected topic', responding to 'a teacher-devised non-examined assignment' addressing AO4 and AO5, with 'the purpose should be to narrate, describe or entertain' and a 'Total of 30 marks for this assignment'. The topic 'could be suggested by a piece in the anthology' or be 'an unrelated topic relating to a set of images', and 'students may write about real or imagined experiences'. The response is approximately 700 words, and that count 'is advisory only and there is no penalty for exceeding this recommendation'. The assessment objectives and the 18/12 split are identical to the examined task, so everything you practise transfers; what changes is time, which means drafting and real proofreading are expected rather than hoped for. A top-level answer on either route can be measured against the same five AO4 and AO5 levels, so judge your practice pieces by those descriptors and not by whether you enjoyed writing them.","code":"Assignment B: Imaginative Writing (Component 3)"}],"examples":[{"title":"Choosing between the three prompts and planning in five minutes","setup":"Component 2, Section B, Question 2. 30 marks (AO4 18, AO5 12), 45 minutes advised. Choose one: (a) 'The visitor'; (b) 'A frightening experience'; (c) 'During the night the wind rose' - write an imaginative and descriptive piece that begins with those words. You have five minutes before you start writing. Decide which prompt to take and produce a plan.","steps":["Test each prompt against detail you already own: (b) invites a generic horror scene, (c) fixes the first six words and pushes towards weather description, (a) lets me use a real kitchen, a real smell and a real radio, so I take (a).","Fix the situation in one sentence I could say aloud: a man returns to his mother's house after eleven years away and finds she has stepped out for twenty minutes.","Decide the shape: open with him already in the doorway, hold back who he is to her until the third paragraph, and end on the sound of her key rather than on their meeting.","List four concrete things that will carry the feeling: lemons cut that morning, a radio with its dial taped, a chair pulled out, a small hard gift in his coat pocket.","Write the last line now, so everything before it can aim at it: 'Outside, a key began its long search for the lock.'"],"cues":["Which of the three prompts could you furnish with detail you actually remember, and which would force you to invent generic material?","Can you say the whole situation in one sentence without using the word 'and' twice?","What would be lost if the two of them actually met on the page?","Which objects in that kitchen could carry the feeling without anyone naming it?","Why is it useful to know the final sentence before you write the first?"]},{"title":"Writing an opening that fixes tone and register (AO4)","setup":"You are writing to the plan above for 'The visitor'. Your first draft opening reads: 'Malik was very nervous. He had not been home for eleven years and he did not know what his mother would say to him when she got back.' Rewrite the opening so that it is sharply focused on the reader, using form, tone and register rather than statement.","steps":["Cut the two labels the draft gives the reader - 'very nervous' and 'did not know what his mother would say' - because a named feeling ends the reader's work.","Put the reader in the room first: 'The kitchen still smelled of the lemons she had cut at six that morning, though the knife had been washed and the window left open to the cold.'","Let an action carry the nerves: 'Malik stood in the doorway with his coat still on and counted the things that had not moved.'","Withhold the relationship and the eleven years for now, and name only the objects: 'the blue jug, the radio with its dial taped at ninety-two, the chair pulled out as if someone had just stood up from it.'","Close the paragraph with the fact the draft opened with, now earned: 'He had rehearsed this arrival for eleven years, and had never once rehearsed the silence.'"],"cues":["Which two phrases in the draft are doing the reader's thinking for them?","What could the room tell us before any person is described?","What small action would a nervous man perform in a doorway?","Why hold back the word 'mother' for another paragraph?","Where should the eleven years appear now that the silence has been shown?"]},{"title":"Turning flat accurate prose into Level 5 AO5 writing","setup":"A paragraph from the middle of a practice piece: 'He walked into the living room. The curtains were closed. There was dust on the television. He sat down on the sofa and he waited for a long time. He thought about all the years he had been away and he felt guilty about it.' The spelling and punctuation are accurate. Rewrite it so that vocabulary and sentence structures are used strategically, for AO5.","steps":["Count the pattern first: five sentences, mostly subject-verb, two joined with 'and', none shorter than four words and none longer than sixteen - so the flatness is structural, not a spelling problem.","Open with a subordinate clause so the rhythm changes: 'Because the curtains had not been opened, the living room held the grey half-light of a room kept for visitors.'","Make the dust do the work of the missing years: 'Dust had settled on the television in a soft even coat, the way snow settles on something nobody intends to move.'","Land the guilt on a short sentence instead of naming it: 'He sat down. He waited. Eleven years is a long time to sit in a room you grew up in.'","Read the rewrite aloud and check the variety is real: one complex opening, one extended simile, two very short sentences (three words, then two), one general statement - four different shapes in five sentences."],"cues":["Before changing any word, what is the pattern shared by all five sentences?","How could the first sentence begin with something other than 'He' or 'The'?","What everyday image would suggest time passing without saying 'years'?","Which sentence in the rewrite should be the shortest, and why that one?","How can you prove to yourself that the variety is deliberate rather than accidental?"]},{"title":"A piece that must end with given words","setup":"Component 2, Section B prompt: 'The moment had passed' - write an imaginative piece ending with these words. 30 marks (AO4 18, AO5 12). You have chosen to write about a girl who does not tell her friend the truth on the last evening of a summer holiday. Build the piece so that the given words are the final four and are earned.","steps":["Treat the words as a target: the whole piece must build to a single opportunity that opens and then closes, so I choose one evening, one bench and one thing unsaid.","Decide what the moment is: Nadia means to tell Jess that she is not coming back to school in September, and has decided to say it when the ferry lights appear.","Build the pressure through three short scenes - the walk down, the wait on the bench, the ferry passing - so the reader is watching for the sentence too.","Put the near-miss on the page: 'She got as far as Jess's name. Jess turned, still laughing at something from an hour before, and the laugh was so easy that Nadia let it stand.'","Write the final two sentences so the given words land as a judgement, not a summary: 'The ferry drew its light across the water and took it away again. The moment had passed.'"],"cues":["If the piece must end on those four words, what must the reader be waiting for by the middle?","What exactly is the thing that could be said, and what would saying it cost?","How many scenes can you afford in about 600 words?","How do you show someone almost speaking without letting them speak?","What must the sentence before the given words do, so that the words are not just tacked on?"]},{"title":"The last three minutes: proofreading for AO5","setup":"Three minutes are left. This is your final paragraph exactly as written: 'The house was quiet, he could hear the clock in the hall. Its hands moved slowly, their was no other sound. He should of gone to her years ago he thought, and he new it now. Outside a key began its long search for the lock.' Correct it without rewriting the content.","steps":["Repair the comma splice in the first sentence: 'The house was quiet. He could hear the clock in the hall.'","Fix the homophone 'their' for 'there': 'Its hands moved slowly; there was no other sound.' - and the semicolon now links two connected statements instead of a second splice.","Correct 'should of' to 'should have', which is the single most common error in this paper.","Punctuate the reported thought and correct 'new' to 'knew': 'He should have gone to her years ago, he thought, and he knew it now.'","Add the comma the last sentence needs after its opening adverbial: 'Outside, a key began its long search for the lock.'"],"cues":["Which sentence joins two complete statements with only a comma?","Which word in the second sentence is the wrong homophone?","What is the correct form of 'should of', and why does it appear so often?","How should a reported thought inside a sentence be punctuated?","What does the final sentence need after its first word?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many marks does Section B carry, and how are they divided between the assessment objectives?","a":"30 marks in total: AO4 18 marks and AO5 12 marks."},{"q":"You are offered three prompts. How many do you answer, and how long are you advised to spend on the section?","a":"One task only, and 45 minutes are advised for Section B."},{"q":"Name the three purposes the specification sets for imaginative writing.","a":"To narrate, to describe or to entertain."},{"q":"Give two things AO5 rewards that have nothing to do with your idea.","a":"Any two of: range of vocabulary, range of sentence structures, appropriate paragraphing, accurate spelling, accurate grammar, accurate punctuation."},{"q":"A prompt says the piece must begin with given words. What is the safest thing to do with them?","a":"Use them exactly and as the opening words, without paraphrase, then build the piece from them."}]},"room":{"facts":["Component 2, Section B, Question 2 is one imaginative writing task chosen from three given prompts, worth 30 marks, with 45 minutes advised.","The 30 marks split AO4 18 and AO5 12, so three fifths of the section (18 of 30) is imaginative communication and form, tone and register, and two fifths (12 of 30) is vocabulary, sentence structures, paragraphing, spelling, grammar and punctuation.","AO4 is marked in five levels out of 18: Level 1 communicates at a basic level with little awareness of form, tone and register; Level 3 communicates clearly with appropriate use of form, tone and register; Level 5 is perceptive and subtle, sharply focused on purpose and the reader, with sophisticated use of form, tone and register. The bands are Level 1 1–3 marks, Level 2 4–7, Level 3 8–11, Level 4 12–15 and Level 5 16–18.","AO5 is marked in five levels out of 12, from basic and often misspelt vocabulary with basic control of punctuation, up to Level 5, which manipulates complex ideas with structural and grammatical features that support coherence and cohesion, uses extensive vocabulary strategically and punctuates with accuracy to aid emphasis and precision. The bands are Level 1 1–2 marks, Level 2 3–4, Level 3 5–7, Level 4 8–10 and Level 5 11–12.","The purpose of imaginative writing is to narrate, describe or entertain, and students may write about real or imagined experiences.","In the non-examined alternative, Component 3 Assignment B is a teacher-devised imaginative writing task of approximately 700 words, also 30 marks with AO4 18 and AO5 12; the word count is advisory only and there is no penalty for exceeding it.","Marking is by levels of response on a best-fit basis: an answer may show features of more than one level, and the examiner uses professional judgement, so a piece that is mostly Level 3 with some Level 4 features may be placed at a high Level 3."],"questions":[{"q":"State the tariff of Component 2, Section B: the total marks, the marks for AO4 and the marks for AO5.","a":"30 marks in total; AO4 18 and AO5 12.","sol":"Section B carries a total of 30 marks for one imaginative writing task. Of these, 18 are awarded for AO4 - communicating effectively and imaginatively, adapting form, tone and register for specific purposes and audiences - and 12 for AO5 - writing clearly with a range of vocabulary and sentence structures, appropriate paragraphing and accurate spelling, grammar and punctuation. Because AO4 is worth half as much again as AO5, the shaping and voice of the piece matter more than technical accuracy alone, but the 12 AO5 marks are the most reliably winnable in the section.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["This is the section overview rather than anything about your writing.","Two assessment objectives are assessed here, one for the writing itself and one for the written language.","Give the total first, then the two parts that make it up."],"codes":["Assessment overview — Section B (Component 2)","AO4 (Imaginative Writing)","AO5 (Imaginative Writing)"]},{"q":"How many prompts are offered in Section B, how many tasks do you answer, and how long are you advised to spend on the section?","a":"Three prompts are given, one task is answered, and 45 minutes are advised.","sol":"There is one 30-mark imaginative writing task based on one of three given prompts, and students answer one question in this section. Students are advised to allocate 45 minutes to Section B. In practice that divides well into about five minutes choosing a prompt and planning a shape, about thirty-five minutes writing, and the last three to five minutes reading the piece back for punctuation, homophones and sentence boundaries.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Three numbers are wanted: prompts offered, tasks answered, minutes advised.","The middle number is what trips people up - the choice is not an invitation to write twice.","Give the numbers in the order the question asks for them."],"codes":["Assessment overview — Section B (Component 2)"]},{"q":"Name the three purposes the specification sets for imaginative writing, and state whether the experience written about must be real.","a":"To narrate, describe or entertain; the experience may be real or imagined.","sol":"The purpose of an imaginative writing task should be to narrate, describe or entertain. The specification also states that students may write about real or imagined experiences, so a piece drawn from a real afternoon and a piece entirely invented are equally acceptable. What decides the mark is not which of the two you choose but whether the writing is shaped for a reader and controlled in its language; a real memory is often the better choice simply because it supplies detail you can remember rather than invent.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The three purposes are the verbs the specification uses for this kind of writing.","They are the same three whichever route a centre takes, examined or coursework.","The second part of the question has a two-word answer joined by 'or'."],"codes":["Assignment B: Imaginative Writing (Component 3)","Section B: Imaginative Writing"]},{"q":"List four features rewarded by AO5 in imaginative writing.","a":"Any four of: range of vocabulary; range of sentence structures; appropriate paragraphing; accurate spelling; accurate grammar; accurate punctuation.","sol":"AO5 rewards writing clearly using a range of vocabulary and a range of sentence structures, with appropriate paragraphing and accurate spelling, grammar and punctuation. At the top level these are described as extensive vocabulary used strategically, a range of structural and grammatical features supporting coherence and cohesion, and punctuation used accurately to aid emphasis and precision. Note that each item is about control rather than difficulty: a plain word used exactly and a full stop in the right place both earn here, while a long word used wrongly loses more than it gains.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["This objective is about the written language rather than the story.","Think of the four things a careful reader would notice if they read your piece aloud.","Each item should be a named feature, not a comment on your plot."],"codes":["AO5 (Imaginative Writing)"]},{"q":"What does AO4 require you to adapt, and what must it be adapted to?","a":"Form, tone and register, adapted to specific purposes and audiences.","sol":"AO4 requires students to communicate effectively and imaginatively, adapting form, tone and register of writing for specific purposes and audiences. In imaginative writing the purpose is to narrate, describe or entertain, and the audience is a general reader who has to be interested from the first line. The levels turn on how far that adaptation goes: Level 1 shows little awareness of form, tone and register, Level 3 uses them appropriately, and Level 5 is sharply focused on purpose and the expectations of the intended reader with sophisticated use of form, tone and register.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Three nouns are named in the objective itself, and they come as a set.","The second half of the objective names who and what the writing is for.","Answer as a single sentence containing both halves."],"codes":["AO4 (Imaginative Writing)"]},{"q":"In the Component 3 non-examined route, what length is Assignment B, and what happens if a student writes more than that?","a":"Approximately 700 words; the count is advisory only and there is no penalty for exceeding it.","sol":"Assignment B is a piece of imaginative writing of approximately 700 words, responding to a teacher-devised task whose purpose should be to narrate, describe or entertain. The specification states that the word count is advisory only and that there is no penalty for exceeding the recommendation. That does not make length an advantage: the marks are the same 18 for AO4 and 12 for AO5, and a piece that runs long usually loses control of shape and accuracy, which are exactly what those levels reward.","m":2,"d":1,"hints":["This is the coursework alternative to the examined Component 2 task.","The figure is a round number of words, given as an approximation.","The second half of the answer is about penalties, and the answer is a plain one."],"codes":["Assignment B: Imaginative Writing (Component 3)"]},{"q":"'The visitor'. Write an imaginative piece with this title. (30 marks: AO4 18, AO5 12. 45 minutes advised.)","a":"A controlled narrative or description in which a single visitor arrives; tone and register held to one voice, detail chosen for effect, a deliberate opening, developed middle and shaped ending, varied sentence structures and accurate punctuation.","sol":"The kitchen still smelled of the lemons she had cut at six that morning, though the knife had been washed and put away and the window left open to the cold. Malik stood in the doorway with his coat still on and counted the things that had not moved: the blue jug, the radio with its dial taped at ninety-two, the chair pulled out as if someone had just stood up from it. Under the floor the boiler coughed twice and gave up. He had rehearsed this arrival for eleven years - the speech, the apology, the small hard gift in his pocket - and had never once rehearsed the silence. Because the curtains had not been opened, the living room held the grey half-light of a room kept for visitors, and that was the word that stopped him. Visitor. He had a key in his pocket that no longer fitted anything he could name. In the hall the clock went on with its patient arithmetic. He thought of the letters, eleven Decembers of them, each one shorter than the last, and understood that a person can be forgiven long before they are ready to be. He sat down on the edge of the sofa, the way people sit when they are not staying. Outside, a key began its long search for the lock.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Before writing, decide who is visiting whom, and what has been left unsaid between them.","Build the piece from four or five concrete objects and let them carry the feeling instead of naming it.","Plan the last sentence first, then start the piece as late into the situation as you can."],"codes":["Section B: Imaginative Writing","AO4 (Imaginative Writing)","AO5 (Imaginative Writing)"]},{"q":"'During the night the wind rose'. Write your own imaginative and descriptive piece that begins with those words. (30 marks: AO4 18, AO5 12. 45 minutes advised.)","a":"The piece opens with the given words exactly; description dominant and sensory, one situation held throughout, structure moving from night to a changed morning, sentence lengths varied for effect, accurate punctuation.","sol":"During the night the wind rose. It came off the water first as a low complaint in the gutters, then as something with weight to it, shouldering the back gate until the latch gave and the gate began its slow argument with the fence. Aunt Sheila slept through it, as she slept through everything now. I lay with my eyes open and listened to the house being inventoried: the loose tile above the bathroom, the aerial, the plastic chair that crossed the yard in three separate journeys. By two o'clock the rain had joined in, thrown in handfuls against the glass. I thought of the boats and then made myself stop thinking of the boats. Morning came scrubbed and blameless. The sky was the pale blue of a plate that has been washed too often, and the street was littered with things that belonged to other people - a fence panel, a child's welly, half a trampoline folded like a dropped letter. Sheila stood at the window in her dressing gown and said it was a shame about the gate. The gate was fine. The gate had been taken down to the corner and set against a wall, as neatly as if someone had chosen the spot, and it was only the fence it used to belong to that was gone.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Copy the given opening words exactly and let the sentence that follows do the first piece of description.","Choose one place and one night, and track the storm through what can be heard before what can be seen.","Decide before you write what the morning reveals that the night could not, so the piece has somewhere to arrive."],"codes":["Section B: Imaginative Writing","AO4 (Imaginative Writing)","AO5 (Imaginative Writing)"]},{"q":"'The moment had passed'. Write an imaginative piece ending with these words. (30 marks: AO4 18, AO5 12. 45 minutes advised.)","a":"A piece built towards one opportunity that opens and closes; the given words are the final words, unaltered and earned; tension held through a small number of scenes; varied sentences with a short one used for the near-miss.","sol":"They walked down to the harbour the long way, past the shut arcade and the bins that smelled of the whole summer at once. Nadia had decided on the ferry. When the ferry lights came round the headland, she would say it: that she was not coming back in September, that her father's job had gone, that the flat had already been let to someone else. It was a good plan, and it lasted as far as the bench. Jess talked about the exam timetable and a boy called Owen and whether the chips were better from the van or the shop, and each of these things was a small warm room Nadia could not bring herself to leave. She got as far as Jess's name. Jess turned, still laughing at something from an hour before, and the laugh was so easy, so entirely unprepared for bad news, that Nadia let it stand. Then the lights swung out from behind the headland and the ferry was there, huge and ordinary, dragging its reflection across the black water. Jess stood up to see it better. She said something about how it always looked smaller than you remembered. The ferry drew its light across the water and took it away again. The moment had passed.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Work backwards: something must be nearly said or nearly done, and the reader must know what it is well before the end.","Keep to one evening and no more than three scenes, so the pressure has time to build in a short piece.","Make sure the sentence immediately before the given words gives them something to judge."],"codes":["Section B: Imaginative Writing","AO4 (Imaginative Writing)","AO5 (Imaginative Writing)"]},{"q":"Practice task, not an exam question (the marks shown are a practice tariff) (Section B sets a single 30-mark task). Planning in five minutes. You have chosen the prompt 'A frightening experience'. Produce the plan only, in four parts: the situation in one sentence; the single thread the whole piece will hold; four concrete details that will carry the fear without naming it; and the final sentence, written out in full. Do not write the piece.","a":"All four parts present: a one-sentence situation narrow enough for 45 minutes; a thread stated as an idea rather than an event; four specific physical details rather than adjectives; a final sentence written out that the rest of the piece can aim at.","sol":"Situation in one sentence: I stayed too long photographing the sandbar at Barlow Sands and the tide filled the channel behind me. Thread: that the sea is not malicious but indifferent, and indifference is worse - so nothing in the piece is allowed to be dramatic. Four details that will carry the fear: the exact width of the new channel, about nine metres; the phone going into a pocket; the count of minutes the walk to the sea wall takes; a man on the wall waving both arms whose shouting cannot be heard. The final sentence, written out: 'That was the part nobody warns you about.' Two notes on why this plan is worth the five minutes. It fixes the shape, so the piece opens on the sandbar rather than on breakfast, and it decides in advance that the fear will be produced by measurements and timings rather than by adjectives, which is the difference between Level 3 and Level 5 on AO4. It also leaves the last sentence waiting, so the ending is aimed at from the first line and the piece stops where it means to rather than where the time runs out.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Five minutes buys four decisions and no finished sentences except one.","Take the situation from somewhere you can picture exactly; a plan you cannot see is a plan you will abandon at the third paragraph.","Start with the one-sentence situation, and test it by saying it aloud without using 'and' twice."],"codes":["Section B: Imaginative Writing","AO4 (Imaginative Writing)","Assessment overview — Section B (Component 2)"]},{"q":"'The last time I saw...'. Write an imaginative piece using this as your title, completing it as you wish. (30 marks: AO4 18, AO5 12. 45 minutes advised.)","a":"A first-person retrospective built on one remembered occasion; the title completed and honoured; time handled deliberately (then and now); detail selected for effect; varied sentence structures and accurate punctuation throughout.","sol":"The last time I saw the shop it was still called Konstantin's, although Konstantin had been dead for six years and his daughter had never got round to the sign. I was fifteen and I had been sent for bread. The bell over the door did the same three notes it had always done. Inside, the light was brown and the shelves had begun their long retreat: two kinds of soup where there had been nine, a freezer switched off and filled with boxes, a rack of postcards of a seafront that had been rebuilt twice since the photograph. Rina was behind the counter doing a crossword in pen. She asked after my mother. She told me, as she always did, that I had grown, and then, as she never had, that they were finishing at the end of the month. I remember saying that was a shame, in the voice you use at fifteen for other people's endings. I remember the bread being warm through the paper. I did not go back, and by August the window was whitewashed from the inside, and now there is a phone repair place there with a bright sign and no bell at all. The three notes are the thing I still have. I can hear them exactly, and I could not tell you one word of what Rina and I said after that.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Complete the title with something specific enough to describe closely - a place, a person, an object.","Anchor the piece in one remembered occasion rather than summarising a whole relationship.","Plan how the ending will return to the present, since the title has already promised a last time."],"codes":["Section B: Imaginative Writing","AO4 (Imaginative Writing)","AO5 (Imaginative Writing)"]},{"q":"Practice task, not an exam question (the marks shown are a practice tariff) (Section B sets a single 30-mark task). Using the AO4 and AO5 level descriptors, explain why the following passage cannot reach Level 5, then rewrite it so that it could: 'I was really scared. The room was dark and cold and horrible. I could hear a noise and it was getting louder and louder. I did not know what to do. I was very frightened indeed.'","a":"Feelings are stated not created, register is flat, and the sentences are almost all short simple statements with repeated intensifiers; the rewrite must show varied structures, precise vocabulary and implication rather than statement.","sol":"The passage communicates clearly enough to be understood, which is Level 3 territory at best on AO4, but it shows little adaptation of tone and register for the reader: the fear is announced twice ('really scared', 'very frightened indeed') instead of being produced in the reader, and 'dark and cold and horrible' piles up general adjectives where one exact detail would do more. On AO5 the vocabulary is basic and the sentence structures barely vary - five statements, all five beginning with 'I' or 'The', with intensifiers doing the work that verbs and nouns should do, so there is nothing being manipulated for emphasis or precision. A rewrite: 'The cold in that room was the cold of somewhere that had not been heated since the spring, and it got into my hands first. Somewhere below me, a sound had started - regular, unhurried, the sort of sound a person makes when they are not trying to be quiet because they do not yet know anyone is there. I counted it. Nine, ten, eleven. It was climbing.' Here the temperature is placed rather than labelled, the noise is characterised instead of amplified, one long sentence is followed by a three-word one for emphasis, and the fear is never named, so the reader has to supply it - which is what perceptive and subtle communication means.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Read the passage once for what it tells the reader and once for what it lets the reader work out.","Look at how each sentence begins and how long each one is before you look at any single word.","When your rewrite is drafted, put it through the same two checks you used on the passage before you decide it is finished."],"codes":["AO4 (Imaginative Writing)","AO5 (Imaginative Writing)"]},{"q":"Practice task, not an exam question (the marks shown are a practice tariff) (Section B sets a single 30-mark task). Your reading of prose fiction is supposed to feed your own writing. Take one structural device you have met in a novel or short story - for example a delayed reveal, a cyclical ending or a shift in time - and show how you would use it on the prompt 'My most embarrassing moment', explaining what it would gain.","a":"One named device, borrowed as a structure rather than as content; a concrete plan showing where it operates in the piece; a clear statement of the effect on the reader that a chronological telling would not achieve.","sol":"The device I would borrow is the cyclical ending, where a story returns to its opening image so that the same words mean something different the second time. I have met it in short fiction where a story opens and closes on the same object in the same room. On 'My most embarrassing moment' I would open with a single line of dialogue heard out of context - a teacher saying 'and this is the one we've all been waiting for' - and then go back forty minutes to the corridor, the borrowed jacket, the rehearsed thank-you speech and the certificate with the wrong name on it. The piece would run forward to the moment the line is actually spoken and stop there, with the same ten words as the final sentence. The gain is twofold. For AO4 it lets me control what the reader knows: the opening line is neutral on first reading and unbearable on second, which is the kind of effect that is sharply focused on the reader rather than merely clear. For AO5 it gives the piece a shape that holds it together - the repetition is a cohesive device, not a filler - and it earns a deliberate short paragraph at the end, where the white space does the work that a paragraph of explanation would have done badly.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Choose a device you can actually point to in something you have read, not one you have only heard named.","Borrow the structure and not the content; the situation must be yours.","Say what the reader experiences with the device that they would not experience without it."],"codes":["Content — reading fiction to inform writing","Section B: Imaginative Writing","AO4 (Imaginative Writing)"]},{"q":"Practice task, not an exam question (the marks shown are a practice tariff): Component 3's Assignment B is a single 30-mark assignment, and this exercise is not marked on its grids. Component 3, Assignment B: a piece of imaginative writing of approximately 700 words based on a selected topic, whose purpose should be to narrate, describe or entertain. Devise a task for yourself suggested by a set of images of a flooded town, then write the opening 200 words or so and explain in two sentences how you would develop and proofread the rest.","a":"A self-devised task with a stated purpose; an opening that establishes voice, place and situation; a development plan that keeps to about 700 words; a proofreading plan naming the writer's own recurring errors.","sol":"Task: narrate the day a family returns to a flooded house, from the point of view of the youngest child. Opening: They would not let us in until the man with the clipboard had been round twice. From the top of the road the town looked ordinary, except that every hedge had a brown line on it at the height of my chest, as if somebody had come through in the night with a ruler and a tin of paint. Dad kept saying it was only water. Mum did not say anything at all, which was worse. Our gate was where it had always been, and behind it the garden had been rearranged by somebody with no interest in gardens: the compost bin in the flower bed, a traffic cone under the apple tree, and everywhere a fine grey silt that took my footprints as neatly as snow. The smell reached us before the door did. It was not a dirty smell exactly. It was the smell of a river that had been somewhere it should not have been and had touched everything with its hands. Inside, the hall carpet had lifted and set again in ridges, like a beach at low tide, and my wellingtons made a sound on it that I have never heard anything else make. Development and proofreading: I would give the middle 350 words to three rooms in turn - kitchen, front room, my bedroom - so the piece is structured by movement through the house rather than by chronology, and end on one object that survived, without a stated moral. In proofreading I would read the piece aloud for comma splices and for the past tense slipping into the present, which are the two errors I make most.","m":10,"d":3,"hints":["Set yourself a task narrow enough to be finished in about 700 words, and name its purpose before you write.","Let the images give you specific things to describe, then choose a point of view that makes those things mean something.","Your development plan should say how the piece is organised, not what happens next."],"codes":["Assignment B: Imaginative Writing (Component 3)","AO4 (Imaginative Writing)","AO5 (Imaginative Writing)"]},{"q":"Practice task, not an exam question (the marks shown are a practice tariff) (Section B sets a single 30-mark task). Two candidates are given the same three prompts. One writes 900 words of an ambitious time-travel plot with several characters; the other writes 550 words about a bus journey with her grandmother. Using the AO4 and AO5 levels, explain which is more likely to reach the top levels and why, and state the one change that would most improve the first piece.","a":"Ambition is not assessed; the bus journey is likelier to reach the top because control of form, tone, register, structure and accuracy is sustainable within it; the main fix for the first piece is to cut the scope to one scene rather than to add description.","sol":"Neither length nor ambition appears anywhere in the levels. AO4 rewards communication that is perceptive and subtle and sharply focused on the reader, with sophisticated use of form, tone and register; AO5 rewards vocabulary used strategically, structural and grammatical features supporting coherence and cohesion, and punctuation that aids emphasis and precision. A 900-word time-travel plot with several characters spends most of its words on exposition, which usually forces a flat narrating register and long stretches of summary, and the longer the piece the more likely the sentence structures repeat and the punctuation slips. The 550-word bus journey can hold one voice, one setting and a handful of exactly observed details, and it leaves the writer time to proofread - so it can plausibly be Level 4 or 5 on both objectives. The single most useful change to the first piece is not more description but less story: cut it to one scene at one moment of the time travel, with one character, and let everything the reader needs to know be implied by what that character notices. That change alone converts summary into scene, which is where tone, register and sentence control can actually be demonstrated.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Check whether the level descriptors mention scope, originality or length at all.","Think about what a long plot forces the writer to do with their sentences and their time.","List two or three changes you could name, then test each against the AO4 and AO5 descriptors and keep only the one that moves the most."],"codes":["AO4 (Imaginative Writing)","AO5 (Imaginative Writing)","Assessment overview — Section B (Component 2)"]}],"exit":[{"q":"How many marks does the Section B imaginative writing task carry, and how many of them are AO5?","a":"30 marks in total, of which 12 are AO5."},{"q":"How many prompts are given, and how many do you answer?","a":"Three prompts are given; you answer one."},{"q":"Complete the specification's purposes for imaginative writing: to narrate, to ... or to ...","a":"To describe or to entertain."},{"q":"Which assessment objective covers form, tone and register?","a":"AO4."}]},"cards":[{"front":"AO4 in imaginative writing","back":"Communicate effectively and imaginatively, adapting form, tone and register of writing for specific purposes and audiences. Worth 18 of the 30 marks, marked in five levels: 1–3, 4–7, 8–11, 12–15, 16–18.","code":"AO4 (Imaginative Writing)","open":false},{"front":"AO5 in imaginative writing","back":"Write clearly, using a range of vocabulary and sentence structures, with appropriate paragraphing and accurate spelling, grammar and punctuation. Worth 12 of the 30 marks, marked in five levels: 1–2, 3–4, 5–7, 8–10, 11–12.","code":"AO5 (Imaginative Writing)","open":false},{"front":"Section B tariff, choice and timing","back":"One 30-mark imaginative writing task based on one of three given prompts; one question answered; 45 minutes advised for the section.","code":"Assessment overview — Section B (Component 2)","open":false},{"front":"The purposes of imaginative writing","back":"To narrate, describe or entertain. Students may write about real or imagined experiences.","code":"Assignment B: Imaginative Writing (Component 3)","open":false},{"front":"Component 3, Assignment B","back":"Teacher-devised imaginative writing task of approximately 700 words, 30 marks (AO4 18, AO5 12). The word count is advisory only, with no penalty for exceeding it.","code":"Assignment B: Imaginative Writing (Component 3)","open":false},{"front":"AO4 Level 5 descriptor","back":"Communication is perceptive and subtle, sharply focused on purpose and the expectations or requirements of the intended reader, with sophisticated use of form, tone and register. Level 5 is 16–18 of the 18 AO4 marks.","code":"AO4 (Imaginative Writing)","open":false},{"front":"AO4 Level 3 descriptor","back":"Communicates clearly, with appropriate use of form, tone and register. Clear is not the same as subtle, which is why Level 3 pieces usually name feelings instead of creating them. Level 3 is 8–11 of 18.","code":"AO4 (Imaginative Writing)","open":false},{"front":"AO5 Level 5 descriptor","back":"Manipulates complex ideas, using structural and grammatical features to support coherence and cohesion, uses extensive vocabulary strategically, and punctuates with accuracy to aid emphasis and precision. Level 5 is 11–12 of the 12 AO5 marks.","code":"AO5 (Imaginative Writing)","open":false},{"front":"Best-fit marking","back":"Marks are awarded within a level on a best-fit basis, balancing strengths and weaknesses. Work showing features of two levels is placed by professional judgement, for example at a high Level 3, near the top of AO4's 8–11 band.","code":"Assessment overview — Section B (Component 2)","open":false},{"front":"What the specification says feeds this section","back":"Reading. Students use the writer's craft met in their reading of fiction, read prose fiction from a range of genres and cultures, and develop creative writing techniques, planning and proofreading skills.","code":"Content — reading fiction to inform writing","open":false},{"front":"In medias res","back":"Opening in the middle of the action, with the situation explained afterwards or not at all. Buys immediacy and lets the writer withhold information from the reader deliberately.","code":"Content — reading fiction to inform writing","open":false},{"front":"Cyclical structure","back":"Ending on the image, line or setting the piece began with, so the repetition carries a changed meaning. A cohesive device as well as a shaping one.","code":"Content — reading fiction to inform writing","open":false},{"front":"Semantic field","back":"A group of words in a piece that belong to the same area of meaning, such as cold, rust and silence. Sustained across paragraphs it builds atmosphere without any statement of mood.","code":"Content — reading fiction to inform writing","open":false},{"front":"One way to use sentence structure for effect","back":"A short sentence after long ones to land a moment; a subordinate clause first to delay the subject; a list of three to build pace and then break it.","code":"AO5 (Imaginative Writing)","open":true},{"front":"An example of a proofreading check worth making in the last three minutes","back":"Comma splices joining two statements; should have written as should of; homophones such as their, there and knew, new.","code":"AO5 (Imaginative Writing)","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 5 (AO4 and AO5)","items":["The paragraph: The kitchen still smelled of the lemons she had cut at six that morning, though the knife had been washed and put away and the window left open to the cold. Malik stood in the doorway with his coat still on and counted the things that had not moved: the blue jug, the radio with its dial taped at ninety-two, the chair pulled out as if someone had just stood up from it. Under the floor the boiler coughed twice and gave up. He had rehearsed this arrival for eleven years - the speech, the apology, the small hard gift in his pocket - and had never once rehearsed the silence.","Point of the paragraph: it establishes a returning visitor and eleven years of absence without either fact being announced.","AO4 - purpose and reader: the reader is given objects and left to draw the conclusion; nothing states that Malik is nervous or that he is her son, so the paragraph is focused on what the reader will work out rather than on what the writer knows.","AO4 - form, tone and register: one quiet, observational voice is held throughout; the register stays domestic and exact (jug, dial, chair) so that silence, the abstract noun held back for the paragraph's last word, carries weight.","AO5 - vocabulary used strategically: counted, rehearsed and coughed each do work a plainer verb would not; the gift is small and hard rather than nice, which characterises it and the giver in two words.","AO5 - structure and punctuation for emphasis: a long opening sentence, a list held by a colon, a ten-word sentence about the boiler, then a sentence whose dashes hold back the ending - four different shapes, each chosen, and the last one is where the paragraph lands.","Mini-judgement: this reaches Level 5 because the effects look chosen rather than lucky, and because the reader is trusted; it would be marked as perceptive and subtle communication supported by accurate, emphatic punctuation. On the grids that is the 16–18 band of AO4 and the 11–12 band of AO5."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Level 3 (AO4 and AO5)","items":["The paragraph: The kitchen smelled of lemons. Malik came in and stood by the door. He looked at the blue jug and the radio and the chair. He felt very sad because he had not been there for a long time. He had thought a lot about what he would say to his mother. He had a present in his pocket. It was very quiet in the house and he did not know what to do next.","Point of the paragraph: the same situation, told rather than shown.","AO4 - why it stops at Level 3: it communicates clearly and the reader always knows what is happening, but the feelings are announced (very sad, did not know what to do) so there is nothing for the reader to infer, and the relationship is explained rather than revealed. On AO4 that is the 8–11 band out of 18, where Level 5 is 16–18.","AO4 - form, tone and register: appropriate but undifferentiated; this voice would suit any situation, which is exactly what appropriate use of form, tone and register means at Level 3 rather than sophisticated use at Level 5.","AO5 - vocabulary: accurate and entirely general - sad, present, quiet, a long time - with intensifiers (very, a lot) standing in for precision.","AO5 - sentence structures: seven sentences, six beginning with a pronoun or article, none shorter than five words, and the one list is joined by repeated and, so there is a range of ideas but no range of structures.","The difference to notice: both paragraphs contain the same information. The Level 5 version withholds two facts, gives one exact detail in place of each general one, and varies its sentence shapes deliberately; the Level 3 version states everything once, in one shape. Rewriting for the top level is therefore a matter of subtraction and precision, not of adding longer words."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file — the imaginative writing task and the specification's own prompts","items":["Where it sits: Component 2, Section B, Question 2 - Imaginative Writing, the second of the two questions on the Component 2 paper.","Tariff: 30 marks in total, AO4 18 and AO5 12; 45 minutes advised for the section.","Choice: one 30-mark task based on one of three given prompts; one question is answered.","Purpose: to narrate, describe or entertain; students may write about real or imagined experiences.","Specification prompt 1: 'The moment had passed' - write an imaginative piece ending with these words (the given words must be the last words, unaltered).","Specification prompt 2: 'The last time I saw...' - a title to be completed by the student.","Specification prompt 3: after reading 'Significant Cigarettes' from Part 2 of the anthology, write what you imagine might be the next episode in Lev's story (a continuation prompt tied to a text studied).","Specification prompt 4: 'During the night the wind rose' - write your own imaginative and descriptive piece that begins with those words (the given words must be the opening words).","Specification prompt 5: 'The visitor' - a title prompt.","Specification prompt 6: 'A frightening experience' - a title prompt.","Specification prompt 7: 'My most embarrassing moment' - a title prompt.","The four prompt types to recognise in the exam: a title; a completed-title opener; a fixed first line; a fixed last line. The specification also lists a continuation of a text studied among its prompts, without saying which route that prompt belongs to.","Non-examined alternative: Component 3, Assignment B, a teacher-devised task of approximately 700 words, same 30 marks and same AO4 18 / AO5 12 split; the word count is advisory only and there is no penalty for exceeding it.","Where the topic may come from in Component 3: a piece in the anthology, or an unrelated topic relating to a set of images.","Marking: levels of response, awarded on a best-fit basis, with professional judgement placing work that shows features of more than one level."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Plan — 'The visitor' (Component 2, Section B, Question 2; 30 marks: AO4 18, AO5 12; 45 minutes)","items":["Decoded task: a title prompt, so nothing is fixed except that someone visits or is visited. Narrate, describe or entertain; no set length; the examiner is reading for form, tone and register held to a reader (18) and for vocabulary, sentence structures, paragraphing, spelling, grammar and punctuation (12). The title itself earns nothing, so the visit must be a situation, not a subject.","Timing: 5 minutes choosing and planning, about 35 writing, the last 3 to 5 reading back. Roughly 550 to 700 words.","Situation in one sentence: a man returns to his mother's house after eleven years and finds she has stepped out for twenty minutes.","Thread to hold throughout: he is a visitor in the house he grew up in - so every detail is either unchanged (and therefore accusing) or changed (and therefore excluding).","Paragraph 1 - the room before the person: the smell of cut lemons, the washed knife, the open window. Long opening sentence; no names yet.","Paragraph 2 - the visitor in the doorway: the coat still on, the counting of unmoved objects, the jug, the taped radio, the pulled-out chair. End with the rehearsed arrival and the unrehearsed silence.","Paragraph 3 - the reveal, placed late: the word visitor lands on him; he has a key that no longer fits anything he could name. This is where the eleven years and the word mother finally appear.","Paragraph 4 - the pressure: the clock in the hall, the eleven Decembers of shortening letters, the understanding that forgiveness may have arrived long before he was ready for it.","Paragraph 5 - the close: he sits on the edge of the sofa the way people sit when they are not staying, and the final sentence is the key beginning its long search for the lock. They do not meet on the page.","The evaluation thread (what the piece is arguing): that returning is not the same as being back. Every object is chosen to test that, so the ending can be quiet rather than dramatic.","What the ending must do: refuse the reunion and leave the judgement to the reader; one short paragraph, one sentence, no explanation of what he feels.","AO5 checklist before handing in: no comma splices; should have, not should of; their / there and knew / new; at least one deliberate short sentence per page; paragraph breaks at each shift of attention; dialogue, if any, punctuated and on a new line for each speaker."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:23:47Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"Third pass, counting everything myself: all fourteen count claims now check out - examples[2] step 1 (five sentences, 4 to 16 words, two joined with 'and', 'mostly subject-verb' now exact) and step 5 (three words then two, four shapes in five sentences), examples[0]'s six fixed words, examples[3]'s final four, questions[11] (five statements all opening 'I' or 'The'; a three-word sentence after a 34-word one), questions[12]'s now-correct ten words, questions[13]'s opening at 198 words against a stem asking for \"200 words or so\", extras[0]'s ten-word boiler sentence and extras[1]'s seven sentences with six pronoun/article openings and none shorter than five - and the AO4 (1-3, 4-7, 8-11, 12-15, 16-18 of 18) and AO5 (1-2, 3-4, 5-7, 8-10, 11-12 of 12) bands, the 18/12 split and every specification quotation match the map; no answer key is wrong, so this is publishable once the four presentational points above are tidied."}}},"spoken-language-endorsement":{"id":"EDX-4EA1","topic":"spoken-language-endorsement","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"This is the one part of the course that is optional, carries no marks at all, and still ends up printed on your certificate. The spoken language endorsement (paper code 4EA1/E) is assessed by your own teacher under controlled conditions and monitored by Pearson, and it is graded Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction — a grade reported separately from your 9 to 1 grade for the qualification. Because there is no mark scheme to accumulate marks against, everything depends on criteria you either meet or do not meet: to be awarded a grade you must satisfy every criterion for it. That makes this room unusual. The work is not learning to write more; it is knowing exactly what the rules are — the two permitted forms, the ten-minute limit, the audience rule, the general criteria, the criteria at each grade and the recording and sampling arrangements — and then planning a presentation that meets the ones you are aiming at, including the part most candidates neglect, the questions at the end.","idea":[{"h":"An optional component, graded separately, worth no marks","t":"The preparation and assessment of spoken language is an optional part of the course of study. If you complete it, the grade appears on your certificate as a separately reported grade alongside your overall grade; it does not raise or lower the 9 to 1 grade, and it cannot be exchanged for marks anywhere else. The assessment requirements table describes it as one presentation to an audience with questions, internally set and assessed and externally monitored by Pearson, with the number of raw marks given as not applicable. Paper code 4EA1/E: an oral examination of at most ten minutes, taken under controlled conditions, available in the June and the November series, graded Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction. What a top answer does with this idea: it treats the endorsement as a criteria test, not a marks test, and plans backwards from the criteria for the grade it wants rather than trying to do everything well at once.","code":"Overview"},{"h":"Two permitted forms, both prepared, neither over ten minutes","t":"The specification allows the presentation to take a variety of forms, and names two. The first is a speech or talk by the student, followed by questions from the audience. The second is a formal debate or dialogue — an interview, for example — in which the student is able to prepare extended responses to questions or prompts that have been shared in advance, again followed by questions from the audience. Two conditions apply to both: the presentation must be prepared, and it must last no longer than ten minutes. The words that decide a dialogue's legitimacy are shared in advance: if you first see the interviewer's questions when you sit down, your responses are not prepared extended responses and the form has not been met. What a top answer does with this idea: it chooses the form that suits the material — a single argued case points to a talk, a set of positions to be probed points to a dialogue — and then holds to the two conditions the specification actually states, that the presentation is prepared and lasts no longer than ten minutes, while treating the audience's questions as part of the assessment rather than as something that happens once the real work is over.","code":"Presentation"},{"h":"The audience always includes the teacher","t":"You must give the presentation to an audience, and that audience must always include the teacher. Its size and composition are determined by the teacher, in discussion with you, so a class, a smaller group or a single listener are all possible. There is one extra requirement when the teacher is the only audience: the presentation and dialogue should then be designed so that it could have a potentially wider audience than one person — the specification's own illustration is that it replicates a television interview. The point of that rule is that the formal register, the signposting and the explanation of anything the listener might not know all have to survive, which is easy to lose when you are speaking to one familiar person. What a top answer does with this idea: it fixes the audience early, with the teacher, and then writes for that audience, because meeting the needs of the audience is written into the criteria at Pass, Merit and Distinction alike.","code":"Audience"},{"h":"A grade, not a mark, and every criterion has to be met","t":"Students are assessed either live or from recordings. There are no marks for the endorsement: if you attempt it you are awarded a grade — Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction — using the published assessment criteria, and the grade goes on your certificate. The rule that governs everything else is this: to achieve a particular grade you must meet all of the criteria for that grade. The record sheet states the same thing twice over, adding that you must also fulfil all the criteria for preceding levels where applicable. So a performance that is dazzling on three Distinction criteria and silent on the fourth is not a high Distinction; it is a Merit, if the Merit criteria are all met. What a top answer does with this idea: it audits a performance criterion by criterion and awards the highest grade at which nothing is missing, instead of forming a general impression and then looking for evidence.","code":"Assessment criteria"},{"h":"The four things every graded student must do","t":"Before any of the grade criteria are considered, there are general criteria that apply to Pass, Merit and Distinction alike. To be awarded any of the three grades you must be audible and you must use spoken Standard English. For the purposes of the assessment you must also be intelligible, and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation. These are not marks to be balanced against strengths elsewhere; they are gates. A candidate with genuinely sophisticated ideas who cannot be heard from the back of the room, or who slides into the register of a conversation with friends, has not met them, and no grade follows. What a top answer does with this idea: it checks the general criteria first, in that order, before discussing anything about ideas, structure or questions, because if one of them fails the grade is Not Classified whatever else is true.","code":"General criteria"},{"h":"Pass: straightforward ideas, an attempt at shape, an appropriate reply","t":"In addition to the general criteria, a Pass performance must meet all four of these: it expresses straightforward ideas, information and feelings; it makes an attempt to organise and structure the presentation; it makes an attempt to meet the needs of the audience; and it listens to questions and feedback and provides an appropriate response in a straightforward manner. The word that recurs is attempt. At this grade the shape is visible but not sustained, the audience has been thought about but not consistently served, and the answers given at the end are relevant without being developed. Nothing here is wrong; it is simply undeveloped. What a top answer does with this idea: it can name precisely what a Pass performance is doing — a real attempt — and what it is not yet doing, which is the same four things carried through consistently rather than begun.","code":"Pass"},{"h":"Merit: challenging ideas, clear structure, purpose achieved, detailed replies","t":"Merit replaces every attempt with an achievement. In addition to the general criteria, a Merit performance must meet all four of these: it expresses challenging ideas, information and feelings using a range of vocabulary; it organises and structures the presentation clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience; it achieves the purpose of the presentation; and it listens to questions and feedback, responding formally and in some detail. Two of those are new rather than upgraded. Achieving the purpose is a criterion in its own right, which is why you cannot plan a Merit presentation until you can say in one sentence what you want the audience to know, believe or do. And the response to questions must now be formal and detailed, not merely appropriate. What a top answer does with this idea: it fixes the purpose in writing first, then tests every section of the talk against it and cuts what does not serve it.","code":"Merit"},{"h":"Distinction: sophisticated ideas, strategies that engage, perceptive replies","t":"In addition to the general criteria, a Distinction performance must meet all of these: it expresses sophisticated ideas, information and feelings using a sophisticated repertoire of vocabulary; it organises and structures the presentation using an effective range of strategies to engage the audience; it achieves the purpose of the presentation and listens to questions and feedback; and it responds perceptively and, if appropriate, elaborates with further ideas and information. The jump from Merit is concentrated in two places. Structure is no longer only clear: it uses a range of strategies, and those strategies have to engage the audience. And the response to questions moves from detailed to perceptive, with elaboration — answering what the question is really asking and then adding something the questioner did not have. What a top answer does with this idea: it plans the question phase as deliberately as the talk, deciding in advance which further ideas and information are held back for it.","code":"Distinction"},{"h":"Recordings, record sheets and digital submission","t":"For each June or November series, every centre must provide audio-visual recordings of the presentations of a sample of students drawn from across the teaching groups; there is no requirement to record the presentations of all students, only of those in the sample. All learner work must be submitted digitally. A record sheet should be completed for each student, and the centre uploads the record sheets for the thirty students in its sample and retains the remaining sheets. This is what makes the endorsement internally assessed but externally monitored: the centre awards the grades, and Pearson checks a slice of them against the recordings. What a top answer does with this idea: it distinguishes the two obligations that students often merge — a record sheet for everyone, an audio-visual recording and an uploaded sheet for the sample — and remembers that the recording is audio-visual, not audio only.","code":"External monitoring"},{"h":"How the sample is made up","t":"The sampling table sets it out by centre size. If the centre has thirty or fewer students, all students are recorded; if it has more than thirty, thirty are recorded. In both cases the minimum number of students at each grade — Distinction, Merit and Pass — is ten, with the qualification that a centre takes all students at a grade if it has fewer than the stated minimum. Students assessed as Not Classified should not be included, so in a centre of thirty or fewer the presentations recorded are those of all the students awarded Distinction, Merit or Pass. The sample should be representative of the teaching groups within the centre, so it cannot be drawn from one class or from one teacher's candidates. A small point of naming: the table is headed Marking up the sample on the page, while the Issue 7 changes table calls the new section Making up the sample. What a top answer does with this idea: it works the rule in order — centre size first, then the minimum at each grade, then the exclusion of Not Classified, then the spread across teaching groups.","code":"Marking up the sample"}],"examples":[{"title":"Choosing the form, the topic and the audience","setup":"You have decided to attempt the endorsement in the June series. Your teacher reminds you that the presentation must be prepared, must last no longer than ten minutes, and must be given to an audience that always includes her. You are comfortable talking about the campaign to keep your town's bus route open, and you have already interviewed a local councillor about it. Before you draft a word, settle four things: what exactly the topic is, which of the two permitted forms you will use, who the audience will be, and the purpose you will state.","steps":["Narrow the topic against the time limit first: a history of the route cannot be delivered in the time available, so reduce it to one question — should the subsidy continue?","Weigh the two permitted forms: a speech or talk followed by audience questions puts the whole session in your hands, while a formal debate or dialogue, such as an interview, is legitimate only if the questions or prompts reach you beforehand so your extended responses can genuinely be prepared.","Choose the speech or talk, because your material is an argument you assembled yourself rather than a set of answers waiting to be drawn out by someone else.","Agree the audience with your teacher: your class plus the teacher gives you real questioners, and the size and composition of that audience are the teacher's decision, taken in discussion with you.","Write the purpose down in a single sentence — to persuade this audience that the subsidy should continue — since achieving the purpose is itself a criterion at Merit and at Distinction, and a purpose you have not fixed cannot be achieved."],"cues":["What does a ten-minute ceiling do to a topic this wide?","Which of the two forms depends on questions shared beforehand?","Which form suits material you gathered for yourself?","Who decides the size of the audience, and in discussion with whom?","Why must the purpose be fixed before any drafting begins?"]},{"title":"Shaping a prepared talk within the ten-minute limit","setup":"Your prepared talk will argue that the town's bus route should keep its subsidy. You have three pieces of material: an interview with a councillor, a passenger count you made yourself, and your own story of missing a hospital appointment when a service was cut. The talk may last no longer than ten minutes and will be followed by questions from the audience. Build a structure that meets the Merit criterion for organisation and prepares for the questions as well.","steps":["Plan the length before choosing any content: the conditions the specification places on the talk are that it is prepared and that it lasts no longer than ten minutes. Advice rather than a rule: rehearse to finish comfortably inside that limit instead of exactly on it, so an overrun never threatens the one condition that is stated.","Put the purpose in the first half minute, stated outright, so the audience knows from the start what you are asking them to accept.","Order the three pieces by weight rather than by the order you found them: the passenger count establishes the problem, the councillor's answer shows what the council believes, and the hospital story goes last because it is the one the audience will carry away.","Signpost each move in a full spoken sentence, since organising and structuring clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience is exactly what separates Merit from a Pass candidate's attempt at structure.","Close deliberately, returning to the purpose and handing over to the audience, rather than letting the talk trail off when the material runs out."],"cues":["Which conditions does the specification actually place on the talk itself?","What should the audience know within the opening seconds?","Which of the three pieces should land last, and why?","What turns a sequence of points into a clear structure?","How should a prepared talk end if questions follow?"]},{"title":"Three ways of answering the same audience question","setup":"You have finished the bus subsidy talk and the audience asks three questions: whether the council can afford the subsidy, whether you counted passengers on a typical day, and what you would cut instead. Your teacher is listening against the criteria for listening and responding. Work out what a Pass response, a Merit response and a Distinction response to the affordability question would each sound like, and what all three have in common.","steps":["A Pass response listens and replies appropriately in a straightforward manner: yes, the council can afford it, because the sum involved is small beside its transport budget.","A Merit response keeps the register formal and adds detail: it gives the figure the councillor quoted, says where in the budget the money sits, and concedes openly that it has to come from somewhere.","A Distinction response answers perceptively, recognising that the question is really about priorities rather than arithmetic, and then elaborates with further information the questioner did not ask for, such as the cost to the health service of appointments missed for want of a bus.","Notice what does not change across the three: each is audible, in spoken Standard English, intelligible and pitched to a formal setting, because those general criteria are required before any grade at all is possible."],"cues":["What is the minimum a reply must do to count as a Pass?","What does formally and in some detail add to that reply?","What is this question really about, underneath the money?","Which requirements are identical at all three grades?"]},{"title":"Grading a described performance against the criteria","setup":"A candidate gives a prepared talk to her class and her teacher on why the school day should start later. She is clearly audible throughout, uses spoken Standard English, is easy to follow and stays within a formal register. Her ideas are ambitious and her vocabulary is wide; the talk has an introduction, three signposted sections and a planned close, and it achieves the purpose she set. When the audience asks questions she replies formally and in some detail, but she adds nothing beyond what each question asks and develops no answer further. Decide her grade and justify it.","steps":["Clear the general criteria before anything else: audible, spoken Standard English, intelligible, language suited to the formal setting — all four are met, so a grade can be awarded.","Test each Merit criterion in turn: challenging ideas with a range of vocabulary, organisation that is clear and appropriate for this audience, purpose achieved, and questions answered formally and in some detail. Every one is satisfied.","Test the Distinction criteria: the case gives an introduction, three signposted sections and a planned close, which is clear organisation but is not evidence of an effective range of strategies to engage the audience; and the response to questions stops exactly where Merit stops, since she never responds perceptively or elaborates with further ideas and information.","Apply the governing rule that all of the criteria for a grade must be met: a single unmet Distinction criterion rules Distinction out, however strong everything else is.","Award Merit, and give her the one change that matters: treat each question as an opening to add something, not a box to close."],"cues":["Which criteria have to be cleared before any grade is possible?","Are all four Merit criteria actually satisfied here?","Where exactly does this performance stop short of Distinction?","What does the all-criteria rule do to a near miss?","What single change would lift this candidate next time?"]},{"title":"Making up a centre's recording sample","setup":"A centre enters forty-eight students for the endorsement in the June series. Its teachers award Distinction to fourteen of them, Merit to seventeen, Pass to twelve, and Not Classified to the remaining five. The head of department asks you how many presentations must be recorded, which students should be in the sample, and what paperwork has to go to Pearson. Work it out from the specification's sampling arrangements.","steps":["Start with centre size, because that decides the total: this centre has more than thirty students entered, so thirty presentations are recorded rather than all of them.","Apply the minimum at each grade next: at least ten at Distinction, ten at Merit and ten at Pass, and here every grade has enough candidates, so no minimum has to be reduced.","Exclude the Not Classified students altogether, since they are not included in the sample.","Now state the composition, because the arithmetic settles it: thirty recordings must be spread over the three graded outcomes, and the minimum of ten at each already accounts for all thirty, so the sample is forced to be exactly ten Distinction students, ten Merit students and ten Pass students.","Check the spread before naming names: the sample must be representative of the teaching groups in the centre, so draw the students at each grade from across the groups rather than from one class.","Finish with the paperwork: a record sheet is completed for every student, the sheets for the sampled students are uploaded with the audio-visual recordings, and the remaining sheets are retained by the centre."],"cues":["Which figure does the centre's size decide?","What is the minimum at each of the three grades?","Which students are left out of the sample entirely?","Once thirty recordings have to cover three grades with ten as the minimum at each, how much choice is left over the numbers?","What stops a sample being taken from one class?","Who gets a record sheet, and which sheets are uploaded?"]}],"check":[{"q":"How many marks does the endorsement carry, and what appears on the certificate?","a":"No marks at all. A separately reported grade of Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction appears on the certificate alongside the overall 9 to 1 grade."},{"q":"A student prepares a dialogue in which the interviewer's questions are handed to him as he sits down. Does this meet the second permitted form?","a":"No. In a formal debate or dialogue the questions or prompts must be shared in advance so that the student can prepare extended responses."},{"q":"Which four things must a student do to be awarded any grade at all?","a":"Be audible; use spoken Standard English; be intelligible; and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation."},{"q":"A centre enters twenty-six students, two of whom are assessed as Not Classified. Whose presentations must be recorded?","a":"The twenty-four students awarded Distinction, Merit or Pass. With thirty or fewer students at the centre all students are recorded, and students assessed as Not Classified are not included in the sample."}]},"room":{"facts":["The spoken language endorsement is optional, has paper code 4EA1/E, carries no marks, and is reported on the certificate as a separate grade alongside the overall grade for the qualification.","It is an oral examination of at most ten minutes: one prepared presentation to an audience with questions, internally set and assessed under controlled conditions and externally monitored by Pearson, available in the June and November series.","The two permitted forms are a speech or talk by the student followed by questions from the audience, and a formal debate or dialogue — for example an interview in which the student can prepare extended responses to questions or prompts shared in advance — also followed by questions from the audience.","The audience must always include the teacher; its size and composition are decided by the teacher in discussion with the student; and where the teacher is the only audience, the presentation and dialogue must be designed so that they could have a potentially wider audience, for example by replicating a television interview.","The general criteria for any grade: be audible, use spoken Standard English, be intelligible, and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting.","The four grades are Not Classified, Pass, Merit and Distinction, and a student must meet all of the criteria for a grade to be awarded it, together with all the criteria for preceding levels where applicable.","Students may be assessed live or from recordings, but the centre must provide audio-visual recordings of a sample of students from across the teaching groups for each series, and all learner work is submitted digitally.","Sampling: thirty or fewer students at the centre means all of them are recorded; more than thirty means thirty are recorded. The minimum at each of Distinction, Merit and Pass is ten, or all students at that grade where the centre has fewer; students assessed as Not Classified are not included, so in a small centre the students recorded are all those awarded a grade; and the sample must be representative of the teaching groups.","A record sheet should be completed for each student, the sheets for the sampled students are uploaded — thirty of them where the centre records thirty, and the sheets of every student recorded where a centre of thirty or fewer records them all — and the remaining sheets are retained by the centre.","The specification states what the student must do: demonstrate their presentation skills in a formal setting, listen and respond to questions and feedback, and use spoken English effectively."],"questions":[{"q":"State the two forms the spoken language presentation may take, and say what must follow each of them.","a":"A speech or talk by the student, followed by questions from the audience; or a formal debate or dialogue, such as an interview in which the student prepares extended responses to questions or prompts shared in advance, also followed by questions from the audience.","sol":"The specification says the presentation may take a variety of forms and names two. The first is a speech or talk given by the student, after which the audience asks questions. The second is a formal debate or dialogue — an interview is the example given — in which the student is able to prepare extended responses to questions or prompts that have been shared in advance; this too is followed by questions from the audience. Both forms carry the same two conditions: the presentation must be prepared, and it must last no longer than ten minutes.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Go back to the sentence saying the presentation may take a variety of forms, and read on to the forms the specification then names.","For each form it names, note what the specification says happens once the presentation has been delivered, and any condition it attaches to that form.","Set out each form in turn, then add the condition the specification attaches to the second."],"codes":["Presentation"]},{"q":"State the maximum length of the endorsement presentation and three other conditions the specification places on how it is taken.","a":"No longer than ten minutes; it must be prepared; it is internally assessed under controlled conditions and externally monitored by Pearson; it is available in the June and November series.","sol":"The oral examination lasts a maximum of ten minutes. The presentation must be prepared, not improvised. It is internally set and assessed by the centre under controlled conditions and externally monitored by Pearson, and it takes the form of one presentation to an audience with questions. It is available in the June series and the November series. The number of raw marks is given as not applicable, because there are no marks: students are graded Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction, and the grade is endorsed separately on the certificate.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["This is detail about how the component is run, not about how it is graded.","Picture the practical arrangements a centre has to have in place before any student can sit it at all.","Give the time limit first, then take the remaining conditions one to a sentence."],"codes":["Presentation","Overview"]},{"q":"Who must always be part of the audience, who decides its size and composition, and what extra requirement applies when the teacher is the only audience?","a":"The teacher must always be in the audience; the teacher determines its size and composition in discussion with the student; and where the teacher is the only audience the presentation and dialogue should be designed so they could have a potentially wider audience, for example replicating a television interview.","sol":"Students must give their presentations to an audience, and the teacher must always be part of it. The size and composition of that audience are determined by the teacher, in discussion with the student, so a whole class, a small group or the teacher alone are all permissible. Where the audience is the teacher only, the specification adds one requirement: the presentation and dialogue should be designed in such a way that they could have a potentially wider audience than just one person — its own illustration is that the session replicates a television interview. In practice that means the formal register, the signposting and the explanation of unfamiliar detail all have to be maintained, because they are what a wider audience would need.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["One person is always there, whatever else the audience looks like.","Read the specification's audience paragraph in the order it is written: it settles one thing, then another, then a special case.","Answer in three parts, ending with the rule that applies when the audience is one person."],"codes":["Audience"]},{"q":"List the general criteria that a student must meet to be awarded any grade for the endorsement, and explain what happens if one of them is not met.","a":"Audible; uses spoken Standard English; intelligible; generally uses language appropriate to the formal setting. If one is not met, no grade follows — the outcome is Not Classified.","sol":"To be awarded a Pass, a Merit or a Distinction a student must be audible and must use spoken Standard English. For the purposes of the spoken language assessment the student must also be intelligible and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation. These four are general criteria: they sit in addition to, and beneath, the criteria for each grade. Because a student must meet all of the criteria for a grade in order to be awarded it, failing any one of the general criteria means that none of the three grades can be awarded and the student is Not Classified, no matter how strong the ideas, the structure or the answers to questions may be.","m":5,"d":1,"hints":["These criteria sit underneath the grades: they are the conditions for being awarded any grade at all.","The specification gives them in two sentences, the second beginning 'For the purposes of the spoken language assessment'.","List them, then state what the all-criteria rule produces when one of them is missing."],"codes":["General criteria","Assessment criteria"]},{"q":"Explain how the endorsement is graded, how a grade is arrived at, and how the result is reported.","a":"There are no marks: students are graded Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction against published criteria, live or from recordings, and must meet all the criteria for the grade awarded; the grade is reported separately on the certificate alongside the overall grade.","sol":"Students are assessed either live or from recordings. There are no marks: a student who attempts the assessment is awarded a grade of Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction using the published assessment criteria. To achieve a particular grade, a student must meet all of the criteria for that grade, and the record sheet adds that all the criteria for preceding levels must be fulfilled as well, where applicable. The grade then appears on the student's certificate as a separately reported grade alongside the overall grade issued for the qualification, which is itself awarded on the nine-grade scale from 9 to 1. The endorsement therefore changes nothing about the 9 to 1 grade; it stands beside it.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Begin from the fact that there is nothing to add up.","One rule decides how criteria combine into a grade — it is about completeness, not balance.","Cover three things in order: the scale, the all-criteria rule, and where the result is printed."],"codes":["Overview","Assessment criteria"]},{"q":"A student expresses straightforward ideas, makes an attempt to organise the talk and to meet the audience's needs, and answers questions appropriately in a straightforward manner. Using the criteria, explain why this cannot be awarded a Merit and state precisely what would have to change.","a":"It meets the Pass criteria but not the Merit ones: Merit requires challenging ideas with a range of vocabulary, clear and appropriate organisation for the audience, the purpose achieved, and responses that are formal and detailed.","sol":"Every criterion described is a Pass criterion, and each is phrased as an attempt or as straightforwardness. Merit asks for four different things. First, the ideas, information and feelings must be challenging rather than straightforward, and delivered using a range of vocabulary, so the content and the word choice both have to rise. Second, the presentation must actually be organised and structured clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience, so an attempt at structure is not enough; the shape must hold from the opening to the close and must be built for these listeners. Third, Merit introduces a criterion that has no Pass equivalent: the presentation must achieve its purpose, which means the purpose has to be defined before the planning begins and every section has to serve it. Fourth, the response to questions and feedback must be formal and given in some detail, rather than simply appropriate and brief. Because a student must meet all the criteria for a grade, all four changes are required, not the most convenient one.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Look at the verbs at Pass: they keep saying the same thing about effort.","Compare the two grades criterion by criterion rather than as overall impressions.","One Merit criterion has no Pass counterpart at all — find it and start there."],"codes":["Pass","Merit"]},{"q":"Outline a structure for a prepared talk of no longer than ten minutes that would satisfy the Merit criterion for organisation, and justify each decision by reference to the criteria and to the form.","a":"The purpose stated outright at the opening; three signposted sections ordered by weight rather than by the order the material was gathered; a deliberate close that returns to the purpose and hands over to the audience; the whole talk prepared and lasting no longer than ten minutes.","sol":"Plan the session before the content. The specification's conditions on the talk are that it is prepared and that it lasts no longer than ten minutes, and the form places questions from the audience after it, so the questions are part of the assessment rather than an optional extra. Open by stating the purpose outright, because Merit requires the purpose to be achieved and an audience cannot help you achieve a purpose it has not been told. Then take three sections, ordered by weight rather than by the order in which you gathered the material: the section that establishes the problem, the section that gives the other side's position, and last the section with the strongest human detail, which is the one the audience will still be holding when the questions begin. Signpost each transition in a full spoken sentence, since organising and structuring clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience is what distinguishes Merit from a Pass candidate's attempt. Close deliberately by returning to the purpose and inviting questions, rather than stopping because the material has run out. One piece of practical advice, which the specification does not state as a rule: rehearse to a length that finishes comfortably inside the limit, so that overrunning never puts the one stated condition at risk. Every decision in that shape can be traced to a criterion or to the form itself.","m":6,"d":2,"hints":["Decide how the session is divided before deciding what goes in it.","Remember that the questions from the audience are part of the form, not something that begins after the assessment.","Give the shape in order, and attach the criterion that justifies each part."],"codes":["Merit","Presentation"]},{"q":"Explain the difference between the Merit and Distinction criteria for listening and responding to questions and feedback, and show what that difference sounds like in practice.","a":"Merit requires listening and responding formally and in some detail; Distinction requires listening and then responding perceptively and, if appropriate, elaborating with further ideas and information.","sol":"Both grades require the student to listen to questions and feedback, so the listening itself is not what separates them. At Merit the response must be formal in register and given in some detail: the answer is accurate, developed and properly pitched, but it stays inside the boundaries of the question. At Distinction the response must be perceptive and, where appropriate, must elaborate with further ideas and information. Perceptive means answering what the question is actually driving at rather than its surface wording — recognising, for instance, that a question about whether something can be afforded is really a question about priorities. Elaboration means adding something the questioner did not have: a further consequence, a second piece of evidence, a qualification the student had held back for exactly this moment. In practice, the Merit answer closes the question and the Distinction answer opens it, which is why a Distinction candidate plans the question phase in advance and decides which material to keep out of the talk for use there.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["The listening requirement is common to both; the difference is in the reply.","One grade adds a quality of insight, the other adds only detail and formality.","Quote the two criteria side by side, then illustrate each with the same question answered twice."],"codes":["Merit","Distinction"]},{"q":"A student's talk is ambitious in its ideas and carefully structured, but he drops his voice in the second half and several listeners cannot make out whole sentences. Decide the grade and justify it from the criteria.","a":"Not Classified: the general criteria of being audible and intelligible are not met, and they must be met before any of Pass, Merit or Distinction can be awarded.","sol":"The general criteria come before the grade criteria. To be awarded a Pass, a Merit or a Distinction, a student must be audible and use spoken Standard English, and for the purposes of the assessment must also be intelligible and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting. This student fails on two of them: for a substantial part of the presentation he is not audible, and where he cannot be heard he cannot be intelligible either. Because a student must meet all of the criteria for a grade, strength elsewhere cannot compensate — this is not a levels-marked essay where a best fit judgement balances strengths against weaknesses, and there are no marks to trade. The outcome is Not Classified. The feedback that matters is entirely practical: pace, projection and a checked position in the room, because the ambitious content is already there and is worth nothing until it can be heard.","m":4,"d":2,"hints":["Ask which criteria have to be satisfied before any grade is available.","There is no balancing of strengths against weaknesses in this assessment.","Name the criteria that fail, apply the all-criteria rule, then state the outcome."],"codes":["General criteria","Assessment criteria"]},{"q":"Explain how the audience for a presentation is decided, and why a presentation given to the teacher alone must still be designed for a potentially wider audience.","a":"The teacher determines the size and composition of the audience in discussion with the student, and must always be in it; with an audience of one, the presentation and dialogue must be designed so they could have a wider audience, for example replicating a television interview.","sol":"Students must present to an audience and the teacher must always be part of it; beyond that, the size and composition are determined by the teacher in discussion with the student, so the audience may be a class, a group or the teacher alone. Where the teacher is the only listener, the specification requires that the presentation and dialogue be designed in such a way that they could have a potentially wider audience than just one person, and gives replicating a television interview as its example. The reason is that the criteria at every grade are written around an audience: a Pass makes an attempt to meet the needs of the audience, a Merit organises the presentation appropriately to meet those needs, and a Distinction uses an effective range of strategies to engage the audience. Speaking to one familiar person invites the register, the shorthand and the assumed knowledge of a conversation, all of which would destroy those criteria. Designing for a wider audience is what keeps the formality, the signposting and the explanation in place.","m":5,"d":2,"hints":["Two people are involved in the decision, and only one of them makes it.","Ask what the criteria at each grade assume about who is listening.","Deal with the general rule first, then the special case of an audience of one."],"codes":["Audience","Distinction"]},{"q":"A centre enters fifty-two students for the endorsement and awards Distinction to nine, Merit to twenty, Pass to eighteen and Not Classified to five. Work out the recording sample, explaining each decision, and state what the centre must submit.","a":"Thirty recordings: all nine Distinctions, because the centre has fewer than the minimum of ten at that grade, at least ten Merits and at least ten Passes, with the remaining place filled so the sample is representative of the teaching groups; the five Not Classified students are excluded. Record sheets for all students are completed, those for the sample are uploaded with the audio-visual recordings, and the rest are retained.","sol":"Take the rule in order. First, centre size: more than thirty students are entered, so thirty presentations are recorded rather than all of them. Second, the minimum at each grade: ten at Distinction, ten at Merit and ten at Pass, except that a centre takes all students at a grade where it has fewer than the stated minimum. This centre has only nine Distinctions, so all nine are recorded. Third, the exclusion: students assessed as Not Classified are not included, so the five of them play no part. Ten Merits and ten Passes alongside the nine Distinctions account for twenty-nine of the thirty, leaving one further place, which is filled from the Merit or Pass candidates in whatever way keeps the sample representative of the teaching groups within the centre — it cannot be drawn from a single class or a single teacher's set. Finally the paperwork: a record sheet is completed for every student; the record sheets for the students in the sample are uploaded along with the audio-visual recordings, and the remaining sheets are retained by the centre. All learner work is submitted digitally.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Work through the rule in a fixed order: size, then grade minimums, then exclusions, then spread.","One grade here has fewer candidates than the stated minimum, and the rule says what to do.","Set out the total first, then account for the places grade by grade, then the paperwork."],"codes":["Marking up the sample","External monitoring"]},{"q":"A centre enters twenty-eight students and awards Distinction to four, Merit to ten, Pass to eleven and Not Classified to three. State exactly whose presentations must be recorded and what must be sent to Pearson, and explain why the minimum numbers at each grade cause no difficulty here.","a":"Twenty-five presentations: the four Distinctions, the ten Merits and the eleven Passes. A centre of thirty or fewer records all its students, and the three assessed as Not Classified are not included in the sample. A record sheet is completed for every student; the sampled students' sheets go to Pearson with the audio-visual recordings and the rest are retained, all submitted digitally.","sol":"Take the sampling table in order. Centre size decides the scale: with thirty or fewer students entered, all students are recorded rather than a selection of thirty, so nobody is left out for want of places. The exclusion then fixes who is actually recorded, because students assessed as Not Classified should not be included in the sample: the three Not Classified students stand outside it, and twenty-five presentations are recorded — the four Distinctions, the ten Merits and the eleven Passes. The minimum number at each grade causes no difficulty, because the rule takes all students at a grade where the centre has fewer than the stated minimum: four Distinctions is below ten and all four are recorded, while Merit and Pass stand at ten or above in any case. The sample must still be representative of the teaching groups within the centre, which matters even at this size if the entry is split between two teachers. Finally the paperwork: a record sheet should be completed for each student, the record sheets for the sampled students are uploaded along with the audio-visual recordings, the remaining sheets are retained by the centre, and all learner work is submitted digitally.","m":6,"d":3,"hints":["Check the centre's size against the threshold before doing anything else.","The per-grade minimum carries a qualification for small entries — find it.","Deal with the recordings first, then the record sheets, then the group spread."],"codes":["Marking up the sample","External monitoring"]},{"q":"Design a dialogue-form assessment for a student whose only audience will be the teacher, and explain how your design satisfies the rule about the form, the rule about the audience, and the Distinction criteria.","a":"An interview in which the teacher's questions or prompts are shared in advance so the student can prepare extended responses, staged as a broadcast-style interview so it could have a wider audience, no longer than ten minutes, with open audience questions at the end that the student answers perceptively and elaborates on.","sol":"The form first. The second permitted form is a formal debate or dialogue, such as an interview in which the student is able to prepare extended responses to questions or prompts shared in advance, followed by questions from the audience. So the teacher issues the interview questions or prompts beforehand, the student prepares extended responses to them, and unseen questions come only at the end; the whole thing is prepared and runs to no more than ten minutes. The audience next. The teacher must always be in the audience and is here the whole of it, so the session must be designed so that it could have a potentially wider audience than one person: stage it as a broadcast interview, with the teacher in the interviewer's role, the student introduced as the guest, and every reference explained as it would have to be for viewers who know nothing of the school. That single design decision protects the formal register and the signposting. The Distinction criteria last. Sophisticated ideas and a sophisticated repertoire of vocabulary come from choosing a subject with real complexity and rehearsing precise terms for it; an effective range of strategies to engage the audience is available even in an interview, through a striking opening answer, a concrete illustration, a considered pause before the hardest question and a closing statement that lands; the purpose must be fixed and achieved; and the closing questions must be answered perceptively, with material deliberately held back so that the student can elaborate with further ideas and information.","m":8,"d":3,"hints":["Start from what makes a dialogue legitimate at all under this specification.","An audience of one triggers one extra design requirement — build the staging around it.","Take the three demands in turn and show what your design does about each."],"codes":["Presentation","Audience","Distinction"]},{"q":"A student meets every Distinction criterion except that she never elaborates on her answers to questions. Explain what grade she can be awarded and why, and what this shows about the way the criteria operate.","a":"Merit, provided all the Merit and general criteria are met: a student must meet all of the criteria for a grade to be awarded it, so one unmet Distinction criterion rules Distinction out however strong the rest is.","sol":"The rule is that in order to achieve a particular grade a student must meet all of the criteria for that grade, and the record sheet adds that all the criteria for preceding levels must be fulfilled as well, where applicable. Distinction requires the student to respond perceptively and, if appropriate, to elaborate with further ideas and information; if she never elaborates, that criterion is unmet and Distinction cannot be awarded, no matter how sophisticated her ideas and vocabulary, how engaging her strategies or how completely her purpose is achieved. The grade is therefore the highest one at which nothing is missing: Merit, provided her responses are formal and in some detail, her structure clear and appropriate for the audience, her ideas challenging with a range of vocabulary, her purpose achieved, and the general criteria all met. What this shows is the difference between criteria and marks. In a levels-marked essay a marker balances strengths against weaknesses and awards the best fit. Here there is no balancing and nothing to add up: each criterion is a gate, and a grade is a set of gates all of which must be open. That is also why the outcome is a grade of Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction reported separately on the certificate, rather than marks feeding into the 9 to 1 grade.","m":5,"d":3,"hints":["One sentence in the criteria governs how a set of criteria becomes a grade.","Ask what the highest grade is at which nothing at all is missing.","Contrast this with how a levels-marked essay is judged, then state the grade."],"codes":["Assessment criteria","Distinction","Overview"]}],"exit":[{"q":"What is the maximum length of the endorsement presentation?","a":"Ten minutes."},{"q":"Who must always be part of the audience?","a":"The teacher."},{"q":"Name the four grades the endorsement uses.","a":"Not Classified, Pass, Merit and Distinction."},{"q":"A student meets all the Pass criteria and three of the four Merit criteria. What grade is awarded, and why?","a":"Pass — a student must meet all of the criteria for a grade in order to be awarded that grade."}]},"cards":[{"front":"Spoken language endorsement (4EA1/E)","back":"The optional spoken language component: one prepared presentation to an audience with questions, internally assessed under controlled conditions, externally monitored by Pearson, carrying no marks and reported separately on the certificate.","code":"Overview","open":false},{"front":"What the endorsement asks a student to do","back":"Demonstrate presentation skills in a formal setting, listen and respond to questions and feedback, and use spoken English effectively. One presentation to an audience with questions.","code":"Overview","open":false},{"front":"The two permitted presentation forms","back":"A speech or talk by the student followed by audience questions; or a formal debate or dialogue, such as an interview with extended responses prepared from questions or prompts shared in advance, also followed by audience questions.","code":"Presentation","open":false},{"front":"Maximum length of the presentation","back":"Ten minutes. The presentation must also be prepared, whichever of the two permitted forms is used.","code":"Presentation","open":false},{"front":"The audience rule","back":"The presentation must be given to an audience that always includes the teacher. The teacher determines the size and composition of that audience, in discussion with the student.","code":"Audience","open":false},{"front":"Teacher-only audience","back":"Where the teacher is the only audience, the presentation and dialogue must be designed so that they could have a potentially wider audience than one person — for example, by replicating a television interview.","code":"Audience","open":false},{"front":"The general criteria","back":"Required for Pass, Merit or Distinction alike: be audible; use spoken Standard English; be intelligible; and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting of the presentation.","code":"General criteria","open":false},{"front":"Pass criteria","back":"Beyond the general criteria: expresses straightforward ideas, information and feelings; attempts to organise and structure the presentation; attempts to meet the audience's needs; listens to questions and feedback and responds appropriately in a straightforward manner.","code":"Pass","open":false},{"front":"Merit criteria","back":"Beyond the general criteria: expresses challenging ideas, information and feelings using a range of vocabulary; organises and structures the presentation clearly and appropriately for the audience; achieves its purpose; listens and responds formally and in some detail.","code":"Merit","open":false},{"front":"Distinction criteria","back":"Beyond the general criteria: sophisticated ideas, information and feelings with a sophisticated repertoire of vocabulary; structure using an effective range of strategies to engage the audience; purpose achieved; listens and responds perceptively, elaborating with further ideas and information where appropriate.","code":"Distinction","open":false},{"front":"One way a Distinction response to questions goes beyond a Merit response","back":"It responds perceptively rather than only formally and in some detail; it elaborates with further ideas and information where appropriate, adding what the questioner did not have; it answers what the question is really driving at rather than its surface wording.","code":"Distinction","open":true},{"front":"The all-criteria rule","back":"To achieve a grade a student must meet all of the criteria for that grade, and all the criteria for preceding levels where applicable. There is no balancing of strengths against weaknesses.","code":"Assessment criteria","open":false},{"front":"Live or recorded assessment","back":"Students may be assessed either live or from recordings. There are no marks: a student who attempts the assessment is awarded Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction against the published criteria.","code":"Assessment criteria","open":false},{"front":"Recordings and record sheets","back":"Each series, centres record a sample of students from across teaching groups; not all students are recorded. A record sheet is completed for every student; the sample's sheets are uploaded and the rest retained. All work is submitted digitally.","code":"External monitoring","open":false},{"front":"Making up the sample","back":"Thirty or fewer at the centre: all recorded. Over thirty: thirty recorded. Minimum ten each at Distinction, Merit and Pass, or all at that grade if fewer. Not Classified excluded. Representative of teaching groups. Specification heading: Marking up the sample.","code":"Marking up the sample","open":false}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Distinction standard (opening of a prepared talk)","items":["The task: a prepared talk of no longer than ten minutes, given to the class and the teacher and followed by questions from the audience. Topic: should the town keep subsidising the number 14 bus route? Purpose: to persuade this audience that the subsidy should continue.","The opening, as spoken: Good afternoon. I want to begin with a number that has nothing to do with buses. Forty-one. That is how many appointments the surgery on Mill Street recorded as missed in a single month after the evening service was withdrawn — and every one of them belonged to somebody who had no other way of getting there. We tend to argue about this route as though it were a line on a transport budget. I am going to ask you to look at it as something else: as a piece of public infrastructure that other services quietly depend on. I will put three things to you. First, what the passenger figures actually show, because I counted them myself and they are not what the council's consultation assumed. Second, what the council says in its own defence — I interviewed Councillor Hale, and I want you to hear her case properly before I answer it. And third, what it costs the rest of us when the route is not there. By the end I want you to be able to say not simply that the bus is useful, but that withdrawing it would move a cost from one column to another and call it a saving.","Why this is Distinction standard: the ideas are sophisticated rather than merely challenging — the argument is not the bus is good but the saving is a transfer of cost, which is a genuinely difficult claim, and the vocabulary supports it precisely (public infrastructure, consultation, transfer of cost) without reaching for words the speaker cannot control.","Why this is Distinction standard: it organises and structures the talk using an effective range of strategies to engage the audience — an opening statistic withheld from its context for one sentence, a direct reframing of what the audience assumes the argument is about, a three-part roadmap, and the promise that the opposing case will be heard fairly before it is answered.","Why this is Distinction standard: the purpose is stated openly and the close of the opening names the exact change of mind the speaker is working for, so that everything after it can be judged against a declared aim.","What holds the grade during the questions: the speaker has deliberately kept the health-service cost figures out of the talk so that when someone asks whether the council can afford it, the answer can go beyond the arithmetic to the question of priorities and then elaborate with information the questioner did not have — which is exactly what responding perceptively and elaborating with further ideas and information means.","What to copy: one arresting concrete detail first; a sentence that renames what the argument is really about; a three-part map; a declared purpose; and at least one piece of evidence held back for the questions."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph — Pass standard (opening of the same talk)","items":["The task: the same one. A prepared talk of no longer than ten minutes to the class and the teacher, followed by questions, arguing that the town should keep subsidising the number 14 bus route.","The opening, as spoken: Hello, my talk today is about the number 14 bus and why I think it should be kept. Buses are quite important for a lot of people in our town, especially older people and people who do not have a car. I am going to talk about a few different things. I did a passenger count and I also spoke to a councillor about it. There are quite a lot of people who use the bus and I think the council should think about that before they cut it. Some people say it costs too much money but I do not really agree with that. Anyway, I will start with my passenger count.","Why this reaches Pass and no further: the ideas are straightforward rather than challenging — quite important, a lot of people, I do not really agree — and the vocabulary has no range to it, so the first Merit criterion is not met.","Why this reaches Pass and no further: there is an attempt to organise and structure the talk, which is what Pass asks for, but it is only an attempt: a few different things is not a roadmap, the order of the material is the order in which the speaker happened to collect it, and Anyway hands the structure over to chance rather than to a plan.","Why this reaches Pass and no further: there is an attempt to meet the needs of the audience — older people and people without a car are mentioned — but nothing is explained for a listener who does not already know the route, and the register slips towards conversation (Hello, Anyway, quite a lot), which sits uneasily with the formal setting.","Why this reaches Pass and no further: no purpose is fixed. Why I think it should be kept tells the audience the speaker's opinion but does not say what the audience is being asked to do with it, so the Merit criterion that the presentation achieves its purpose cannot be satisfied — there is no purpose to achieve.","Two changes that would lift it to Merit: state the purpose in one sentence at the start and order the three pieces of material by weight with a signposted transition between each; then answer the audience's questions formally and in some detail rather than in a sentence. To go further, to Distinction, add strategies that engage the audience from the first line and hold back one piece of evidence for the question phase."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the endorsement as the specification sets it","items":["Status: the preparation and assessment of spoken language is an optional component of the course of study.","Reporting: if a student completes the endorsement, it appears on the certificate as a separately reported grade, alongside the overall grade issued for the qualification.","Paper code: 4EA1/E.","What the student must do: demonstrate presentation skills in a formal setting, listen and respond to questions and feedback, and use spoken English effectively.","Assessment type: internally assessed under controlled conditions; one presentation to an audience with questions, internally set and assessed, externally monitored by Pearson.","Length: an oral examination of a maximum of ten minutes.","Availability: the June and November series.","Grades: Not Classified, Pass, Merit, Distinction.","Marks: the number of raw marks is given as not applicable — the endorsement carries no marks.","Preparation: the presentation must be prepared, whichever permitted form is used.","Audience: students must give their presentations to an audience, which must always include the teacher; its size and composition are determined by the teacher, in discussion with the student.","Audience of one: where the audience is the teacher only, the presentation and dialogue should be designed so that they could have a potentially wider audience than just one person — the specification's example is that it replicates a television interview.","Assessment method: students should be assessed either live or from recordings.","The governing rule: in order to achieve a particular grade, a student must meet all of the criteria for that grade; the record sheet adds that all the criteria for preceding levels must also be fulfilled, where applicable.","General criteria, required for Pass, Merit or Distinction: be audible; use spoken Standard English. For the purposes of the assessment the student must also be intelligible and generally use language appropriate to the formal setting.","Pass, in addition to the general criteria: expresses straightforward ideas, information and feelings; makes an attempt to organise and structure the presentation; makes an attempt to meet the needs of the audience; listens to questions and feedback and provides an appropriate response in a straightforward manner.","Merit, in addition to the general criteria: expresses challenging ideas, information and feelings using a range of vocabulary; organises and structures the presentation clearly and appropriately to meet the needs of the audience; achieves the purpose of the presentation; listens to questions and feedback, responding formally and in some detail.","Distinction, in addition to the general criteria: expresses sophisticated ideas, information and feelings using a sophisticated repertoire of vocabulary; organises and structures the presentation using an effective range of strategies to engage the audience; achieves the purpose of the presentation and listens to questions and feedback; responds perceptively and, if appropriate, elaborates with further ideas and information.","Recording: for each June and/or November series, all centres must provide audio-visual recordings of the presentations of a sample of students from across teaching groups; there is no requirement to record the presentations of all students.","Submission: all learner work must be submitted digitally.","Record sheets: a record sheet should be completed for each student; the centre uploads the record sheets for the students in its sample — thirty of them where the centre records thirty, fewer where a centre of thirty or fewer records all its graded students — and retains the remaining sheets.","Sampling by centre size: thirty or fewer students at the centre — all students recorded; more than thirty — thirty recorded.","Sampling by grade: the minimum number of students at each grade (Distinction, Merit, Pass) is ten, with all students at a grade taken if the centre has fewer than the stated minimum.","Sampling exclusion: students assessed as Not Classified should not be included.","Sampling spread: the sample should be representative of the teaching groups within the centre.","A naming point: the table is headed Marking up the sample on the page, while the Issue 7 changes table refers to the new section as Making up the sample.","Relation to the 9 to 1 grade: the qualification itself is graded and certificated on the nine-grade scale from 9 to 1; the endorsement carries no marks and its grade is reported separately alongside that overall grade."]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the two permitted presentation forms","items":["Form A, as the specification names it: a speech or talk by a student, followed by questions from the audience.","Form A — who speaks: one student, throughout, with the audience contributing only in the question phase at the end.","Form A — what it suits: a single argued case, a persuasive proposal, an explanation of something the speaker has researched or gathered personally, where the shape of the whole session is the speaker's to design.","Form A — the planning consequence: the specification states only that the presentation is prepared and lasts no longer than ten minutes, and that questions from the audience follow it; it does not set a separate length for the question phase. Advice, not a rule: rehearse the talk to a length you can hold to, and prepare for the questions as part of the assessment.","Form B, as the specification names it: a formal debate or dialogue, such as an interview where the student is able to prepare extended responses to questions or prompts which have been shared in advance, followed by questions from the audience.","Form B — the condition that makes it legitimate: the questions or prompts must be shared in advance, so that the student's extended responses are genuinely prepared. Questions first seen at the moment of the assessment do not meet this.","Form B — what it suits: a subject with more than one defensible position, or a student who performs better when drawn out by a questioner than when holding the floor alone.","Form B — the audience of one case: an interview format sits naturally with the requirement that a teacher-only assessment be designed so it could have a wider audience, because a television interview is the specification's own illustration of that design.","Common to both forms: the presentation must be prepared; it must last no longer than ten minutes; it is followed by questions from the audience; and the audience must always include the teacher.","Common to both forms: the same general criteria and the same Pass, Merit and Distinction criteria apply. The choice of form changes how the student meets them, never which ones apply."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Presentation plan — a prepared talk with questions, planned to Distinction standard","items":["The task, decoded: one prepared presentation to an audience with questions, no longer than ten minutes, given to an audience that always includes the teacher, assessed against the general criteria plus the criteria for the grade aimed at. There are no marks; the outcome is Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction, and every criterion for the grade must be met.","Step 1 — choose the form: a speech or talk followed by audience questions, because the material is an argument assembled by the speaker rather than a set of positions to be drawn out by an interviewer. (The alternative form would require the questions or prompts to be shared in advance.)","Step 2 — choose the topic by three tests: can it be argued, not just described? Can it be covered in the time with something left over? Does the speaker know something about it the audience does not? Reject any topic that fails one of the three.","Step 3 — fix the purpose in one written sentence: what this audience should know, believe or do differently at the end. Merit and Distinction both require the purpose to be achieved, and a purpose that has not been written down cannot be tested against.","Step 4 — agree the audience with the teacher: size and composition are the teacher's decision, taken in discussion with the student. If the teacher will be the only listener, design the session so it could have a wider audience.","Step 5 — settle the length before writing anything: the specification's conditions here are that the presentation is prepared and lasts no longer than ten minutes, and that questions from the audience follow it. Advice, not a rule from the specification: rehearse to finish comfortably inside the limit, and plan the question phase as deliberately as the talk.","Opening (about one minute): one concrete detail or figure that the audience does not expect; a sentence that renames what the argument is really about; the purpose stated outright; a three-part roadmap.","Section one: establish the problem with evidence the speaker gathered personally, so the credibility of everything after it is set early.","Section two: state the opposing case fairly, in its strongest form, and attribute it. Engaging an audience includes not insulting the position half of them may hold.","Section three: the answer to that case, ending with the most human piece of material, because it is the one the audience will still be holding when the questions start.","Close (about thirty seconds): return to the purpose in the words used at the start, name the change of mind being asked for, and hand over to the audience deliberately rather than trailing off.","The engagement thread that runs through the whole talk: a single controlling idea, repeated in the same words at the opening, at each transition and at the close, so that the range of strategies the Distinction criterion asks for is not a set of tricks but one argument held in view.","The question phase, planned in advance: list the three questions most likely to come, including the hostile one, and decide for each what the question is really asking. Keep at least one piece of evidence out of the talk so that it can be produced here as elaboration.","What the close of the question phase must do: answer perceptively rather than defensively, concede what is genuinely conceded, and finish on the purpose rather than on the last objection raised.","Self-check before the assessment, in order: audible from the back; spoken Standard English; intelligible throughout; register appropriate to a formal setting; then the criteria for the grade aimed at, one by one, with evidence from the plan for each. Any criterion without evidence is the one to work on."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"How this room is graded, and how to answer its questions","items":["There are no marks anywhere in the endorsement: the outcome is Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction. The number beside each question in this room counts the separate points your answer has to make, so that you can see how much is wanted; it is not a tariff from the specification, and there is no tariff to quote.","When a question asks you to judge a described performance, your answer is a grade and a justification, never a mark. Name the criteria that are met, name the criterion that is not, and apply the rule that all the criteria for a grade must be met.","Work in a fixed order every time: general criteria first (audible, spoken Standard English, intelligible, language appropriate to the formal setting), then the criteria for the grade in question, one by one.","Do not import the best fit language of the levels-marked components. In the examined papers a marker balances strengths against weaknesses; in the endorsement there is nothing to balance, because each criterion is a gate.","When a question asks about the sampling arrangements, take the rule in the same order each time: centre size, then the minimum at each grade, then the exclusion of Not Classified students, then the requirement that the sample be representative of the teaching groups, then the record sheets."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:08:53Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.82,"notes":"All three refusal faults of the first examination are gone - there is no 'Assessment objective 6' anywhere (room.facts[9] and cards[1] now carry the specification's own 'demonstrate presentation skills... listen and respond... use spoken English effectively'), the invented seven-to-eight-minute rule is replaced by the stated ten-minute cap with the rehearsal advice explicitly labelled 'advice, not a rule', and the 28-student sampling key now says plainly that twenty-five presentations are recorded; every answer key re-solved correctly, so what remains is a worked example that never states the forced ten-per-grade sample and a 'thirty sampled students' fact that reads as unconditional."}}},"reading-writing-skills":{"id":"EDX-4EA1","topic":"reading-writing-skills","family":"essay","lesson":{"why":"Every mark in this qualification is an assessment objective mark. There are 150 raw marks in total and the specification tells you exactly where they sit: 23 for AO1, 30 for AO2, 22 for AO3, 45 for AO4 and 30 for AO5, with AO6 carrying no weighting at all. That means the objectives are not a page of jargon at the front of the specification - they are the instructions for every answer you will ever write on this course. Once you know that Component 1 Question 2 pays only for AO2, you stop retelling the extract in it; once you know that writing carries 75 of the 150 raw marks, half the qualification, you stop treating the writing tasks as the bit you do if there is time. This room teaches you to read a question backwards, from the objective it pays for to the sentences that earn it.","idea":[{"h":"AO1: the marks are for interpreting, not for retelling","t":"The specification sets AO1 as 'read and understand a variety of texts, selecting and interpreting information, ideas and perspectives'. It is 15% of the International GCSE - 7% in Component 1 and 8% in Component 2 or 3 - and 23 raw marks: 11 at Component 1 Question 1 and 12 inside the 30-mark Question 1 of Component 2 or 3. The levels reward three separate things: the selection of information, ideas and perspectives, the use of quotations and textual references, and the interpretation itself. What the specification says about interpretation is that it must go beyond retelling or paraphrasing - restating the text, which is exactly what a nervous student does when time is short, is the one thing the grid does not pay for - while the bottom of the printed AO1 grid is described as limited selection with a basic understanding. A top-level answer makes every point a claim about what the text means rather than about what happens in it, supports it with the shortest quotation that proves the claim, and earns the top band's language: 'detailed and persuasive' selection, 'discriminating' use of quotations and a 'convincing' understanding of ideas and perspectives.","code":"AO1"},{"h":"AO2: the largest reading tariff is paid for effects, not for spotting","t":"AO2 is 'understand and analyse how writers use linguistic and structural devices to achieve their effects'. It is 20% of the qualification - 8% in Component 1, 12% in Component 2 or 3 - and 30 raw marks: 12 at Component 1 Question 2 and 18 of the 30 marks at Component 2 or 3 Question 1, the biggest single objective tariff on the reading side. Its five levels climb from 'limited identification of language and/or structural devices' with 'basic and simple comment on the effect', through 'clear explanations of the effects', to 'a detailed exploration', and finally to 'subtle and discriminating selection' with 'a perceptive analysis of the effects of language and structure'. Naming a device earns almost nothing. A top-level answer names the method, quotes the smallest unit of text that proves it, explains what the method does to the reader at that moment, and then says what it does for the passage as a whole - and it chooses unobvious examples, because 'discriminating selection' means the choice of evidence is itself an argument.","code":"AO2"},{"h":"AO3: one answer, one chance, twenty-two marks","t":"AO3 is 'explore links and connections between writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed'. It is 15% of the International GCSE and all of it sits in one place: the 22-mark Question 3 of Component 1, on the Part 1 anthology text and the previously unseen extract. Component 2 and Component 3 carry no AO3 marks at all, so this objective is examined once, in a single answer, with no second chance. Under Key qualification features the specification says students develop the ability to 'make comparisons between texts and analyse the ways in which writers achieve their effects'. Read the objective as two halves joined by a comma: what the writers think, and how that thinking is conveyed. A top-level answer never writes a summary of text one followed by a summary of text two; it builds each paragraph on a single shared idea, puts both writers inside that paragraph with evidence from each, and ends by weighing whose method presses the perspective harder and why.","code":"AO3"},{"h":"AO4: the biggest objective in the whole qualification is a writing one","t":"AO4 is 'communicate effectively and imaginatively, adapting form, tone and register of writing for specific purposes and audiences'. At 30% it is the largest single objective in the International GCSE - 18% in Component 1 and 12% in Component 2 or 3 - worth 45 raw marks: 27 in the transactional task of Component 1 Section B and 18 in the imaginative task of Component 2 Question 2 or Component 3 Assignment B. Under Key qualification features the specification says students develop the ability to 'construct and convey meaning in written language, matching style to audience and purpose'. The levels reward realisation of the task according to the writer's purpose and the expectations and requirements of the intended reader, so a brilliant essay that ignores the named audience is capped. A top-level answer decides three things before writing a word - who is reading, what the piece is for, and which of the six transactional forms it must look like - then holds that register from the first sentence to the last and lets the form show, in a headline and standfirst, a salutation and sign-off, or a speech's direct address.","code":"AO4"},{"h":"AO5: technical accuracy is a fifth of the qualification","t":"AO5 is 'write clearly, using a range of vocabulary and sentence structures, with appropriate paragraphing and accurate spelling, grammar and punctuation'. It is 20% of the International GCSE - 12% in Component 1, 8% in Component 2 or 3 - and 30 raw marks: 18 in the transactional task and 12 in the imaginative one. Both components' content overviews require students to 'use spelling, punctuation and grammar accurately', and the qualification aims require students to 'write effectively and coherently using Standard English appropriately' and to 'use grammar correctly, punctuate and spell accurately'. The levels look at expression and ordering of information and ideas, vocabulary and spelling, paragraphing, punctuation and sentence structures. Notice that range and accuracy are marked together: a flawless piece of writing in one sentence shape sits as far from the top as an ambitious piece riddled with errors. A top-level answer varies sentence length on purpose, paragraphs where the thought turns rather than every five lines, punctuates beyond the comma and full stop, and spends the last five minutes proofreading, because every correction is an AO5 mark recovered.","code":"AO5"},{"h":"AO6: the endorsement that carries no marks and still appears on the certificate","t":"AO6 covers speaking and listening: 'demonstrate presentation skills in a formal setting; listen and respond appropriately to spoken language, including to questions and feedback to presentations; use spoken Standard English effectively in speeches and presentations'. Its weighting is given as 'Not applicable' because the optional Spoken Language endorsement, 4EA1/E, carries no marks towards the International GCSE. It is graded Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction, a student must meet all the criteria for a grade to be awarded it, and the grade is reported separately on the certificate. The presentation is prepared, lasts no longer than ten minutes and is given to an audience that must always include the teacher. A student aiming at the top does not treat the endorsement as a formality: they prepare a formal register rather than a chatty one, build in the handling of questions because responding to questions and feedback is part of the objective, and remember that all the criteria for a grade must be met, not most of them.","code":"AO6"},{"h":"The aims: read widely, read critically, write accurately","t":"The specification says the aims and objectives are to enable students to 'read a wide range of texts fluently and with good understanding'; 'read critically and use knowledge gained from wide reading to inform and improve their own writing'; 'write effectively and coherently using Standard English appropriately'; 'use grammar correctly, punctuate and spell accurately'; 'acquire and apply a wide vocabulary alongside knowledge and understanding of grammatical terminology, and linguistic conventions for reading, writing and spoken language'; and 'listen to and understand spoken language, and use spoken Standard English effectively'. The second aim is the one students ignore and examiners can see: reading is described as the raw material of writing, not as a separate half of the course. A top-level answer to a writing task shows the reading behind it - a structural trick borrowed from an article, a sentence shape borrowed from a memoir, a vocabulary that belongs to the form rather than to the student.","code":"Qualification aims and objectives"},{"h":"The three abilities the design is built to develop","t":"Under Key qualification features, in the section headed Broad and deep development of students' skills, the specification says that students develop the ability to 'read and respond to material from a variety of sources'; to 'make comparisons between texts and analyse the ways in which writers achieve their effects'; and to 'construct and convey meaning in written language, matching style to audience and purpose'. It also says that throughout the qualification students 'should develop the skills of interpretation and analysis' in the non-fiction strand and 'the skills of inference and analysis' in the poetry and prose strand. Line those up with the objectives and the paper makes sense: source variety feeds AO1, comparison and effects feed AO2 and AO3, matching style to audience and purpose feeds AO4. A top-level answer treats these as the revision plan rather than the rubric: practise on unfamiliar sources, practise comparing rather than summarising, and practise writing the same content twice for two different readers.","code":"Broad and deep development of students’ skills"}],"examples":[{"title":"Turning retelling into interpretation for AO1","setup":"Passage A, The Wednesday Round, a memoir extract written for this room. My grandfather delivered bread in a van with no heater and a door that had to be held shut with a length of orange baler twine. On Wednesdays he took me. We left at half past four, and the town at that hour was not the town I knew: the chip shop dark, the bus shelter empty, a single lit window above the bank where someone was either up very early or had not been to bed. He did not talk much. He named things instead. That is the Hendersons. That is where the mill was. That is the corner your mother came off her bike. By six the sky had gone the colour of weak tea and the streets had filled with people I had never once considered: a man walking twelve dogs, two nurses arguing about a film, a woman in a dressing gown carrying a chair. I had thought the world woke up when I did. It had been going for hours without me, and it did not need me at all, and I have never got over how happy that made me. The task, in the style of Component 1 Question 1, AO1 only, 11 marks: select and interpret what you understand about the writer's feelings towards these early mornings. Support each point with a quotation or a reference to a specific moment.","steps":["Reject the retelling answer first: saying the boy went out in a van with his grandfather scores at Level 1 because nothing has been interpreted, so turn each detail into a claim about feeling before writing it down.","Interpret the twine and the missing heater as affection rather than hardship: the discomfort is remembered fondly, and the detail survives because the round mattered, not because the van was bad.","Read the naming of places - the Hendersons, the mill, the corner - as inherited knowledge, so the silence between the two of them is companionable and the grandfather is teaching a map of the family rather than making conversation.","Use the list of strangers at six to show the world widening: the boy who had never once considered these people is being shown that ordinary life is crowded with other lives.","Interpret the closing admission as the point of the whole piece: discovering that the world did not need him makes him happy, so the feeling is relief and wonder at being small, and that reading turns a nice anecdote into a perspective."],"cues":["What separates a point that scores from a sentence that only retells?","Is the broken van a complaint or a keepsake, and how can you tell?","Why does the grandfather name places instead of chatting?","What has changed for the boy between half past four and six?","Why is being unnecessary a happy discovery here?"]},{"title":"Climbing the AO2 ladder on one sentence","setup":"Passage B, The Case for Getting Up in the Dark, an opinion column written for this room. Nobody who claims to be a morning person is telling you the whole truth. What they have found out is simply this: the first hour of the day is the only hour nobody else wants. The phone is silent. The inbox is a closed door. Nobody has yet had an idea that requires your attendance at a meeting about it. You are, for sixty minutes, unreachable, and being unreachable is the last luxury that money cannot buy. I am not going to pretend the alarm is pleasant. It is not. It is a small daily bereavement. But consider the alternative. You can begin the day already answering, already behind, already somebody else's afternoon. Or you can begin it in the dark, with a kettle, in a house that is still asleep, doing the one thing you would never otherwise get to. The dark is not the price. The dark is the point. The task, in the style of Component 1 Question 2, AO2 only, 12 marks: analyse how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to persuade the reader that early rising is worth it. Work on the metaphor calling the alarm a small daily bereavement and on the two closing sentences.","steps":["Write the Level 1 version so you can recognise it: the writer uses a metaphor, which makes it more interesting. It identifies a device, comments generally, and proves nothing.","Move to a clear explanation: calling the alarm a bereavement admits that waking early feels like a loss, so the reader trusts the argument because the writer has conceded the obvious objection first.","Explore the word choice rather than the device label: bereavement belongs to grief and death, and shrinking it with small and daily makes the grief comic, so the concession is made and undercut in the same breath.","Turn to the structure of the ending, where two short declaratives repeat the same subject and swap the complement, so the argument is settled by symmetry rather than by evidence.","Finish with the effect on the whole piece: the column has argued in the voice of someone who admits the cost and then redefines it, and the reader is left agreeing with a redefinition they were never invited to test."],"cues":["What is missing from an answer that only names the device?","Why does admitting the alarm is unpleasant help the writer persuade?","Which two words shrink the grief, and what does that do to the tone?","What pattern do the last two sentences share, and what does the swap achieve?","What has the reader been persuaded of by the end, and how quietly?"]},{"title":"Building one AO3 paragraph that holds both texts","setup":"The two passages above are set side by side: Passage A, The Wednesday Round, a memoir of bread rounds with a grandfather that ends with the boy happy to find the world did not need him, and Passage B, The Case for Getting Up in the Dark, a column arguing that the first hour of the day is the only hour nobody else wants and that the dark is the point. The task, in the style of Component 1 Question 3, AO3 only, 22 marks: explore the links and connections between the writers' ideas and perspectives on early mornings, and how those ideas and perspectives are conveyed. Build a single paragraph rather than a whole answer.","steps":["Choose the shared idea before choosing any evidence: both writers value the early hour because nobody else is in it, so the paragraph is about solitude as a reward rather than about waking up.","State the difference inside that shared idea: the memoirist finds a world already crowded with other people, while the columnist finds an hour emptied of them, so one is delighted to be surplus and the other delighted to be unreachable.","Bring in Passage A's method: the discovery arrives through narrative and a list of strangers, so the perspective is earned slowly and offered as a memory the reader may share.","Set Passage B's method against it: the same value is asserted through direct address, a redefinition of a word and paired short sentences, so the perspective is argued at the reader rather than remembered for them.","Close the paragraph with a weighing: the column persuades faster because it tells the reader what to conclude, while the memoir persuades more durably because it lets the reader reach the conclusion a sentence before the writer states it."],"cues":["What must be fixed before you look for quotations in either text?","Where do the two writers part company inside that shared idea?","How does the memoir deliver its perspective to the reader?","How does the column deliver the same value differently?","Which half of AO3 is still unmarked until you weigh the two methods?"]},{"title":"Deciding an AO4 register before writing a word","setup":"A Component 1 Section B prompt in the style of the transactional task, 45 marks in total, of which AO4 carries 27 and AO5 carries 18: your local council is considering closing the town's only public library on Saturdays. Write the text of a speech to be given at a council meeting, persuading councillors to keep it open. You have five minutes before you start writing. Decide the three things AO4 marks before the content is planned.","steps":["Fix the reader first: councillors, not friends, so the register is formal, the argument budget-aware, and any joke has to be the kind a committee will forgive.","Fix the purpose next: to persuade towards a decision they can actually make, so the piece must ask for one specific thing rather than praise libraries in general.","Fix the form third: a speech is heard once, so it needs direct address, a signalled structure, repetition the ear can follow and a final line worth applauding.","Turn those three decisions into an opening that shows all of them at once: address the chamber, name the decision before the meeting, and promise a single argument rather than a list.","Protect AO5 inside the plan: mark where the paragraph breaks fall, plan one deliberately short sentence for the turn, and keep the last few minutes for proofreading."],"cues":["Who is in the room, and what does that rule out?","What exactly are you asking them to do?","What does a piece written to be heard need that an essay does not?","What must the first twenty seconds prove about the writer?","Which objective is quietly lost if you write to the last second?"]},{"title":"Repairing a paragraph for AO5","setup":"A student's paragraph from an imaginative writing task, Component 2 Question 2, where AO4 carries 18 marks and AO5 carries 12. The paragraph reads: I walked into the house and it was quiet and I could hear the clock and the clock was loud and I felt scared and I walked into the kitchen and there was a cup on the table and it was still warm and I new someone had been there and I was on my own and I didnt know what to do. The task: identify what is capping the AO5 mark and rewrite the paragraph so that the range is deliberate.","steps":["Diagnose the sentence structure first: the whole paragraph is a single chain of clauses joined by and, so there is no range to reward however tense the content is.","Correct the spelling and punctuation errors: new should be knew, didnt needs its apostrophe, and the run-on needs full stops rather than more conjunctions.","Rebuild the shape so length carries meaning: a long sentence to walk through the house, then a very short one at the discovery, so the reader feels the stop.","Use punctuation the levels can see, such as a colon before the detail that changes everything and a dash for the afterthought, rather than commas throughout.","Write the repaired version: The house was quiet in the way that houses are not supposed to be quiet, and the clock in the hall sounded like something being counted down. In the kitchen there was a cup on the table. I touched it: still warm. Somebody had been standing where I was standing - minutes ago, no more - and I did not know whether to call out or leave."],"cues":["What single habit is holding the whole paragraph at a low level?","Which three technical errors would an examiner mark first?","Where should the shortest sentence fall, and why there?","Which punctuation marks are missing from the student's range?","What does the rewrite keep from the original, and what does it drop?"]}],"check":[{"q":"Which assessment objective is examined in only one question in the whole qualification, and which question is it?","a":"AO3, in the 22-mark Question 3 of Component 1; there are no AO3 marks in Component 2 or Component 3."},{"q":"Component 2 Question 1 is worth 30 marks. How are they split between the objectives?","a":"AO1 12 marks and AO2 18 marks, so two thirds of the answer must be about how the writer uses language and structure."},{"q":"How many of the 150 raw marks in the qualification are writing marks?","a":"75 - AO4 45 and AO5 30 - which is exactly half the qualification."},{"q":"What does the specification mean by a textual reference, as opposed to an example?","a":"A textual reference is any supporting evidence from the text, whether a direct quotation or a reference to a specific moment or event; an example is a specific feature of language or structure cited from the text."}]},"room":{"facts":["AO1 (select and interpret) is 15% of the International GCSE - 7% in Component 1, 8% in Component 2 or 3 - and 23 raw marks: 11 at Component 1 Question 1 and 12 inside Component 2 or 3 Question 1.","AO2 (analyse linguistic and structural devices and their effects) is 20% and 30 raw marks: 12 at Component 1 Question 2 and 18 inside Component 2 or 3 Question 1 - the largest reading tariff.","AO3 (explore links and connections) is 15% and 22 raw marks, all of them at Component 1 Question 3; Component 2 and Component 3 carry no AO3 marks.","AO4 (communicate effectively and imaginatively, adapting form, tone and register) is 30%, the largest objective, and 45 raw marks: 27 in the transactional task and 18 in the imaginative task.","AO5 (clarity, vocabulary, sentence structures, paragraphing, spelling, grammar and punctuation) is 20% and 30 raw marks: 18 in the transactional task and 12 in the imaginative task.","AO6 has no weighting: the optional Spoken Language endorsement 4EA1/E is graded Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction and reported separately on the certificate.","The qualification carries 150 raw marks: Component 1 has 90 (11 + 12 + 22 + 45) and Component 2 or Component 3 has 60 (30 + 30). Reading and writing carry 75 each."],"questions":[{"q":"State the assessment objective assessed by Question 3 of Component 1, give its raw mark tariff, and state its percentage weighting in the International GCSE.","a":"AO3 - explore links and connections between writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed; 22 raw marks; 15% of the qualification.","sol":"Question 3 of Component 1 assesses AO3 only: explore links and connections between writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed. It carries 22 raw marks, the largest single question tariff in Section A, and AO3 is worth 15% of the International GCSE. All of that 15% sits in this one question, because Component 2 and Component 3 assess no AO3 at all.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Question 3 is the long reading answer that works across two texts at once.","Only one objective in the qualification is examined in a single question - find the one with no marks in Component 2 or 3.","Give three things: the objective's letter and wording, its raw marks, and its percentage."],"codes":["AO3"]},{"q":"State how many raw marks AO4 and AO5 carry between them, and how those marks are divided between the transactional writing task and the imaginative writing task.","a":"75 raw marks in total: AO4 45 (27 transactional, 18 imaginative) and AO5 30 (18 transactional, 12 imaginative).","sol":"AO4 carries 45 raw marks and AO5 carries 30, so writing is worth 75 raw marks of the 150 in the qualification - exactly half. The transactional task in Component 1 Section B carries 45 of those marks, split AO4 27 and AO5 18. The imaginative task, Component 2 Question 2 or Component 3 Assignment B, carries 30, split AO4 18 and AO5 12. In both tasks AO4 is worth more than AO5, so matching form, tone and register to the audience always outweighs accuracy - but never replaces it.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Writing is assessed by two objectives, one about the reader and one about accuracy; find their percentage weightings first.","A percentage of the qualification converts to a share of its raw mark total, and the two writing tasks are not the same size.","Give four figures - each writing objective in the transactional task, then each in the imaginative task - and a total."],"codes":["AO4","AO5"]},{"q":"Define, in the specification's own terms, what counts as a textual reference and what counts as an example, and state why the difference matters when you are answering a question worth AO1 and AO2 marks together.","a":"A textual reference is any reference to supporting evidence from the text - a direct quotation or a reference to a specific moment or event; an example is citing a specific feature of language and/or structure. References support AO1 points; examples support AO2 points.","sol":"The specification defines a textual reference as any reference to supporting evidence from the text, meaning either a direct quotation or a reference to a specific moment or event within it. An example is defined as citing specific examples of features of language and/or structure from the text. The difference matters in Component 2 or 3 Question 1, where 12 marks are AO1 and 18 are AO2: a reference to a moment can support an interpretation and earn AO1, but AO2 credit needs the language or structural feature itself to be quoted or located, because the levels reward selection of devices and comment on their effects.","m":4,"d":1,"hints":["Both terms are defined in the specification's guidance on the reading assessment grids.","One definition is about evidence for what the text says; the other is about evidence for how it is written.","Finish by naming the two objectives each type of support feeds."],"codes":["AO1","AO2"]},{"q":"State which assessment objective has no weighting in the International GCSE, and explain how a student's performance in it is recorded.","a":"AO6 (speaking and listening). Its weighting is Not applicable: the optional Spoken Language endorsement 4EA1/E is graded Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction and appears as a separately reported grade on the certificate.","sol":"AO6 covers speaking and listening: presentation skills in a formal setting, listening and responding appropriately to spoken language including questions and feedback, and the effective use of spoken Standard English. Its weighting is given as Not applicable because the Spoken Language endorsement, 4EA1/E, is optional and carries no marks towards the International GCSE. Students are graded Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction, must meet all the criteria for a grade to be awarded it, and the grade is reported separately on the certificate rather than folded into the 9 to 1 grade.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["Five objectives carry percentages; one carries the phrase Not applicable.","The endorsement has its own code ending in E and its own four-point scale.","Name the four possible outcomes and say where they appear."],"codes":["AO6"]},{"q":"The specification's Key qualification features describe the broad and deep development of students' skills. State the three abilities it says students develop, and match each to the assessment objective it feeds.","a":"Read and respond to material from a variety of sources (AO1); make comparisons between texts and analyse the ways in which writers achieve their effects (AO3 and AO2); construct and convey meaning in written language, matching style to audience and purpose (AO4).","sol":"The specification says students develop the ability to read and respond to material from a variety of sources, which is the AO1 skill of selecting and interpreting across unfamiliar texts; to make comparisons between texts and analyse the ways in which writers achieve their effects, which joins AO3's links and connections to AO2's analysis of devices and effects; and to construct and convey meaning in written language, matching style to audience and purpose, which is AO4 in almost the same words. The same section adds that students should develop the skills of interpretation and analysis in non-fiction and the skills of inference and analysis in poetry and prose.","m":3,"d":1,"hints":["The three abilities are listed after the words for example in that section of the specification.","One is about sources, one about comparison and effects, one about audience and purpose.","Give each ability and then the objective whose wording it echoes."],"codes":["Broad and deep development of students’ skills"]},{"q":"Read this extract, written for this room. Passage A, The Wednesday Round. My grandfather delivered bread in a van with no heater and a door that had to be held shut with a length of orange baler twine. On Wednesdays he took me. We left at half past four, and the town at that hour was not the town I knew: the chip shop dark, the bus shelter empty, a single lit window above the bank where someone was either up very early or had not been to bed. He did not talk much. He named things instead. That is the Hendersons. That is where the mill was. That is the corner your mother came off her bike. By six the sky had gone the colour of weak tea and the streets had filled with people I had never once considered: a man walking twelve dogs, two nurses arguing about a film, a woman in a dressing gown carrying a chair. I had thought the world woke up when I did. It had been going for hours without me, and it did not need me at all, and I have never got over how happy that made me. In the style of Component 1 Question 1 (AO1 only, 11 marks): read and understand the extract, selecting and interpreting what you learn about the writer's relationship with his grandfather and with the town. Support each point with a quotation or a reference to a specific moment.","a":"Affection remembered through discomfort (the heaterless van, the twine); a companionable silence in which the grandfather teaches the town by naming it; the boy's inherited map of family history; the town revealed as unfamiliar and then crowded; a final perspective of happy insignificance.","sol":"The relationship is one of inherited knowledge rather than conversation. The grandfather does not talk much but names things instead, and what he names is not the town's landmarks but the family's: the Hendersons, the site of the mill, and the corner where the writer's mother came off her bike. Naming is how affection is expressed here, and the writer's memory of it suggests he understood that even as a child. The van itself, with no heater and a door held shut with orange baler twine, is remembered with fondness rather than complaint, which tells us the discomfort was part of the privilege of being taken. The writer's relationship with the town changes inside the extract. At half past four it is not the town I knew, and it is described by what is absent - the chip shop dark, the bus shelter empty - with a single lit window offering a small mystery. By six it has filled with people the boy had never once considered, and the specificity of a man walking twelve dogs or two nurses arguing about a film suggests a world of lives running in parallel with his own. The final interpretation is the most important one. He had thought the world woke up when I did, and finding that it had been going for hours without him could have been humiliating; instead he has never got over how happy that made me. The perspective, then, is gratitude for being made small - the grandfather gave him not company but proportion.","m":11,"d":2,"hints":["AO1 pays for interpretation, so every point should say what a detail means, not what happened.","Work through the extract in order and ask of each detail what it reveals about the man, the boy, or the town.","Open with a claim about the relationship rather than a summary of the journey, then support it with the shortest reference that proves it."],"codes":["AO1"]},{"q":"Read this extract, written for this room. Passage B, The Case for Getting Up in the Dark. Nobody who claims to be a morning person is telling you the whole truth. What they have found out is simply this: the first hour of the day is the only hour nobody else wants. The phone is silent. The inbox is a closed door. Nobody has yet had an idea that requires your attendance at a meeting about it. You are, for sixty minutes, unreachable, and being unreachable is the last luxury that money cannot buy. I am not going to pretend the alarm is pleasant. It is not. It is a small daily bereavement. But consider the alternative. You can begin the day already answering, already behind, already somebody else's afternoon. Or you can begin it in the dark, with a kettle, in a house that is still asleep, doing the one thing you would never otherwise get to. The dark is not the price. The dark is the point. In the style of Component 1 Question 2 (AO2 only, 12 marks): analyse how the writer uses linguistic and structural devices to persuade the reader that rising early is worth it.","a":"Opening accusation and colon that withholds the claim; the inbox as a closed door; triple repetition of already; the bereavement metaphor shrunk by small and daily; the antithetical closing pair that swaps price for point.","sol":"The column opens with an accusation - nobody is telling you the whole truth - which positions the writer as someone about to reveal a secret, so the reader reads on to be let in. The colon after simply this delays the claim by a beat, and what follows is framed as a discovery rather than an opinion. The metaphors are chosen to make an abstract benefit physical: the inbox is a closed door, which turns the absence of work into a room the reader is safely inside. The boldest choice is calling the alarm a small daily bereavement. Bereavement belongs to grief and death, so the writer concedes that early rising genuinely costs something and earns the reader's trust; but shrinking the grief with small and daily makes the concession comic, so the objection is admitted and disarmed in the same breath. Structurally, the argument is built on a pair of alternatives. The triple repetition of already - already answering, already behind, already somebody else's afternoon - piles up the cost of the ordinary morning, and the rhythm makes lateness feel cumulative and inevitable. Against it stands a sentence of quiet, concrete nouns: the dark, a kettle, a sleeping house. The ending is two short declaratives with the same subject and a swapped complement, so the dark is not the price, the dark is the point. Nothing has been proved; the symmetry itself performs the reversal, and the reader accepts a redefinition rather than an argument.","m":12,"d":2,"hints":["AO2 marks nothing for naming a device - the marks are for the effect on the reader at that moment.","Choose two or three methods you can say something unobvious about, including at least one structural pattern.","Open on the boldest word choice in the extract and explain what the word normally belongs to before saying what it does here."],"codes":["AO2"]},{"q":"In the style of Component 1 Section B, Questions 4 and 5, Transactional Writing (45 marks: AO4 27, AO5 18; 45 minutes advised). Your local council is considering closing the town's only public library on Saturdays in order to save money. Write the text of a speech to be given at a council meeting, persuading the councillors to keep the library open on Saturdays.","a":"A speech in a formal but warm register, addressed to councillors, conceding the budget pressure, built on one developed argument with concrete local detail, using direct address, signposting and repetition, and ending with a specific request rather than a general plea.","sol":"A top-level answer begins by showing it knows the room. Madam Chair, councillors - thank you for hearing me. I want to make one argument this evening, not ten, and I want to begin by agreeing with you: the budget you are working from is not a choice you made. The opening establishes the form, the audience and a conciliatory register, which is where AO4 marks are won. The body develops a single argument with concrete detail. On Saturdays that library is not a building full of books; it is the only warm room in this town that costs nothing to sit in. Last Saturday the study tables were full of students whose homes are full of younger brothers. The speech then handles the obvious objection in the councillors' own language of cost, offering a reduced-hours compromise rather than demanding everything, because a speech that asks for something achievable is realising its purpose. Structurally it uses the resources of a text written to be heard: direct address, a rule of three, one rhetorical question, and short paragraphs that the ear can follow. It closes on a specific request and a memorable final line - not a plea to love libraries in general but a request to reinstate six hours on a Saturday, and a last sentence that returns to the warm room. For AO5, the writing varies sentence length deliberately, paragraphs at the turns of the argument, punctuates with colons and dashes as well as commas, spells the formal vocabulary correctly, and is proofread in the final minutes.","m":45,"d":2,"hints":["AO4 is worth more than half these marks, so decide the reader, the purpose and the form before you plan the content.","A speech is heard once: it needs direct address, signposting, repetition the ear can hold, and a final line worth remembering.","Open by naming your audience and conceding something real, so the councillors are listening before you ask for anything."],"codes":["AO4","AO5"]},{"q":"In the style of Component 2 Section B, Question 2, Imaginative Writing (30 marks: AO4 18, AO5 12; 45 minutes advised). Write about a time when someone arrived unexpectedly. Your writing may be about real or imagined experiences, and its purpose should be to narrate, describe or entertain.","a":"A controlled shape (clear opening, developed middle, deliberate ending), a sustained voice, selective sensory detail rather than plot, and punctuation and sentence lengths varied for effect.","sol":"A top-level answer chooses a small event and gives it a shape. The opening lands the reader inside a moment rather than explaining the situation: The knock came during the part of the evening when nobody knocks. The middle develops rather than accumulates - not a sequence of events but a deepening of one, so the narrator's reaction is described in physical detail (the tea going cold, counting the steps to the door) while the arrival itself is withheld. Imagery is selective and specific: an aunt who has not visited in years is described through a coat that smells of another house. The ending is deliberate and returns to the opening image with the meaning changed, so the piece closes rather than stops. This shape and sustained register is where the AO4 marks sit. For AO5 the writing varies sentence structure with intent: a long sentence for the walk to the door, a three-word sentence for the face on the step. It paragraphs at the turns, uses a colon to introduce the detail that changes everything and a dash for an afterthought, keeps to one tense, and spells ambitious vocabulary correctly because ambition without accuracy is capped. A student writing this well plans for five minutes, writes for thirty-five and proofreads for five.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["The specification sets the purpose as to narrate, describe or entertain, so a plot summary is not the task.","Shape is what AO4 rewards here: decide the last image before you write the first sentence.","Begin inside the moment rather than explaining who everyone is, and let one withheld detail carry the tension."],"codes":["AO4","AO5"]},{"q":"Read this extract, written for this room. Passage C, The Last Load, the closing section of a short story written for this room to stand in for a prose text from Part 2 of the anthology. The van took three trips and the house took four hours, and at the end of it my mother stood in the middle of the front room and could not decide where to put her hands. Without furniture the room was larger and meaner than it had ever been. There were squares on the wallpaper where the pictures had hung, paler than the paper around them, so that the wall looked like a page with the important lines cut out. A radiator ticked. Somewhere above us a tap that had dripped for eleven years went on dripping for whoever came next. She said we should go. She did not go. She walked once round the room, touching the window ledge, the door frame, the cold hard shoulder of the fireplace, in the way you check a child for a temperature. Then she straightened, and the flat voice she uses for buses and doctors came back. Right, she said. That's that. Lock the door, love. In the style of Component 2 Section A, Question 1 (30 marks: AO1 12, AO2 18; 45 minutes advised): discuss how the writer uses language and structure to present the mother's feelings about leaving the house. Include textual references to illustrate the points you make.","a":"AO2 (18): the room built out of absence and the simile of a page with the important lines cut out; the three-word sentence A radiator ticked; the contradiction of She said we should go, She did not go; the fireplace personified as a cold hard shoulder; the comparison with checking a child for a temperature; the clipped closing dialogue. AO1 (12): grief managed by doing rather than saying, and tenderness spent on the child instead of the house.","sol":"The extract is built out of absence, and the structure delivers the absence before it names any feeling. It opens on logistics - three trips, four hours - so the reader begins in work rather than in emotion, and the mother appears at the exact moment the work runs out: she stands in the middle of the front room and cannot decide where to put her hands. The detail is chosen because hands are what she has been using all day, and the emptied room leaves grief with nothing to do. The room is then described by what has been taken from it. The pale squares where the pictures hung make the wall look like a page with the important lines cut out, a simile that turns the house into something readable and then censors it, so what has been lost is meaning rather than furniture. Around that image the writer sets two small continuing sounds. A radiator ticked is a three-word sentence that stops the paragraph dead, and the tap that has dripped for eleven years will go on dripping for whoever came next - the house is indifferent to the leaving, which is the thought the mother will not say aloud. The turn is written as a contradiction: She said we should go, followed immediately by She did not go. Two short sentences with the same subject and opposite verbs put the reader inside the gap between deciding and being able to. What follows is the most careful writing in the extract. She touches the window ledge, the door frame and the cold hard shoulder of the fireplace, a personification that lends the house a body and lets the idiom of the cold shoulder suggest that the house is already refusing her; and comparing her touch to the way you check a child for a temperature makes the farewell maternal, so she handles the house the way she has handled her family. The presentation, then, is of grief managed by doing rather than saying. The flat voice she uses for buses and doctors is a voice kept for occasions that must be got through, and its return closes the mourning she has allowed herself. The last lines are clipped - Right, That's that - and the final word of the extract is an endearment: the tenderness she cannot spend on the house is spent on her child instead, and the door she asks to have locked is the door frame she has just touched, so the ending closes the extract rather than merely stopping it.","m":30,"d":2,"hints":["Two thirds of these marks are for method, so read the extract for how it is assembled before you read it for what happens.","Follow the order the writer releases things in: what the room is made of, what interrupts it, what the mother does, how it ends.","Begin with what the room is described by rather than what it contains, and say what that does to the reader."],"codes":["AO1","AO2"]},{"q":"In the style of Component 1 Section A, Question 3 (AO3 only, 22 marks), using Passage A, The Wednesday Round, and Passage B, The Case for Getting Up in the Dark, both printed above: explore the links and connections between the writers' ideas and perspectives on early mornings, as well as how these are conveyed.","a":"Shared value of the hour before the world is watching, but the memoirist finds it crowded and the columnist finds it empty; memoir conveys its perspective through narrative, listing and a cumulative ending, the column through direct address, a shrunken metaphor and antithesis; the memoir lets the reader arrive, the column tells them where to arrive.","sol":"Both writers treat the early morning as a privilege, but they value opposite things in it. For the memoirist the reward is company he had never noticed - the streets fill with people he had never once considered, and the pleasure is in discovering he is one small life among many. For the columnist the reward is the absence of everyone: the first hour is the only hour nobody else wants, and to be unreachable is the last luxury. The link is solitude as freedom; the difference is whether that freedom comes from joining the world early or escaping it. How the two perspectives are conveyed differs just as sharply. The memoir works by narrative and accumulation. It gives the reader a closed town described in negatives, then a widening list of strangers, then a long final sentence that adds clause to clause until the admission that the world did not need him arrives as the reader's own conclusion a beat before it is stated. The column works by assertion. It addresses the reader directly, redefines a word - the alarm as a small daily bereavement, the grief admitted then shrunk by two adjectives - and settles its case with a pair of short symmetrical sentences in which the dark stops being the price and becomes the point. Both writers concede a cost before claiming a reward: the memoirist accepts the cold van held shut with twine, the columnist accepts that the alarm is unpleasant. That shared move is what makes both perspectives persuasive. Weighed against each other, the column persuades faster because it hands the reader its conclusions, while the memoir persuades more durably because it makes the reader do the last step of the thinking, and a perspective the reader has reached is harder to argue with than one they have been given.","m":22,"d":3,"hints":["AO3 has two halves: what the writers think, and how that thinking is conveyed - an answer missing either half is capped.","Build each paragraph on one shared idea with both texts inside it, rather than writing about one text and then the other.","Start by naming what both writers value in the early hour, then immediately say where they part company inside that shared value."],"codes":["AO3"]},{"q":"In the style of Component 3, Assignment A: Poetry and Prose Texts (30 marks: AO1 12, AO2 18; approximately 1200 words, no time limit): Discuss how the writers use language and structure to present the idea of home in the three texts you have chosen from Part 2 of the anthology, at least one of them a poetry text and at least one a prose text. Discuss the ideas and perspectives of the writers about the chosen focus, discuss how they use language and structure to achieve their effects, and include textual references to illustrate the points you make. Set out the response you would write.","a":"Three Part 2 texts, at least one poetry and one prose; sections organised by aspects of the focus with more than one text inside each; every point built on a named method with a textual reference and an explanation of its effect (AO2 18) alongside interpretation of the writers' ideas and perspectives (AO1 12); a conclusion that weighs the three presentations.","sol":"A top-level response is organised by the focus, not by the texts. The opening states what the three writers share about home and where they part company, and names the line of argument the assignment will follow, so the reader knows there is a position before any text is discussed. Each section then takes one aspect of the focus - home as a place, home as the people in it, home as something lost - and brings at least two of the three texts into that section, so the comparison happens inside the paragraphs rather than in a summary at the end. Because 18 of the 30 marks are AO2, every section is built on a method. From the poetry text, form and structure carry the argument: where the lines break, what the stanza shape does to the pace, whether a rhyme closes a thought or leaves it open, and what the poem withholds until its last line. From the prose text, narration carries it: who is telling the story, what the narrator does not say, how sentence length changes at the turn, and how the order of information controls what the reader knows. Each method is illustrated with a short textual reference and followed by a developed explanation of the effect on the reader, and then by a sentence that says what that effect reveals about the writer's perspective - which is where the AO1 marks are earned, because interpretation is being asked for and not summary. The conclusion weighs rather than repeats: which writer's method presses their view of home on the reader hardest, and what the three together say about the focus. With no time limit and about 1200 words, the response is planned, drafted, cut and proofread; the mix of at least one poetry text and one prose text is checked before anything is written, because a response that discusses three poems has not met the requirement.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["Two thirds of these marks are for how the writers write, so the sections should be named after methods rather than after texts.","Decide the aspects of the focus first, and test each one by asking whether more than one of your texts can live inside it.","Check the specification's requirement about the kinds of text before you choose them, then open on the shared idea and the fault line inside it."],"codes":["AO1","AO2"]},{"q":"In the style of Component 3, Assignment B: Imaginative Writing (30 marks: AO4 18, AO5 12), a piece of approximately 700 words with no time limit: write a piece of imaginative writing suggested by a house on the morning it is emptied. The purpose should be to narrate, describe or entertain, and you may write about real or imagined experiences.","a":"A piece whose purpose is plainly to narrate, describe or entertain; one small moment given a deliberate shape and a sustained voice; selective concrete detail rather than plot; sentence structures and punctuation varied for particular effects; and, because there is no time limit, redrafted and proofread so that the accuracy matches the ambition.","sol":"A top-level piece chooses one moment and refuses to summarise a day. The opening puts the reader inside the moment rather than explaining the situation - a first line about the sound a room makes when the furniture has gone - and the situation is then supplied in passing, so exposition never has a paragraph of its own. The middle deepens rather than accumulates: one object is followed through the piece, perhaps a curtain hook left in the rail, so the reader has something to measure the loss against. Voice is sustained: if the narrator is a child, the observations stay a child's and the vocabulary does not slip into an adult's summary of the day. The ending is decided before the first sentence is written, and it returns to the opening image with its meaning changed, so the piece closes rather than stops. That shape, that control of register and that sense of a reader being carried are where the 18 AO4 marks sit. The 12 AO5 marks are earned by making the range deliberate: a long sentence for walking through the rooms and a three-word sentence for the moment the door shuts, paragraph breaks at the turns of feeling rather than every few lines, a colon to introduce the detail that changes everything, a dash for the afterthought, one tense held throughout, and ambitious vocabulary spelled correctly. The advantage of this route is time: the word count is advisory and there is no penalty for exceeding it, so the piece can be drafted, cut by a tenth and proofread twice, which means technical errors are no longer forgivable as exam pressure.","m":30,"d":3,"hints":["The purpose the specification sets is to narrate, describe or entertain, so a summary of events is not the task.","Shape is what carries the larger tariff here: choose the last image before you choose the first sentence.","Pick one object in the emptied house and let it carry the feeling, so the reader has something to measure the change against."],"codes":["AO4","AO5"]},{"q":"In the style of Component 1 Section B, Questions 4 and 5, Transactional Writing (45 marks: AO4 27, AO5 18; 45 minutes advised). Your old primary school has asked former pupils to help prepare its Year 6 class for secondary school. Write the text of a guide for those pupils, telling them what the first term is really like and how to manage it.","a":"A guide for ten and eleven year olds: second-person address, short signposted sections in the order the reader will need them, practical and concrete advice rather than platitudes, a warm register that never patronises, and accurate, varied writing with paragraphing that marks each new stage.","sol":"A top-level answer fixes the reader before the content: children who are excited and frightened in the same breath, so the register is warm and direct, the advice is concrete enough to act on, and nothing is written down to them. The form is visible from the first line. A guide is read in pieces rather than straight through, so it is organised into short signposted sections - the first morning, finding your way, the homework, the people - each opening with a sentence that says what the section is for. The opening earns trust by conceding something true: Everyone tells you that secondary school is enormous. It is. What nobody tells you is that by the third week you will stop seeing it. Second person is held throughout, the imperative carries the advice itself, and one worked detail is given for each claim - not be organised but pack your bag the night before, because the corridor at half past eight is not the place to discover you have the wrong books. The guide anticipates the reader's real fears rather than the ones adults assume, handles the worst of them plainly, and ends on a single reassurance rather than a list. That realisation of purpose and of the expectations of the intended reader is where the 27 AO4 marks are won. For the 18 AO5 marks the writing varies its sentence structures on purpose - a long sentence to walk the reader through the first morning, a short one for the point that matters - paragraphs at each new stage, punctuates with colons and dashes as well as commas, keeps the vocabulary ambitious but spelled correctly, and leaves the last few minutes for proofreading.","m":45,"d":3,"hints":["The larger of the two objectives here is about the reader, so decide who is reading, what they are afraid of and what a guide has to look like before planning content.","A guide is not read straight through: think about how the reader finds the part they need, and what each section has to announce.","Turn one piece of general advice into something a Year 6 pupil could do tomorrow morning, and write that instead."],"codes":["AO4","AO5"]}],"exit":[{"q":"Which two assessment objectives are assessed in Component 2 Question 1, and how many marks does each carry?","a":"AO1, 12 marks, and AO2, 18 marks."},{"q":"How many raw marks does AO3 carry, and in which single question are they awarded?","a":"22 raw marks, all at Component 1 Section A Question 3."},{"q":"What percentage of the International GCSE is AO4, and what does it require?","a":"30% - communicate effectively and imaginatively, adapting form, tone and register of writing for specific purposes and audiences."},{"q":"What is the total raw mark for the qualification, and how does it divide between the two components?","a":"150 raw marks: 90 in Component 1 and 60 in Component 2 or Component 3."}]},"cards":[{"front":"AO1","back":"Read and understand a variety of texts, selecting and interpreting information, ideas and perspectives. 15% of the qualification; 23 raw marks - 11 at Component 1 Question 1, 12 inside Component 2 or 3 Question 1.","code":"AO1","open":false},{"front":"AO2","back":"Understand and analyse how writers use linguistic and structural devices to achieve their effects. 20%; 30 raw marks - 12 at Component 1 Question 2 and 18 inside Component 2 or 3 Question 1.","code":"AO2","open":false},{"front":"AO3","back":"Explore links and connections between writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed. 15%; 22 raw marks, all at Component 1 Question 3. No AO3 marks exist in Component 2 or 3.","code":"AO3","open":false},{"front":"AO4","back":"Communicate effectively and imaginatively, adapting form, tone and register of writing for specific purposes and audiences. 30%, the largest objective; 45 raw marks - 27 transactional, 18 imaginative.","code":"AO4","open":false},{"front":"AO5","back":"Write clearly, using a range of vocabulary and sentence structures, with appropriate paragraphing and accurate spelling, grammar and punctuation. 20%; 30 raw marks - 18 transactional, 12 imaginative.","code":"AO5","open":false},{"front":"AO6 and the Spoken Language endorsement","back":"Presentation skills in a formal setting, responding to spoken language and questions, and spoken Standard English. Weighting: Not applicable. Endorsement 4EA1/E is graded Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction and reported separately.","code":"AO6","open":false},{"front":"Textual reference (specification definition)","back":"Any reference to supporting evidence from the text - direct quotations or references to specific moments or events within the text.","code":"AO1","open":false},{"front":"Example (specification definition)","back":"Citing specific examples of features of language and/or structure from the text - the support AO2 credit depends on.","code":"AO2","open":false},{"front":"The AO2 level ladder out of 18","back":"1-3 limited identification, basic comment; 4-6 some devices, some comment; 7-10 clear explanations of effects; 11-14 thorough selection, detailed exploration; 15-18 discriminating selection, perceptive analysis.","code":"AO2","open":false},{"front":"The AO1 level ladder out of 12","back":"1-3 limited selection, basic understanding; 4-6 some selection and comment; 7-9 clear and relevant interpretation; 10-12 detailed and persuasive selection, discriminating quotations, convincing understanding.","code":"AO1","open":false},{"front":"Best fit marking","back":"Markers award within a range on a best fit basis, balancing strengths and weaknesses. An answer showing features of two levels is placed by professional judgement - for example a high Level 3 rather than a Level 4.","code":"AO2","open":false},{"front":"The writing share of the qualification","back":"AO4 45 plus AO5 30 equals 75 of the 150 raw marks - exactly half, and equal to the reading total of AO1 23, AO2 30 and AO3 22.","code":"AO4","open":false},{"front":"The three abilities under Broad and deep development of students' skills","back":"Read and respond to material from a variety of sources; make comparisons between texts and analyse the ways in which writers achieve their effects; construct and convey meaning in written language, matching style to audience and purpose.","code":"Broad and deep development of students’ skills","open":false},{"front":"The qualification aims","back":"Read widely and fluently; read critically to improve your own writing; write effectively in Standard English; use grammar, punctuation and spelling accurately; acquire a wide vocabulary and grammatical terminology; understand and use spoken Standard English.","code":"Qualification aims and objectives","open":false},{"front":"One way to move an AO2 paragraph from clear explanation into perceptive analysis","back":"Choose a smaller, less obvious piece of evidence than the one everyone quotes; analyse a structural choice - an order, a break, a pattern - rather than a named device; explain why the writer needed that method at that point rather than elsewhere.","code":"AO2","open":true}],"extras":[{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 5 (AO2, 15-18 of 18): Passage A, The Wednesday Round","items":["The task: discuss how the writer uses language and structure to present the boy's discovery of the adult world.","Point: the writer withholds the adult world before he reveals it, so the discovery is staged structurally rather than announced.","Evidence: the town at half past four is built from negatives - the chip shop dark, the bus shelter empty - with one unexplained lit window above the bank.","Analysis of method: describing a place by what is missing forces the reader to assemble it from absences, which is exactly the boy's own position, and the single window is the one piece of evidence that other lives are running without him. The writer refuses to explain it, offering instead the alternatives of someone up very early or not yet to bed, so the adult world enters the passage as an unanswered question.","Development to structure: that question is answered by the list two paragraphs later, where the strangers arrive in ascending eccentricity - a man walking twelve dogs, two nurses arguing about a film, a woman in a dressing gown carrying a chair - so the accumulation itself performs the widening of the boy's world and the reader feels the street filling in real time.","Mini-judgement: the presentation of discovery is therefore structural before it is stated; by the time the writer admits the world did not need him, the passage has already proved it, which is why the final clause reads as relief rather than as self-pity.","Why this is Level 5: the selection is discriminating - a negative catalogue and an ordered list rather than the obvious image of the sky - and the analysis explores the effect of each choice on the reader and links it to the shape of the whole extract."]},{"kind":"paragraph","title":"Model paragraph - Level 2 (AO2, 4-6 of 18): the same task, written weakly","items":["Point: the writer uses lots of language techniques to show the boy discovering the adult world.","Evidence: he uses a metaphor when he says the sky had gone the colour of weak tea, and he uses a list of people in the street.","Analysis offered: this makes it more descriptive and helps the reader picture the scene, and the list shows there were lots of people about, which makes it interesting.","Mini-judgement offered: overall the writer uses good language to describe the morning.","Why this is only Level 2: the devices are identified and the examples are appropriate, so it is not Level 1, but the comment is basic and general - helps the reader picture it and makes it interesting could be written about any text at all.","What is missing: no explanation of why weak tea, a domestic and unromantic image, is the right comparison for this particular sky; no attention to the order of the list or to why it ends with the woman carrying a chair; no link between the effect and the argument of the extract. The label is at least correct - the comparison is implied, with no like or as, so it is a metaphor and not a simile - but naming a device accurately is the floor of AO2, not the ceiling.","The repair in one move: after each example, answer the question so what for this text - what does this choice do to the reader here, and why did the writer need it at this point?"]},{"kind":"factfile","title":"Fact file: the six assessment objectives, their weightings and where their marks sit","items":["AO1 - Read and understand a variety of texts, selecting and interpreting information, ideas and perspectives. 15% of the International GCSE (Component 1 7%, Component 2 or 3 8%).","AO1 raw marks: 23 in total - 11 at Component 1 Section A Question 1, and 12 of the 30 at Component 2 Section A Question 1 or Component 3 Assignment A.","AO2 - Understand and analyse how writers use linguistic and structural devices to achieve their effects. 20% (Component 1 8%, Component 2 or 3 12%).","AO2 raw marks: 30 in total - 12 at Component 1 Section A Question 2, and 18 of the 30 at Component 2 or 3 Question 1. This is the largest single objective tariff on the reading side.","AO3 - Explore links and connections between writers' ideas and perspectives, as well as how these are conveyed. 15%, entirely in Component 1: 22 raw marks at Section A Question 3, and zero marks in Component 2 or Component 3.","AO4 - Communicate effectively and imaginatively, adapting form, tone and register of writing for specific purposes and audiences. 30%, the largest objective (Component 1 18%, Component 2 or 3 12%); 45 raw marks - 27 in Transactional Writing, 18 in Imaginative Writing.","AO5 - Write clearly, using a range of vocabulary and sentence structures, with appropriate paragraphing and accurate spelling, grammar and punctuation. 20% (Component 1 12%, Component 2 or 3 8%); 30 raw marks - 18 in Transactional Writing, 12 in Imaginative Writing.","AO6 - Speaking and listening: presentation skills in a formal setting, listening and responding appropriately including to questions and feedback, and spoken Standard English. Weighting: Not applicable.","The Spoken Language endorsement (4EA1/E) carries no marks: graded Not Classified, Pass, Merit or Distinction, all criteria for a grade must be met, and it appears as a separately reported grade on the certificate. The presentation is prepared, lasts no longer than 10 minutes, and its audience must always include the teacher.","Question tariffs in Component 1: Question 1 - 11 marks (AO1); Question 2 - 12 marks (AO2); Question 3 - 22 marks (AO3); Questions 4 and 5 - 45 marks (AO4 27, AO5 18). Section A is 45 marks with 1 hour 30 minutes advised; Section B is 45 marks with 45 minutes advised.","Question tariffs in Component 2: Question 1 - 30 marks (AO1 12, AO2 18), 45 minutes advised; Question 2 - 30 marks (AO4 18, AO5 12), 45 minutes advised. Component 3 mirrors these as Assignment A (about 1200 words) and Assignment B (about 700 words), internally assessed and externally moderated.","Totals: 150 raw marks - Component 1 90, Component 2 or Component 3 60. Reading (AO1, AO2, AO3) is 75 marks and writing (AO4, AO5) is 75 marks. The qualification is graded 9 to 1; individual components are not graded. Some percentage figures have been rounded up or down.","The two kinds of support the specification defines: a textual reference is any reference to supporting evidence from the text, whether a direct quotation or a reference to a specific moment or event; an example is citing specific examples of features of language and/or structure from the text.","Marking is by levels on a best fit basis: markers balance strengths and weaknesses, and an answer showing features of more than one level is placed by professional judgement - for example a high Level 3 rather than a Level 4."]},{"kind":"plan","title":"Essay plan - Component 1 Section A Question 3: explore the links and connections between the writers' ideas and perspectives on early mornings, and how these are conveyed (22 marks, AO3)","items":["Decoding the question: explore is the command - not compare in a list but examine a relationship. Links and connections means the paragraphs must hold both texts at once. Ideas and perspectives means what each writer thinks and the attitude behind it. As well as how these are conveyed means half the credit depends on method, so every paragraph must name the technique or structural choice that carries the idea.","The texts: Passage A, The Wednesday Round, a memoir of a bread round with a grandfather, ending with the boy happy to discover the world did not need him; Passage B, The Case for Getting Up in the Dark, a column arguing that the first hour of the day is the only hour nobody else wants.","Timing: about 44 minutes pro rata within the 1 hour 30 minutes advised for Section A - roughly 6 minutes planning, 34 writing, 4 checking.","Opening (three sentences, no biography and no throat-clearing): state the shared perspective - both writers treat the early hour as a privilege - and the fault line inside it: the memoirist prizes the company he discovers, the columnist prizes the absence of everyone.","Paragraph 1 - the shared idea of the hour before the world is watching. Evidence from both: the town described by what is absent at half past four; the claim that the first hour is the one nobody else wants. Method: negative description in A against direct address and assertion in B. Weigh which method makes the value feel earned.","Paragraph 2 - the cost each writer concedes. A accepts the cold van held shut with twine; B admits the alarm is unpleasant and calls it a small daily bereavement. Method: fond understatement against a metaphor shrunk by two adjectives. Both concede before claiming, which is the clearest link between them.","Paragraph 3 - what the hour reveals. A fills the street with strangers in an ascending list; B empties the hour of everyone and fills it with objects instead - a kettle, a sleeping house. Method: cumulative listing against a pared-down noun catalogue, so one writer expands the world and the other shrinks it.","Paragraph 4 - how each writer closes the argument. A ends with a long cumulative sentence that reaches the admission gradually; B ends with two short symmetrical declaratives that swap price for point. Weigh: assertion persuades faster, narrative persuades more durably.","The evaluation thread running through all four paragraphs: which writer's method presses their perspective on the reader harder, and at what cost - the column wins speed and loses the reader's own thinking; the memoir wins the reader's agreement by letting them reach it first.","What the conclusion must say: name the connection in one sentence (both writers value the early hour as time that belongs to nobody else), state the real difference (solitude found among strangers against solitude found in their absence), and end on the methods rather than the ideas, because the objective marks how these are conveyed as well as what is conveyed.","What would cap this answer: writing about Passage A and then about Passage B; quoting from only one text in a paragraph; naming devices without saying what they do; or leaving the how until the final paragraph."]},{"kind":"notes","title":"What the specification prints in its level grids, and what it does not","items":["The grids are printed under Component 3, and those AO tariffs are exactly the tariffs of the two examined Component 2 questions. AO1 is marked out of 12 in four levels - 1-3, 4-6, 7-9, 10-12: limited selection with a basic understanding; some selection with some comment; clear and relevant selection, appropriate references and a clear understanding; detailed and persuasive selection, discriminating use of quotations and a convincing understanding of information, ideas and perspectives.","AO2 is marked out of 18 in five levels - 1-3, 4-6, 7-10, 11-14, 15-18: limited identification of devices with basic and simple comment on the effect; some devices and some comment; clear explanations of the effects of language and structure; thorough and confident selection with a detailed exploration of effects; subtle and discriminating selection with a perceptive analysis.","AO4 is printed out of 18 in five levels (1-3, 4-7, 8-11, 12-15, 16-18) and AO5 out of 12 in five levels (1-2, 3-4, 5-7, 8-10, 11-12). Those are the tariffs of Component 2 Question 2 and of Component 3 Assignment B. AO4 rewards communication, the grasp of purpose and of the expectations and requirements of the intended reader, and the use of form, tone and register; AO5 rewards expression and the ordering of information and ideas, vocabulary and spelling, paragraphing, punctuation and sentence structures.","The Component 1 transactional task is marked out of 27 for AO4 and 18 for AO5, and the specification prints no band boundaries at those totals - only the grids above. The qualities being judged are the same; the paper's marks simply run to a higher ceiling, and each level takes a proportionally wider band of it. So any list of 27-mark or 18-mark bands you are shown, including one worked out from the printed grids, is somebody's arithmetic rather than the specification's own: learn the descriptors and treat the scaled numbers as approximate.","Best fit: markers award within a range by balancing strengths and weaknesses, and an answer with features of two levels is placed by professional judgement - a passage at Level 4 in an answer that mostly sits at Level 3 gives a high Level 3.","Reading the ladders as a to-do list: AO1 asks what does this mean; AO2 asks what does this do to me and why here; AO3 asks what do these two share and how differently do they say it; AO4 asks who am I writing to and what for; AO5 asks is the range deliberate and the accuracy checked."]}],"built":{"at":"2026-09-11T23:13:19Z","by":"claude-code","models":["claude-opus-5","claude-opus-5"],"promptVersion":"2026-09-10.2","judge":{"score":0.85,"notes":"All six faults from the first examination are fixed - the alarm 'bereavement' is now correctly called a metaphor and the Passage C image 'looked like a page with the important lines cut out' is correctly a simile; Question 9 is reset on Passage C as a prose stand-in for Part 2, so the Component 2 Question 1 text type now matches the 30-mark AO1 12 / AO2 18 tariff; Question 10 carries AO3 alone; idea[0] no longer pins 'retelling or paraphrasing' to a named band and matches the kit's own AO1 ladder; extras[4].items[2] and [3] now print only the AO4/18 and AO5/12 grids the specification actually gives and say explicitly that any 27- or 18-mark bands are somebody's arithmetic; the Question 1 hints no longer hand over the 45 and 30; and lesson.examples[4] now delivers both the colon and the dash it promises. I re-solved all fourteen questions and checked roughly 64 quoted phrases - about 30 specification wordings (the six AO statements, the six aims, the three Key qualification features, the AO1/AO2/AO4/AO5 level descriptors and band boundaries, the textual reference and example definitions, best fit, 'some figures have been rounded up or down', 'no penalty for exceeding this recommendation') and about 34 lifted from the three original passages - and every one is verbatim or a fair paraphrase. All arithmetic reconciles: 23+30+22+45+30=150, Component 1 11+12+22+45=90, Component 2/3 30+30=60, reading 75 and writing 75, and the plan's 6+34+4=44 minutes sit inside the 1 hour 30 advised for Section A. Copyright is comfortably met: Passages A, B and C are original to the kit, no anthology text is named or quoted anywhere, and the longest run of any real set text is zero words. Publishable as it stands; tidy the two model-answer sentences in Questions 5 and 9 and relabel Questions 12 and 13 to d=2 at the next pass."}}}} };
